https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b013de22d2d69e366946136353c4c2dcd2fb6340","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/b013de22d2d69e366946136353c4c2dcd2fb6340","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b013de22d2d69e366946136353c4c2dcd2fb6340/comments","author":{"login":"erni-a","id":229583271,"node_id":"U_kgDODa8ppw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/229583271?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a","html_url":"https://github.com/erni-a","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"e4a695e68fd24dee2c64b82dd14d6e33aa3cdbde","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e4a695e68fd24dee2c64b82dd14d6e33aa3cdbde","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e4a695e68fd24dee2c64b82dd14d6e33aa3cdbde"}]},{"sha":"e4a695e68fd24dee2c64b82dd14d6e33aa3cdbde","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGU0YTY5NWU2OGZkMjRkZWUyYzY0YjgyZGQxNGQ2ZTMzYWEzY2RiZGU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T21:17:25Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T21:17:25Z"},"message":"fix(reports): anchor working-on lane keywords to word starts (#773)\n\n## What\n\n`working_on` lane keywords now match at a **word start** instead of\nanywhere in the string.\n\n## The bug is bigger than the flake that surfaced it\n\nLane matching runs *before* the memory-type fallback, so any substring\nhit silently outranks the type. The matcher was a bare `k in text`, and\nthe collisions are ordinary English:\n\n| content | contains | wrong lane |\n|---|---|---|\n| customer **feedback** reviewed | `dba` | Operating |\n| **ownership** / **partnership** | `ship` | Building |\n| **install** the CLI | `stall` | Operating |\n| **distrust** between teams | `trust` | Governing |\n\nA `decision` saying \"customer feedback reviewed\" was filed under\nOperating rather than Governing. Checked the full keyword list against\n`/usr/share/dict/words` — `\"ship\"` alone matches **1044** words it was\nnever meant to (airship, leadership, relationship…).\n\n## How it surfaced\n\n`tests/test_reports.py` seeds a `uuid4().hex` id into content. `d`, `b`\nand `a` are all hex digits, so ~0.145% of ids contain `\"dba\"` — an\nOperating keyword. That is the ~0.6%-per-run flake behind\n`test_report_internal_group_durable_filter` failing on [CI run\n31634…](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/actions/runs/31636233722)\nwith `Governing 1` instead of `2` (2/1/0 → 1/1/1). The identical commit\npassed on re-run with no code change.\n\n## The fix\n\nOne precompiled pattern per lane, each alternative anchored `\\b` at the\nword **start** but deliberately **not** at the word end. The trailing\nhalf is what would break things: the list relies on deliberate prefixes,\nso `\\bcomplian\\b` would stop matching \"compliance\". Verified the\nintended prefixes still match — `complian`→compliance,\n`reliab`→reliability, `build`→building, `audit`→auditing, and the\nhyphenated `on-call`.\n\n**Trade accepted:** prefixed forms no longer match (`rebuild`,\n`redeploy`, `unhealthy`, `unreliable`). They fall through to the\nmemory-type lane — the intended default for text the heuristic can't\nplace, and a better failure than matching inside arbitrary words.\nFlagging it rather than burying it, since it's a real behaviour change.\n\n## Two changes, deliberately\n\nEither alone would stop the flake; they fix different things.\n\n1. **`reports.py`** — the product behaviour above.\n2. **`tests/test_reports.py`** — seeds a decimal-only unique suffix\ninstead of conftest's hex `uid()`, so these assertions no longer depend\non random data at all. Digits cannot spell a lane keyword. Only this\nmodule asserts on lanes, so nothing else is affected.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe new test fails without the fix. Reverting only the matcher line,\nleaving everything else in place:\n\n```\nAssertionError: {'Building':  {'count': 1, ...},\n                 'Governing': {'count': 0, 'items': []},\n                 'Operating': {'count': 2, ...}}\n```\n\n— all three `decision` rows pulled out of Governing. With the fix:\n3/0/0.\n\nFull suite **4355 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed. `mypy` clean. `ruff\ncheck` and `ruff format --check` clean on `core-api/src/`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"ac2f1014cb37f588dd8ece977420678b3d3a02a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/ac2f1014cb37f588dd8ece977420678b3d3a02a0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/e4a695e68fd24dee2c64b82dd14d6e33aa3cdbde","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfOLlCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvpYQAJ7+KST0G1WxiAmQNZQzeU0E\nAuSG+evR2SsX0yh3Q9JE/eNIR24tln21AVTxnzhi9QFJueLpw6MlBXRVPonA5fCW\nWFfvnBMJ6q5EBfM0TsN1qEFtTRxWZu7adB0Ex9XURAqXqjMUUj3/e1zL1rFuTSMM\nSkEcffj5elXaet0clvRwKkxORaWvo1Tg0hgneHr5d6sTbqvgsMIOtAT6fE08pj8z\n1QcH57XZXky8T6jt9/pMaKmbQbltoHH9YUmYM9SaWhAV5wEGe2CyKgoSBg0emovm\nkHxmigMBiagRfCPA6hdrEY+iANqMapotBXWy2TLZ0KO3zubsZ6UfHpfAIy8E6SCr\nZI35ZJpHhKa0VhWYipURbIQWoal7bSxAYehuRd2ttO97P/vlaFA4cK7GQm69R2g6\nsA+s4ba4WaPZEIvhnENewP5ewxNRJ7ltmxxp5zALA/j7qbRSJuhWcaO0l0HfoTpv\nsPEh69vhALaJxVx1B2gqSgvmlbFGMPwNwW7WNjRRfxHddtgjYbxUGc0T27bmI06F\npbYCXZ0d873+H33T5m0GQf7Z+8ip2lEQu5QomXzDYJeE7Odv//YImCDYGR31t1N0\no8NzJhQmDj/m0baAvH3HErY0WXnknAaOzXRy9JBUTFZZgRew1W1rDk2BTjqixZUX\ntHFqjIrvabqMNTQzSuLj\n=Ie9i\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree ac2f1014cb37f588dd8ece977420678b3d3a02a0\nparent ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786569445 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786569445 +0300\n\nfix(reports): anchor working-on lane keywords to word starts (#773)\n\n## What\n\n`working_on` lane keywords now match at a **word start** instead of\nanywhere in the string.\n\n## The bug is bigger than the flake that surfaced it\n\nLane matching runs *before* the memory-type fallback, so any substring\nhit silently outranks the type. The matcher was a bare `k in text`, and\nthe collisions are ordinary English:\n\n| content | contains | wrong lane |\n|---|---|---|\n| customer **feedback** reviewed | `dba` | Operating |\n| **ownership** / **partnership** | `ship` | Building |\n| **install** the CLI | `stall` | Operating |\n| **distrust** between teams | `trust` | Governing |\n\nA `decision` saying \"customer feedback reviewed\" was filed under\nOperating rather than Governing. Checked the full keyword list against\n`/usr/share/dict/words` — `\"ship\"` alone matches **1044** words it was\nnever meant to (airship, leadership, relationship…).\n\n## How it surfaced\n\n`tests/test_reports.py` seeds a `uuid4().hex` id into content. `d`, `b`\nand `a` are all hex digits, so ~0.145% of ids contain `\"dba\"` — an\nOperating keyword. That is the ~0.6%-per-run flake behind\n`test_report_internal_group_durable_filter` failing on [CI run\n31634…](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/actions/runs/31636233722)\nwith `Governing 1` instead of `2` (2/1/0 → 1/1/1). The identical commit\npassed on re-run with no code change.\n\n## The fix\n\nOne precompiled pattern per lane, each alternative anchored `\\b` at the\nword **start** but deliberately **not** at the word end. The trailing\nhalf is what would break things: the list relies on deliberate prefixes,\nso `\\bcomplian\\b` would stop matching \"compliance\". Verified the\nintended prefixes still match — `complian`→compliance,\n`reliab`→reliability, `build`→building, `audit`→auditing, and the\nhyphenated `on-call`.\n\n**Trade accepted:** prefixed forms no longer match (`rebuild`,\n`redeploy`, `unhealthy`, `unreliable`). They fall through to the\nmemory-type lane — the intended default for text the heuristic can't\nplace, and a better failure than matching inside arbitrary words.\nFlagging it rather than burying it, since it's a real behaviour change.\n\n## Two changes, deliberately\n\nEither alone would stop the flake; they fix different things.\n\n1. **`reports.py`** — the product behaviour above.\n2. **`tests/test_reports.py`** — seeds a decimal-only unique suffix\ninstead of conftest's hex `uid()`, so these assertions no longer depend\non random data at all. Digits cannot spell a lane keyword. Only this\nmodule asserts on lanes, so nothing else is affected.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe new test fails without the fix. Reverting only the matcher line,\nleaving everything else in place:\n\n```\nAssertionError: {'Building':  {'count': 1, ...},\n                 'Governing': {'count': 0, 'items': []},\n                 'Operating': {'count': 2, ...}}\n```\n\n— all three `decision` rows pulled out of Governing. With the fix:\n3/0/0.\n\nFull suite **4355 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed. `mypy` clean. `ruff\ncheck` and `ruff format --check` clean on `core-api/src/`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T21:17:28Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e4a695e68fd24dee2c64b82dd14d6e33aa3cdbde","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e4a695e68fd24dee2c64b82dd14d6e33aa3cdbde","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e4a695e68fd24dee2c64b82dd14d6e33aa3cdbde/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd"}]},{"sha":"ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGJhODMyOGFlYTRkNWQ1ZWFmYjA4MWQ0OWEwNDNiYzg4NGNjM2ZlZmQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T20:59:27Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T20:59:27Z"},"message":"ci: enforce the coverage floors both packages already declare (#769)\n\ncore-api and core-storage-api each declare `[tool.coverage.report]\nfail_under = 50`, and CI enforced **neither**.\n\nThe main test step collects coverage from the repo root with no\n`--cov-config`, and there is no root `pyproject.toml`, so coverage\ndiscovers no config and both floors are silently ignored. Demonstrated\nbefore fixing: running three tests with those exact `--cov` flags passes\nwith no floor check at all, when coverage across all of `core_api` from\nthree tests is obviously far below 50%.\n\nFollow-up to #768, which fixed the same defect for core-operations via\n`--cov-config`.\n\n## Measured first\n\nEnabling a floor that isn't met would just break the build, so I ran the\nfull suite before wiring anything — 4,273 tests, ~6 minutes:\n\n| package | statements | missed | coverage |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| core-api | 15,156 | 2,641 | **83%** |\n| core-storage-api | 5,558 | 1,808 | **67%** |\n| combined | 20,714 | 4,449 | 79% |\n\nBoth clear the declared floor comfortably, so no declaration needed\nadjusting and nothing had to be escalated.\n\n## Per package, not combined\n\nThis is the one real design call. The blend is dominated by core-api —\n15,156 statements against 5,558 — so a single combined floor would mask\na collapse in the smaller package. Concretely: **core-storage-api\nfalling from 67% to 30% still leaves a combined ~69%**, comfortably over\na 50% blended floor. Two declarations mean two floors, and that is what\nthe code already says.\n\n`--rcfile` points each check at that package's own `pyproject.toml`, so\nthe number lives in exactly one place. Hardcoding `50` in the workflow\nwould let it drift from the declaration it exists to enforce.\n\n## No root config\n\nA root `pyproject.toml` holding a shared floor was the obvious\nalternative, and I checked whether it was safe rather than assuming: one\ncontaining only `[tool.coverage.report]` leaves ruff discovery alone —\n`common/constants.py` still resolves `line-length = 88` from\n`common/ruff.toml`, and the root `tests/` tree is unaffected — because\nruff skips a `pyproject.toml` with no `[tool.ruff]` section and keeps\nwalking. So that option was viable, just less faithful. This adds no\nroot file.\n\n## Verified both directions\n\nThe step reuses the data file the pytest run just wrote, so the suite is\n**not** run twice.\n\n```\ncore-api            exit=0   TOTAL 82%\ncore-storage-api    exit=0   TOTAL 67%\n```\n\nAnd it genuinely fails on a regression — temporarily raising\ncore-storage-api's floor to 70:\n\n```\nexit=2\nCoverage failure: total of 67 is less than fail-under=70\n```\n\nWorth noting how this stayed hidden so long: plain `coverage report`\nprints **nothing** when the floor passes and only speaks up on failure,\nso the absence of a \"Required test coverage\" line looked identical to\nsuccess. The exit code is the only signal. That is also why my first\nattempt at the core-operations fix in #768 looked right while still\nbeing inert.\n\n`actionlint` reports one finding on this file (SC2086 at line 70, the\npre-existing install step) both before and after, so this introduces\nnone. Rebased onto main after #768 landed; the new step coexists with\nthe core-operations steps it added.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"b1d027a3d1818adddf26cb895f7f3f616b11f0ce","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/b1d027a3d1818adddf26cb895f7f3f616b11f0ce"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfN6vCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAhwYQAFDPJxP9NWET/EMYL4iKzv/U\naQLIiB0MifGjDKCK5p6YU7bMx0ymKN0x8UV6TAJsbUtb2FqqnhZ0KVolqnISl63y\np3/t+8p8ln0RpPTQwQLagM7vyKP5fZUVOQw/8s3GACrXt6Gq43iz67argReZ7K5O\nUNJ8mjqTiiDNq9sZY8VzZhfViZm4LP1yHjn07i9Zg+EQV6f2RDIVo64uMKkt+K0S\nzPjvIo11QQbA7pA8A0Dfhl1eUqqrJ9z7nSY9vq1ewKT2wupPkY4Qx20GHHzAVjqy\nYysaU/NA4NUJ4k/QysvtAjZ/+1Uk18Ze6Q1a3cb+ikxt9xfqbEEkZX+wXUw3EwMb\nBkbnO5Guy4vCX/fXmh/0y/p9NUoBkh7HPyUcb9pD2HTsVVGOwk1WuyQtqt0QaiMH\nBQAtXTSsMzjm7HbHJlZ8SamFqL6N8QD3avF4W2CDxFwVI0kdjY+QRrG9l04iHSof\n4JI9XJ8lPWhmlO9eskhGr0U6zdiwfnF/2IE96SCCvsEoYrAo79oTJNmG5nRduw/x\nnV0noMsQTMSHD7wizubd+WLZrf8gQ7S8Cj1UC1wuXI3ZgXlcRGDAtZZyiHD3D3MT\nAcQB/2f7TiDm0W1N0qUxxAzI/8zaQrl2FU7rBozG64nWSIuaQsokbgEJ90fJEeI6\nSDKun6W7Jsqv1DayLRva\n=+Fvu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree b1d027a3d1818adddf26cb895f7f3f616b11f0ce\nparent c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786568367 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786568367 +0300\n\nci: enforce the coverage floors both packages already declare (#769)\n\ncore-api and core-storage-api each declare `[tool.coverage.report]\nfail_under = 50`, and CI enforced **neither**.\n\nThe main test step collects coverage from the repo root with no\n`--cov-config`, and there is no root `pyproject.toml`, so coverage\ndiscovers no config and both floors are silently ignored. Demonstrated\nbefore fixing: running three tests with those exact `--cov` flags passes\nwith no floor check at all, when coverage across all of `core_api` from\nthree tests is obviously far below 50%.\n\nFollow-up to #768, which fixed the same defect for core-operations via\n`--cov-config`.\n\n## Measured first\n\nEnabling a floor that isn't met would just break the build, so I ran the\nfull suite before wiring anything — 4,273 tests, ~6 minutes:\n\n| package | statements | missed | coverage |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| core-api | 15,156 | 2,641 | **83%** |\n| core-storage-api | 5,558 | 1,808 | **67%** |\n| combined | 20,714 | 4,449 | 79% |\n\nBoth clear the declared floor comfortably, so no declaration needed\nadjusting and nothing had to be escalated.\n\n## Per package, not combined\n\nThis is the one real design call. The blend is dominated by core-api —\n15,156 statements against 5,558 — so a single combined floor would mask\na collapse in the smaller package. Concretely: **core-storage-api\nfalling from 67% to 30% still leaves a combined ~69%**, comfortably over\na 50% blended floor. Two declarations mean two floors, and that is what\nthe code already says.\n\n`--rcfile` points each check at that package's own `pyproject.toml`, so\nthe number lives in exactly one place. Hardcoding `50` in the workflow\nwould let it drift from the declaration it exists to enforce.\n\n## No root config\n\nA root `pyproject.toml` holding a shared floor was the obvious\nalternative, and I checked whether it was safe rather than assuming: one\ncontaining only `[tool.coverage.report]` leaves ruff discovery alone —\n`common/constants.py` still resolves `line-length = 88` from\n`common/ruff.toml`, and the root `tests/` tree is unaffected — because\nruff skips a `pyproject.toml` with no `[tool.ruff]` section and keeps\nwalking. So that option was viable, just less faithful. This adds no\nroot file.\n\n## Verified both directions\n\nThe step reuses the data file the pytest run just wrote, so the suite is\n**not** run twice.\n\n```\ncore-api            exit=0   TOTAL 82%\ncore-storage-api    exit=0   TOTAL 67%\n```\n\nAnd it genuinely fails on a regression — temporarily raising\ncore-storage-api's floor to 70:\n\n```\nexit=2\nCoverage failure: total of 67 is less than fail-under=70\n```\n\nWorth noting how this stayed hidden so long: plain `coverage report`\nprints **nothing** when the floor passes and only speaks up on failure,\nso the absence of a \"Required test coverage\" line looked identical to\nsuccess. The exit code is the only signal. That is also why my first\nattempt at the core-operations fix in #768 looked right while still\nbeing inert.\n\n`actionlint` reports one finding on this file (SC2086 at line 70, the\npre-existing install step) both before and after, so this introduces\nnone. Rebased onto main after #768 landed; the new step coexists with\nthe core-operations steps it added.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T20:59:30Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ba8328aea4d5d5eafb081d49a043bc884cc3fefd/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9"}]},{"sha":"c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGM1ZTA1NDNlYTBjN2QwOTE1MjViMWMxZDM1MmJhZjVlMmU3NmQzZDk","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T19:14:17Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T19:14:17Z"},"message":"fix(events): declare the embed-backfill subscription in the manifest (#772)\n\nFound while preparing to provision the Pub/Sub topic for #767 — before\nthe flag was flipped, so nothing broke.\n\n## The gap\n\n#767 added `bus.subscribe(Topics.Lifecycle.EMBED_BACKFILL_REQUESTED,\n...)` to core-worker's `register_consumers()`, but never added it to the\ngenerator's hand-maintained list. So\n`common/events/events_manifest.json` does not record that core-worker\nconsumes that topic.\n\nThat matters more than a stale file, because of what reads it. From the\ngenerator's own docstring:\n\n> It is the contract that lets the enterprise repo's\n`check_pubsub_provisioning.py` fail CI when OSS adds a consumed topic\nwithout the matching Terraform subscription — **the gap that took\nstaging down when `memclaw.lifecycle.insights-requested` shipped\nunprovisioned.**\n\nAn incomplete manifest means that guard is blind to the topic, so the\nenterprise side could ship without a subscription and nothing would\nobject. The exact class of incident the manifest exists to prevent.\n\n## Why nothing caught it\n\nThere are two mechanisms, and this fell between them:\n\n- **Lifecycle** topics are captured *dynamically*, by invoking the\nshared registration helpers in `common.events.lifecycle_handlers`\nagainst a recording bus. Anything registered through a helper is picked\nup automatically.\n- **Directly-registered** subscribes can't be captured that way (OSS CI\ndoesn't install the worker package), so they're hand-listed in\n`_MEMORY_DIRECT` and cross-checked against the consumer files by\n`test_memory_direct_matches_consumer_files`.\n\nThat check's regex was `bus\\.subscribe\\(\\s*Topics\\.Memory\\.([A-Z_]+)` —\n**Memory only**. A *lifecycle* topic registered *directly* matched\nneither mechanism. #767 created the first such case, because it\nregisters in core-worker deliberately: the work lives in\n`core_worker.backfill`, and core-api — which implements the same\nlifecycle adapter protocol — cannot run it.\n\n## The fix\n\n1. Add the topic to the direct list, and regenerate the manifest with\n`scripts/gen_events_manifest.py` (it says \"do not edit by hand\", so I\ndidn't).\n2. Widen the guard's regex to `Topics\\.(Memory|Lifecycle)\\.`, closing\nthe seam for the next person.\n3. Rename `_MEMORY_DIRECT` → `_DIRECT_SUBSCRIBES`. It no longer holds\nonly memory-pipeline topics, and a name asserting otherwise is precisely\nthe drift that caused this.\n\n## Verified the guard now bites\n\nRemoving the new entry while leaving the `bus.subscribe` in place — i.e.\nreproducing #767's exact omission:\n\n```\nAssertionError: core-worker: _DIRECT_SUBSCRIBES in scripts/gen_events_manifest.py is out of\nsync with core-worker/src/core_worker/consumer.py.\nFound ['memclaw.lifecycle.embed-backfill-requested', 'memclaw.memory.embed-requested', ...],\nexpected ['memclaw.memory.embed-requested', 'memclaw.memory.enrich-requested'].\n```\n\nBefore this change, that same state passed.\n\nThe only manifest change is one added line:\n\n```diff\n     \"core-worker\": [\n       \"memclaw.lifecycle.archive-expired-requested\",\n       \"memclaw.lifecycle.archive-stale-requested\",\n+      \"memclaw.lifecycle.embed-backfill-requested\",\n```\n\n86 events tests pass; `ruff check` clean.\n\n## What follows\n\nWith the manifest correct, the enterprise `pubsub-provisioning` check\nwill now require a matching Terraform subscription — which is the next\nstep and lives in caura-memclaw-enterprise: topic, subscription and DLQ\nfor `memclaw.lifecycle.embed-backfill-requested`, staging first.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7295664157e6bfba07fde2949cc4cb720419abda","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/7295664157e6bfba07fde2949cc4cb720419abda"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfMYJCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAkxEQAI/Jo9OQ+GSmiVMzxi7y3ZCb\nIYM8oYihtvzUA5vjKXKjK5dFSvmKn6EOXBq1Bgms2hJvbwsrTloruot6QeH5TMET\nQNPQ86zkItEe9UbPpHKGfCf3Z7Uw+h0pgYDUxEEOfzEpzKgzXbGH2+Cx9A+M5kkA\n6eU+Xj+M62E5QNqUbGpx9ZTXESZM7u8eJzLsRg4WIjLC3G8cu0MXlH80GpplZFzS\nJK+2P1QVtfv8YjpmUZjAutDzBOEKcnx+s6rc9ICeXliZu1AtQUiyBewfOoIZOJLp\nk2xcbXgJjm0/40L2geo5gF9OhRMe7BkOAvIBZqeG007uoDAer4IkQk/8R/YJSjJQ\nCvyVYJ2YopX1u17tvU6+gwbvT/xcybayKHbqLH/reogqvUll3lVAgQkkWoXJ3ezF\nv9TYoFyAkzYuAzku0w8IR7QkL+SZM/MfRTHireiAHM0MnShZvgxVElty/0RZWfUQ\nqwAcj4dwsRU47IZ33TMGeEBmXzKVZ5GUuKFzo08Y624rS+jt59DWp4OU6n2muOrB\nTW0nyAUDX0ufl1RBei1F7nqOf3o9BLYAhrRmXyZmCWMDFvl96JwkBBOCO2Wjim//\nfJ6i+8L0SXCU7ppuQqoUo104je+fbTPdkA0HpCIxYTJ+x11xurZ/gOAP4nzjbVSK\n3BmQ7WwrFIayAXC4EIYf\n=BmBi\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 7295664157e6bfba07fde2949cc4cb720419abda\nparent 0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786562057 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786562057 +0300\n\nfix(events): declare the embed-backfill subscription in the manifest (#772)\n\nFound while preparing to provision the Pub/Sub topic for #767 — before\nthe flag was flipped, so nothing broke.\n\n## The gap\n\n#767 added `bus.subscribe(Topics.Lifecycle.EMBED_BACKFILL_REQUESTED,\n...)` to core-worker's `register_consumers()`, but never added it to the\ngenerator's hand-maintained list. So\n`common/events/events_manifest.json` does not record that core-worker\nconsumes that topic.\n\nThat matters more than a stale file, because of what reads it. From the\ngenerator's own docstring:\n\n> It is the contract that lets the enterprise repo's\n`check_pubsub_provisioning.py` fail CI when OSS adds a consumed topic\nwithout the matching Terraform subscription — **the gap that took\nstaging down when `memclaw.lifecycle.insights-requested` shipped\nunprovisioned.**\n\nAn incomplete manifest means that guard is blind to the topic, so the\nenterprise side could ship without a subscription and nothing would\nobject. The exact class of incident the manifest exists to prevent.\n\n## Why nothing caught it\n\nThere are two mechanisms, and this fell between them:\n\n- **Lifecycle** topics are captured *dynamically*, by invoking the\nshared registration helpers in `common.events.lifecycle_handlers`\nagainst a recording bus. Anything registered through a helper is picked\nup automatically.\n- **Directly-registered** subscribes can't be captured that way (OSS CI\ndoesn't install the worker package), so they're hand-listed in\n`_MEMORY_DIRECT` and cross-checked against the consumer files by\n`test_memory_direct_matches_consumer_files`.\n\nThat check's regex was `bus\\.subscribe\\(\\s*Topics\\.Memory\\.([A-Z_]+)` —\n**Memory only**. A *lifecycle* topic registered *directly* matched\nneither mechanism. #767 created the first such case, because it\nregisters in core-worker deliberately: the work lives in\n`core_worker.backfill`, and core-api — which implements the same\nlifecycle adapter protocol — cannot run it.\n\n## The fix\n\n1. Add the topic to the direct list, and regenerate the manifest with\n`scripts/gen_events_manifest.py` (it says \"do not edit by hand\", so I\ndidn't).\n2. Widen the guard's regex to `Topics\\.(Memory|Lifecycle)\\.`, closing\nthe seam for the next person.\n3. Rename `_MEMORY_DIRECT` → `_DIRECT_SUBSCRIBES`. It no longer holds\nonly memory-pipeline topics, and a name asserting otherwise is precisely\nthe drift that caused this.\n\n## Verified the guard now bites\n\nRemoving the new entry while leaving the `bus.subscribe` in place — i.e.\nreproducing #767's exact omission:\n\n```\nAssertionError: core-worker: _DIRECT_SUBSCRIBES in scripts/gen_events_manifest.py is out of\nsync with core-worker/src/core_worker/consumer.py.\nFound ['memclaw.lifecycle.embed-backfill-requested', 'memclaw.memory.embed-requested', ...],\nexpected ['memclaw.memory.embed-requested', 'memclaw.memory.enrich-requested'].\n```\n\nBefore this change, that same state passed.\n\nThe only manifest change is one added line:\n\n```diff\n     \"core-worker\": [\n       \"memclaw.lifecycle.archive-expired-requested\",\n       \"memclaw.lifecycle.archive-stale-requested\",\n+      \"memclaw.lifecycle.embed-backfill-requested\",\n```\n\n86 events tests pass; `ruff check` clean.\n\n## What follows\n\nWith the manifest correct, the enterprise `pubsub-provisioning` check\nwill now require a matching Terraform subscription — which is the next\nstep and lives in caura-memclaw-enterprise: topic, subscription and DLQ\nfor `memclaw.lifecycle.embed-backfill-requested`, staging first.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T19:14:20Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c5e0543ea0c7d091525b1c1d352baf5e2e76d3d9/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa"}]},{"sha":"0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDBlNGM5ZjgwMDlhODQ3MDdhYTk3ZjEyYzI4MGMzMDM0OTNkYjM2ZmE","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-12T19:03:20Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T19:03:20Z"},"message":"perf(contradiction): batch the Path C entity-aware per-candidate LLM judge (A61) (#771)\n\n## What\n\nFinishes **A61** (the OpenAI-cost bug). #770 batched the Path A semantic\nper-candidate LLM fan-out — the measured cost driver. This PR does the\nsame for the remaining fan-out: **Path C's forward entity-overlap\ndetection**.\n\nPath C's forward loop issued **one LLM judge call per candidate**:\n- `_llm_entity_aware_contradiction_check` for candidates with resolved\nentity context on both sides,\n- `_llm_contradiction_check` (base) for the rest,\n\nfanning out up to `_ENTITY_LINKS_DETECTION_FETCH_MAX_CANDIDATES` calls\nper newly-written memory. For a popular subject that is a large\nper-write cost — the same shape #770 fixed on Path A.\n\n## How\n\nCollapse the fan-out into **at most two batched calls**:\n- a new `_llm_entity_aware_contradiction_check_batch` covering the\nentity-aware group, each candidate carrying its **own** resolved entity\ncontext so the authoritative same-subject grounding is preserved per\ncandidate;\n- the existing #770 `_llm_contradiction_check_batch` for the base group.\n\nEverything downstream is unchanged:\n- judge-selection logic (entity-aware vs base) is byte-for-byte the\nsame;\n- each raw judgment runs through `_judge_contradiction`, so the safety\ngates are identical to the per-candidate path;\n- `results` stays aligned to `candidates` and keeps the `(verdict,\nconfidence)` / `Exception` shape the existing loop handles — a\nwhole-batch failure defaults its group to *skipped*, never fabricated;\n- a **single candidate keeps the direct call** (mirrors the Path A `len\n== 1` special case).\n\nPath C forward detection is **status-only** (no engine conflict-record\nwiring — that lives in Path A), so behavior is identical whether\n`contradiction_write_conflict_record` is on or off. Serves both the\nflag-off and flag-on arms.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Updated the three multi-candidate Path C tests (caura_130\npreflight-cap, caura_131 detection-cap + a1_17 matched-set) from\nper-candidate `call_count == len(cands)` assertions to batched-call\nassertions.\n- Added `test_a61_multi_candidate_entity_aware_batches_once_and_flags` —\nthree entity-aware candidates → one batched call, contradiction effect\nstill applied.\n- Full unit suite green (2366 passed); no new mypy errors in the changed\nfile; ruff clean.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"117e3e58a497e1f67f4593647c7dae9de15b723c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/117e3e58a497e1f67f4593647c7dae9de15b723c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfMN4CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAosQQAGeEVM0eGiSU3KQ09gIrBuSJ\nZeT9WQtE5MMaHEIWZYZGd2qMsdNjvnmHH4GbsBEv5NfckUA/715tNgJKOxW7PGvu\nNuzYjDlfbUcO+GPrbmutZav5nu+R4Zi4r2PCs+JzS7GGibBHuGgPzt1LqQLauDkC\nz2nWCY+Gazbqec755o3Lj+bYCXpL/mKn6OFLlB0WwTNsKB7FEYHFwVA4OkRq7mc6\nL8H7hBa0JfQIdbuXC3l28uzR6yl5Xgo7fXCXWT8E7vMaB6eYRTqRTKj7yUkp2GmL\nW7SQubQNt9p68EUUjW5EUWCYcrWkIkGYypxMvNCh0v8yxIrmayencSJtZClNs7oJ\nUSQnOib7vYbImbD4XrRRwkC3JzZ4CEM7kJpk2hDLs4XwiOQWMppRN80yukFo3KMi\nlMVPqaqd+5sdT6yWS3lpem24qSDPCdY5ifkBZwZBT6j+V3qur8LJmdmsOrYgYEuK\nDJLNOweWuS4sJXaRIZwmxFk7O7EHzfq6Xc2o3XgohSCPeMZ03xZnkGWVo4XTCCvZ\nIOJPGN4wPwqM1QD3/yVzyDiJSFtm0s2RyEFAQ3Eok2nXGVdNcIqT69lCiX+V6eVW\n9wBtNqndGaufS+fyqearr6i+jid75DWCX8NSfcIOcSzyGD6OoGJSm7hmXXPES5oN\npo9fODwzMVij9fE0SRcE\n=RFt3\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 117e3e58a497e1f67f4593647c7dae9de15b723c\nparent 86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1786561400 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786561400 +0300\n\nperf(contradiction): batch the Path C entity-aware per-candidate LLM judge (A61) (#771)\n\n## What\n\nFinishes **A61** (the OpenAI-cost bug). #770 batched the Path A semantic\nper-candidate LLM fan-out — the measured cost driver. This PR does the\nsame for the remaining fan-out: **Path C's forward entity-overlap\ndetection**.\n\nPath C's forward loop issued **one LLM judge call per candidate**:\n- `_llm_entity_aware_contradiction_check` for candidates with resolved\nentity context on both sides,\n- `_llm_contradiction_check` (base) for the rest,\n\nfanning out up to `_ENTITY_LINKS_DETECTION_FETCH_MAX_CANDIDATES` calls\nper newly-written memory. For a popular subject that is a large\nper-write cost — the same shape #770 fixed on Path A.\n\n## How\n\nCollapse the fan-out into **at most two batched calls**:\n- a new `_llm_entity_aware_contradiction_check_batch` covering the\nentity-aware group, each candidate carrying its **own** resolved entity\ncontext so the authoritative same-subject grounding is preserved per\ncandidate;\n- the existing #770 `_llm_contradiction_check_batch` for the base group.\n\nEverything downstream is unchanged:\n- judge-selection logic (entity-aware vs base) is byte-for-byte the\nsame;\n- each raw judgment runs through `_judge_contradiction`, so the safety\ngates are identical to the per-candidate path;\n- `results` stays aligned to `candidates` and keeps the `(verdict,\nconfidence)` / `Exception` shape the existing loop handles — a\nwhole-batch failure defaults its group to *skipped*, never fabricated;\n- a **single candidate keeps the direct call** (mirrors the Path A `len\n== 1` special case).\n\nPath C forward detection is **status-only** (no engine conflict-record\nwiring — that lives in Path A), so behavior is identical whether\n`contradiction_write_conflict_record` is on or off. Serves both the\nflag-off and flag-on arms.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Updated the three multi-candidate Path C tests (caura_130\npreflight-cap, caura_131 detection-cap + a1_17 matched-set) from\nper-candidate `call_count == len(cands)` assertions to batched-call\nassertions.\n- Added `test_a61_multi_candidate_entity_aware_batches_once_and_flags` —\nthree entity-aware candidates → one batched call, contradiction effect\nstill applied.\n- Full unit suite green (2366 passed); no new mypy errors in the changed\nfile; ruff clean.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T19:03:23Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0e4c9f8009a84707aa97f12c280c303493db36fa/comments","author":{"login":"arkash20","id":55495209,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1NDk1MjA5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55495209?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20","html_url":"https://github.com/arkash20","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0"}]},{"sha":"86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDg2YzI0Yzg3MmY2M2Q0ZTk3ZGJmNmEyOTQ4YjE2MTZjYTI5ODMwYzA","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-12T18:22:58Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T18:22:58Z"},"message":"perf(contradiction): batch the semantic per-candidate LLM judge (A61) (#770)\n\n## The cost bug\n\nThe semantic contradiction path fired **one `complete_json` (gpt-nano)\ncall per candidate**, up to the candidate cap (20). So each\nnewly-embedded memory triggered ~20 nano judge calls — and prod (~442\nmemories/hr, ~86% one tenant's own agent fleet) was making **~10.8k\nOpenAI calls/hr** on only ~56 searches + 67 writes (~90 calls/op) →\n**~$250 every 2–3 days**. Not a loop — the *designed* per-candidate\nfan-out × memory volume.\n\n## The fix — one batched judge call\n\n- **`_llm_contradiction_check_batch`**: a single `complete_json` listing\nthe NEW statement + all candidates; returns one raw judgment per\ncandidate, **aligned to input order**; a missing/malformed entry\ndefaults to a **safe non-contradiction** (never fabricates or drops\none).\n- **`_detect` Path 2**: a single candidate keeps the direct\nper-candidate call; **≥2 candidates (the prod cost driver) go through\nthe batched call**. Both feed the **same** `_judge_contradiction` gates\n(Gate 1 cross-subject, Gate 2 non_conflict_reason, `_pick_older`\ndirection) → verdict/status/supersede semantics unchanged (the golden\ndifferential test still passes).\n- The batched prompt also emits per-candidate\n**`relationship`/`diagnosis`**, so the A55 engine-ON conflict-record\nwrite can reuse it with **no second LLM call** — **one call serves\nboth** the flag-off (legacy detector) and flag-on (engine) arms.\n\n**~N× fewer LLM calls per memory (up to ~20×).** Cheaper stopgap if\nneeded: drop the candidate cap 20→5. Key-splitting is attribution only,\nnot cost.\n\n## No-degradation evidence\n\n- **Deterministic:** 169 contradiction tests pass, incl. the **golden\ndifferential** (status/supersede byte-identical). Batching changes only\n*how raws are produced*, not the gates.\n- **Real-LLM wet test:** seeded 4 similar \"Dan lives in Tel Aviv…\"\nfacts, wrote the contradicting \"Dan lives in Haifa now.\" Logs show the\nbatch path exercised (`candidates_initial=2/3`); detection fired\ncorrectly — all 4 Tel Aviv → `conflicted`, Haifa → `supersedes_id` set.\nSame effect, one batched call.\n- mypy + ruff clean.\n\nA rigorous accuracy A/B (batch vs per-call over a labeled corpus) is\nregression/STALE-bench territory (A56); the local bench is too noisy\nrun-to-run to certify small deltas from a single pair of runs.\n\nRefs: **A61** (shared task list).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b0b2c64125ce61047e63f29ff6dd070501bf93d4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/b0b2c64125ce61047e63f29ff6dd070501bf93d4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfLoCCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAajYQAFbzGYeI+v0tx2bSi5hx43nu\nacZcoSzwsZHZ/TBTk1NhYczhzuVxuTeJSJtSoPJ8MDqPM4O2M68ArvS2DaQgMXWQ\nYvt0kIb83RZeGno7vAzAqntgeoF9OFtmzmuFdFwKzHpFgWg3uWTh0CMqdLEU9ftn\njdeAWd2L3D0zBAJcFU4JLnkg4frcKuB9BvDMqwGeb2k56oroop0xEP+pulnXGFPF\nt/f/11LYa30uqO4WwfG3thQMvKHr5wGSmW2VRayYlgB+bWYRHKiTWxHE0siGxHz5\nxeB40D63VEb7TXZA5tIaaHV5RTa2O/7X2k1/81Ql0smd7v8nyMK8s2LB0uDtw17y\nFPqBArVkVQ4POYXpPE32rohekUdVe1R76kQ+l09lJeMlHKG3vfXRUZmXO0gjL7nG\nXpIy4aDgdJy6C3pRhGkSXhkQobg5PA1ORlTX4w7kcL7H8VQ0FvzDfZZMQlqTQcrh\nEvYYk72d46Y4zipyHW2+RUo/QEajC2pChRRb/F31RMmpcF/hGk8vAJZmET2HQd+2\nT03pyZ6U07i4tuR6f7JpHCjbPAgYq6wbtL7YPs9nBYTwKKjIxkViIGV/vIbD4AZ7\nM7nfmnWSQzRNwG1+ZXEb6k3S41RPW54nUHoVlJbcyTce4npUFw0SZVnABCA6/MEw\nX7xS4kZBoJ4UAStTKtIk\n=ciAV\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree b0b2c64125ce61047e63f29ff6dd070501bf93d4\nparent 7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1786558978 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786558978 +0300\n\nperf(contradiction): batch the semantic per-candidate LLM judge (A61) (#770)\n\n## The cost bug\n\nThe semantic contradiction path fired **one `complete_json` (gpt-nano)\ncall per candidate**, up to the candidate cap (20). So each\nnewly-embedded memory triggered ~20 nano judge calls — and prod (~442\nmemories/hr, ~86% one tenant's own agent fleet) was making **~10.8k\nOpenAI calls/hr** on only ~56 searches + 67 writes (~90 calls/op) →\n**~$250 every 2–3 days**. Not a loop — the *designed* per-candidate\nfan-out × memory volume.\n\n## The fix — one batched judge call\n\n- **`_llm_contradiction_check_batch`**: a single `complete_json` listing\nthe NEW statement + all candidates; returns one raw judgment per\ncandidate, **aligned to input order**; a missing/malformed entry\ndefaults to a **safe non-contradiction** (never fabricates or drops\none).\n- **`_detect` Path 2**: a single candidate keeps the direct\nper-candidate call; **≥2 candidates (the prod cost driver) go through\nthe batched call**. Both feed the **same** `_judge_contradiction` gates\n(Gate 1 cross-subject, Gate 2 non_conflict_reason, `_pick_older`\ndirection) → verdict/status/supersede semantics unchanged (the golden\ndifferential test still passes).\n- The batched prompt also emits per-candidate\n**`relationship`/`diagnosis`**, so the A55 engine-ON conflict-record\nwrite can reuse it with **no second LLM call** — **one call serves\nboth** the flag-off (legacy detector) and flag-on (engine) arms.\n\n**~N× fewer LLM calls per memory (up to ~20×).** Cheaper stopgap if\nneeded: drop the candidate cap 20→5. Key-splitting is attribution only,\nnot cost.\n\n## No-degradation evidence\n\n- **Deterministic:** 169 contradiction tests pass, incl. the **golden\ndifferential** (status/supersede byte-identical). Batching changes only\n*how raws are produced*, not the gates.\n- **Real-LLM wet test:** seeded 4 similar \"Dan lives in Tel Aviv…\"\nfacts, wrote the contradicting \"Dan lives in Haifa now.\" Logs show the\nbatch path exercised (`candidates_initial=2/3`); detection fired\ncorrectly — all 4 Tel Aviv → `conflicted`, Haifa → `supersedes_id` set.\nSame effect, one batched call.\n- mypy + ruff clean.\n\nA rigorous accuracy A/B (batch vs per-call over a labeled corpus) is\nregression/STALE-bench territory (A56); the local bench is too noisy\nrun-to-run to certify small deltas from a single pair of runs.\n\nRefs: **A61** (shared task list).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T18:23:01Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/86c24c872f63d4e97dbf6a2948b1616ca29830c0/comments","author":{"login":"arkash20","id":55495209,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1NDk1MjA5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55495209?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20","html_url":"https://github.com/arkash20","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256"}]},{"sha":"7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDdjNjA1Zjc3NTcyNjg1NzM1ZTg5MjYxM2ZmY2VhYjk3NzIzNzQyNTY","commit":{"author":{"name":"eyal-bl","email":"eyal.b@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T15:59:04Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T15:59:04Z"},"message":"CAURA-704 feat(doc): mint a memory carrying doc content on every doc … (#756)\n\n…write\n\nThe two stores are not cross-searched: `memclaw_recall` never returns\ndocuments, and a document row embeds only `data[\"summary\"]` — never its\nbody. So a document's CONTENT was unreachable by meaning; you could only\nfind it if you already knew its `collection` + `doc_id`. The companion\n\"reference memory\" the skill docs tell agents to write by hand\n(plugin/skills/memclaw/SKILL.md) had no server-side implementation at\nall: no hook, no event, and routes/documents.py imported nothing from\nmemory_service.\n\nEvery doc write now mints a `fact`-typed memory whose content is the\ndocument body VERBATIM (no prefix, no wrapper), so the body becomes\nreachable by the paths that read `memories.content` directly.\n`memclaw_recall` and the recall brief benefit immediately — neither\ntruncates content.\n\nDeliberate scope boundaries, all deferred to the follow-up:\n\n- INSIGHTS IS UNCHANGED. It truncates every memory to 500 chars via\n`sanitize_content`'s default (core_api/utils/sanitize.py) — a hardcoded\nfunction default with no flag or per-memory override — so insights still\nsees only the first 500 chars of a doc body. Raising that needs its own\nper-run token budget and is a separate change.\n- NO create-vs-update branch and no lookup of previously minted rows.\nRewrite behaviour instead falls out of `write_mode=\"fast\"`:\n`CheckExactDuplicate` 409s an identical body (idempotent re-syncs\nself-dedupe), while an edited body lands with\n`metadata[\"near_duplicate_of\"]` pointing at its predecessor. Nothing\noutdates the older rows yet, so a doc edited N times leaves N active\nmemories — `near_duplicate_of` is the thread a reconciliation pass\nfollows.\n- NO schema or storage-api change: no migration, no new storage filters.\n`source_uri` (`memclaw-doc://<collection>/<doc_id>`) is written as\nprovenance only; nothing queries it yet.\n- No size limit is added to doc writes themselves. Doc `data` is\nunbounded today and stays that way; an over-cap body is SKIPPED rather\nthan truncated, because a truncated body reads as complete.\n\nSkips: `skills` (preserves the staged -> active approval lifecycle — a\nrecallable memory would route around it), `_`-prefixed system\ncollections, bodyless docs (which is what keeps bulk structured records\nlike `{\"plan\": \"business\", \"seats\": 40}` from minting), and bodies over\nMAX_CONTENT_LENGTH. `data[\"summary\"]` is NOT required: it gates doc\nINDEXING, not minting, so a summary-less doc is invisible to `op=search`\nyet its body is still recallable.\n\nTwo details worth reviewer attention:\n\n- `DOC_MEMORY_TYPE` is `MemoryType.FACT`, deliberately not \"reference\" —\n`reference` is not a MemoryType and the `memories_memory_type_check`\nCHECK constraint (migration 013) would reject it. `fact`'s taxonomy\ndescription covers this case explicitly (\"reference material,\ntaxonomies, API/schema documentation ... Includes agent-authored\ndocumentation\").\n- Attribution prefers the real doc writer's agent_id. This is not\ncosmetic: `memclaw_insights` defaults to `scope=\"agent\"` and filters on\n`agent_id`, so a row attributed to the `memclaw-doc-indexer` service\nfallback is invisible to that agent's default insights run. The fallback\napplies only when the caller has no identity at all (REST with no\ngateway-stamped X-Agent-ID).\n\nThe derivation rule lives in `services/doc_indexing.py` beside\n`resolve_embed_source` so the MCP and REST surfaces cannot drift; a test\nasserts neither surface constructs a spec directly. Both call sites wrap\nthe mint in try/except on top of `safe_sync_doc_memory`'s own\nnon-raising contract — a test proved that without it a raising mint\nturns a successful doc write into an error envelope, the one outcome\nthis must never cause.\n\nAlso narrows `collection` to a local `write_collection: str` in the MCP\nwrite branch, which incidentally fixes a pre-existing mypy error on the\nadjacent `resolve_embed_source` call (348 -> 347 tree-wide).\n\nVerification: 55 new tests, all passing; zero new mypy errors; ruff\ncheck and format clean. Full suite shows 500 failures on this branch AND\non clean main (pre-existing local DB schema drift — `audit_log.seq`\nmissing), with +55 passing here, so no regressions. The wet test against\nthe dev stack has not been run yet.\n\n<!-- Thanks for the contribution! Please fill in this template so we can\nreview quickly. -->\n\n## Summary\n\n<!-- What does this PR do? One or two sentences. -->\n\n## Related Issue\n\n<!-- Link the issue this PR addresses, e.g. `Closes #123`. Delete if\nN/A. -->\n\n## Type of Change\n\n- [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change that fixes an issue)\n- [ ] New feature (non-breaking change that adds functionality)\n- [ ] Breaking change (fix or feature that changes existing behavior)\n- [ ] Documentation update\n- [ ] Refactor / internal cleanup\n- [ ] Other:\n\n## How Has This Been Tested?\n\n<!-- Describe the tests you ran. Include commands if possible. -->\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [ ] I have added tests that cover my changes (or explained why none\nare needed)\n- [ ] `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` pass\n- [ ] `mypy` passes\n- [ ] `pytest` passes locally\n- [ ] I have updated relevant documentation (README, docs, etc.)\n- [ ] I have updated `CHANGELOG.md` under the `Unreleased` section (if\nuser-facing)\n\n## Additional Notes\n\n<!-- Anything reviewers should know — tradeoffs, follow-up work, open\nquestions. -->\n\nSigned-off-by: Eyal Blyachman <eyal.b@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"afb7c0040926755b09ce8e20238622dd5d1433a2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/afb7c0040926755b09ce8e20238622dd5d1433a2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfJhICRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4LEQAEDm+YU49xTm6qYzd6UO3fPS\n55WhvSUYjt5ASDf72NIXMnCD2OGxY60H531fTZxLjTe0j7889JPRLYDJExMnwFls\nwA8pcevHjJ+XpDAc3LudVJMY75dfgPnb9OtJyRJ4sp0UccUYXvjW3TeC358wbDsr\n51/GVh5zKEUIu+N9JGuQKdUE8C+ZMr2MIcVTr1LE4v38lbg4iE7Q5ghFLUP5dwuV\nQu70+F1hAYjZ45H/lUVXic058MgGE9uGA6HZbUqNXUNC9+FWX3Ipjy2wPovqmRTT\nKPWe1mL+OcpXnvwIsJk9LkakHj+PJl+fAM0BkWB1E3xHCQbuR6C1fjpQSLawDtVI\nflCdGuU+dK4MKoO1WAXAxm+M8tG11PhZPIXTP5/7zvT5byEEvr7vywEKmpI4D4sG\nVzmTeyd4VFnculDuaPt1F852quSysk2SWx+g5dHMI3xzCzFX8PX0m8Ku1P44nu/R\nBD+8eTOEivZhnEf4BUPLpMMV/QkXbnUqIV3vtI9oSM32wl4S8biq7/ym5EH501Ov\nksCWs2FDi6FiCV91fcq205gU9vvxS4Z2NRV/jcKH2ceB/mmY0veJuSvM72m3hWm+\n607IeOZ5X7Gxh5/8AuDJeojeA1rEARGVAKfOUAdku36FVI5BhAJoZjStJv7OreBg\npLxJxmgMTJXw3kb2rArW\n=3dfV\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree afb7c0040926755b09ce8e20238622dd5d1433a2\nparent 780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939\nauthor eyal-bl <eyal.b@caura.ai> 1786550344 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786550344 +0300\n\nCAURA-704 feat(doc): mint a memory carrying doc content on every doc … (#756)\n\n…write\n\nThe two stores are not cross-searched: `memclaw_recall` never returns\ndocuments, and a document row embeds only `data[\"summary\"]` — never its\nbody. So a document's CONTENT was unreachable by meaning; you could only\nfind it if you already knew its `collection` + `doc_id`. The companion\n\"reference memory\" the skill docs tell agents to write by hand\n(plugin/skills/memclaw/SKILL.md) had no server-side implementation at\nall: no hook, no event, and routes/documents.py imported nothing from\nmemory_service.\n\nEvery doc write now mints a `fact`-typed memory whose content is the\ndocument body VERBATIM (no prefix, no wrapper), so the body becomes\nreachable by the paths that read `memories.content` directly.\n`memclaw_recall` and the recall brief benefit immediately — neither\ntruncates content.\n\nDeliberate scope boundaries, all deferred to the follow-up:\n\n- INSIGHTS IS UNCHANGED. It truncates every memory to 500 chars via\n`sanitize_content`'s default (core_api/utils/sanitize.py) — a hardcoded\nfunction default with no flag or per-memory override — so insights still\nsees only the first 500 chars of a doc body. Raising that needs its own\nper-run token budget and is a separate change.\n- NO create-vs-update branch and no lookup of previously minted rows.\nRewrite behaviour instead falls out of `write_mode=\"fast\"`:\n`CheckExactDuplicate` 409s an identical body (idempotent re-syncs\nself-dedupe), while an edited body lands with\n`metadata[\"near_duplicate_of\"]` pointing at its predecessor. Nothing\noutdates the older rows yet, so a doc edited N times leaves N active\nmemories — `near_duplicate_of` is the thread a reconciliation pass\nfollows.\n- NO schema or storage-api change: no migration, no new storage filters.\n`source_uri` (`memclaw-doc://<collection>/<doc_id>`) is written as\nprovenance only; nothing queries it yet.\n- No size limit is added to doc writes themselves. Doc `data` is\nunbounded today and stays that way; an over-cap body is SKIPPED rather\nthan truncated, because a truncated body reads as complete.\n\nSkips: `skills` (preserves the staged -> active approval lifecycle — a\nrecallable memory would route around it), `_`-prefixed system\ncollections, bodyless docs (which is what keeps bulk structured records\nlike `{\"plan\": \"business\", \"seats\": 40}` from minting), and bodies over\nMAX_CONTENT_LENGTH. `data[\"summary\"]` is NOT required: it gates doc\nINDEXING, not minting, so a summary-less doc is invisible to `op=search`\nyet its body is still recallable.\n\nTwo details worth reviewer attention:\n\n- `DOC_MEMORY_TYPE` is `MemoryType.FACT`, deliberately not \"reference\" —\n`reference` is not a MemoryType and the `memories_memory_type_check`\nCHECK constraint (migration 013) would reject it. `fact`'s taxonomy\ndescription covers this case explicitly (\"reference material,\ntaxonomies, API/schema documentation ... Includes agent-authored\ndocumentation\").\n- Attribution prefers the real doc writer's agent_id. This is not\ncosmetic: `memclaw_insights` defaults to `scope=\"agent\"` and filters on\n`agent_id`, so a row attributed to the `memclaw-doc-indexer` service\nfallback is invisible to that agent's default insights run. The fallback\napplies only when the caller has no identity at all (REST with no\ngateway-stamped X-Agent-ID).\n\nThe derivation rule lives in `services/doc_indexing.py` beside\n`resolve_embed_source` so the MCP and REST surfaces cannot drift; a test\nasserts neither surface constructs a spec directly. Both call sites wrap\nthe mint in try/except on top of `safe_sync_doc_memory`'s own\nnon-raising contract — a test proved that without it a raising mint\nturns a successful doc write into an error envelope, the one outcome\nthis must never cause.\n\nAlso narrows `collection` to a local `write_collection: str` in the MCP\nwrite branch, which incidentally fixes a pre-existing mypy error on the\nadjacent `resolve_embed_source` call (348 -> 347 tree-wide).\n\nVerification: 55 new tests, all passing; zero new mypy errors; ruff\ncheck and format clean. Full suite shows 500 failures on this branch AND\non clean main (pre-existing local DB schema drift — `audit_log.seq`\nmissing), with +55 passing here, so no regressions. The wet test against\nthe dev stack has not been run yet.\n\n<!-- Thanks for the contribution! Please fill in this template so we can\nreview quickly. -->\n\n## Summary\n\n<!-- What does this PR do? One or two sentences. -->\n\n## Related Issue\n\n<!-- Link the issue this PR addresses, e.g. `Closes #123`. Delete if\nN/A. -->\n\n## Type of Change\n\n- [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change that fixes an issue)\n- [ ] New feature (non-breaking change that adds functionality)\n- [ ] Breaking change (fix or feature that changes existing behavior)\n- [ ] Documentation update\n- [ ] Refactor / internal cleanup\n- [ ] Other:\n\n## How Has This Been Tested?\n\n<!-- Describe the tests you ran. Include commands if possible. -->\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [ ] I have added tests that cover my changes (or explained why none\nare needed)\n- [ ] `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` pass\n- [ ] `mypy` passes\n- [ ] `pytest` passes locally\n- [ ] I have updated relevant documentation (README, docs, etc.)\n- [ ] I have updated `CHANGELOG.md` under the `Unreleased` section (if\nuser-facing)\n\n## Additional Notes\n\n<!-- Anything reviewers should know — tradeoffs, follow-up work, open\nquestions. -->\n\nSigned-off-by: Eyal Blyachman <eyal.b@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T15:59:07Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7c605f77572685735e892613ffceab9772374256/comments","author":{"login":"eyal-bl","id":262085605,"node_id":"U_kgDOD58b5Q","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/262085605?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl","html_url":"https://github.com/eyal-bl","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939"}]},{"sha":"780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDc4MGU2NTJmYTBlYTI0ZTUyMmM0NzllMWM2NjA2NzJjMDM3ZjI5Mzk","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T15:35:40Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T15:35:40Z"},"message":"feat(embeddings): nightly sweep that re-embeds NULL-embedding rows (#767)\n\nCloses the gap #765 left behind. That PR deleted\n`_remediate_missing_embeddings` — correctly, since it read a key its\nendpoint never returned and had never re-embedded a row — but the review\nrightly noted it left no periodic self-healing path.\n\n## What actually needed fixing\n\nNormal writes already recover on their own: `schedule_background_tasks`\npublishes `EMBED_REQUESTED` whenever the embedding is `None`, which is\nwhat produces the ~322/48h *\"Storing memory without embedding; deferred\nbackfill scheduled\"* lines in prod. So the gap was never \"transient\nprovider failures go unretried\" — it is **rows that never got that\npublish at all**, with nothing periodically looking for them.\n\n## Reuses the existing machinery\n\n`core_worker.backfill` already does this job properly, so nothing here\nreimplements it. It pages `GET /memories/null-embedding-ids`\n(keyset-paginated, tenant-scoped, deliberately ids-only as\ndefence-in-depth on the unauthenticated storage API), publishes one\n`EMBED_REQUESTED` per row so the provider calls inherit the consumer's\nper-tenant concurrency, retry and DLQ wiring, and is idempotent under\nrestart — a redelivery re-queries and sees only rows still NULL.\n\nThe shape follows the established lifecycle cron pattern exactly:\n\n```\ncore-operations tick (wall-clock aligned)\n  -> POST /admin/lifecycle/fanout/embed-backfill\n    -> one message per active org\n      -> core-worker consumer -> run_embedding_backfill(tenant_id=org)\n```\n\nPer-org is not a preference — the storage endpoint refuses un-scoped\ncalls. And because the page loop runs in the consumer rather than inside\nthe HTTP request, it is not bounded by the 120s Cloud Run budget that\nthe nightly cross-link call currently runs into.\n\n## Two decisions worth reviewing\n\n**Registered in core-worker, not through\n`common.events.lifecycle_handlers`.** The shared adapter protocol is\nimplemented by core-api as well, and core-api cannot run the worker's\nbackfill — routing this through there would have meant adding a method\ncore-api could only `raise NotImplementedError` on. The cost is ~15\nlines of audit bookkeeping this handler does itself instead of\ninheriting from `_run_action`.\n\n**Gated on `embed_backfill_enabled`, default OFF.** The topic, durable\nsubscription and DLQ are Terraform-provisioned — the bus only\nauto-creates *broadcast* subscriptions — so until infra lands, a nightly\nfire would publish into a topic nothing consumes. Gating the\n*registration* rather than no-opping inside the tick also keeps\n`scheduler.is_healthy` meaningful: an unregistered task has no runtime\nslot that could look dead.\n\n**→ To turn this on:** provision\n`memclaw.lifecycle.embed-backfill-requested` + subscription + DLQ, then\nset `EMBED_BACKFILL_ENABLED=true` on core-operations.\n\n## Rate and timing\n\nDefault hour is **04:00, not 02:00**. Every other lifecycle tick\ndefaults to 02:00, and that window is already congested enough that the\ncross-link call hits the request ceiling nightly — while this sweep\nfeeds the same capacity-constrained embedding backend those ticks\ncompete for. `max_inflight` is 25 rather than the library default of\n100, since the sweep shares `EMBED_REQUESTED` with live deferred writes\nand a large backlog in front of them delays real traffic.\n\n## Observability\n\nThe handler logs `scanned` / `published` / `skipped_missing` per org and\nwrites them to the lifecycle audit row. Worth stating plainly: **\"how\nmany rows are unembedded\" was previously unanswerable** without\nconnecting to AlloyDB directly, because the coverage endpoint is\ninternal-ingress only and no metric carried the number.\n\nIt also finalises the audit row on **both** paths. The fanout\npre-creates it as `pending` specifically so that a row which never\nadvances reads as a publish failure; leaving it pending after a\n*consumer* failure would make a failed sweep indistinguishable from an\nundelivered message. The failure path records `failed` with the error\nand re-raises, so Pub/Sub still redelivers.\n\n## What this does NOT fix\n\nSo it is not mistaken for full coverage: `update_memory` on a content\nchange keeps the **previous** content's vector (`memory_service.py`\n~:2597). Those rows are non-NULL, so a NULL-based sweep cannot see them\n— mis-embedded rather than unembedded, and they need their own fix. The\nauto-chunk parent and atomic-fact fan-out paths do land as NULL and are\ncaught here.\n\n## Tests\n\nSix in `tests/test_embedding_backfill.py` (CI-gated), each confirmed\nfailing before the change:\n\n- sweeps the event's `org_id`, with the conservative `max_inflight`\n- logs the per-org counts\n- ack-drops a malformed payload instead of nack-looping it to the DLQ\n- the topic is actually subscribed in `register_consumers` — a handler\ndefined but never subscribed is silently orphaned, since the Pub/Sub\nbackend spawns pull loops from the registry as it stands at `start()`\n- finalises the audit row `success` with stats, and `failed` + re-raise\non error\n- the action is registered in `_ACTION_PUBLISHERS` (an unregistered\naction 404s and the tick just logs-and-returns, so the sweep would\nsilently never run) and the topic string is pinned, since it is infra's\ncontract too\n\nPlus four in `core-operations/tests/` for the registration gating and\nthe fanout path, and a `docs/component-registry.yaml` entry — the AST\ndrift gate in `tests/test_component_registry.py` caught the missing\nentry, exactly as designed.\n\n**4,295 passed** in the root suite, 44 in core-operations. `ruff check`\nclean on all five packages; `ruff format --check` clean on the three\nCI-gated paths.\n\n> Heads-up: `core-operations/tests/` is not run by CI at all today —\nthose four tests are ungated, along with the 44 existing ones covering\nthe whole cron-scheduling surface. Tracked separately; it is a one-step\nworkflow addition and they all pass.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9d58b31ea57c28395a72af3568861e6a49ef8c4e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/9d58b31ea57c28395a72af3568861e6a49ef8c4e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfJLMCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAM3AQAKg9F3ci7SH97UH4kE9tDssC\nh3X0s+NH1MMNgC4otqlSS5yjbYMKCcFtp3Bh5JDSPebhOhsiWQHzy7tkZLuwPLSx\nV5kunZksE9N6uddHP0b+strOJFiSryuXTbBIWYiexRb4Oql0mCPms6cF48WqY2ew\nueAcdVRf9xuRE12ZjkHAPAqFEHK+WVbh/hSfgYitf61Fm2Rn67KCEhHj6427RsWG\nPoI9zhwFpoyu7XbYxxfr21dOylXErPHlt6i4KXszou9HCFY/zi0UvNRbQF6KuUpO\n1zJdUF0+qvkSU/eTXQ1iMRXaWohuvVzBKCJP1wmimMRYYyAqgIUrlzX6pth7omIc\nfnmOj5kTowh/37l7dXKRmmtcYiOsHqxn3xfJ4ODDnDQAIP+tOMZF2KfPFL97y4ys\nR4OA3YgFU2QmgLvKc+IswUA/2t3YG6bHywBNl3M/E9e6yG94MVUneFOv8ZZlcXi+\nwBYEgGAK9zIxA08GSgluBmxa7NMJplreoNdkP9qEYc7FHkenifozfkea3YCxMiJ2\n8QJp3/QXmFL+6V71TPPBKSJ83jSUpJyg0yFCDyroSMV6i2PioU0B0jIS4AxGYMVX\nZ0c9rmoIQgXWRKgtPkbkoY0Z/Rh9zLx7FCRM+qhxRGGjV/uatWMPNTABl4pYZkra\nDWIl1AqoHa4TBJC3yPED\n=NICG\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 9d58b31ea57c28395a72af3568861e6a49ef8c4e\nparent 8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786548940 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786548940 +0300\n\nfeat(embeddings): nightly sweep that re-embeds NULL-embedding rows (#767)\n\nCloses the gap #765 left behind. That PR deleted\n`_remediate_missing_embeddings` — correctly, since it read a key its\nendpoint never returned and had never re-embedded a row — but the review\nrightly noted it left no periodic self-healing path.\n\n## What actually needed fixing\n\nNormal writes already recover on their own: `schedule_background_tasks`\npublishes `EMBED_REQUESTED` whenever the embedding is `None`, which is\nwhat produces the ~322/48h *\"Storing memory without embedding; deferred\nbackfill scheduled\"* lines in prod. So the gap was never \"transient\nprovider failures go unretried\" — it is **rows that never got that\npublish at all**, with nothing periodically looking for them.\n\n## Reuses the existing machinery\n\n`core_worker.backfill` already does this job properly, so nothing here\nreimplements it. It pages `GET /memories/null-embedding-ids`\n(keyset-paginated, tenant-scoped, deliberately ids-only as\ndefence-in-depth on the unauthenticated storage API), publishes one\n`EMBED_REQUESTED` per row so the provider calls inherit the consumer's\nper-tenant concurrency, retry and DLQ wiring, and is idempotent under\nrestart — a redelivery re-queries and sees only rows still NULL.\n\nThe shape follows the established lifecycle cron pattern exactly:\n\n```\ncore-operations tick (wall-clock aligned)\n  -> POST /admin/lifecycle/fanout/embed-backfill\n    -> one message per active org\n      -> core-worker consumer -> run_embedding_backfill(tenant_id=org)\n```\n\nPer-org is not a preference — the storage endpoint refuses un-scoped\ncalls. And because the page loop runs in the consumer rather than inside\nthe HTTP request, it is not bounded by the 120s Cloud Run budget that\nthe nightly cross-link call currently runs into.\n\n## Two decisions worth reviewing\n\n**Registered in core-worker, not through\n`common.events.lifecycle_handlers`.** The shared adapter protocol is\nimplemented by core-api as well, and core-api cannot run the worker's\nbackfill — routing this through there would have meant adding a method\ncore-api could only `raise NotImplementedError` on. The cost is ~15\nlines of audit bookkeeping this handler does itself instead of\ninheriting from `_run_action`.\n\n**Gated on `embed_backfill_enabled`, default OFF.** The topic, durable\nsubscription and DLQ are Terraform-provisioned — the bus only\nauto-creates *broadcast* subscriptions — so until infra lands, a nightly\nfire would publish into a topic nothing consumes. Gating the\n*registration* rather than no-opping inside the tick also keeps\n`scheduler.is_healthy` meaningful: an unregistered task has no runtime\nslot that could look dead.\n\n**→ To turn this on:** provision\n`memclaw.lifecycle.embed-backfill-requested` + subscription + DLQ, then\nset `EMBED_BACKFILL_ENABLED=true` on core-operations.\n\n## Rate and timing\n\nDefault hour is **04:00, not 02:00**. Every other lifecycle tick\ndefaults to 02:00, and that window is already congested enough that the\ncross-link call hits the request ceiling nightly — while this sweep\nfeeds the same capacity-constrained embedding backend those ticks\ncompete for. `max_inflight` is 25 rather than the library default of\n100, since the sweep shares `EMBED_REQUESTED` with live deferred writes\nand a large backlog in front of them delays real traffic.\n\n## Observability\n\nThe handler logs `scanned` / `published` / `skipped_missing` per org and\nwrites them to the lifecycle audit row. Worth stating plainly: **\"how\nmany rows are unembedded\" was previously unanswerable** without\nconnecting to AlloyDB directly, because the coverage endpoint is\ninternal-ingress only and no metric carried the number.\n\nIt also finalises the audit row on **both** paths. The fanout\npre-creates it as `pending` specifically so that a row which never\nadvances reads as a publish failure; leaving it pending after a\n*consumer* failure would make a failed sweep indistinguishable from an\nundelivered message. The failure path records `failed` with the error\nand re-raises, so Pub/Sub still redelivers.\n\n## What this does NOT fix\n\nSo it is not mistaken for full coverage: `update_memory` on a content\nchange keeps the **previous** content's vector (`memory_service.py`\n~:2597). Those rows are non-NULL, so a NULL-based sweep cannot see them\n— mis-embedded rather than unembedded, and they need their own fix. The\nauto-chunk parent and atomic-fact fan-out paths do land as NULL and are\ncaught here.\n\n## Tests\n\nSix in `tests/test_embedding_backfill.py` (CI-gated), each confirmed\nfailing before the change:\n\n- sweeps the event's `org_id`, with the conservative `max_inflight`\n- logs the per-org counts\n- ack-drops a malformed payload instead of nack-looping it to the DLQ\n- the topic is actually subscribed in `register_consumers` — a handler\ndefined but never subscribed is silently orphaned, since the Pub/Sub\nbackend spawns pull loops from the registry as it stands at `start()`\n- finalises the audit row `success` with stats, and `failed` + re-raise\non error\n- the action is registered in `_ACTION_PUBLISHERS` (an unregistered\naction 404s and the tick just logs-and-returns, so the sweep would\nsilently never run) and the topic string is pinned, since it is infra's\ncontract too\n\nPlus four in `core-operations/tests/` for the registration gating and\nthe fanout path, and a `docs/component-registry.yaml` entry — the AST\ndrift gate in `tests/test_component_registry.py` caught the missing\nentry, exactly as designed.\n\n**4,295 passed** in the root suite, 44 in core-operations. `ruff check`\nclean on all five packages; `ruff format --check` clean on the three\nCI-gated paths.\n\n> Heads-up: `core-operations/tests/` is not run by CI at all today —\nthose four tests are ungated, along with the 44 existing ones covering\nthe whole cron-scheduling surface. Tracked separately; it is a one-step\nworkflow addition and they all pass.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T15:35:43Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/780e652fa0ea24e522c479e1c660672c037f2939/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c"}]},{"sha":"8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDg1ODRiNzBlMDU2ZDYyZmFlZWE4YWQ3MjkyY2IxZTE4MGViMWRmMmM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T15:15:40Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T15:15:40Z"},"message":"ci: run and lint core-operations, closing an ungated tree (#768)\n\n`core-operations/tests/` was never run by CI. The workflow had exactly\ntwo pytest steps — `uv run pytest tests/` and `uv run pytest\ncore-storage-api/tests/` — and a repo-wide grep for \"core-operations\"\nacross `.github/workflows/` returned nothing.\n\nThat left the whole cron-scheduling surface ungated: **41 tests** across\n`test_app.py`, `test_tasks.py` and `test_scheduler.py`, covering\nwall-clock alignment, per-job `*_run_at_hour` threading, the fanout POST\npaths, and the scheduler's failure/hot-loop guards.\n\nThis is the exact failure mode the `core-storage-api/tests/` ruff step\nalready documents:\n\n> The test tree was unlinted for as long as it was un-run; both halves\nof that blind spot close together or it just drifts again.\n\nSo both halves close here: `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, and\npytest.\n\n## Three things that made this smaller than the task assumed\n\n**No `PYTHONPATH` needed.** I expected to add `core-operations/src` to a\npath somewhere. It turns out `core-operations/pytest.ini` already sets\nits own `pythonpath = src ..` (`src` for `core_operations`, `..` for\n`common`), and pytest resolves *that* file as the configfile when\ninvoked with this path — it does **not** inherit the root `pytest.ini`.\nConfirmed from pytest's own header:\n\n```\nrootdir: .../memclaw/core-operations\nconfigfile: pytest.ini\n```\n\nSetting `PYTHONPATH` on the step would have been inert, so it is\ndeliberately absent and the comment says why. (For contrast, the root\n`tests/` step gets `core_worker` from the root `pytest.ini`'s\n`pythonpath`, not from its `env:` block — that env var is for the\nmigration/openapi steps.)\n\n**The format check is safe to gate.** The task flagged a risk that\n`core-operations` might have no ruff config and fall back to 88 columns,\ndemanding a reflow. It has its own: `core-operations/pyproject.toml`\ndeclares `line-length = 110`, matching core-api and core-storage-api\nrather than `common/`'s 88. Both `src/` and `tests/` are already clean\nunder it (`9 files already formatted`), so `format --check` is included\nrather than deferred.\n\n**No database.** These tests touch none, so nothing is added to the\nPostgres setup. Postgres is already up for the earlier steps in that\njob, but this step uses it not at all and runs in well under a second —\ncheaper than a separate job that would re-do the uv install.\n\n## The gate is not decoration\n\nI checked it actually catches the regression it exists for. Dropping\n`delay_provider` from one registration — which would make that job fire\nan immediate boot-time tick and drift:\n\n```\nE   AssertionError: lifecycle-insights is not wall-clock aligned\nFAILED core-operations/tests/test_app.py::test_all_lifecycle_jobs_registered_and_wall_clock_aligned\n1 failed, 40 passed\n```\n\nRestoring it returns to `41 passed`.\n\n## Verification\n\nEach new step's command run verbatim in a clean environment (`env -u\nPYTHONPATH`), mirroring CI:\n\n| Step | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `ruff check core-operations/src/ core-operations/tests/` | All checks\npassed! |\n| `ruff format --check core-operations/src/ core-operations/tests/` | 9\nfiles already formatted |\n| `pytest core-operations/tests/ -v --tb=short` | 41 passed in 0.36s |\n\n`actionlint` reports **one** finding on this file — SC2086 at line 70,\nthe pre-existing `Install dependencies` step — both before and after\nthis change, so these additions introduce none. Worth noting: CI does\nnot currently run actionlint on this repo, which is why that one has\nsurvived.\n\nYAML validated by parse, and the three steps confirmed present in the\njob with no empty `env:` blocks.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"6f6c49bd53f6ab63c3293639d2c9b24e98b62697","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/6f6c49bd53f6ab63c3293639d2c9b24e98b62697"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfI4cCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAUqIQAHLYYKJ7Fxx6mR/jq8PNZQqK\nmkNQT2bskrIbxPS/ME93LaQ4FF2SiFBoffB9FnxbVDC2Fcv3NcZys+EsCwv1rx9p\nBg5SIpOJnKQ+hmcmwlIFBGxQFM3ECDAVqVSf413537sqFaDnI8Ox8LKVqcW0gjMD\n7GposSa3uKa5AAIESS7bDOcXyf2wvXr3QdXSEPoIGD0JRfylJwsnKFFiUndv9clj\nV/RNEvSSpeVhggzvwCz+tJ0+RDgmyGwFf0Jt+BR2tJcUFlWN1PnKGT/W5VMwKrdL\nGUXliG7nshmJlZu8/DQSFQ/I0K1IgJqCcJOUGIuCxI3o+r31SyBBXD9VXgKXUbXa\nysnyYUHTEY2CeLVN0765TbHKCfTjzMuMOeV6Jyh7qGgnvbNxhSrQT/aW89TbcaYi\n+NY81wUg5atZlWxsmFNgcCjySzMP/6YUCUxohRDlVz0NDIVrjc9m/OjESlECQlCp\n30QpFEhF3MbGIIPHTyryovr4FnHqdyfpM4pHOvbEF3qpGy96eTin//oM2dZ7fWp0\n8ImWZdfHIONwcikv9QO+1lEzSsIsbkouaqvF8fdYLUasf/PxJfKIktMFUydK8u4n\nVHkzT3G0OrZpDYwNpzdY2cizZEsBR2/ecml1uFluWUDXgRg6AiboimQz2mdG4aPx\ntpx4naEnWY8RufanavRK\n=/tG+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 6f6c49bd53f6ab63c3293639d2c9b24e98b62697\nparent 154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786547740 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786547740 +0300\n\nci: run and lint core-operations, closing an ungated tree (#768)\n\n`core-operations/tests/` was never run by CI. The workflow had exactly\ntwo pytest steps — `uv run pytest tests/` and `uv run pytest\ncore-storage-api/tests/` — and a repo-wide grep for \"core-operations\"\nacross `.github/workflows/` returned nothing.\n\nThat left the whole cron-scheduling surface ungated: **41 tests** across\n`test_app.py`, `test_tasks.py` and `test_scheduler.py`, covering\nwall-clock alignment, per-job `*_run_at_hour` threading, the fanout POST\npaths, and the scheduler's failure/hot-loop guards.\n\nThis is the exact failure mode the `core-storage-api/tests/` ruff step\nalready documents:\n\n> The test tree was unlinted for as long as it was un-run; both halves\nof that blind spot close together or it just drifts again.\n\nSo both halves close here: `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, and\npytest.\n\n## Three things that made this smaller than the task assumed\n\n**No `PYTHONPATH` needed.** I expected to add `core-operations/src` to a\npath somewhere. It turns out `core-operations/pytest.ini` already sets\nits own `pythonpath = src ..` (`src` for `core_operations`, `..` for\n`common`), and pytest resolves *that* file as the configfile when\ninvoked with this path — it does **not** inherit the root `pytest.ini`.\nConfirmed from pytest's own header:\n\n```\nrootdir: .../memclaw/core-operations\nconfigfile: pytest.ini\n```\n\nSetting `PYTHONPATH` on the step would have been inert, so it is\ndeliberately absent and the comment says why. (For contrast, the root\n`tests/` step gets `core_worker` from the root `pytest.ini`'s\n`pythonpath`, not from its `env:` block — that env var is for the\nmigration/openapi steps.)\n\n**The format check is safe to gate.** The task flagged a risk that\n`core-operations` might have no ruff config and fall back to 88 columns,\ndemanding a reflow. It has its own: `core-operations/pyproject.toml`\ndeclares `line-length = 110`, matching core-api and core-storage-api\nrather than `common/`'s 88. Both `src/` and `tests/` are already clean\nunder it (`9 files already formatted`), so `format --check` is included\nrather than deferred.\n\n**No database.** These tests touch none, so nothing is added to the\nPostgres setup. Postgres is already up for the earlier steps in that\njob, but this step uses it not at all and runs in well under a second —\ncheaper than a separate job that would re-do the uv install.\n\n## The gate is not decoration\n\nI checked it actually catches the regression it exists for. Dropping\n`delay_provider` from one registration — which would make that job fire\nan immediate boot-time tick and drift:\n\n```\nE   AssertionError: lifecycle-insights is not wall-clock aligned\nFAILED core-operations/tests/test_app.py::test_all_lifecycle_jobs_registered_and_wall_clock_aligned\n1 failed, 40 passed\n```\n\nRestoring it returns to `41 passed`.\n\n## Verification\n\nEach new step's command run verbatim in a clean environment (`env -u\nPYTHONPATH`), mirroring CI:\n\n| Step | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `ruff check core-operations/src/ core-operations/tests/` | All checks\npassed! |\n| `ruff format --check core-operations/src/ core-operations/tests/` | 9\nfiles already formatted |\n| `pytest core-operations/tests/ -v --tb=short` | 41 passed in 0.36s |\n\n`actionlint` reports **one** finding on this file — SC2086 at line 70,\nthe pre-existing `Install dependencies` step — both before and after\nthis change, so these additions introduce none. Worth noting: CI does\nnot currently run actionlint on this repo, which is why that one has\nsurvived.\n\nYAML validated by parse, and the three steps confirmed present in the\njob with no empty `env:` blocks.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T15:15:42Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8584b70e056d62faeea8ad7292cb1e180eb1df2c/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d"}]},{"sha":"154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDE1NDM1MGI2MmFlNzM1ZTQxMzBjNDVkYmVmMDA2ZDkxMTMzMjU2NmQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T13:45:49Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T13:45:49Z"},"message":"fix(crystallizer): match the /lifecycle-candidates contract; drop inline remediation (#765)\n\nFollow-up to #763, which fixed the same class of bug against a different\nendpoint.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThe task was to fix `_remediate_missing_embeddings`. While verifying it\nI found that **four** crystallizer consumers of `GET\n/memories/lifecycle-candidates` disagree with it — each in a different\nway, and each failing toward a reassuring value. Three are fixed here.\nThe fourth needs a storage-side change and is tracked separately (see\nthe end).\n\nI fixed the three rather than leaving known-broken lines beside the one\nI was sent to fix; a separate PR would touch the same 25 lines and a\nreviewer would rightly ask why.\n\n## The endpoint\n\n`core-storage-api/.../routers/memories.py:812-819` returns exactly two\nkeys, both lists of bare UUID **strings**:\n\n```python\nreturn {\n    \"expired_still_active\": [str(r[0]) for r in expired],\n    \"stale_low_weight\": [str(r[0]) for r in stale],\n}\n```\n\n## The consumers\n\n| Function | Read | Result | This PR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `_check_stale_memories` | `stale_memories` | wrong key → **0 for every\ntenant** | fixed |\n| `_check_expired_still_active` | right key, but `r.get(\"id\")` on\nstrings | **`AttributeError`** whenever a row exists | fixed |\n| `_remediate_missing_embeddings` | `missing_embeddings` | key absent →\n**permanent no-op** | deleted |\n| `_check_short_content` | `short_content` | key absent → **0 for every\ntenant** | tracked separately |\n\nVerified by probe, not by reading — against the endpoint's exact return\nshape:\n\n```\n_check_expired_still_active: RAISED AttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'get'\n_check_stale_memories:       {'count': 0, 'affected_ids': []}\n_check_short_content:        {'count': 0, 'affected_ids': []}\n_remediate:                  update_embedding never called — no-op confirmed\n```\n\nThe expired-check failure is the nastiest: `run_crystallization` catches\nper-check exceptions into `hygiene[name] = {\"error\": True}`, so that\ncheck **errored precisely when it had something to report** and looked\nhealthy when it didn't.\n\n## The decision: delete rather than repair the remediation\n\nRepair already exists, and is better than anything this function could\nbecome. `core_worker.backfill` pages `GET /memories/null-embedding-ids`\n— keyset-paginated, tenant-scoped, deliberately ids-only as\ndefence-in-depth on the unauthenticated storage API — and publishes one\n`EMBED_REQUESTED` per row, inheriting the consumer's per-tenant\nconcurrency, retry and DLQ wiring, idempotent under restart.\n\nThis corrects a premise in the task: a row-level source **does** exist\n(`memory_list_null_embedding_rows` behind that endpoint). That makes the\n\"add an endpoint\" option cheaper than assumed — and makes deletion\nclearly right, because the machinery is already built and well-designed.\nReimplementing it synchronously would duplicate all of it *and* issue\ninline embedding calls into the same process-wide gate that write\ntraffic already oversubscribes, so a hygiene report could stall the\nwrite path it reports on.\n\nSurfacing the count is this service's job — `_check_missing_embeddings`,\nfixed in #763. Acting on it is `python -m core_worker.cli\nbackfill-embeddings --tenant-id X`.\n\n**Nothing claimed the remediation worked.** One caller, no report field,\nand no doc or frontend mentions it (searched `*.py`, `*.ts`, `*.tsx`,\n`*.md` across the project tree; every other \"remediat\" hit is the\nunrelated `governance_remediation`). So there is no stale promise to\nretract. `update_embedding` keeps two callers in `memory_service`, so\nthe deletion orphans nothing.\n\nWorth stating plainly: after this there is **no automated repair** for\nrows that fell through — but there never was, since the function was a\nno-op. The right follow-up is scheduling the worker backfill, not\nresurrecting inline remediation.\n\n## Tests\n\nThree regressions, each confirmed failing before the change:\n\n```\ntest_expired_check_counts_string_ids_without_raising  AttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'get'\ntest_stale_check_reads_stale_low_weight               assert 0 == 1\ntest_inline_remediation_is_gone                       assert not True\n```\n\nPlus a **contract pin** asserting the endpoint's key names *and* that it\nyields `str(r[0])` rather than dicts. That pin is what makes this class\nnon-recurring, and it is deliberately not a mock-based test: a\nhand-written mock proves nothing here, because whoever wrote the\noriginal mocks would have written them from the same wrong assumption as\nthe callers. It correctly passes both before and after — it is a guard,\nnot a regression.\n\n320 passed across the crystallizer, lifecycle and embedding selections.\n`ruff check` and `ruff format --check` clean. Rebased onto `main` after\n#764 landed, with tests re-run after the rebase.\n\n## Deliberately not in this PR: `_check_short_content`\n\nSame bug, but it cannot be fixed on the core-api side alone. The query\nit needs already exists and is **orphaned** —\n`memory_find_short_content` (`postgres_service.py:2792`) has no caller\nanywhere in the repo, so someone wrote it for this check and never wired\nit up. Fixing it means adding `short_content` to the\n`/lifecycle-candidates` response and moving\n`CRYSTALLIZER_SHORT_CONTENT_CHARS` into `common/constants.py` so both\nservices can share it — a cross-service change with its own review\nsurface, kept separate rather than smuggled in here. Note it carries the\nsame `r.get(\"id\")`-on-strings shape bug, so it would raise rather than\ncount once data flows.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"0177941d9293962b979cba1fcefca6cde86f6c29","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/0177941d9293962b979cba1fcefca6cde86f6c29"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfHkNCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAPkgQAJyndVjckotGIOz4K2ZkxUUR\n5HKhMBWzgmEwGtpEAEQlxdlEJw2tWcxXEaeIHkXHuCvijK0mtf2SN25NroHq4psj\nOVOCnAdV8NX/OGYJQgHpyDvtidUpIJWqZ/xrw5opXdtwxKgLCj/XQy+sIGOouOuV\n5W54zYLJA/DJO7MBq/Em7g+8saNT2ZYOp1Qo7pOs4u3zBUsIQR9Xr/T6hk1+g05e\nJV89TK1ZY+kP/GUi5lagodTWEgx44ZVgMaRQULnmElFvKLEiZhwbCF/2Cxof2W10\nK4qSax7uPOSaSxmwvPQ2T1Q82wkKBSagg/rtwlYS0Y3nW4FDXuv1X9BkE9URkyES\nqSrDswRu+oHG+RzibJKBGIMBPTsdL8HZwLOUuPNpoyOEBp/4SNPaRc7otYqOWyQW\nJMR0vqPhb+3t3IKA1du61e1FEkLJlcV4GaMqKM1ZmOQf+szLH8e5DBfu1nuPz6/R\nrA5xoUtiiX/4ZxUqS9o9ftZssMOKD9vNcNIDv0zFIlB72STt8KA3hsAT5OsQ9hKI\nSXSHy34G4l+z4H3+vjw42k+CQu5TAR8IH/A4qBl8laQeK4Ck+LeF5+ouZGx5uBt8\n3da1rGeEetolzCSgHjnpp1eowtml1u0y/iBBkgnN3rraiUKmd+/JkKo7cQeDQgB8\nLTfOhWdNd4ib+nsiT+HR\n=01Ca\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 0177941d9293962b979cba1fcefca6cde86f6c29\nparent 2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786542349 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786542349 +0300\n\nfix(crystallizer): match the /lifecycle-candidates contract; drop inline remediation (#765)\n\nFollow-up to #763, which fixed the same class of bug against a different\nendpoint.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThe task was to fix `_remediate_missing_embeddings`. While verifying it\nI found that **four** crystallizer consumers of `GET\n/memories/lifecycle-candidates` disagree with it — each in a different\nway, and each failing toward a reassuring value. Three are fixed here.\nThe fourth needs a storage-side change and is tracked separately (see\nthe end).\n\nI fixed the three rather than leaving known-broken lines beside the one\nI was sent to fix; a separate PR would touch the same 25 lines and a\nreviewer would rightly ask why.\n\n## The endpoint\n\n`core-storage-api/.../routers/memories.py:812-819` returns exactly two\nkeys, both lists of bare UUID **strings**:\n\n```python\nreturn {\n    \"expired_still_active\": [str(r[0]) for r in expired],\n    \"stale_low_weight\": [str(r[0]) for r in stale],\n}\n```\n\n## The consumers\n\n| Function | Read | Result | This PR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `_check_stale_memories` | `stale_memories` | wrong key → **0 for every\ntenant** | fixed |\n| `_check_expired_still_active` | right key, but `r.get(\"id\")` on\nstrings | **`AttributeError`** whenever a row exists | fixed |\n| `_remediate_missing_embeddings` | `missing_embeddings` | key absent →\n**permanent no-op** | deleted |\n| `_check_short_content` | `short_content` | key absent → **0 for every\ntenant** | tracked separately |\n\nVerified by probe, not by reading — against the endpoint's exact return\nshape:\n\n```\n_check_expired_still_active: RAISED AttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'get'\n_check_stale_memories:       {'count': 0, 'affected_ids': []}\n_check_short_content:        {'count': 0, 'affected_ids': []}\n_remediate:                  update_embedding never called — no-op confirmed\n```\n\nThe expired-check failure is the nastiest: `run_crystallization` catches\nper-check exceptions into `hygiene[name] = {\"error\": True}`, so that\ncheck **errored precisely when it had something to report** and looked\nhealthy when it didn't.\n\n## The decision: delete rather than repair the remediation\n\nRepair already exists, and is better than anything this function could\nbecome. `core_worker.backfill` pages `GET /memories/null-embedding-ids`\n— keyset-paginated, tenant-scoped, deliberately ids-only as\ndefence-in-depth on the unauthenticated storage API — and publishes one\n`EMBED_REQUESTED` per row, inheriting the consumer's per-tenant\nconcurrency, retry and DLQ wiring, idempotent under restart.\n\nThis corrects a premise in the task: a row-level source **does** exist\n(`memory_list_null_embedding_rows` behind that endpoint). That makes the\n\"add an endpoint\" option cheaper than assumed — and makes deletion\nclearly right, because the machinery is already built and well-designed.\nReimplementing it synchronously would duplicate all of it *and* issue\ninline embedding calls into the same process-wide gate that write\ntraffic already oversubscribes, so a hygiene report could stall the\nwrite path it reports on.\n\nSurfacing the count is this service's job — `_check_missing_embeddings`,\nfixed in #763. Acting on it is `python -m core_worker.cli\nbackfill-embeddings --tenant-id X`.\n\n**Nothing claimed the remediation worked.** One caller, no report field,\nand no doc or frontend mentions it (searched `*.py`, `*.ts`, `*.tsx`,\n`*.md` across the project tree; every other \"remediat\" hit is the\nunrelated `governance_remediation`). So there is no stale promise to\nretract. `update_embedding` keeps two callers in `memory_service`, so\nthe deletion orphans nothing.\n\nWorth stating plainly: after this there is **no automated repair** for\nrows that fell through — but there never was, since the function was a\nno-op. The right follow-up is scheduling the worker backfill, not\nresurrecting inline remediation.\n\n## Tests\n\nThree regressions, each confirmed failing before the change:\n\n```\ntest_expired_check_counts_string_ids_without_raising  AttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'get'\ntest_stale_check_reads_stale_low_weight               assert 0 == 1\ntest_inline_remediation_is_gone                       assert not True\n```\n\nPlus a **contract pin** asserting the endpoint's key names *and* that it\nyields `str(r[0])` rather than dicts. That pin is what makes this class\nnon-recurring, and it is deliberately not a mock-based test: a\nhand-written mock proves nothing here, because whoever wrote the\noriginal mocks would have written them from the same wrong assumption as\nthe callers. It correctly passes both before and after — it is a guard,\nnot a regression.\n\n320 passed across the crystallizer, lifecycle and embedding selections.\n`ruff check` and `ruff format --check` clean. Rebased onto `main` after\n#764 landed, with tests re-run after the rebase.\n\n## Deliberately not in this PR: `_check_short_content`\n\nSame bug, but it cannot be fixed on the core-api side alone. The query\nit needs already exists and is **orphaned** —\n`memory_find_short_content` (`postgres_service.py:2792`) has no caller\nanywhere in the repo, so someone wrote it for this check and never wired\nit up. Fixing it means adding `short_content` to the\n`/lifecycle-candidates` response and moving\n`CRYSTALLIZER_SHORT_CONTENT_CHARS` into `common/constants.py` so both\nservices can share it — a cross-service change with its own review\nsurface, kept separate rather than smuggled in here. Note it carries the\nsame `r.get(\"id\")`-on-strings shape bug, so it would raise rather than\ncount once data flows.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T13:45:51Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/154350b62ae735e4130c45dbef006d911332566d/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73"}]},{"sha":"2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDJmMTZhOGYyZTllZjNkMmYwYTUzODVlMzJhMzgyYzc5YzY0MTVjNzM","commit":{"author":{"name":"eyal-bl","email":"eyal.b@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T12:25:36Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T12:25:36Z"},"message":"CAURA-716 fix(memory): honour agent_provided_fields on the inline enrich path (#762)\n\n## Problem\n\n`_schedule_enrich_or_inline` computes `agent_provided_fields` — which\nenrichment columns the caller set explicitly, from Pydantic's\n`model_fields_set` — and forwards it to the deferred/worker branch.\n**The inline (OSS) branch dropped it**, inferring caller intent instead\nby comparing the stored value against the schema default:\n\n```python\nif mem.get(\"memory_type\") == \"fact\"   and enrichment.memory_type: ...\nif mem.get(\"status\")      == \"active\" and enrichment.status:      ...\nif mem.get(\"weight\")      == 0.5      and enrichment.weight:      ...\n```\n\nThe dropped forward carried a comment asserting the gates were\nequivalent — *\"there's no observable behavioural delta between the gates\ntoday.\"* There is one, and it's silent.\n\nThat comparison can't distinguish **\"caller pinned this to the\ndefault\"** from **\"caller said nothing.\"** And `fact` / `active` are the\ndefaults *precisely because* they're the neutral choices — which is\nexactly why a caller pins them.\n\n## Evidence (live VM, real provider)\n\nA write passing `memory_type=\"fact\", status=\"active\"` INSERTs correctly,\nthen gets rewritten seconds later:\n\n| | `memory_type` | `status` | `weight` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| at insert | `fact` | `active` | `0.5` |\n| +8s | **`decision`** | **`confirmed`** | `0.9` |\n\n`weight` is correct there — not pinned, so enrichment rightly owns it.\nBoth pinned fields lost.\n\nFound while wet-testing #756 (CAURA-704), where the lost\n`status=\"active\"` made doc-derived memories invisible to `insights\nfocus=patterns` (selects `status='active'` only) — the document never\nreached the reflection pass.\n\n## Fix\n\nForward the list; gate on it when present. A small `_agent_pinned`\nhelper routes all five override fields (`memory_type`, `weight`,\n`status`, `ts_valid_start`, `ts_valid_end`) through one decision so they\ncan't drift apart again.\n\nThe value-vs-default comparison is **retained as the fallback** when no\nlist is passed (`agent_provided_fields is None`), so behaviour is\nunchanged for every existing caller. `reference_datetime` is still\ndeliberately not forwarded — the inline path resolves relative dates\nagainst the enrichment call's own clock, a separate concern.\n\n## Scope note for reviewers\n\nThis is in the write path every memory traverses. The behavioural delta\nis precisely: *a caller who explicitly passed one of the five override\nfields now keeps their value on the inline path* — which the deferred\npath already does and the dropped comment already claimed. **No caller\ngains or loses enrichment it was previously getting.**\n\n## Verification\n\n- **14 new tests. 10 fail against pre-fix source**; the other 4 assert\ndeliberately-unchanged behaviour (legacy fallback, deferred path, the\n`model_fields_set` mechanism) and pass on both sides.\n- Full suite: **526 failed / 3688 passed** here vs **526 failed / 3674\npassed** on clean `main` — identical failure count (pre-existing local\nDB schema drift), **+14 passing**.\n- **0 new mypy errors** (343 both sides). `ruff check` / `format` clean.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Eyal Blyachman <eyal.b@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"edb9eb574fa0c1397b16d88e6fb92035c09c1c01","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/edb9eb574fa0c1397b16d88e6fb92035c09c1c01"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfGZACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAEzcQAIdtSAZrYV+YkJJfaa3hjkv8\ngEu3DMfvcYRpPF0yIqAcXOCSgSIro9oDya5x98q0i48fI9VguZuY1DrrNoKUiKJQ\nEYBIeN55VmQm3OGu7ou040d/aVk9osn1dnChUGshK3+nwOGO1aKbu5qjQx9Gjx7q\nilqbIfBSouZn76glrJTLUt797lFrO6mN6uGYYX/F5WLr7nR2LdMBCyQ349pjXExw\nfn+KoBUTLW7qFxwlz8vsLPiPykvVdQf6jhltLL2ihYKB1D9X0E5X1ktYUMalYepy\ngz8wBYnwqit5MqrJcwIpM+1667itn2iyLLPlV8+g3iamRP9Ygw+tZgMQ+dlxKKc3\nu5h+x4MY/vubiOC6HnYYx3IErCmVwprqL3BedDX08JE7bs+XooUhqMUj7s8c6fFk\nFA+MKuzP//1xTWrAbH2EtCBph0vQATEaVhgXDnQmBGTp1ntM+ZqqPral/teXjC8W\n16E+W6pjyMaZFRnfyzbwBG5eo7RnQgWkLF+sDpmLfrGirxecQDLCJO/uRb+J624L\n0O6iSFRvLJX4N+pbIIAUGXVVwi6NSrK3CUHyELt3bgGN2FzYB/woOXJckSY1sLQL\nWFgazpyvQE9SunGcEAyAtfvk7M5b4aD1i4xg7BcO8gO3w2ZbIp3UpIJr9r8zGB+B\n2tDsceWN+7LAaRzk4zRl\n=yyQh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree edb9eb574fa0c1397b16d88e6fb92035c09c1c01\nparent fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe\nauthor eyal-bl <eyal.b@caura.ai> 1786537536 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786537536 +0300\n\nCAURA-716 fix(memory): honour agent_provided_fields on the inline enrich path (#762)\n\n## Problem\n\n`_schedule_enrich_or_inline` computes `agent_provided_fields` — which\nenrichment columns the caller set explicitly, from Pydantic's\n`model_fields_set` — and forwards it to the deferred/worker branch.\n**The inline (OSS) branch dropped it**, inferring caller intent instead\nby comparing the stored value against the schema default:\n\n```python\nif mem.get(\"memory_type\") == \"fact\"   and enrichment.memory_type: ...\nif mem.get(\"status\")      == \"active\" and enrichment.status:      ...\nif mem.get(\"weight\")      == 0.5      and enrichment.weight:      ...\n```\n\nThe dropped forward carried a comment asserting the gates were\nequivalent — *\"there's no observable behavioural delta between the gates\ntoday.\"* There is one, and it's silent.\n\nThat comparison can't distinguish **\"caller pinned this to the\ndefault\"** from **\"caller said nothing.\"** And `fact` / `active` are the\ndefaults *precisely because* they're the neutral choices — which is\nexactly why a caller pins them.\n\n## Evidence (live VM, real provider)\n\nA write passing `memory_type=\"fact\", status=\"active\"` INSERTs correctly,\nthen gets rewritten seconds later:\n\n| | `memory_type` | `status` | `weight` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| at insert | `fact` | `active` | `0.5` |\n| +8s | **`decision`** | **`confirmed`** | `0.9` |\n\n`weight` is correct there — not pinned, so enrichment rightly owns it.\nBoth pinned fields lost.\n\nFound while wet-testing #756 (CAURA-704), where the lost\n`status=\"active\"` made doc-derived memories invisible to `insights\nfocus=patterns` (selects `status='active'` only) — the document never\nreached the reflection pass.\n\n## Fix\n\nForward the list; gate on it when present. A small `_agent_pinned`\nhelper routes all five override fields (`memory_type`, `weight`,\n`status`, `ts_valid_start`, `ts_valid_end`) through one decision so they\ncan't drift apart again.\n\nThe value-vs-default comparison is **retained as the fallback** when no\nlist is passed (`agent_provided_fields is None`), so behaviour is\nunchanged for every existing caller. `reference_datetime` is still\ndeliberately not forwarded — the inline path resolves relative dates\nagainst the enrichment call's own clock, a separate concern.\n\n## Scope note for reviewers\n\nThis is in the write path every memory traverses. The behavioural delta\nis precisely: *a caller who explicitly passed one of the five override\nfields now keeps their value on the inline path* — which the deferred\npath already does and the dropped comment already claimed. **No caller\ngains or loses enrichment it was previously getting.**\n\n## Verification\n\n- **14 new tests. 10 fail against pre-fix source**; the other 4 assert\ndeliberately-unchanged behaviour (legacy fallback, deferred path, the\n`model_fields_set` mechanism) and pass on both sides.\n- Full suite: **526 failed / 3688 passed** here vs **526 failed / 3674\npassed** on clean `main` — identical failure count (pre-existing local\nDB schema drift), **+14 passing**.\n- **0 new mypy errors** (343 both sides). `ruff check` / `format` clean.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Eyal Blyachman <eyal.b@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T12:25:37Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/2f16a8f2e9ef3d2f0a5385e32a382c79c6415c73/comments","author":{"login":"eyal-bl","id":262085605,"node_id":"U_kgDOD58b5Q","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/262085605?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl","html_url":"https://github.com/eyal-bl","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/eyal-bl/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe"}]},{"sha":"fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGZhYjUyZTRiOThhN2MxOGVlOThkOTRmMzJlODk5NGFhNThkNzUyZmU","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-12T12:07:52Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T12:07:52Z"},"message":"feat(memory): wire conflict-record write into the detector, flag-gated (A55 1d) (#766)\n\n## What & why\n\nCompletes the **conflict-record side of A55 1d**: when\n`contradiction_write_conflict_record` is **ON** (default **OFF**),\n`_detect` additionally persists a `memory_conflicts` classification\nrecord for each confirmed conflict. **Additive** — it never touches the\n`status`/`supersedes_id` effect, so retrieval is unchanged.\n\n## How\n\n- **config:** `contradiction_write_conflict_record` (default `False`).\n- **`_detect`:** collects `(candidate, kind, confidence)` pairs at the\nRDF + semantic confirm sites and — behind the flag, *after* the effect\nis applied — calls `record_detected_conflicts` (deferred import to avoid\nthe detector↔resolver import cycle).\n- **resolver:**\n- `record_conflict(..., result=…)` skips re-classification when the\ncaller already has it.\n- `record_conflict_from_verdict` records a detector-confirmed conflict\n**without a second LLM call** — aligned to the supersede effect\n(`exact_value` / `temporal_change` / `supersede`).\n- `record_detected_conflicts` dispatches `rdf` → classify\n(deterministic, no LLM) vs `semantic` → verdict.\n\n## No-degradation\n\n- Flag-ON test: records the RDF pair **and** preserves the\n`outdated`+`supersedes` effect.\n- Flag-OFF test: records nothing but still applies the effect (identical\nto today).\n- Broad contradiction sweep: **497 passed**, only the known pre-existing\ncreate_all `ph5b` failure — **zero new failures**. Golden differential\nunchanged. mypy + ruff clean.\n\n## Validation still to do (with the flag)\n\nA `.dev`/local **wet test with both flags ON** — write two contradicting\nmemories, confirm a `memory_conflicts` row lands and status/supersedes\nare unchanged. Belongs to the flag-rollout step.\n\n## Explicitly out of scope (follow-ups)\n\n- Path C (entity-aware) record wiring + richer semantic-conflict\nclassification.\n- Populating `memories.confidence`/`is_inferred`/`scope` → **A59**.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"abeb02db03adce0bc6437623f0c5345a3aa36b5c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/abeb02db03adce0bc6437623f0c5345a3aa36b5c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfGIYCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAif0QADIaHVYwUXQ80t7UyMESk3jY\nk8mzDK2J9D8VCy/oFGfh1dGNeZPNJvkmzgPZMVWAz/wCX8SO9em9zM2q9Nyup9p1\nt57YkDdkZZfHSKnG1vcUgxkoBnIa+1QUK1JvKHjMpFhYRm0ZI2ArNa4UWU4ontzZ\no0xBagwGT2UAjc01iWMDRO8p2NY7UqJtxrZPV0BNPDFv3ERMJioYZL4C7BCXm1wr\njQhywabZOnDkaLRZPAv0hVnCUu23AVoN2P8gIclEBSaQCpzt0fNEXJBWEG2B4bYM\nJicMOPK9o8TsSkbJWxRAg/MnbQnv0lX8/NmfzsW9RpALbx44mEgdcOefT3gsVAcg\nNCKw3pBz0PGM+2GktvptCcGP/DegzqrOmQLqwNhffo/X37tTK9u+Lt9cW+RnBABK\nfE1TR77qUX/oozA8TvFy1CPdqBl9ZCtkIzVDtDsazn1pXftR7UHwjArNsQSQPvbB\nThJrfO/nx9u2c6OV+En4mdpOjQChdEWOhrh+45gX3mGYw4NC39QSVLIymBusWiMw\nmyIpEeSP4cBFv7VbrSyE2jrJRYgp4tzlfHFKCsjcix5geAsr6k6C3XNTCA4/iY7i\nD0E5892i9zAfmKCYcOk7tUQtcgrSnrl+kewNaFe5nmJu7aYHJpRTZhDGMT0WZea7\nMR1g1SBDa60hD+mfjuiB\n=tAjK\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree abeb02db03adce0bc6437623f0c5345a3aa36b5c\nparent 14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1786536472 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786536472 +0300\n\nfeat(memory): wire conflict-record write into the detector, flag-gated (A55 1d) (#766)\n\n## What & why\n\nCompletes the **conflict-record side of A55 1d**: when\n`contradiction_write_conflict_record` is **ON** (default **OFF**),\n`_detect` additionally persists a `memory_conflicts` classification\nrecord for each confirmed conflict. **Additive** — it never touches the\n`status`/`supersedes_id` effect, so retrieval is unchanged.\n\n## How\n\n- **config:** `contradiction_write_conflict_record` (default `False`).\n- **`_detect`:** collects `(candidate, kind, confidence)` pairs at the\nRDF + semantic confirm sites and — behind the flag, *after* the effect\nis applied — calls `record_detected_conflicts` (deferred import to avoid\nthe detector↔resolver import cycle).\n- **resolver:**\n- `record_conflict(..., result=…)` skips re-classification when the\ncaller already has it.\n- `record_conflict_from_verdict` records a detector-confirmed conflict\n**without a second LLM call** — aligned to the supersede effect\n(`exact_value` / `temporal_change` / `supersede`).\n- `record_detected_conflicts` dispatches `rdf` → classify\n(deterministic, no LLM) vs `semantic` → verdict.\n\n## No-degradation\n\n- Flag-ON test: records the RDF pair **and** preserves the\n`outdated`+`supersedes` effect.\n- Flag-OFF test: records nothing but still applies the effect (identical\nto today).\n- Broad contradiction sweep: **497 passed**, only the known pre-existing\ncreate_all `ph5b` failure — **zero new failures**. Golden differential\nunchanged. mypy + ruff clean.\n\n## Validation still to do (with the flag)\n\nA `.dev`/local **wet test with both flags ON** — write two contradicting\nmemories, confirm a `memory_conflicts` row lands and status/supersedes\nare unchanged. Belongs to the flag-rollout step.\n\n## Explicitly out of scope (follow-ups)\n\n- Path C (entity-aware) record wiring + richer semantic-conflict\nclassification.\n- Populating `memories.confidence`/`is_inferred`/`scope` → **A59**.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T12:07:55Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/fab52e4b98a7c18ee98d94f32e8994aa58d752fe/comments","author":{"login":"arkash20","id":55495209,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1NDk1MjA5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55495209?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20","html_url":"https://github.com/arkash20","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30"}]},{"sha":"14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDE0ZjFiMmI5MGViYWIwY2MyYmNiYjUxMzU5NDYyZWJmYjY0ZmNjMzA","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-12T11:48:50Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T11:48:50Z"},"message":"feat(memory): conflict-record write machinery — storage + resolver (A55 1d) (#764)\n\n## What & why\n\nThe machinery to persist the L1/L2/L3 classification as a\n`memory_conflicts` row. **Additive and dormant** — nothing calls the\nresolver in production yet (the detector wiring is a following slice\nbehind its own flag), and it writes only the classification **record**;\nthe memory-row `status`/`supersedes_id` effect stays with the detector,\nunchanged. The golden differential test is unaffected.\n\n## Storage\n- `postgres_service.memory_conflict_record` — insert a\n`memory_conflicts` row, validating enum-like fields against the model\nvocab (bad value → **400**, not a DB CHECK 500).\n- `POST /memories/conflicts` + `_MEMORY_CONFLICT_FIELDS`.\n- `storage_client.record_memory_conflict`.\n\n## Resolver — `services/contradiction/resolver.py`\n`record_conflict(new_memory, candidate, …)`:\n1. `classify()` (L1 relationship + L2 diagnosis)\n2. `resolve()` (L3 action + audit_reason; `is_inferred` from either\nmemory gates destructive actions)\n3. build payload (+ `evidence_strength`) → `record_memory_conflict()`\n\nBest-effort: a storage failure returns `None` (logged), never breaks the\nwrite path.\n\n## No-degradation\nRecord-only — no `status`/`supersedes_id` mutation. The RDF-pair test\nasserts `exact_value/temporal_change → supersede`, matching today's RDF\neffect.\n\n## Tests\nStorage round-trip + bad-enum 400; resolver RDF→supersede,\nLLM→classification, inferred→invariant downgrade, storage-failure\nswallowed. **Storage suite 118 passed; core-api contradiction suite 71\npassed.** mypy + ruff clean.\n\n## Next (final sub-slice, not here)\nWire `record_conflict` into the detector where it already holds both\nmemories (RDF/semantic/Path-C), behind a new\n`contradiction_write_conflict_record` flag (default OFF), avoiding the\ndouble-LLM on the semantic path; new test that flag-ON writes a row +\ngolden proves status/supersedes unchanged; then a `.dev` wet test.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9b57200610feeb9d76cbcb62a1734508506ae5dc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/9b57200610feeb9d76cbcb62a1734508506ae5dc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfF2iCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmqMQABpB2mhsjAJ9BiUrX3c3UA3p\nif0kCGvhSoFjcSa9vkCKYTob1cIEbQPzZJr2F/eAaLvwqhrOxzdDcOC7XY/QbvfX\neV/jEaRCR1n/Jnt7ojSNJjNhyAD7rNVn8TujbdUjTAPH6h2l3we7xshJdMUusMIy\nc98mD9PhCWWqRAhsSQzExYQSHl991V/jPaH4OArlq6lMfW1J0Yxkl03kD+FU+bUM\nE+qw9YcpftMscYXANfaE6TnBbSJHlvmVKj9kK5wT06kpE9/zwjhQeYgZ0tI5IKza\nXiOZuRFs/AX5okne9RD0VvCx/zSSfAjKs1Q38YmmUsb2QiE+6T8t1qVD9H+9phwL\ni4G5yNEg/XMEXrsbhkbY3uUTG28gCzbucoWPWYZYjA/cihCoYflx7or6UP5I6zNv\nk+JL5WF4MlkVgnFbdZshfwo9OjhHYSZVapGUBwEjSqg4ii2vcTwH7iubkxyHRuRO\nYFLXaKPDoK5FY1+j3HVTmomz7/K1FmiAXo6yQRyz3bu7j6/+ePHmuHDVCHzeFk+b\n/paNzwf0OmmsbKjFnn8nyxaC2izcrddV8X2A4TUTMdJAVnm4IZE9KXZJ4hhdJTiR\nePYv+kds1IMxjS9/gn4lPkoHSPHglc05cu/CWK9/LZqGbLK9lmRJ7Xpu28gcVjvA\n+Hc/wZuaPHfpJ0wEWw8v\n=qAPZ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 9b57200610feeb9d76cbcb62a1734508506ae5dc\nparent 7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1786535330 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786535330 +0300\n\nfeat(memory): conflict-record write machinery — storage + resolver (A55 1d) (#764)\n\n## What & why\n\nThe machinery to persist the L1/L2/L3 classification as a\n`memory_conflicts` row. **Additive and dormant** — nothing calls the\nresolver in production yet (the detector wiring is a following slice\nbehind its own flag), and it writes only the classification **record**;\nthe memory-row `status`/`supersedes_id` effect stays with the detector,\nunchanged. The golden differential test is unaffected.\n\n## Storage\n- `postgres_service.memory_conflict_record` — insert a\n`memory_conflicts` row, validating enum-like fields against the model\nvocab (bad value → **400**, not a DB CHECK 500).\n- `POST /memories/conflicts` + `_MEMORY_CONFLICT_FIELDS`.\n- `storage_client.record_memory_conflict`.\n\n## Resolver — `services/contradiction/resolver.py`\n`record_conflict(new_memory, candidate, …)`:\n1. `classify()` (L1 relationship + L2 diagnosis)\n2. `resolve()` (L3 action + audit_reason; `is_inferred` from either\nmemory gates destructive actions)\n3. build payload (+ `evidence_strength`) → `record_memory_conflict()`\n\nBest-effort: a storage failure returns `None` (logged), never breaks the\nwrite path.\n\n## No-degradation\nRecord-only — no `status`/`supersedes_id` mutation. The RDF-pair test\nasserts `exact_value/temporal_change → supersede`, matching today's RDF\neffect.\n\n## Tests\nStorage round-trip + bad-enum 400; resolver RDF→supersede,\nLLM→classification, inferred→invariant downgrade, storage-failure\nswallowed. **Storage suite 118 passed; core-api contradiction suite 71\npassed.** mypy + ruff clean.\n\n## Next (final sub-slice, not here)\nWire `record_conflict` into the detector where it already holds both\nmemories (RDF/semantic/Path-C), behind a new\n`contradiction_write_conflict_record` flag (default OFF), avoiding the\ndouble-LLM on the semantic path; new test that flag-ON writes a row +\ngolden proves status/supersedes unchanged; then a `.dev` wet test.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T11:48:53Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/14f1b2b90ebab0cc2bcbb51359462ebfb64fcc30/comments","author":{"login":"arkash20","id":55495209,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1NDk1MjA5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55495209?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20","html_url":"https://github.com/arkash20","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af"}]},{"sha":"7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDcyMTlkODBkMGM0NjFkYWE4ZDUyOTZlOTQ0NzU4NTI2MGEwNGQwYWY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T11:38:30Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T11:38:30Z"},"message":"fix(crystallizer): read the embedding-coverage keys the endpoint returns (#763)\n\n## The bug\n\n`_check_missing_embeddings` read keys that have never existed:\n\n```python\nmissing_count = coverage.get(\"missing_count\", 0)\nmissing_ids = coverage.get(\"missing_ids\", [])\n```\n\n`GET /memories/embedding-coverage`\n(`core-storage-api/.../routers/memories.py:751-772`) returns exactly\nthree keys:\n\n```python\n{\"total_active\": total, \"missing_embeddings\": missing, \"coverage_pct\": ...}\n```\n\nNeither `missing_count` nor `missing_ids` is among them, so both\n`.get()` calls fell through to their defaults and the check returned\n`{\"count\": 0, \"affected_ids\": []}` **unconditionally**. Every tenant\nreported a clean bill of health regardless of how many rows were\nunembedded — and because it fails to the \"healthy\" value, nothing ever\nlooked wrong.\n\n**This is live, not theoretical.** Prod logs `Storing memory without\nembedding; deferred backfill scheduled`\n(`core_api.pipeline.steps.write.write_memory_row`) **322 times in the\nlast 48 hours** and over 5,000 in 30 days. The condition this check\nexists to surface happens constantly and has never once been reported.\n\n## Why `affected_ids` is dropped rather than repaired\n\nThe endpoint exposes only counts; there is no id source behind it.\n`memory_count_missing_embeddings` (`postgres_service.py:2371`) is the\nonly relevant service method and it returns a `COUNT`.\n\nDropping the key is safe and changes nothing on the wire:\n\n- `_generate_issues` builds every finding with `\"affected_ids\":\n(affected_ids or [])[:MAX_AFFECTED_IDS]`, so the field is always present\nand the `MISSING_EMBEDDINGS` finding keeps the same shape as its five\nsiblings.\n- The response is **byte-identical to today's**, because today that list\nis *already* always empty — that was the second half of the same bug.\n- Nothing in the repo reads `affected_ids`: a search across `*.py`,\n`*.ts`, `*.tsx` and `*.md` for the whole project tree returns no\nconsumer outside `crystallizer_service.py` itself.\n\nPopulating it would mean extending the storage endpoint to select ids as\nwell. Worth doing if a consumer appears; not worth a cross-service\nchange for a field nobody reads.\n\n`MAX_AFFECTED_IDS` is still used by nine other call sites, so no dead\nconstant.\n\n## Not affected\n\n`_compute_health` reads `coverage.get(\"coverage_pct\", 0.0)` — a real key\n— and keeps working. That asymmetry is what made this hard to spot: the\ncoverage percentage on the health report has been correct the whole\ntime, so the report looked internally consistent while the hygiene check\nbeside it said zero.\n\n## Tests\n\nThree, in a new `TestMissingEmbeddingsCheck`:\n\n1. **The regression** — a coverage response of `{\"total_active\": 10,\n\"missing_embeddings\": 4, \"coverage_pct\": 60.0}` must report `count ==\n4`. Confirmed failing before the fix:\n\n   ```\n   >       assert result[\"count\"] == 4\n   E       assert 0 == 4\n   ```\n\n2. Full coverage still reports `0`, so the read can't be inverted into\n\"count the ones that are fine\".\n\n3. **The endpoint's key names, asserted against the endpoint's own\nsource.** The failure mode here was a silent key mismatch across a\nservice boundary — a unit test with a hand-written mock would have\npassed happily against the broken code, since the mock would have been\nwritten from the same wrong assumption. Pinning the contract means a\nchange on the storage side fails here rather than quietly reverting the\ncheck to 0.\n\n47 passed across the three crystallizer test files. `ruff check` and\n`ruff format --check` clean.\n\n## One thing deliberately left out of this PR\n\n`_remediate_missing_embeddings` (same file, ~line 227) has the **same\nclass of bug** and is also a permanent no-op: it reads\n`candidates.get(\"missing_embeddings\", [])` from `GET\n/lifecycle-candidates`, which returns only `expired_still_active` and\n`stale_low_weight`. It is called unconditionally from\n`run_crystallization`, so no crystallization run has ever re-embedded\nanything.\n\nIt is not in scope here because **it cannot be fixed by renaming a key**\n— no endpoint or service method returns missing-embedding *rows* (`id` +\n`content`), which is what it needs. The real options are to add such an\nendpoint, or to delete the function in favour of\n`core-worker/src/core_worker/backfill.py`, which already scans NULL rows\nand publishes `EMBED_REQUESTED` through the normal deferred path. That\nis a design decision, and inlining embedding writes into a\ncrystallization run also competes for the same saturated concurrency\ngate behind the current prod bursts. Tracked separately.\n\nTogether the two bugs explain the pattern: nothing surfaced missing\nembeddings, and nothing repaired them.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4f8d4f2a66b742d5cfec8a100a377bf8fcbacd44","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/4f8d4f2a66b742d5cfec8a100a377bf8fcbacd44"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfFs2CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAADBYQAJrM9jOucBckK4zbFsCzTz4X\nwjVzG/8kTHu3oFDWuFHCCzU3S9AKgV/fv8Bxe3mKTjEKb7UaljiqDoBNUgvJcpGt\nNY14AtQLqtD+q7BUyl8Zz8K8rUtKrWKFFMkfh0TzW3JszUCoM+yhgQw3kU/PWXE4\nDsTdP409H0oDCGtBC3r/ik34HlDYy1frmEXshjpEepLVVUprm3T7ReNJnvIuH4pf\nU07msaK34yCvmjaCPR2WxtaHtwHstCd2WacuqxMKTJj0mI5JpNvXBQsuNiAB6T4H\nuE5iWMJ5lE6/d9SXjtGdzqC2ZEPAFbE/KMB6xv3+k6OXebPOhs+pse+2bpgWpj07\n5uuWTqaA+lIgrpOz28qmonoKu0FG5I95DtQJ3N9OF2BVblh0OSkmaDkZSK8sRwZp\nefwPr20bswTV0o1zVxdAP1vu9qIaw2aZZuWMvDOFHRE0kM8E/V1KUTyxsVcEZxiX\nvVr4uGb6oBSFY2WcKzfs5OMOBG/Y/tovEI2RAYD9XQeJus+a6wWcM9dQYKn2/BHw\nPSiaWqjuJXoLbXL3Dzex+Mxdp/bGCPCoVVCduzZZt6F7UDM/GZjgDteTPBXuwmb9\nZpJ8J+x9DeOP892YVJspqgGu6/oew7muhKYItR90aUyfOeWVINh8I+DiFFj8bTqa\njcrGSF/AYNswaKGGQ8ry\n=4GSu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 4f8d4f2a66b742d5cfec8a100a377bf8fcbacd44\nparent ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786534710 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786534710 +0300\n\nfix(crystallizer): read the embedding-coverage keys the endpoint returns (#763)\n\n## The bug\n\n`_check_missing_embeddings` read keys that have never existed:\n\n```python\nmissing_count = coverage.get(\"missing_count\", 0)\nmissing_ids = coverage.get(\"missing_ids\", [])\n```\n\n`GET /memories/embedding-coverage`\n(`core-storage-api/.../routers/memories.py:751-772`) returns exactly\nthree keys:\n\n```python\n{\"total_active\": total, \"missing_embeddings\": missing, \"coverage_pct\": ...}\n```\n\nNeither `missing_count` nor `missing_ids` is among them, so both\n`.get()` calls fell through to their defaults and the check returned\n`{\"count\": 0, \"affected_ids\": []}` **unconditionally**. Every tenant\nreported a clean bill of health regardless of how many rows were\nunembedded — and because it fails to the \"healthy\" value, nothing ever\nlooked wrong.\n\n**This is live, not theoretical.** Prod logs `Storing memory without\nembedding; deferred backfill scheduled`\n(`core_api.pipeline.steps.write.write_memory_row`) **322 times in the\nlast 48 hours** and over 5,000 in 30 days. The condition this check\nexists to surface happens constantly and has never once been reported.\n\n## Why `affected_ids` is dropped rather than repaired\n\nThe endpoint exposes only counts; there is no id source behind it.\n`memory_count_missing_embeddings` (`postgres_service.py:2371`) is the\nonly relevant service method and it returns a `COUNT`.\n\nDropping the key is safe and changes nothing on the wire:\n\n- `_generate_issues` builds every finding with `\"affected_ids\":\n(affected_ids or [])[:MAX_AFFECTED_IDS]`, so the field is always present\nand the `MISSING_EMBEDDINGS` finding keeps the same shape as its five\nsiblings.\n- The response is **byte-identical to today's**, because today that list\nis *already* always empty — that was the second half of the same bug.\n- Nothing in the repo reads `affected_ids`: a search across `*.py`,\n`*.ts`, `*.tsx` and `*.md` for the whole project tree returns no\nconsumer outside `crystallizer_service.py` itself.\n\nPopulating it would mean extending the storage endpoint to select ids as\nwell. Worth doing if a consumer appears; not worth a cross-service\nchange for a field nobody reads.\n\n`MAX_AFFECTED_IDS` is still used by nine other call sites, so no dead\nconstant.\n\n## Not affected\n\n`_compute_health` reads `coverage.get(\"coverage_pct\", 0.0)` — a real key\n— and keeps working. That asymmetry is what made this hard to spot: the\ncoverage percentage on the health report has been correct the whole\ntime, so the report looked internally consistent while the hygiene check\nbeside it said zero.\n\n## Tests\n\nThree, in a new `TestMissingEmbeddingsCheck`:\n\n1. **The regression** — a coverage response of `{\"total_active\": 10,\n\"missing_embeddings\": 4, \"coverage_pct\": 60.0}` must report `count ==\n4`. Confirmed failing before the fix:\n\n   ```\n   >       assert result[\"count\"] == 4\n   E       assert 0 == 4\n   ```\n\n2. Full coverage still reports `0`, so the read can't be inverted into\n\"count the ones that are fine\".\n\n3. **The endpoint's key names, asserted against the endpoint's own\nsource.** The failure mode here was a silent key mismatch across a\nservice boundary — a unit test with a hand-written mock would have\npassed happily against the broken code, since the mock would have been\nwritten from the same wrong assumption. Pinning the contract means a\nchange on the storage side fails here rather than quietly reverting the\ncheck to 0.\n\n47 passed across the three crystallizer test files. `ruff check` and\n`ruff format --check` clean.\n\n## One thing deliberately left out of this PR\n\n`_remediate_missing_embeddings` (same file, ~line 227) has the **same\nclass of bug** and is also a permanent no-op: it reads\n`candidates.get(\"missing_embeddings\", [])` from `GET\n/lifecycle-candidates`, which returns only `expired_still_active` and\n`stale_low_weight`. It is called unconditionally from\n`run_crystallization`, so no crystallization run has ever re-embedded\nanything.\n\nIt is not in scope here because **it cannot be fixed by renaming a key**\n— no endpoint or service method returns missing-embedding *rows* (`id` +\n`content`), which is what it needs. The real options are to add such an\nendpoint, or to delete the function in favour of\n`core-worker/src/core_worker/backfill.py`, which already scans NULL rows\nand publishes `EMBED_REQUESTED` through the normal deferred path. That\nis a design decision, and inlining embedding writes into a\ncrystallization run also competes for the same saturated concurrency\ngate behind the current prod bursts. Tracked separately.\n\nTogether the two bugs explain the pattern: nothing surfaced missing\nembeddings, and nothing repaired them.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T11:38:33Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7219d80d0c461daa8d5296e9447585260a04d0af/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f"}]},{"sha":"ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGVmOGJlNGIyYTVmYTZhMGJhNTY1NThlNmRiNzAzMzRlNzQ4MGFmNmY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T11:16:42Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T11:16:42Z"},"message":"fix(embedding): set per-phase httpx timeouts, matching the LLM client (#761)\n\n## What\n\nReplaces the bare-float `timeout` on the embedding provider's\n`AsyncOpenAI` client with an explicit `httpx.Timeout`, mirroring what\n`common/llm/providers/openai.py` already does.\n\n## Why\n\n`AsyncOpenAI(timeout=<float>)` looks like it sets the whole budget, but\nit leaves **httpx's default 5 s connect phase** in place. On Cloud Run\nwith a VPC connector in `all-traffic` egress mode, outbound connects\nintermittently exceed 5 s — so calls failed as `httpcore.ConnectTimeout`\nwell inside the nominal 25 s request budget.\n\nThis is not a new diagnosis. The LLM client took exactly this fix, and\n`common/llm/constants.py:110-113` names the two symptoms it was meant to\ncure:\n\n> ...handlers (\"pubsub handler raised; nacking for redelivery\" + \"Entity\nextraction failed\" — the latter permanently skips entity links for that\nmemory).\n\nThe embedding client was simply missed, so the same trickle continued\nthere.\n\n## Why it turned into 321 alerts\n\nThe worker's `provider.embed(...)` in `core-worker/consumer.py` is the\none Pub/Sub handler that can propagate an exception —\n`handle_enrich_request` falls through to a heuristic and core-api's\nhandlers use `track_task`. When it propagates,\n`common/events/pubsub.py:749-758` nacks with `\"ack_deadline_seconds\":\n0`, i.e. redeliver immediately, and there is no delivery-attempt counter\nor dead-letter topic anywhere in the code. Staging logged 8,720\noccurrences and sent **321 alert notifications** off roughly two dozen\ndistinct failures.\n\nWorth being precise about scope: **this bounds the per-attempt connect,\nnot the redelivery loop.** A dead-letter topic and a retry policy on the\nstaging subscriptions are still wanted, and by design those live in\ninfra rather than here (`pubsub.py:7-14`). This removes the cause; that\nwould cap the blast radius.\n\n## Compatibility\n\nOnly connect gains headroom (15 s, env-tunable). `read` and `write` keep\n`OPENAI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, and `pool` tracks it unless explicitly\ndecoupled via `EMBEDDING_HTTPX_POOL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` — so **no existing\ndeployment sees a shorter budget than the bare float gave it**,\nincluding one running `OPENAI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60`. Same shape\nand naming as the LLM-side constants.\n\n## Test\n\n`test_timeout_sets_connect_phase_not_bare_float` asserts the client is\nconstructed with an `httpx.Timeout` whose connect phase beats httpx's 5\ns default, and that read/write/pool still carry the request budget. It\npins the two providers together so they cannot drift apart again.\n\nConfirmed it fails without the fix:\n\n```\nE       AssertionError: expected httpx.Timeout so the connect phase is explicit, got float\n```\n\n51 passed in that module; 78 passed across the embedding suites. `ruff\ncheck` and `ruff format --check` clean on the touched files.\n\n> Deliberately **not** included: raising `EMBEDDING_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH`\nfrom 32 to 128. I had that on my list, but the constant's own comment is\nright — the local `docker-compose` stack starts TEI without\n`--max-client-batch-size`, so TEI's own default of 32 applies and a\ncode-default change would cause 413s there. That belongs in the\nprod/staging deployment env, where TEI is explicitly launched with 128.\n\nFound while triaging the prod/staging error-alerts backlog.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"19492bea062a2a99b07b8c64fbc014c3d1f4cf90","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/19492bea062a2a99b07b8c64fbc014c3d1f4cf90"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfFYaCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5C0QAGWGj+XBGJ6FD5Rvj1Awxoxv\ntoWLmONXMO/SdpHpHpf2IDZPOXFQMxP5rASw4jQNAvfXU37eO4i8HbfV5NA5vXjQ\nGdvmmsT/7amN33bEC5ZD7/D6p1pXUZfN6tv/XAiAU6a4m2S8I385lmpd81uhaX47\nDXyQYkNi3clnyyTCJDK68bjMT+cjREXaN7ag+f/E0HeCosfsF/iyRX9OfaQRzxVE\nIsTk2zIw0NOqrUc0GEgAKOFWXdnCbHh3CFZ6keizGrucMh5zyKxUdf9D8WwhvBf2\nzzXYsKeIf7VVeV5tNPoIpYmMkrR3Y1u9SJtlUso0LezEwl3NFfmqQbWz7MlhTxde\nTgdNRJJugwrGt2MVHflYu1sS0cfKzm0SllNGL2/s6mzzwVjXymcYCWOoOZlbvJ9X\nlkOvZWhNWSUt6zkurLJmHlIPE9nE+a4Cw3dnfS3886e3skZGbCR42jYC99T/WWzx\nKLiu9mH66DcMj6IR8wAvgDRMLZfuz/Ckmvk3ODfY5aOGhEToA4KGasX4jOIScA4s\nej1rwvaZaQHtVDWf8JS+tcKN6RhxQXzmcmYj+7ERmvPE6goc7+vVPlWyzmC1GOey\n+FkqCe1y9QhwR0GJ7GzTuTC1wewwmID8lq6Sz5vPTBiOcLl+c32If3mT93ho5dEG\nC0i2oNATFf2lVjvql7tK\n=2EvN\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 19492bea062a2a99b07b8c64fbc014c3d1f4cf90\nparent 077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786533402 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786533402 +0300\n\nfix(embedding): set per-phase httpx timeouts, matching the LLM client (#761)\n\n## What\n\nReplaces the bare-float `timeout` on the embedding provider's\n`AsyncOpenAI` client with an explicit `httpx.Timeout`, mirroring what\n`common/llm/providers/openai.py` already does.\n\n## Why\n\n`AsyncOpenAI(timeout=<float>)` looks like it sets the whole budget, but\nit leaves **httpx's default 5 s connect phase** in place. On Cloud Run\nwith a VPC connector in `all-traffic` egress mode, outbound connects\nintermittently exceed 5 s — so calls failed as `httpcore.ConnectTimeout`\nwell inside the nominal 25 s request budget.\n\nThis is not a new diagnosis. The LLM client took exactly this fix, and\n`common/llm/constants.py:110-113` names the two symptoms it was meant to\ncure:\n\n> ...handlers (\"pubsub handler raised; nacking for redelivery\" + \"Entity\nextraction failed\" — the latter permanently skips entity links for that\nmemory).\n\nThe embedding client was simply missed, so the same trickle continued\nthere.\n\n## Why it turned into 321 alerts\n\nThe worker's `provider.embed(...)` in `core-worker/consumer.py` is the\none Pub/Sub handler that can propagate an exception —\n`handle_enrich_request` falls through to a heuristic and core-api's\nhandlers use `track_task`. When it propagates,\n`common/events/pubsub.py:749-758` nacks with `\"ack_deadline_seconds\":\n0`, i.e. redeliver immediately, and there is no delivery-attempt counter\nor dead-letter topic anywhere in the code. Staging logged 8,720\noccurrences and sent **321 alert notifications** off roughly two dozen\ndistinct failures.\n\nWorth being precise about scope: **this bounds the per-attempt connect,\nnot the redelivery loop.** A dead-letter topic and a retry policy on the\nstaging subscriptions are still wanted, and by design those live in\ninfra rather than here (`pubsub.py:7-14`). This removes the cause; that\nwould cap the blast radius.\n\n## Compatibility\n\nOnly connect gains headroom (15 s, env-tunable). `read` and `write` keep\n`OPENAI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, and `pool` tracks it unless explicitly\ndecoupled via `EMBEDDING_HTTPX_POOL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` — so **no existing\ndeployment sees a shorter budget than the bare float gave it**,\nincluding one running `OPENAI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60`. Same shape\nand naming as the LLM-side constants.\n\n## Test\n\n`test_timeout_sets_connect_phase_not_bare_float` asserts the client is\nconstructed with an `httpx.Timeout` whose connect phase beats httpx's 5\ns default, and that read/write/pool still carry the request budget. It\npins the two providers together so they cannot drift apart again.\n\nConfirmed it fails without the fix:\n\n```\nE       AssertionError: expected httpx.Timeout so the connect phase is explicit, got float\n```\n\n51 passed in that module; 78 passed across the embedding suites. `ruff\ncheck` and `ruff format --check` clean on the touched files.\n\n> Deliberately **not** included: raising `EMBEDDING_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH`\nfrom 32 to 128. I had that on my list, but the constant's own comment is\nright — the local `docker-compose` stack starts TEI without\n`--max-client-batch-size`, so TEI's own default of 32 applies and a\ncode-default change would cause 413s there. That belongs in the\nprod/staging deployment env, where TEI is explicitly launched with 128.\n\nFound while triaging the prod/staging error-alerts backlog.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T11:16:45Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ef8be4b2a5fa6a0ba56558e6db70334e7480af6f/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03"}]},{"sha":"077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDA3N2YzMGE0MmE5ZTIzZTI2ODkxMWExMDY1ZWIzNmJmN2ViODhhMDM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T11:12:17Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T11:12:17Z"},"message":"fix(memory): unshadow HTTPException so pipeline failures surface as 500 (#760)\n\n## What\n\nDeletes one redundant inline `from fastapi import HTTPException` in\n`_create_memory_pipeline`, and adds the regression test that was\nmissing.\n\n## Why\n\nThe inline import sat two conditionals deep inside the STM branch, so it\nalmost never executed — but it made `HTTPException` **function-local for\nthe whole function body**. The module already imports the name at top\nlevel, so it was pure redundancy with a sharp edge.\n\nVerified at the bytecode level, before the fix:\n\n```\nline 290: STORE_FAST        HTTPException\nline 292: LOAD_FAST_BORROW  HTTPException   <- safe, dominated by the STORE\nline 307: LOAD_FAST_CHECK   HTTPException   <- UnboundLocalError\nline 323: LOAD_FAST_CHECK   HTTPException   <- UnboundLocalError\nline 407: LOAD_FAST_CHECK   HTTPException   <- UnboundLocalError\n```\n\n`LOAD_FAST_CHECK` is the opcode that emits `cannot access local variable\n'X' where it is not associated with a value`. After the fix all four\nsites are `LOAD_GLOBAL`.\n\nLine 407 is on the standard persist path (`_USE_PIPELINE_WRITE = True`\nis a hard constant), reached whenever **any** write-pipeline step fails\nwithout raising — `pipeline/runner.py` catches a non-`HTTPException`\nstep error, records `FAILED` and returns rather than re-raising. So the\ntrigger is not exotic; it is any upstream failure. Observed ~1100 times\non staging since 2026-06-24.\n\n## Two consequences beyond the 500\n\n1. **Metrics recorded these failures as successes.** The error fires\nwhile evaluating the right-hand side, so the assignment to `_exc` never\ncompletes and `_exc` stays `None`. The `finally` block then logs\n`memory_write_latency` with `\"success\": _exc is None`. Captured from the\npre-fix test run:\n\n   ```\n{\"path\": \"memory-write\", ..., \"success\": true, \"message\":\n\"memory_write_latency\"}\n   ```\n\nAny dashboard built on that field has been reporting these as clean\nwrites.\n\n2. **It masked the real incidents.** The runner already logs the failing\nstep with `exc_info`, so each `UnboundLocalError` had a companion line\nnaming the actual culprit — that is how the storage-side 500s and the\nnightly `discover_cross_links` 504s were found.\n\n## Test\n\n`test_pipeline_failed_result_surfaces_http_500_not_unbound_local` drives\nthe `result.failed` branch and asserts both symptoms.\n\nThe existing `test_pipeline_emits_latency_log_on_pipeline_failure`\ncovers the path where `Pipeline.run` **raises**, where `_exc` is bound\nby the `except BaseException` handler — which is why it stayed green\nthrough all of this. The branch where `run` returns a failed result had\nno coverage at all.\n\nConfirmed the test fails without the fix:\n\n```\n>       _exc = HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=\"Memory write pipeline failed unexpectedly\")\nE       UnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable 'HTTPException' where it is not associated with a value\ncore-api/src/core_api/services/memory_service.py:407: UnboundLocalError\n```\n\nand passes with it. Full module: 35 passed. `ruff check` and `ruff\nformat --check` both clean.\n\nFound while triaging the prod/staging error-alerts backlog.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f585a507681bc090c7daf2ed8e6d73b29d87136f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/f585a507681bc090c7daf2ed8e6d73b29d87136f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfFUSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAuswQAGfQgv8VwAAzve1HzIRcL3ZE\nbJsshw7IkKbwQFpxTo7A/95mcBSLUy+zyCl14RhkhGNLJ9xvZrEQBs5lCt0A3koF\nl3mbeDkMaYIc3m8XC0p2MMZtpdE6tXUZ+ZuqemiKVNrc8Bn0kMJDlXHeMcAw2+L5\nfOiHYAuNeMJlekirlTtMXUKLZQI2MdH1LQkHfVx1Zz4OB2uRGYf/Gn2NrCScic6z\n7YE0LcrLoUouiUsHEuqKQHZ8zIaoI6/tR15MFhosmswl6Y5TxMPcWl3uyDqHZYy2\n4XsKjrbbfQHcXNqHlzce8QkFzIJ7raqn5OVIzoFyUS+jCC85guN5znobv6bVHX73\nP3FdC0gBuua7mFqr4i9kLNTeLRpEYlreoOpf+LbHIkh0bigbMncYVAagMlDbbtth\nJT8Y7rwNeJ/ay5j6/EoMHbm2VVVj0SdGdT+QyTnAMOwsoCidx32mGCizOra7YZDc\ntpIsSbtPnNLwlyWgXumw6PML6b4jv4sqABa1hfbn1LrJ/sonL64sI6s3TqDozJCx\nWXujNYmpYAdgKs0cEjlgyJWBkFEj5xt3LIU1L3I8Qr2XYBbK0DYmxXfb4odzY8Sp\nYjjBkrE4k3D5cl1WmXzoYv6qphXW6Ib+xAxIfh2NHZe6BVZ8ld70meRSB4zRH8XW\nSrUbH+2MvWg9NHkpBrmR\n=shXe\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree f585a507681bc090c7daf2ed8e6d73b29d87136f\nparent 9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786533137 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786533137 +0300\n\nfix(memory): unshadow HTTPException so pipeline failures surface as 500 (#760)\n\n## What\n\nDeletes one redundant inline `from fastapi import HTTPException` in\n`_create_memory_pipeline`, and adds the regression test that was\nmissing.\n\n## Why\n\nThe inline import sat two conditionals deep inside the STM branch, so it\nalmost never executed — but it made `HTTPException` **function-local for\nthe whole function body**. The module already imports the name at top\nlevel, so it was pure redundancy with a sharp edge.\n\nVerified at the bytecode level, before the fix:\n\n```\nline 290: STORE_FAST        HTTPException\nline 292: LOAD_FAST_BORROW  HTTPException   <- safe, dominated by the STORE\nline 307: LOAD_FAST_CHECK   HTTPException   <- UnboundLocalError\nline 323: LOAD_FAST_CHECK   HTTPException   <- UnboundLocalError\nline 407: LOAD_FAST_CHECK   HTTPException   <- UnboundLocalError\n```\n\n`LOAD_FAST_CHECK` is the opcode that emits `cannot access local variable\n'X' where it is not associated with a value`. After the fix all four\nsites are `LOAD_GLOBAL`.\n\nLine 407 is on the standard persist path (`_USE_PIPELINE_WRITE = True`\nis a hard constant), reached whenever **any** write-pipeline step fails\nwithout raising — `pipeline/runner.py` catches a non-`HTTPException`\nstep error, records `FAILED` and returns rather than re-raising. So the\ntrigger is not exotic; it is any upstream failure. Observed ~1100 times\non staging since 2026-06-24.\n\n## Two consequences beyond the 500\n\n1. **Metrics recorded these failures as successes.** The error fires\nwhile evaluating the right-hand side, so the assignment to `_exc` never\ncompletes and `_exc` stays `None`. The `finally` block then logs\n`memory_write_latency` with `\"success\": _exc is None`. Captured from the\npre-fix test run:\n\n   ```\n{\"path\": \"memory-write\", ..., \"success\": true, \"message\":\n\"memory_write_latency\"}\n   ```\n\nAny dashboard built on that field has been reporting these as clean\nwrites.\n\n2. **It masked the real incidents.** The runner already logs the failing\nstep with `exc_info`, so each `UnboundLocalError` had a companion line\nnaming the actual culprit — that is how the storage-side 500s and the\nnightly `discover_cross_links` 504s were found.\n\n## Test\n\n`test_pipeline_failed_result_surfaces_http_500_not_unbound_local` drives\nthe `result.failed` branch and asserts both symptoms.\n\nThe existing `test_pipeline_emits_latency_log_on_pipeline_failure`\ncovers the path where `Pipeline.run` **raises**, where `_exc` is bound\nby the `except BaseException` handler — which is why it stayed green\nthrough all of this. The branch where `run` returns a failed result had\nno coverage at all.\n\nConfirmed the test fails without the fix:\n\n```\n>       _exc = HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=\"Memory write pipeline failed unexpectedly\")\nE       UnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable 'HTTPException' where it is not associated with a value\ncore-api/src/core_api/services/memory_service.py:407: UnboundLocalError\n```\n\nand passes with it. Full module: 35 passed. `ruff check` and `ruff\nformat --check` both clean.\n\nFound while triaging the prod/staging error-alerts backlog.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T11:12:20Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/077f30a42a9e23e268911a1065eb36bf7eb88a03/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56"}]},{"sha":"9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDliOWQzYWVhMGViMDdiMjM4NDAyNzFjNmFjOTc2OGYyODJjYjdiNTY","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-12T10:58:28Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T10:58:28Z"},"message":"feat(memory): contradiction classification logic — L1/L2/L3 (A55 1d) (#759)\n\n## What & why\n\nThe unified contradiction model's **decision layer** — L1 (relationship)\n→ L2 (diagnosis) → L3 (action) — as **pure, dormant modules**. This is\nthe classification brain for A55 1d, landing *ahead* of the write slice\nthat wires it onto the engine-ON path.\n\n**Behaviour-neutral by construction:** nothing calls these in production\nyet, and the legacy detector / `CONTRADICTION_PROMPT` / verdict path is\nuntouched. So this PR provably changes nothing.\n\n## The three layers\n\n- **`relationship.py` (L1)** — `classify_relationship` /\n`extract_relationship`. Deterministic RDF exact-value short-circuit (no\nLLM); otherwise the detector's `CONTRADICTION_PROMPT` extended **on a\ncopy** with a relationship instruction, plus a derivation fallback from\nthe model's existing `non_conflict_reason` taxonomy. → one of the 7\n`RELATIONSHIPS` + confidence.\n- **`diagnosis.py` (L2)** — `extract_diagnosis` + the combined\n`classify()`. **One LLM call** yields both the relationship and the\ndiagnosis; most diagnoses derive from `same_subject` /\n`non_conflict_reason`. → one of the 6 `DIAGNOSES`.\n- **`resolution.py` (L3)** — `resolve()` maps `(relationship × diagnosis\n× evidence) → action + audit_reason`, applying the **safety\ninvariants**: inference must not overturn an explicit fact; a\nprobabilistic relationship softens (`downweight`); low confidence flags\n(`mark_disputed`).\n\n## No-degradation\n\n`resolve()` only **recommends** an action for the future\n`memory_conflicts` record — it does **not** apply any\n`status`/`supersedes_id` effect (that stays with the detector). So\ncurrent RDF/semantic/Path-C outcomes are unchanged;\n`test_temporal_change_maps_to_supersede_matching_today` anchors the\nalignment. The golden differential test (separate) remains the gate when\nthe write slice wires this in.\n\n## Tests\n\n**56 cases** — L1 21, L2 17, L3 18. mypy clean (196 files), ruff +\nformat clean.\n\n## Next slice (not in this PR)\n\nThe **write**: a storage endpoint + client to persist a\n`memory_conflicts` row and set\n`memories.confidence`/`is_inferred`/`scope`, wire\n`classify()`+`resolve()` into the engine resolver on the ON path\n(additive, status/supersedes untouched), + a `.dev` wet test with the\nflag on.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a5559f1e2b2fcae6ca57e2a109933115fd4f42b4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/a5559f1e2b2fcae6ca57e2a109933115fd4f42b4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfFHUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAArY8QAJd2R4daV4xuSB2d7y4/w5Lk\n2Mumdp2US5f+NOXNA3cYq314IJNcc9gGKsQyaoHKjN3T+perwr590jT+eHL/4wuW\nFgfGyuLDI59la3a6Wf8alrbEnQuDJbqG8MvYlKwkN2tEiAiVZi2ywsSCtTYxUMzt\ndvB25s8w1PJmWmHN1Lh4D1/DPG25i7skJROhJdfvwXLBvtxFnpmL7jN1qoHUhdTX\nwJlbo74oS1cVRqyUmLaKMFb4OIMLiusbto+FYGY3Ma9pawd7NAissaSHnhrsgREW\n6mHE4VRnSNFzO9iOryvynMIWeYYZs8u3uPQKFD9X/4snK+NCmr9yR7QSJKlyY0MT\nUnJRGZ54gmCQWzTPQaL9tTYhK0zdlW/yiTPBeDskx3wVMt6mLoUdCb6nvlkkwlsF\nQyi8DDI+G/PD6/U3v/nTj5/4XCdqRC+n7F+/W8wbAej/Gg2r9w9nRldDdU2Jq+lK\n1mScZfm+BykofvfL4z0+9Io8FwZpTnwwroaLqrQzineNy9dp4rn6x1t61jvsRUsP\nF4Q7FFUAa7XkF/kRW+tYfd07e4+U3twI6uTQHqSpo9NrFKUber20+DV+G7wySanx\nhAKgTgzHxAzPt+kzisLvRmksuRc9xsDv629BG8gQNX/8edAISyJNZiHN1kWiUx3u\n3Y86eY7AIs29N9PsjsCt\n=at7k\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree a5559f1e2b2fcae6ca57e2a109933115fd4f42b4\nparent aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1786532308 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786532308 +0300\n\nfeat(memory): contradiction classification logic — L1/L2/L3 (A55 1d) (#759)\n\n## What & why\n\nThe unified contradiction model's **decision layer** — L1 (relationship)\n→ L2 (diagnosis) → L3 (action) — as **pure, dormant modules**. This is\nthe classification brain for A55 1d, landing *ahead* of the write slice\nthat wires it onto the engine-ON path.\n\n**Behaviour-neutral by construction:** nothing calls these in production\nyet, and the legacy detector / `CONTRADICTION_PROMPT` / verdict path is\nuntouched. So this PR provably changes nothing.\n\n## The three layers\n\n- **`relationship.py` (L1)** — `classify_relationship` /\n`extract_relationship`. Deterministic RDF exact-value short-circuit (no\nLLM); otherwise the detector's `CONTRADICTION_PROMPT` extended **on a\ncopy** with a relationship instruction, plus a derivation fallback from\nthe model's existing `non_conflict_reason` taxonomy. → one of the 7\n`RELATIONSHIPS` + confidence.\n- **`diagnosis.py` (L2)** — `extract_diagnosis` + the combined\n`classify()`. **One LLM call** yields both the relationship and the\ndiagnosis; most diagnoses derive from `same_subject` /\n`non_conflict_reason`. → one of the 6 `DIAGNOSES`.\n- **`resolution.py` (L3)** — `resolve()` maps `(relationship × diagnosis\n× evidence) → action + audit_reason`, applying the **safety\ninvariants**: inference must not overturn an explicit fact; a\nprobabilistic relationship softens (`downweight`); low confidence flags\n(`mark_disputed`).\n\n## No-degradation\n\n`resolve()` only **recommends** an action for the future\n`memory_conflicts` record — it does **not** apply any\n`status`/`supersedes_id` effect (that stays with the detector). So\ncurrent RDF/semantic/Path-C outcomes are unchanged;\n`test_temporal_change_maps_to_supersede_matching_today` anchors the\nalignment. The golden differential test (separate) remains the gate when\nthe write slice wires this in.\n\n## Tests\n\n**56 cases** — L1 21, L2 17, L3 18. mypy clean (196 files), ruff +\nformat clean.\n\n## Next slice (not in this PR)\n\nThe **write**: a storage endpoint + client to persist a\n`memory_conflicts` row and set\n`memories.confidence`/`is_inferred`/`scope`, wire\n`classify()`+`resolve()` into the engine resolver on the ON path\n(additive, status/supersedes untouched), + a `.dev` wet test with the\nflag on.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T10:58:30Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9b9d3aea0eb07b23840271c6ac9768f282cb7b56/comments","author":{"login":"arkash20","id":55495209,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1NDk1MjA5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55495209?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20","html_url":"https://github.com/arkash20","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06"}]},{"sha":"aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGFlYzMxOTY5MjU4NTIyNTQxNTBmN2UxZWQwYjU0ZjQzNWVkOWRlMDY","commit":{"author":{"name":"erni-a","email":"erni@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T10:52:57Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T10:52:57Z"},"message":"CAURA-000- docs: Caura prose sweep — README body, guides, skills, client READMEs (#758)\n\n## What (rebrand Phase 3c, OSS side)\n\nCase-sensitive whole-word `MemClaw` → `Caura`: 126 occurrences / 13\nprose files — README body, AGENT-INSTALL, CONTRIBUTING,\n`static/docs/integration-guide.md`, both `SKILL.md` copies,\ncompany-brain skill, python + TS client READMEs, `docs/*`.\n\n## Protected / unchanged\n\n- Sentinel-protected verbatim: the hero bridge line, `Caura (formerly\nMemClaw)` heading, trademark line\n- All 107 `memclaw_*` tool literals across the two SKILL.md copies —\ngrep-verified intact\n- `memclaw.net` URLs (flip at cutover) · package names (Phase 6) · logo\nassets (pending wordmark)\n\n## Testing\n\nLocal run of skill/manifest tests hits the DB-fixture wall (integration\ntests need Postgres) — CI is authoritative here; the diff is\nmarkdown-only.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: erni <erni@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"119b65d22018fd41220499883890f9580ee13256","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/119b65d22018fd41220499883890f9580ee13256"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfFCJCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAOSEQAEXHFPMjEHEMzVnY2egCWhQe\n+fWqaql2elCCwix1aX6wg3YreCTy1fgaeBk3x15wn9Eufc9rgNFh7MyAa6BSM4s/\noeDrmtE+AXJnz9+U52Y/05vQxzXaTn8iMK9K9JqPUjfyZItycwoFLTFaMuAWwAom\nmwTITGorMZB1Gvdn9AI4A2icEC+PSrgmvHhSWPAW67VByf6ccNg/+mCq/31MMrlc\n7caNJSp+0TLd5EpSZu4eJlZNpT6UO+Lstc3FBC5bgPmOBAoiO24yvyZFwRzAW3Co\nYNi2LzC/h/92ekThe/wGyQBdHm8RJXY9itjpkPDKw2AU3grus2nD3lO8i+ISHvvf\nv5M9rnvBR5/3T9vZQS7pqi10wEFi4HKMbQj+TVvDfnKLF+qNHW+55hBPdm9w/w2P\nPZ6HkYA68l+U3t+dmPFW+y2bU/dxFUtXUgl/MblyfUNaVt+R1PtEV8qXjvX+hHKz\nX3R2nIRTuG4LFT8LI5rd0c7R/Li2RRznAFlftMVg014NYPhpdh1P7zK0SHfbZUUU\nWBSgRaH5VnRZgxObTJ9n/QZdu7bEdwza/INH3Su901ZVlFf9cBdBusk3cXH0WXUy\nXUNMpMqwM+E8dWoTD2Z/fVeU4cUrw1pOrawi6e1h8uaYAN9UWx3qHK25w0nA/j+d\nqVCFRNkIuVDGOX5p2YQQ\n=7SkF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 119b65d22018fd41220499883890f9580ee13256\nparent 69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e\nauthor erni-a <erni@caura.ai> 1786531977 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786531977 +0300\n\nCAURA-000- docs: Caura prose sweep — README body, guides, skills, client READMEs (#758)\n\n## What (rebrand Phase 3c, OSS side)\n\nCase-sensitive whole-word `MemClaw` → `Caura`: 126 occurrences / 13\nprose files — README body, AGENT-INSTALL, CONTRIBUTING,\n`static/docs/integration-guide.md`, both `SKILL.md` copies,\ncompany-brain skill, python + TS client READMEs, `docs/*`.\n\n## Protected / unchanged\n\n- Sentinel-protected verbatim: the hero bridge line, `Caura (formerly\nMemClaw)` heading, trademark line\n- All 107 `memclaw_*` tool literals across the two SKILL.md copies —\ngrep-verified intact\n- `memclaw.net` URLs (flip at cutover) · package names (Phase 6) · logo\nassets (pending wordmark)\n\n## Testing\n\nLocal run of skill/manifest tests hits the DB-fixture wall (integration\ntests need Postgres) — CI is authoritative here; the diff is\nmarkdown-only.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: erni <erni@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T10:52:59Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/aec3196925852254150f7e1ed0b54f435ed9de06/comments","author":{"login":"erni-a","id":229583271,"node_id":"U_kgDODa8ppw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/229583271?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a","html_url":"https://github.com/erni-a","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e"}]},{"sha":"69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDY5YzkxMGIyNGFmYzU0N2Y1MmQzODdlMjk3NmYyMTM4YzU3MGU2NmU","commit":{"author":{"name":"erni-a","email":"erni@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T10:04:11Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T10:04:11Z"},"message":"CAURA-000- docs(readme): announce the Caura rebrand in the README hero (#757)\n\n## What (rebrand Phase 1 — \"one story, one voice\")\n\n- Bridge line under the hero tagline: **MemClaw is becoming Caura** —\nsame product, one name, with the explicit promise that `memclaw_*` tool\nnames, packages, env vars and URLs keep working unchanged.\n- First heading now pairs name + category: **Caura (formerly MemClaw) —\nthe shared governed memory layer for AI agent fleets** (never ship the\nnew name without the category phrase — it's the mitigation for \"Caura\"\nbeing a weaker search token than the coined \"MemClaw\").\n\nNarrative layer only: logo, deep prose, and memclaw.net links are\nuntouched until the Phase 3 sweep.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: erni <erni@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1bd550e7a3e2e7f1397037916b52534834016285","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/1bd550e7a3e2e7f1397037916b52534834016285"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfEUbCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4FEQAEfJkJCBdWnRhRm2Duei7WDy\niobu+uWp+4JyFTwqLw+dpOgZRfYEg4MuQee8Oi5qxDgLUGLo80PCXo6HHBLE4LQb\nrXdk3nDXQbogsWPl8U7KZ+2oSyr+y2iuLoVGaaOLXbGrlVjLYEWqK651D+HTq7RR\njjwovmQh4m+JOWx6qf4a53kH+8jL0Emw/r0nnzEIW35KrTJm6JOcLkmz6AIKrrMH\nAzSQS2xNFdKXHdJpT7p6hDs7q8QH+RGjx9QPgFFZ6Opy7fA6AOLerPpKtobPkUQu\nGu+JKtuGR8f6LCHOFnP3c7CokaLM0mYMjAZpCVMVdEs1S6/9QReiEtHI0zCEwWHg\nhzAIY/GzYwloqQxVdsgwWvpl4C3Mzc4zoeBLRHhMtykkn75Qtiv+HguZS/TqsN/f\nUMYvAPB3HTu+Ed0mTtgev7k2bmz0rRnKEGN8wiYPiTQi6+Z8yp6G32oQSU0aT3PJ\nDIRhxzjFwQxPlgM/0fn5d6f0OQLXfoRosLB+B1eiOhOdXn+cLKIIkVOJjHUnULrA\nqkd81wQzym7nLrwEAUUj5c9/NfCiXMwq2tkq6/7fueLbhE+Nu2TGecWiqrguMoxl\nJMy802Bcl5s4zoT/tav+HJRAIFFRMvrmfwg8Erfq1USSgbNCFBooxEWXJQoEjodl\n3XOb4XyjcfId4uEtrDNu\n=gauF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 1bd550e7a3e2e7f1397037916b52534834016285\nparent 5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6\nauthor erni-a <erni@caura.ai> 1786529051 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786529051 +0300\n\nCAURA-000- docs(readme): announce the Caura rebrand in the README hero (#757)\n\n## What (rebrand Phase 1 — \"one story, one voice\")\n\n- Bridge line under the hero tagline: **MemClaw is becoming Caura** —\nsame product, one name, with the explicit promise that `memclaw_*` tool\nnames, packages, env vars and URLs keep working unchanged.\n- First heading now pairs name + category: **Caura (formerly MemClaw) —\nthe shared governed memory layer for AI agent fleets** (never ship the\nnew name without the category phrase — it's the mitigation for \"Caura\"\nbeing a weaker search token than the coined \"MemClaw\").\n\nNarrative layer only: logo, deep prose, and memclaw.net links are\nuntouched until the Phase 3 sweep.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: erni <erni@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T10:04:14Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/69c910b24afc547f52d387e2976f2138c570e66e/comments","author":{"login":"erni-a","id":229583271,"node_id":"U_kgDODa8ppw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/229583271?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a","html_url":"https://github.com/erni-a","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/erni-a/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6"}]},{"sha":"5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDVjMGY3ZjY4OThjZGRmODc2MGY2YTdhYzQ1OTFjYTA2ZTk1MGU3YzY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-12T07:20:04Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T07:20:04Z"},"message":"fix(capture): mirror the shared pipeline's diagnostics, effort and decline test (#755)\n\n`caura-ai/.github` shipped **v1.5.2** today after two days of chasing an\nempty extraction. This ports what that produced — **adapted, not\ncopied**, since this repo's copy is deliberately not a fork.\n\n## Ported\n\n**The thread diagnostic.** One notice with the thread length and\nsurviving comment count, logged *before* the verdict gate. Its absence\nis what cost the shared pipeline two days: every round reasoned about\n\"the thread\" as read from a maintainer's laptop, and nothing established\nthat the job sees the same thing. It need not — this runs as\n`github.token`, and `author_association` is **viewer-dependent**. If a\ncomment is missing, `[]` is the *correct* answer to what the model was\nhanded.\n\n**`--effort max`, with a non-blocking probe.** The empty extraction\nupstream turned out to be a collapse in *reasoning*, not input — the run\nthat worked generated thousands of output tokens, the ones that didn't\ngenerated two characters. This invocation never passed `--effort`, so it\nran at the CLI's default. The probe omits the flag and warns if the\npinned CLI lacks it; passing an unsupported flag is worse, since the CLI\nrejects the whole invocation and capture extracts *nothing* rather than\nmerely extracting badly.\n\n**The empty-extraction log.** `No declined findings` was the only silent\nfailure path left in this file — every other one already logs its\npayload — and it's ambiguous between three outcomes needing different\nresponses.\n\n**The decline test.** Two questions: did the code change in response,\nand did the maintainer **concede** the finding is correct. The old\nwording said to ignore findings *\"fixed, applied, or otherwise\naddressed\"* — and a maintainer writing \"already satisfied\" reads as\n*addressed*, so the most valuable case (a finding refuted because its\npremise was false) was being instructed away.\n\n## Deliberately **not** ported — the reasons matter more than the list\n\n**The jq trust filter.** Upstream moved authority enforcement out of the\nprompt into a `select` on `author_association`. Porting it here would\n**break capture outright**: its bot condition requires a `<!--\ncaura-reviewer: -->` marker, and this repo's verdicts don't carry one —\n*checked, not assumed* — so every verdict would be filtered out and\nthere'd be nothing left to decline.\n\nThe prompt-based OWNER/MEMBER control here is correct for a **public**\nrepo and deliberately narrower than upstream's\nOWNER/MEMBER/COLLABORATOR, matching this repo's own retrigger gate.\nUpstream also never demonstrated the move fixed anything.\n\n**`MAX_THREAD_CHARS`.** Upstream raised it to 90000 because two\nreviewers put twice the automated text in a thread. This repo runs one;\n60000 is right for it.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n**The shared drift checker cannot see this flag.** It extracts the CLI\nflag set from the literal invocation, and `--effort` now sits in a\nconditional array on both sides — so it reports \"in sync\" whether or not\neither repo passes it. That's a blind spot introduced upstream when the\nprobe was added, not a pre-existing one.\n\n## Measured, not assumed\n\n- The prompt was rendered from the assembled variable to confirm the\nescaped quotes survive.\n- Both repos' capture currently reports `[]` on recent merges — but the\ncaura-memclaw threads I checked (#751, #753) contain **no declines at\nall**; every maintainer reply is \"Applied\" or \"Good catch, verified\". So\n`[]` is correct there and says nothing about breakage.\n\n`bash -n` and `shellcheck` clean. DCO signed off.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"4a54e405a7459a4ee52d48fd13a933e9099e84f9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/4a54e405a7459a4ee52d48fd13a933e9099e84f9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfB6kCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA09sQABErOrgmYE7T4wg656pbj02E\n1vDgos3+JEXwPahwsLMoFHlcVfTwYUHVsFcYGfP+Bt0BEf7oN2p8FwomBCq9lMWL\nmEvZKFcO63FoZ/qQMC65ZP3Zald+eB95RR9d1JCqVK9UAEJNH6FGWycTentLefA+\nakpjnWAacU+p6Kfl0gwKVy3qoxlRdK61Nko/YjoSV1tg89fIevh5TR7EL7za2Ho5\n8YbsDznAAaYYHZFvqgxs0mvqyD6tt+Ls45tyvNgM6Y+AzBUDlOr3Q9jm9DYakods\n2CE1EnS/1aRLZuTC3Xczc0Vq4ahFSLA/TCsg4HQOKp6Y5Ty8R37VtMixPCxa3prU\nz4tOakY3LEI6rMRNwQGyGAVCaR2tm2Pa6mwFGMwGEv+Npqnubnf6ayl8jeFv1vud\nHGhKw8O1rnhf05kh/WHXb7/05pnUzyMxA0DdeWfIjHsmEQUh27aYy7zFReclY0L0\njEEwWg8hQHz+Eepe1DAwOnVsSwYs7HhH5BQU2wP2xZL5P4c/9XVRjJ7aMsdngQce\nh7PQZeQt3vS6oeLs3q9/RebOH3yQ0jm/JbIuAyVmW5Z1ZfgCQkiSJKW7YMMYiWmv\nu//1jLKGQxrOGkCCAFM3Rk3tE7pbQmzzOjnkOtnOpIbU0iDNsrWyOnZc9CzAz8iC\nEBTzPaDhaOptcd73VfDx\n=Vs5l\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 4a54e405a7459a4ee52d48fd13a933e9099e84f9\nparent 541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786519204 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786519204 +0300\n\nfix(capture): mirror the shared pipeline's diagnostics, effort and decline test (#755)\n\n`caura-ai/.github` shipped **v1.5.2** today after two days of chasing an\nempty extraction. This ports what that produced — **adapted, not\ncopied**, since this repo's copy is deliberately not a fork.\n\n## Ported\n\n**The thread diagnostic.** One notice with the thread length and\nsurviving comment count, logged *before* the verdict gate. Its absence\nis what cost the shared pipeline two days: every round reasoned about\n\"the thread\" as read from a maintainer's laptop, and nothing established\nthat the job sees the same thing. It need not — this runs as\n`github.token`, and `author_association` is **viewer-dependent**. If a\ncomment is missing, `[]` is the *correct* answer to what the model was\nhanded.\n\n**`--effort max`, with a non-blocking probe.** The empty extraction\nupstream turned out to be a collapse in *reasoning*, not input — the run\nthat worked generated thousands of output tokens, the ones that didn't\ngenerated two characters. This invocation never passed `--effort`, so it\nran at the CLI's default. The probe omits the flag and warns if the\npinned CLI lacks it; passing an unsupported flag is worse, since the CLI\nrejects the whole invocation and capture extracts *nothing* rather than\nmerely extracting badly.\n\n**The empty-extraction log.** `No declined findings` was the only silent\nfailure path left in this file — every other one already logs its\npayload — and it's ambiguous between three outcomes needing different\nresponses.\n\n**The decline test.** Two questions: did the code change in response,\nand did the maintainer **concede** the finding is correct. The old\nwording said to ignore findings *\"fixed, applied, or otherwise\naddressed\"* — and a maintainer writing \"already satisfied\" reads as\n*addressed*, so the most valuable case (a finding refuted because its\npremise was false) was being instructed away.\n\n## Deliberately **not** ported — the reasons matter more than the list\n\n**The jq trust filter.** Upstream moved authority enforcement out of the\nprompt into a `select` on `author_association`. Porting it here would\n**break capture outright**: its bot condition requires a `<!--\ncaura-reviewer: -->` marker, and this repo's verdicts don't carry one —\n*checked, not assumed* — so every verdict would be filtered out and\nthere'd be nothing left to decline.\n\nThe prompt-based OWNER/MEMBER control here is correct for a **public**\nrepo and deliberately narrower than upstream's\nOWNER/MEMBER/COLLABORATOR, matching this repo's own retrigger gate.\nUpstream also never demonstrated the move fixed anything.\n\n**`MAX_THREAD_CHARS`.** Upstream raised it to 90000 because two\nreviewers put twice the automated text in a thread. This repo runs one;\n60000 is right for it.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n**The shared drift checker cannot see this flag.** It extracts the CLI\nflag set from the literal invocation, and `--effort` now sits in a\nconditional array on both sides — so it reports \"in sync\" whether or not\neither repo passes it. That's a blind spot introduced upstream when the\nprobe was added, not a pre-existing one.\n\n## Measured, not assumed\n\n- The prompt was rendered from the assembled variable to confirm the\nescaped quotes survive.\n- Both repos' capture currently reports `[]` on recent merges — but the\ncaura-memclaw threads I checked (#751, #753) contain **no declines at\nall**; every maintainer reply is \"Applied\" or \"Good catch, verified\". So\n`[]` is correct there and says nothing about breakage.\n\n`bash -n` and `shellcheck` clean. DCO signed off.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T07:20:07Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/5c0f7f6898cddf8760f6a7ac4591ca06e950e7c6/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b"}]},{"sha":"541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDU0MWNjMDUwZDJmYzdlM2I2NGM2MmNiM2EyOWZkNTBmMzU2NDJjMGI","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-12T06:48:25Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-12T06:48:25Z"},"message":"feat(memory): contradiction engine seam + arch flag (A55 Phase 1) (#754)\n\n## What & why\n\nIntroduces a **single seam** over contradiction detection behind a flag,\nso the scattered trigger sites route through one entry point and the\nlegacy arch can be retired later. Detection today lives in\n`contradiction_detector.py` and is fired from **8+ scattered sites**;\nthis consolidates them.\n\n**Behaviour-preserving and OFF by default** — when ON, the engine\ndelegates to the same detector, so this PR is a **no-op** until the\nfollow-up (Phase 2 / A55 1d) adds the `memory_conflicts` record write\nbehind the ON path.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Flag:** `settings.contradiction_engine_enabled` (default `False`).\n- **`services/contradiction/`** — `ContradictionEngine.evaluate_async`\nfacade (`Trigger` enum: WRITE/EMBED/UPDATE/BULK/REEMBED → Path A; ENTITY\n→ Path C) + `run_contradiction_detection` flag-router. The router is a\n**sync function returning an awaitable**, so the legacy path keeps the\nexact schedule-time semantics of the old direct\n`detect_contradictions_async(...)` calls.\n- **All 12 trigger sites** now route through the seam: consumer\nback-channel ×2, `ScheduleBackgroundTasks` ×2,\n`entity_extraction_worker` ×1, `memory_service` ×7. No direct legacy\ndetector calls remain outside detector/engine/dispatch.\n\n## Tests / guardrails\n\n- **routing-parity** — for every trigger, flag ON reaches the same\ndetector fn with the same args as flag OFF.\n- **golden differential** — freezes today's detection *outcomes*\n(`status`/`supersedes_id`) for 7 scenarios (RDF both directions,\nsemantic ±, Path C forward ±, retraction) into a committed fixture; the\nengine must reproduce it byte-for-byte.\n- **trigger-coverage** — structural guard: no module bypasses the seam,\nand every `Trigger` kind is wired (catches a future dropped/bypassing\nsite).\n- Two observability fixtures repointed to the detector source symbol —\n**all assertions unchanged**.\n\n## No-degradation evidence\n\nFull top-level suite: failure set **byte-for-byte identical to the\npre-existing baseline** (create_all integration tests only) — **zero\nregressions**. mypy clean (193 files), ruff + format clean.\n\nDesign + preservation table + Excalidraw current-vs-engine diagram:\n`benchmark/a55/subtasks/10-…` / `11-…` (local).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"72c9c5656ceea331358aed0fc0d25e5807ea549d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/72c9c5656ceea331358aed0fc0d25e5807ea549d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfBc5CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAD+cQAKZY8d4F60QcHeY85KK6jBG9\naX02wD7ZUIqfjeUhSVA+OXdqSCQ+aFlYc0Kyx/g+wcHvtiWX80QG+idH/6v5wxV8\nbcVHpSV5AhPQ4TeoxCTOdGvEF57lOq0HaFpbmALpj9REgKBMvEYEk7I9FHDE6z7Y\nThRnNaN8ponFUYsZJbCMROWVDPHGvXydQ70sFRHTuxI3r3FqQLuSYzFgblIutvAs\nmh+WjwwrptqtEhe5MKrzZMixmM34na8W383irD684dJiLgpv+3B2K7BCzfQc2wPr\n5HVrGhwtoiND9ebu7a13Q+v8j85NJxE8XjBwhdgI2IhwpX5mD+z2aWMy+XUEFtEf\nh3vwdrZKE5yLzIOaUy+bcIuA4NnUnuttbHk8MTFmcVb99NfQwZYsbLxOeaiwObWZ\nBE9yEkP0jrqoukGv8a5lYjnoACYZNyJf/cJae+ZUzY/LAiLFxrLJnablgS39karz\nl4VKblXjnFtW8KfQi2P2/lolUB6wTeOY9rE5GMRy7gde0EwW30YfGp3PJoO2BpN5\nFIHEO1xgspA2kqE4Hzt9oAAYS6V6kxEBQiZbI3F8/91wOiP2SJyDaizvfmSy5ucg\nLQt8NahC3n+SdUQfHwB6OoZPkkSZbDMIb1UAJrZqK/L32NI9LCt7pJ+OjSdT4fW+\nGVf1N1Y6l5m6vy0x+Wo2\n=JaH0\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 72c9c5656ceea331358aed0fc0d25e5807ea549d\nparent c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1786517305 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786517305 +0300\n\nfeat(memory): contradiction engine seam + arch flag (A55 Phase 1) (#754)\n\n## What & why\n\nIntroduces a **single seam** over contradiction detection behind a flag,\nso the scattered trigger sites route through one entry point and the\nlegacy arch can be retired later. Detection today lives in\n`contradiction_detector.py` and is fired from **8+ scattered sites**;\nthis consolidates them.\n\n**Behaviour-preserving and OFF by default** — when ON, the engine\ndelegates to the same detector, so this PR is a **no-op** until the\nfollow-up (Phase 2 / A55 1d) adds the `memory_conflicts` record write\nbehind the ON path.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Flag:** `settings.contradiction_engine_enabled` (default `False`).\n- **`services/contradiction/`** — `ContradictionEngine.evaluate_async`\nfacade (`Trigger` enum: WRITE/EMBED/UPDATE/BULK/REEMBED → Path A; ENTITY\n→ Path C) + `run_contradiction_detection` flag-router. The router is a\n**sync function returning an awaitable**, so the legacy path keeps the\nexact schedule-time semantics of the old direct\n`detect_contradictions_async(...)` calls.\n- **All 12 trigger sites** now route through the seam: consumer\nback-channel ×2, `ScheduleBackgroundTasks` ×2,\n`entity_extraction_worker` ×1, `memory_service` ×7. No direct legacy\ndetector calls remain outside detector/engine/dispatch.\n\n## Tests / guardrails\n\n- **routing-parity** — for every trigger, flag ON reaches the same\ndetector fn with the same args as flag OFF.\n- **golden differential** — freezes today's detection *outcomes*\n(`status`/`supersedes_id`) for 7 scenarios (RDF both directions,\nsemantic ±, Path C forward ±, retraction) into a committed fixture; the\nengine must reproduce it byte-for-byte.\n- **trigger-coverage** — structural guard: no module bypasses the seam,\nand every `Trigger` kind is wired (catches a future dropped/bypassing\nsite).\n- Two observability fixtures repointed to the detector source symbol —\n**all assertions unchanged**.\n\n## No-degradation evidence\n\nFull top-level suite: failure set **byte-for-byte identical to the\npre-existing baseline** (create_all integration tests only) — **zero\nregressions**. mypy clean (193 files), ruff + format clean.\n\nDesign + preservation table + Excalidraw current-vs-engine diagram:\n`benchmark/a55/subtasks/10-…` / `11-…` (local).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-12T06:48:28Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/541cc050d2fc7e3b64c62cb3a29fd50f35642c0b/comments","author":{"login":"arkash20","id":55495209,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1NDk1MjA5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55495209?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20","html_url":"https://github.com/arkash20","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4"}]},{"sha":"c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGM5NzRiYjQ3MzZiMjAxNzc5NjUwZWUzN2EwZWUyM2EzZjE3MmNhZjQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-11T19:13:51Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-11T19:13:51Z"},"message":"feat(broker): gate the four memory operations the broker already calls (#753)\n\n## The gap\n\n`core-api/openapi.broker.json` exists so that a breaking change to a\nbroker-facing\nendpoint fails CI. **It covered 4 operations. The broker calls 8.**\n\nOnce memclawd's MCP dispatcher was wired to serve `memory_recall`,\n`memory_list`,\n`memory_update` and `memory_delete` — all five capability interfaces are\nlive in\n`newCloudDispatcher` today — the broker started depending on:\n\n| | | broker caller |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/memories` | `cloud.Client.ListMemories`\n(`memory_list`) |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/memories/{memory_id}` | `cloud.Client.GetMemory`\n(`memory_recall`) |\n| `PATCH` | `/api/v1/memories/{memory_id}` | `cloud.Client.UpdateMemory`\n(`memory_update`) |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/memories/{memory_id}` |\n`cloud.Client.DeleteMemory` (`memory_delete`) |\n\nThe baseline was never widened, so a breaking change to any of them\n**passed the gate\nsilently**. This is the same failure mode as the #723–#736 series, one\nlayer out: the\ncode grew a dependency and nothing declared it.\n\n## Method-level, not path-level\n\n`BROKER_OPERATIONS` is now `path -> methods`. `/api/v1/memories` also\nserves POST and\nDELETE that the broker never calls — gating the whole path item would\nfail this gate on\nunrelated changes to those operations, which is how a gate gets trained\ninto noise.\n\nThe missing-operation preflight checks methods too. A path that survives\nwhile the\noperation the broker calls is renamed is exactly as breaking as losing\nthe path, and the\nold path-only check sailed straight past it. Verified by adding a `PUT`\nthe app doesn't\nserve:\n\n```\ngen_broker_openapi: broker operation(s) missing from core-api OpenAPI: PUT /api/v1/memories\n```\n\n## Additive — `CONTRACT_VERSION` stays v1\n\nThis widens what the gate *protects*; it changes no API shape. Verified\nthree ways:\n\n- the four pre-existing path items are **byte-identical**, nothing\nremoved\n- `info` unchanged (`v1`)\n- **the real gate passes**: I ran the exact CI oasdiff image *and\ndigest* against main's\n  baseline → `No breaking changes to report`, exit 0 (4 ops → 8 ops)\n\n## Docs, because a list in a comment is what went stale\n\n`ci.yml`'s comment enumerated four endpoints while the broker had grown\nto eight. It now\npoints at `BROKER_OPERATIONS` rather than restating it.\n\n`core-api/scripts/README.md` gains the **cross-repo obligation** that\ncaused this gap:\nnothing in this repo can detect that the broker started calling a new\ncloud endpoint, so\na new `internal/cloud` client method must add its row here in the same\nchange.\n\n## Same gap in the other repo — not fixed here\n\nEnterprise has its own per-service broker baselines. As of 2026-08-11\nthey cover\n`/installs/{claim,heartbeat,policy/stream,register}` and `POST\n/api/v1/audit-log`, but\n**not** `POST /api/v1/installs/leave` or `POST\n/api/v1/installs/commands/ack`, which the\nbroker calls from `internal/cloud/leave.go` and\n`internal/cloud/command_ack.go`. That\nneeds a separate enterprise PR; recorded in the README so it isn't lost.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/`: **4187 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed**;\n`core-storage-api/tests/`: **116 passed**\n- mypy clean both projects; ruff clean at all four CI scopes;\n`plugin/tools.json` byte-identical\n- `gen_broker_openapi.py` and `--check` both clean (8 operations)\n- `actionlint` on `ci.yml`: **1 finding before my change, 1 after** —\npre-existing, not\nintroduced. (My first comparison showed 0-vs-1 and was wrong: actionlint\nskips files\noutside a workflows path, so the baseline run had silently linted\nnothing.)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"dc95b350ddd1a2c6426f5aa529ead7de21b342ae","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/dc95b350ddd1a2c6426f5aa529ead7de21b342ae"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqe3RvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAKugQAH/kazRA5JWxbg9nwm3zXakP\nlyOtdnFBG/Zw9KqildRTbgI13AttfUE0ku9i19RKOO+EsPFwQ7qmHGk23r5esvRD\nLQOSMbuGm4X46/BdYS0Wv0P2hqIJW1LTb6FguTj6xwfpDW0slVV2ec5Zl1H9kRiV\nB2QpKXLAoADN1IUPoC0ft4LFQkuc2vgDjzULwwSCgeEEuMsYIRbFR4965NCt/hJf\nejLCxRLLrC5QVL5JOu8htK0GUAjrkHieMGmYubE+Xb52LbTMW4tsOKYohLuLkY7w\ngkMjg2uH9ygKa5cqOH9mAC58GrZDX6Gk+ZLZ5JT0BSCsUFVYlIJrMBgoUQdtzYbi\n+lfNr71pf/k+3nQUh/Yk7KAU+IdthB/xcyB6nEYCBUKhl6of090nNH5+AJsk/TXS\nj73qJ/4/VUvCkJmw0H6HhS9HaMwA0aDL2IlKFHWH7BjqM6Dfplyy57raQOLh6bR2\nFtTXj8y3TG8A7fxI3iotdTKKQ2b8D1wUBv2jqzpq7jN9yIUNoa1uQ6FqqTjjQfpL\nv+SbC531/BOnwsyrnQ/9r4tQq0SMoTv41pgp+x1S62MG4XZPHAswb61kY2OTLXva\nQ6jpNf58juCuG53EYyJYZnrK+4tUgqIEGSRrD3jGubRI9E5dWl7ztn0wNX5xg5v1\n9jnBBHBU5AluUdRnRoDM\n=GvJF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree dc95b350ddd1a2c6426f5aa529ead7de21b342ae\nparent e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786475631 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786475631 +0300\n\nfeat(broker): gate the four memory operations the broker already calls (#753)\n\n## The gap\n\n`core-api/openapi.broker.json` exists so that a breaking change to a\nbroker-facing\nendpoint fails CI. **It covered 4 operations. The broker calls 8.**\n\nOnce memclawd's MCP dispatcher was wired to serve `memory_recall`,\n`memory_list`,\n`memory_update` and `memory_delete` — all five capability interfaces are\nlive in\n`newCloudDispatcher` today — the broker started depending on:\n\n| | | broker caller |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/memories` | `cloud.Client.ListMemories`\n(`memory_list`) |\n| `GET` | `/api/v1/memories/{memory_id}` | `cloud.Client.GetMemory`\n(`memory_recall`) |\n| `PATCH` | `/api/v1/memories/{memory_id}` | `cloud.Client.UpdateMemory`\n(`memory_update`) |\n| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/memories/{memory_id}` |\n`cloud.Client.DeleteMemory` (`memory_delete`) |\n\nThe baseline was never widened, so a breaking change to any of them\n**passed the gate\nsilently**. This is the same failure mode as the #723–#736 series, one\nlayer out: the\ncode grew a dependency and nothing declared it.\n\n## Method-level, not path-level\n\n`BROKER_OPERATIONS` is now `path -> methods`. `/api/v1/memories` also\nserves POST and\nDELETE that the broker never calls — gating the whole path item would\nfail this gate on\nunrelated changes to those operations, which is how a gate gets trained\ninto noise.\n\nThe missing-operation preflight checks methods too. A path that survives\nwhile the\noperation the broker calls is renamed is exactly as breaking as losing\nthe path, and the\nold path-only check sailed straight past it. Verified by adding a `PUT`\nthe app doesn't\nserve:\n\n```\ngen_broker_openapi: broker operation(s) missing from core-api OpenAPI: PUT /api/v1/memories\n```\n\n## Additive — `CONTRACT_VERSION` stays v1\n\nThis widens what the gate *protects*; it changes no API shape. Verified\nthree ways:\n\n- the four pre-existing path items are **byte-identical**, nothing\nremoved\n- `info` unchanged (`v1`)\n- **the real gate passes**: I ran the exact CI oasdiff image *and\ndigest* against main's\n  baseline → `No breaking changes to report`, exit 0 (4 ops → 8 ops)\n\n## Docs, because a list in a comment is what went stale\n\n`ci.yml`'s comment enumerated four endpoints while the broker had grown\nto eight. It now\npoints at `BROKER_OPERATIONS` rather than restating it.\n\n`core-api/scripts/README.md` gains the **cross-repo obligation** that\ncaused this gap:\nnothing in this repo can detect that the broker started calling a new\ncloud endpoint, so\na new `internal/cloud` client method must add its row here in the same\nchange.\n\n## Same gap in the other repo — not fixed here\n\nEnterprise has its own per-service broker baselines. As of 2026-08-11\nthey cover\n`/installs/{claim,heartbeat,policy/stream,register}` and `POST\n/api/v1/audit-log`, but\n**not** `POST /api/v1/installs/leave` or `POST\n/api/v1/installs/commands/ack`, which the\nbroker calls from `internal/cloud/leave.go` and\n`internal/cloud/command_ack.go`. That\nneeds a separate enterprise PR; recorded in the README so it isn't lost.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/`: **4187 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed**;\n`core-storage-api/tests/`: **116 passed**\n- mypy clean both projects; ruff clean at all four CI scopes;\n`plugin/tools.json` byte-identical\n- `gen_broker_openapi.py` and `--check` both clean (8 operations)\n- `actionlint` on `ci.yml`: **1 finding before my change, 1 after** —\npre-existing, not\nintroduced. (My first comparison showed 0-vs-1 and was wrong: actionlint\nskips files\noutside a workflows path, so the baseline run had silently linted\nnothing.)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-11T19:13:54Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c974bb4736b201779650ee37a0ee23a3f172caf4/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9"}]},{"sha":"e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGU2MDEwMjE5MTQ4ZWIzYTEyZDMwNWM4ZWUyN2E3OTVlMjg3MzdhZTk","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-11T11:47:54Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-11T11:47:54Z"},"message":"feat(memory): unified contradiction-model schema + read serialization (A55) (#752)\n\n## What & why\n\nIntroduces the schema for the **unified contradiction-handling model**,\nwhich today is flattened onto `memories.status` +\n`memories.supersedes_id`. This PR is **additive and read-only** —\n*nothing populates the new fields yet* (that lands in a follow-up), so\nbehaviourally it is a **no-op**. It unblocks the follow-up work\n(populating conflict records) and measuring against the STALE\ncontradiction benchmark.\n\n## Schema — migration `035` (on top of `034`)\n\n- **`memories`** — three new columns:\n- `confidence` (REAL, nullable) — advisory confidence in the claim;\nguards the model's invariant *\"weak evidence must not delete strong\"*.\n- `is_inferred` (BOOL, default `false`) — system-materialised vs\ndirectly stated.\n- `scope` (JSONB, default `'{}'`) — structured validity qualifiers\n(role/task/location).\n- All nullable/defaulted with constant defaults → **metadata-only**\ncolumn adds (no table rewrite).\n- **`memory_conflicts`** (new) — one row per detected candidate\nconflict: relationship / diagnosis / evidence_strength / action +\nconfidences + audit_reason. Classification is kept **separate** from\napplied state (the effect still lives on the memory row). Enum-like\ncolumns are `TEXT` + `CHECK` so the vocabulary can grow with a one-line\nchange during iteration.\n- **`memory_derivations`** (new) — lineage edges (inferred memory ←\nsource) enabling revalidation when a source is later corrected/deleted.\n- **No backfill.**\n\n## Serialization (system-populated, read-only on the API)\n\n- Storage `MEMORY_FIELDS` + core-api `MemoryOut` carry the three fields\n→ recall/get/list surface them.\n- SQLite backend parity (DDL columns + `scope` JSON-decode).\n- **Public write schemas intentionally untouched** — these are\nsystem-only, never agent-supplied.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Storage HTTP round-trip + legacy-default; SQLite parity;\nmigration-chain head bumped `034 → 035`.\n\n## Wet test\n\n- Full alembic chain `001 → 035` via the real runner (up / down / up) —\nsingle head `035`.\n- `mypy` clean (storage 77 + core-api 190); `ruff` clean on all `src/` +\n`common/` + storage `tests/`.\n- Storage suite **116 passed**; ORM e2e round-trip + FK-CASCADE\nverified.\n- Top-level suite: zero new failures vs baseline (a set of\nFTS/entity/insights integration tests fail identically with and without\nthis change under the local `create_all` harness because they depend on\nmigration-built triggers — proven by a stash-and-rerun comparison).\n\n## Deploy safety\n\nAdditive expand migration; nothing writes the fields yet. Metadata-only\ncolumn adds; two new empty tables. Only operational note: adding the two\nFKs (`memory_conflicts → memories.id`) takes a brief lock on `memories`\nto install the referential trigger (child tables empty → no data scan).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a59e0c6512705a05c01c09af0c8d5b4c9a700b97","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/a59e0c6512705a05c01c09af0c8d5b4c9a700b97"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqewvqCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAlaEQAEsQ9N18M2LLxKIT8JUBlMWt\n3l1VQfCMg2Ptt+Tu0oVhgeQX4z17K3gmq/r9eTc+Tp0PZmeNzW1pTwT2ehCYC8/z\nIW309aNzfzRx0xf28dfFkf5/WUDZaQgeHjh5GkshHbhV7WXPGarM46xcp+gc9DGi\nD3nEcwIRTEOzW/MQ6YBBiEs/9LzpYbTbWtAFcIw5hP19IVHijZlpoj6m59IVWjym\nTfyrb87pOzWe5xHlDSuKzD5Jarb2W6QGR3viXVj8OhRyTZ2yopNrQGLD1mFO75qD\nwy+YHOf+xK6v7VErK+lnak0fZBbPOXlteb6ro7V2Na0hyW6osP9E/Q6afSh9FbnC\nyB+Pn2fH37SSOni0VIcRIuHHaL7sCYVZAq44IaybbSGjTEH7NMt5niA3w+luwHf9\nkMVciyVk5zswQA74g7ZPAHcEz5T74kqXEbKoBvRq1ZLr8BZqK7XRunTN0SHGw2jy\nzlFJrz7uoZ3Wo54gQtelt6eZzTvNcFfZO6i50N0uZVkCti/wI+kkxBW4QVXikQan\n3tAqLlGF7KUA6cCc+FCA7virhD0OhJiDK8/0p7a3UVs041Htiph6vZrHshF1xSJf\nTXy7nqhvLKA8SOeBeHMc8xoaajZDPt2v6NYAVPUte6DzQ88zEupGkRRC8v/gZeka\nviukJTAjWFkAixBTMBkg\n=EQqw\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree a59e0c6512705a05c01c09af0c8d5b4c9a700b97\nparent 160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1786448874 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786448874 +0300\n\nfeat(memory): unified contradiction-model schema + read serialization (A55) (#752)\n\n## What & why\n\nIntroduces the schema for the **unified contradiction-handling model**,\nwhich today is flattened onto `memories.status` +\n`memories.supersedes_id`. This PR is **additive and read-only** —\n*nothing populates the new fields yet* (that lands in a follow-up), so\nbehaviourally it is a **no-op**. It unblocks the follow-up work\n(populating conflict records) and measuring against the STALE\ncontradiction benchmark.\n\n## Schema — migration `035` (on top of `034`)\n\n- **`memories`** — three new columns:\n- `confidence` (REAL, nullable) — advisory confidence in the claim;\nguards the model's invariant *\"weak evidence must not delete strong\"*.\n- `is_inferred` (BOOL, default `false`) — system-materialised vs\ndirectly stated.\n- `scope` (JSONB, default `'{}'`) — structured validity qualifiers\n(role/task/location).\n- All nullable/defaulted with constant defaults → **metadata-only**\ncolumn adds (no table rewrite).\n- **`memory_conflicts`** (new) — one row per detected candidate\nconflict: relationship / diagnosis / evidence_strength / action +\nconfidences + audit_reason. Classification is kept **separate** from\napplied state (the effect still lives on the memory row). Enum-like\ncolumns are `TEXT` + `CHECK` so the vocabulary can grow with a one-line\nchange during iteration.\n- **`memory_derivations`** (new) — lineage edges (inferred memory ←\nsource) enabling revalidation when a source is later corrected/deleted.\n- **No backfill.**\n\n## Serialization (system-populated, read-only on the API)\n\n- Storage `MEMORY_FIELDS` + core-api `MemoryOut` carry the three fields\n→ recall/get/list surface them.\n- SQLite backend parity (DDL columns + `scope` JSON-decode).\n- **Public write schemas intentionally untouched** — these are\nsystem-only, never agent-supplied.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Storage HTTP round-trip + legacy-default; SQLite parity;\nmigration-chain head bumped `034 → 035`.\n\n## Wet test\n\n- Full alembic chain `001 → 035` via the real runner (up / down / up) —\nsingle head `035`.\n- `mypy` clean (storage 77 + core-api 190); `ruff` clean on all `src/` +\n`common/` + storage `tests/`.\n- Storage suite **116 passed**; ORM e2e round-trip + FK-CASCADE\nverified.\n- Top-level suite: zero new failures vs baseline (a set of\nFTS/entity/insights integration tests fail identically with and without\nthis change under the local `create_all` harness because they depend on\nmigration-built triggers — proven by a stash-and-rerun comparison).\n\n## Deploy safety\n\nAdditive expand migration; nothing writes the fields yet. Metadata-only\ncolumn adds; two new empty tables. Only operational note: adding the two\nFKs (`memory_conflicts → memories.id`) takes a brief lock on `memories`\nto install the referential trigger (child tables empty → no data scan).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-11T11:47:57Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e6010219148eb3a12d305c8ee27a795e28737ae9/comments","author":{"login":"arkash20","id":55495209,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1NDk1MjA5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55495209?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20","html_url":"https://github.com/arkash20","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28"}]},{"sha":"160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDE2MGYyNzFlYmRhODQ1YjU5MWM2Zjk5M2NmNWI1ZGUzOWRkZmJiMjg","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-11T11:10:07Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-11T11:10:07Z"},"message":"perf(db): index the FK columns that reference memories.id / entities.id (#751)\n\n## The defect\n\nPostgreSQL enforces a foreign key from the **referencing** side, once\nper deleted\nparent row. An unindexed referencing column therefore turns every delete\ninto a full\nscan of that table. Three were missing one:\n\n| referencing | → target | on delete | index |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `memories.supersedes_id` | `memories.id` | SET NULL | **none** |\n| `relations.evidence_memory_id` | `memories.id` | SET NULL | **none** |\n| `memory_entity_links.entity_id` | `entities.id` | CASCADE | **none\nusable** |\n\nThe third is easy to miss and was **not** in the original report — the\nPK is\n`(memory_id, entity_id)`, so the memories-side FK is covered by the\nleading column\nwhile `entity_id` is not. A btree cannot serve a trailing column as a\nprefix.\n\n## Measured\n\nDeleting 4,334 rows from a 30,801-row `memories` table, one statement,\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE`:\n\n| FK trigger | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `memories_supersedes_id_fkey` | **15,343 ms** | **8.7 ms** |\n| `memory_entity_links_memory_id_fkey` | 77 ms | 29.7 ms |\n| `relations_evidence_memory_id_fkey` | 17 ms | 7.4 ms |\n\nThe `supersedes_id` trigger was **98.7% of the statement** at 3.5 ms per\ndeleted row —\nand only **87 of 30,801 rows** had a non-NULL `supersedes_id`. The cost\nis the scan, not\nthe matches. Linear in table size for a fixed batch (prod's ~92k rows ≈\n3× worse),\nquadratic for a fixed-*fraction* purge where the batch grows with the\ntable.\n\n**Not a test-only concern.** `memory_purge_soft_deleted` (batched, via\nthe CAURA-656\nretention fanout), `purge_tenant_data` and `purge_fleet_data` all delete\nin bulk — and\nthe RI scan is **not tenant-scoped**, so deleting a tenant's children\nfirst doesn't\nshrink it: every other tenant's rows get scanned once per deleted\nparent.\n\n## The part worth reviewing: the invalid-index guard\n\nAn interrupted `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` leaves the index in\n`pg_index` with\n`indisvalid = false`. `IF NOT EXISTS` then **skips** it, and the planner\nrefuses to use\nit — so the migration stamps green while leaving an index that is\npermanently useless\n*and* still charges write overhead on every insert. Nothing later\nrevisits it.\n\nThis is reachable in production because `CONCURRENTLY` waits on\nconcurrent transactions\nwith no upper bound, and Cloud Run kills the container at the 240 s\nstartup probe.\n\n**Reproduced end to end, on the real table:**\n\n1. Forced an abort with `lock_timeout` behind an open writer →\n`indisvalid=false` ✓\n2. Ran a bare `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF NOT EXISTS` → `NOTICE:\nrelation\n\"ix_memories_supersedes_id\" already exists, skipping`, **still invalid**\n✓\n3. Ran this migration → guard dropped and rebuilt it; all three\n`indisvalid = t` ✓\n\n**An earlier draft of this PR had no guard and its docstring claimed `IF\nNOT EXISTS`\nmade the migration idempotent.** That claim was false, and it was the\nstated reason no\nguard was added. Migrations 005/007/026 all carry this guard — they are\nexactly the\nthree that index large pre-existing tables. 035 now matches them.\n\n## A guard so the next one fails at PR time\n\n`test_every_fk_referencing_column_is_index_leading` walks SQLAlchemy\nmodel metadata (no\nDB) and asserts every FK's referencing column is usable as an index\nprefix. **It is what\nfound the `memory_entity_links` case.** Mutation-tested: removing that\nindex fails the\ntest naming `memory_entity_links(entity_id) -> entities.id`.\n\nDeclaration-level by design — it catches a new FK added without an\nindex, and can't\ncatch an index declared but never migrated. The CONCURRENTLY guard and\nchain tests cover\nthe migration side.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/`: **4186 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed** (4185 baseline + 1 new\nguard)\n- `core-storage-api/tests/`: **114 passed**\n- mypy clean; ruff clean at all four CI scopes (incl. `common/`);\nartifacts byte-identical\n- **downgrade round-trip**: 035 → 034 drops all three, → 035 recreates;\nre-running\n  `upgrade` is a clean no-op\n- Build cost is not the risk: `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` on\n`supersedes_id` took\n**17.9 ms** for 26,927 rows (only 87 non-NULL, 200 kB index) — ~61 ms\nextrapolated to\nprod. The unbounded part is CONCURRENTLY's *wait*, which is what the\nguard covers.\n\n## Notes for the reviewer\n\n- Each `CREATE` is a **literal one-liner on purpose**:\n`test_no_plain_create_index_on_large_tables`\nregex-scans this file and matches neither a split string nor an\ninterpolated `{name}`.\nA tidier loop would silently drop this migration out of guard coverage.\n- Which also means **007 and 026 are currently outside that guard**\n(their f-string index\nnames don't match `\\w+`). Worth fixing the regex separately, along with\na test asserting\nevery CONCURRENTLY migration carries the `indisvalid` guard — nothing\nenforces that\n  pattern today, which is how this PR could omit it and still pass CI.\n- `test_single_head` hardcodes the expected head, so every migration PR\nedits a string in\na test. It could derive it (assert exactly one head, plus a contiguous\nnumeric run)\n  without losing coverage. Left alone here to keep this PR to one idea.\n- `relations.evidence_memory_id` is currently unmeasurable (that table\nis near-empty in\ndev) and included deliberately: it is written by a live co-occurrence\ninference path so\nit grows pairwise, the index costs one btree entry per insert, and at\nprod scale an\n  unindexed version projects to ~5.7 s per 500-row retention batch.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"a677e24db2f6b8289b3837145024637ee5c0d6c6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/a677e24db2f6b8289b3837145024637ee5c0d6c6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqewMPCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmC4QADG7oR1oAJLdGjNXjajPOAUs\n3yd2a5Q6EXjUvxUaXv/chOyx2KrXNI14/aSImwPSgN7/VcNXsagk8qsP2fBBDVOF\nPEDjnYyFEvFLlBmGBlRsQOtnMX8lU61aQuRBB0U2mTvjyvxTpO8XE0+rm2Ipy055\nwdpnk/XGIFqHYpYTn0K6TVZAUVq0A/7QdoSUvgHl+uRX+5045V40b62WTJgN/Gu7\nhfptBnLx9Te9GGdCZ583nDlX3U4OFnhCew1pMYXa5QXdtK60ch5yiPshhD2fOoQA\nXYdE1pqYbYIk7Ia2aZo9Og6H6iXT65Yq3Gpl7othZNccrocSC1f9R8YyoT0jewMv\n1VT3sMMdc4k+M7y3Xa3n94NCmCakRbTQTm6Lv2EENbX9NuBNmICphIP9UyvXTFra\nrVD7edSB5LQ50kzRO4jo6hPIlLlC6Gue0weGeSWgYKw5FydI95vGMaXKa3XAtMDm\nuUs5uZt6BeYJCHPG8JbaCq23z9QrGD5d5HiiT1G4RArEI7PH4tCgj5W9jf2U3t6K\ng+tv0fm5qH/5zB4huTNdbY4adyeSM50E/FCuO+nOlvFxWfkZOSMNy6tXYelYWOOx\n1legEga7r1lVbCwAspcaDv+o3UQUNc77PutBbTaTR75+cPGD1wLDsAiA21fzwOKW\n30SEYpDmTI/OvnuyFppU\n=Vv87\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree a677e24db2f6b8289b3837145024637ee5c0d6c6\nparent 0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786446607 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786446607 +0300\n\nperf(db): index the FK columns that reference memories.id / entities.id (#751)\n\n## The defect\n\nPostgreSQL enforces a foreign key from the **referencing** side, once\nper deleted\nparent row. An unindexed referencing column therefore turns every delete\ninto a full\nscan of that table. Three were missing one:\n\n| referencing | → target | on delete | index |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `memories.supersedes_id` | `memories.id` | SET NULL | **none** |\n| `relations.evidence_memory_id` | `memories.id` | SET NULL | **none** |\n| `memory_entity_links.entity_id` | `entities.id` | CASCADE | **none\nusable** |\n\nThe third is easy to miss and was **not** in the original report — the\nPK is\n`(memory_id, entity_id)`, so the memories-side FK is covered by the\nleading column\nwhile `entity_id` is not. A btree cannot serve a trailing column as a\nprefix.\n\n## Measured\n\nDeleting 4,334 rows from a 30,801-row `memories` table, one statement,\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE`:\n\n| FK trigger | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `memories_supersedes_id_fkey` | **15,343 ms** | **8.7 ms** |\n| `memory_entity_links_memory_id_fkey` | 77 ms | 29.7 ms |\n| `relations_evidence_memory_id_fkey` | 17 ms | 7.4 ms |\n\nThe `supersedes_id` trigger was **98.7% of the statement** at 3.5 ms per\ndeleted row —\nand only **87 of 30,801 rows** had a non-NULL `supersedes_id`. The cost\nis the scan, not\nthe matches. Linear in table size for a fixed batch (prod's ~92k rows ≈\n3× worse),\nquadratic for a fixed-*fraction* purge where the batch grows with the\ntable.\n\n**Not a test-only concern.** `memory_purge_soft_deleted` (batched, via\nthe CAURA-656\nretention fanout), `purge_tenant_data` and `purge_fleet_data` all delete\nin bulk — and\nthe RI scan is **not tenant-scoped**, so deleting a tenant's children\nfirst doesn't\nshrink it: every other tenant's rows get scanned once per deleted\nparent.\n\n## The part worth reviewing: the invalid-index guard\n\nAn interrupted `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` leaves the index in\n`pg_index` with\n`indisvalid = false`. `IF NOT EXISTS` then **skips** it, and the planner\nrefuses to use\nit — so the migration stamps green while leaving an index that is\npermanently useless\n*and* still charges write overhead on every insert. Nothing later\nrevisits it.\n\nThis is reachable in production because `CONCURRENTLY` waits on\nconcurrent transactions\nwith no upper bound, and Cloud Run kills the container at the 240 s\nstartup probe.\n\n**Reproduced end to end, on the real table:**\n\n1. Forced an abort with `lock_timeout` behind an open writer →\n`indisvalid=false` ✓\n2. Ran a bare `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF NOT EXISTS` → `NOTICE:\nrelation\n\"ix_memories_supersedes_id\" already exists, skipping`, **still invalid**\n✓\n3. Ran this migration → guard dropped and rebuilt it; all three\n`indisvalid = t` ✓\n\n**An earlier draft of this PR had no guard and its docstring claimed `IF\nNOT EXISTS`\nmade the migration idempotent.** That claim was false, and it was the\nstated reason no\nguard was added. Migrations 005/007/026 all carry this guard — they are\nexactly the\nthree that index large pre-existing tables. 035 now matches them.\n\n## A guard so the next one fails at PR time\n\n`test_every_fk_referencing_column_is_index_leading` walks SQLAlchemy\nmodel metadata (no\nDB) and asserts every FK's referencing column is usable as an index\nprefix. **It is what\nfound the `memory_entity_links` case.** Mutation-tested: removing that\nindex fails the\ntest naming `memory_entity_links(entity_id) -> entities.id`.\n\nDeclaration-level by design — it catches a new FK added without an\nindex, and can't\ncatch an index declared but never migrated. The CONCURRENTLY guard and\nchain tests cover\nthe migration side.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/`: **4186 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed** (4185 baseline + 1 new\nguard)\n- `core-storage-api/tests/`: **114 passed**\n- mypy clean; ruff clean at all four CI scopes (incl. `common/`);\nartifacts byte-identical\n- **downgrade round-trip**: 035 → 034 drops all three, → 035 recreates;\nre-running\n  `upgrade` is a clean no-op\n- Build cost is not the risk: `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` on\n`supersedes_id` took\n**17.9 ms** for 26,927 rows (only 87 non-NULL, 200 kB index) — ~61 ms\nextrapolated to\nprod. The unbounded part is CONCURRENTLY's *wait*, which is what the\nguard covers.\n\n## Notes for the reviewer\n\n- Each `CREATE` is a **literal one-liner on purpose**:\n`test_no_plain_create_index_on_large_tables`\nregex-scans this file and matches neither a split string nor an\ninterpolated `{name}`.\nA tidier loop would silently drop this migration out of guard coverage.\n- Which also means **007 and 026 are currently outside that guard**\n(their f-string index\nnames don't match `\\w+`). Worth fixing the regex separately, along with\na test asserting\nevery CONCURRENTLY migration carries the `indisvalid` guard — nothing\nenforces that\n  pattern today, which is how this PR could omit it and still pass CI.\n- `test_single_head` hardcodes the expected head, so every migration PR\nedits a string in\na test. It could derive it (assert exactly one head, plus a contiguous\nnumeric run)\n  without losing coverage. Left alone here to keep this PR to one idea.\n- `relations.evidence_memory_id` is currently unmeasurable (that table\nis near-empty in\ndev) and included deliberately: it is written by a live co-occurrence\ninference path so\nit grows pairwise, the index costs one btree entry per insert, and at\nprod scale an\n  unindexed version projects to ~5.7 s per 500-row retention batch.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-11T11:10:10Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/160f271ebda845b591c6f993cf5b5de39ddfbb28/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21"}]},{"sha":"0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDBlOTI0NWMyOTA2MDA1NGVjZmU5MmViMGI3ZGY2NzhhOGU2OTllMjE","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-11T08:27:44Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-11T08:27:44Z"},"message":"test: interleave the pipeline-vs-legacy latency paths to stop the flake (#750)\n\n## The threshold wasn't the problem; the ordering was\n\n`test_pipeline_overhead_bounded_relative_to_legacy` timed **ten legacy\nwrites, then\nten pipeline writes**, and compared medians against a 1.5x ceiling. It\nfailed at\n1.545x on 2026-08-09 — a 3% overshoot — and passed on rerun with\nidentical code.\n\nWhichever block ran second met the worse conditions: runner drift, plus\ncaches gone\ncold after the first block's work. That was always pipeline. Measured\nover 20 paired\nreplicates locally:\n\n| shape | ratio CV | worst replicate | margin to 1.5x |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| separate blocks (old) | **10.5%** | **1.403** | 7% |\n| interleaved (new) | **4.9%** | 1.175 | 28% |\n\nThe block shape reached 1.403 on an *idle laptop* in 20 replicates,\nwhich is entirely\nconsistent with 1.545 on a shared runner. There was also a concrete\ncold-cache\nartefact: in block form the pipeline's first write averaged **20.1 ms vs\n14.6 ms** for\nits second, because its warmup had happened ~10 writes earlier.\nInterleaved, that\nfirst write is 14.2 ms.\n\nWidening the ratio would have left the bias in place.\n\n## What I got wrong on the first attempt\n\nAn earlier version of this change also moved the gate to **min-vs-min**,\narguing that\nscheduling noise is strictly additive so the minimum is the cleanest\nestimator. A\n`/simplify` review measured that and it was wrong twice over:\n\n- **Latency climbs ~28% from the first write to the tenth**, because the\ndedup query\nscans the tenant's rows rather than an ANN index (`EXPLAIN ANALYZE`\nshows\n`Index Scan` + `Sort`, no HNSW). So per-write cost grows with rows\nalready written,\nand `min` lands on the first iteration — argmin was `i=0` in 15 of 20\nreplicates. It\n  measures the **smallest candidate set**, not the least noise.\n- **It bought no stability anyway** once interleaved: CV 4.50% (min) vs\n4.71%\n(median) across 10 cross-process runs. min was only better in the\n*block* shape —\ni.e. it was compensating for the variance interleaving already removes.\n- And it went blind to the likeliest regression class here. A\nper-candidate cost added\nto dedup: **0% detected by a min gate, 90-100% by the median.** An\nintermittent\n  slowdown on 70% of writes: 2.9% vs 92.9%.\n\nSo the statistic is unchanged. **min is now printed as a diagnostic\nonly**, and the\ncomment no longer claims noise is the dominant source of within-run\nspread.\n\n## Also: this test leaked rows on every run, forever\n\nIts tenants used a `test-lat-` prefix, but conftest's session teardown\nsweeps\n`test-tenant-%`. The rows are committed through the service layer, so\nnothing else\nremoved them — **every run leaked 22 rows permanently, and 4,334 had\naccumulated in\none dev database.** Renamed to `test-tenant-lat-*`; verified the sweep\nnow collects\nthem (0 rows remain after a run).\n\n## Verification\n\n- **8 consecutive local runs**: median ratio **1.00–1.10x** against the\n1.5x gate\n- `tests/`: **4185 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed** (unchanged);\n`core-storage-api/tests/`: **114 passed**\n- mypy clean both projects; ruff clean at all four CI scopes; generated\nartifacts byte-identical\n- Timing boundary preserved: `_make_input` is still evaluated at the\ncall site, so\n`MemoryCreate` construction stays outside the timed region exactly as\nbefore\n\n## Known residual, in the safe direction\n\nWithin each interleaved pair the second write measures ~3.8pp slower,\nand pipeline\nruns second — so the ratio is if anything *overstated*. Recorded in the\ncomment.\n\n## Deliberately not in this PR\n\nThe review made a strong case that this test is in the wrong tier\nentirely, and I\nagree, but it is a CI-policy change rather than a test fix:\n\n- **It is the only wall-clock ratio test that gates CI.** An\nidentically-shaped\nassertion (`assert ratio < 3.0`) in `tests/test_p5_p6_benchmarks.py:96`\nis\n`@pytest.mark.benchmark` and therefore deselected. **But nothing ever\nruns\n`-m benchmark`** — the tier holds 8 tests and is write-only, so marking\nthis one\nwithout also adding a non-gating scheduled job would trade a flake for\nsilent zero\n  coverage.\n- `tests/test_p1_contradiction.py:606` and `:646` are pure\nmicrobenchmarks whose only\nassertion is a wall-clock number, marked `integration`, gating CI,\nburning 100k\n  timed iterations per run.\n- **A timing-free formulation exists**: spy on `get_storage_client` and\nassert the\n  pipeline issues no more storage round-trips than legacy, plus assert\n`result.step_count`. The 1.5x gate permits ~16 ms of slack on a ~33 ms\nfloor, which\nis ~500-1000x the per-step glue it names, so it cannot resolve that\ninvariant at all.\n- This test also bundles a durable field-equivalence guard (17 columns +\nembeddings +\nmetadata) with the timing gate, so a runner hiccup and a real\nequivalence break\n  present as the same failure.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"7e2ecc3509d32ef185b7d25f4e634a25462ca580","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/7e2ecc3509d32ef185b7d25f4e634a25462ca580"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqet0ACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAMqQQABglB5HzsdtfIrTbZcxl1L9G\njw0g1NFjj1UTDlBj9iW1PICddAC9LW0o9pojPn+MLAwMIFTDsvC7ob2JdgsJQhgb\nuZXo+aAtP0zyNNBk+w3J4iwRlXhTigUKUipgBh+jgTu+ZnSCGb9VVrjLGpIejheG\nW6x5TrF6CufWnjsjBKbmRmlrkjV+uOosCpMEbYIXDyFLiTSokdGtTAl/kTa1rrRN\n0FhoXErjNobuTuqwoJpqNatoQBLfpNdFM7brpZbhrwQZriWAEjnqtT2lw3rx1Ehv\nbdgY00erMSaCYJvQxnDp5/ryL4ar3kG1taSJO8spsWOs4sDyMPVSxGfSm/n5H7+k\nPe3OpdRgO8hoOTkn/ysSFPpW7xmGuZtmWREz7ey9OkR7WhRdiAhby1NDGdHXxTCW\nsnaYYou8Ae40S++Ho0K4VvLk4oSslGt0uV3DDhV73NCiP/6CUx+FNyL7ZcBHTVR5\nqzbPQ9TgMVMf8NLQKah9uw+7GabX6UvG4d2FFEdch5WGgp2L6Ug3zY/l/8Im899i\nn/UlG7OJSVfZjU2hl/DuFSVgouOFNt8qMYMHxOuebZEagsP4RoD7LhMr2t1iMlKj\nWcKXZNcIqpcyMV4LSI9dp9KO0VtDN1GyryV5MhScfTCaBuF5hvTcICSpa7qT9GHV\n5uhfiHYSKZj4xl1XXPxH\n=ct8T\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 7e2ecc3509d32ef185b7d25f4e634a25462ca580\nparent d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786436864 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786436864 +0300\n\ntest: interleave the pipeline-vs-legacy latency paths to stop the flake (#750)\n\n## The threshold wasn't the problem; the ordering was\n\n`test_pipeline_overhead_bounded_relative_to_legacy` timed **ten legacy\nwrites, then\nten pipeline writes**, and compared medians against a 1.5x ceiling. It\nfailed at\n1.545x on 2026-08-09 — a 3% overshoot — and passed on rerun with\nidentical code.\n\nWhichever block ran second met the worse conditions: runner drift, plus\ncaches gone\ncold after the first block's work. That was always pipeline. Measured\nover 20 paired\nreplicates locally:\n\n| shape | ratio CV | worst replicate | margin to 1.5x |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| separate blocks (old) | **10.5%** | **1.403** | 7% |\n| interleaved (new) | **4.9%** | 1.175 | 28% |\n\nThe block shape reached 1.403 on an *idle laptop* in 20 replicates,\nwhich is entirely\nconsistent with 1.545 on a shared runner. There was also a concrete\ncold-cache\nartefact: in block form the pipeline's first write averaged **20.1 ms vs\n14.6 ms** for\nits second, because its warmup had happened ~10 writes earlier.\nInterleaved, that\nfirst write is 14.2 ms.\n\nWidening the ratio would have left the bias in place.\n\n## What I got wrong on the first attempt\n\nAn earlier version of this change also moved the gate to **min-vs-min**,\narguing that\nscheduling noise is strictly additive so the minimum is the cleanest\nestimator. A\n`/simplify` review measured that and it was wrong twice over:\n\n- **Latency climbs ~28% from the first write to the tenth**, because the\ndedup query\nscans the tenant's rows rather than an ANN index (`EXPLAIN ANALYZE`\nshows\n`Index Scan` + `Sort`, no HNSW). So per-write cost grows with rows\nalready written,\nand `min` lands on the first iteration — argmin was `i=0` in 15 of 20\nreplicates. It\n  measures the **smallest candidate set**, not the least noise.\n- **It bought no stability anyway** once interleaved: CV 4.50% (min) vs\n4.71%\n(median) across 10 cross-process runs. min was only better in the\n*block* shape —\ni.e. it was compensating for the variance interleaving already removes.\n- And it went blind to the likeliest regression class here. A\nper-candidate cost added\nto dedup: **0% detected by a min gate, 90-100% by the median.** An\nintermittent\n  slowdown on 70% of writes: 2.9% vs 92.9%.\n\nSo the statistic is unchanged. **min is now printed as a diagnostic\nonly**, and the\ncomment no longer claims noise is the dominant source of within-run\nspread.\n\n## Also: this test leaked rows on every run, forever\n\nIts tenants used a `test-lat-` prefix, but conftest's session teardown\nsweeps\n`test-tenant-%`. The rows are committed through the service layer, so\nnothing else\nremoved them — **every run leaked 22 rows permanently, and 4,334 had\naccumulated in\none dev database.** Renamed to `test-tenant-lat-*`; verified the sweep\nnow collects\nthem (0 rows remain after a run).\n\n## Verification\n\n- **8 consecutive local runs**: median ratio **1.00–1.10x** against the\n1.5x gate\n- `tests/`: **4185 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed** (unchanged);\n`core-storage-api/tests/`: **114 passed**\n- mypy clean both projects; ruff clean at all four CI scopes; generated\nartifacts byte-identical\n- Timing boundary preserved: `_make_input` is still evaluated at the\ncall site, so\n`MemoryCreate` construction stays outside the timed region exactly as\nbefore\n\n## Known residual, in the safe direction\n\nWithin each interleaved pair the second write measures ~3.8pp slower,\nand pipeline\nruns second — so the ratio is if anything *overstated*. Recorded in the\ncomment.\n\n## Deliberately not in this PR\n\nThe review made a strong case that this test is in the wrong tier\nentirely, and I\nagree, but it is a CI-policy change rather than a test fix:\n\n- **It is the only wall-clock ratio test that gates CI.** An\nidentically-shaped\nassertion (`assert ratio < 3.0`) in `tests/test_p5_p6_benchmarks.py:96`\nis\n`@pytest.mark.benchmark` and therefore deselected. **But nothing ever\nruns\n`-m benchmark`** — the tier holds 8 tests and is write-only, so marking\nthis one\nwithout also adding a non-gating scheduled job would trade a flake for\nsilent zero\n  coverage.\n- `tests/test_p1_contradiction.py:606` and `:646` are pure\nmicrobenchmarks whose only\nassertion is a wall-clock number, marked `integration`, gating CI,\nburning 100k\n  timed iterations per run.\n- **A timing-free formulation exists**: spy on `get_storage_client` and\nassert the\n  pipeline issues no more storage round-trips than legacy, plus assert\n`result.step_count`. The 1.5x gate permits ~16 ms of slack on a ~33 ms\nfloor, which\nis ~500-1000x the per-step glue it names, so it cannot resolve that\ninvariant at all.\n- This test also bundles a durable field-equivalence guard (17 columns +\nembeddings +\nmetadata) with the timing gate, so a runner hiccup and a real\nequivalence break\n  present as the same failure.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-11T08:27:46Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0e9245c29060054ecfe92eb0b7df678a8e699e21/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf"}]},{"sha":"d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGQ5M2U2NDE0ODNiNzg4YjVlNDUxZDFhZTY3ZmZjZjhkZTJmNDYwY2Y","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-10T21:26:59Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-10T21:26:59Z"},"message":"test: stop 107 tests advertising isolation they don't have (#749)\n\n## The signal that was wrong\n\n`tests/conftest.py`'s `db` fixture is a per-test session inside a\ntransaction that\nis **rolled back** at teardown. Requesting it reads as \"this test is\ntransactionally\nisolated.\"\n\nAlmost everything in `tests/` writes through the service layer instead,\nwhich commits\non its own connections — so that rollback never touches those rows.\n**107 test\nsignatures had drifted into requesting `db` and never using it**: a\nfalse isolation\nclaim, plus a connection, transaction and rollback bought for nothing.\n\nNothing is lost by dropping it. The autouse `_patch_storage_client`\n(`tests/conftest.py:185`) already depends on `_engine` and\n`_setup_schema`, so every\ntest gets schema setup either way.\n\n## 73 were direct; 34 were hidden\n\nFive helpers take `db` and never touch it — `_seed_governance`,\n`_seed_memories`, two\ndifferent `_insert_memory`, and `_insert_entity`. `helper(db, ...)`\nlooks like a use\nof the name while the parameter goes nowhere, so their callers had to\nkeep requesting\na session that was discarded. Those helpers and their **46 call sites**\nlose the\nargument too.\n\nWorth knowing for anyone repeating this: **seven different\n`_insert_memory` helpers\nexist across `tests/`, and some genuinely use `db`** (e.g.\n`test_redistribute.py:88`).\nMatching by bare name would have broken them, so this was scoped per\nfile.\n\n26 tests still request `db`, and all 26 genuinely use it.\n\n## A guard, so it cannot silently come back\n\n`tests/test_fixture_hygiene.py` asserts two things:\n\n1. no **helper** may take `db` without using it, and\n2. no **test** may request `db` without using it.\n\n(1) is what makes (2) sufficient — without it, forwarding to a helper\nthat drops the\nparameter reads as a use, which is exactly how 34 of the 107 stayed\nhidden from the\nfirst sweep. Verified by mutation: reintroducing a dead `db` on a test\ntrips (2),\nreintroducing one on a helper trips (1).\n\n## No speedup, and none claimed\n\nThe fixture costs **~0.111 ms per use**, so 73 removals is **~0.008 s**\nagainst a\n~210 s suite. This is hygiene, not performance. (The 258.7 s → 255.7 s I\nfirst\nobserved is noise, ~375× larger than the real saving.)\n\n## The concrete damage: the dedup benchmarks measured an empty table\n\n`tests/test_p2_semantic_dedup.py`'s benchmarks seeded via `db.add()` +\n`db.flush()` —\ninto the transaction that rolls back — while the query under test\nreached Postgres on\na separate connection. **Measured: seed 100, the query sees 0.**\n\nSo both `test_latency_100_memories` and `test_latency_1000_memories`\ntimed an empty\ntable, met their 10 ms and 50 ms budgets comfortably, and said nothing.\nThey were also\nusing the run's **shared** tenant, whose size is a running total of\nwhatever every\nother test left behind rather than the stated count.\n\nThey now commit their corpus under their own tenant, **assert it is\nactually visible\nbefore timing anything**, and clean up after. That assertion is\nload-bearing, not a\nsanity check — it is precisely what was missing. Real numbers, and they\nscale now:\n\n| corpus | mean | median | max |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 100 memories | 3.54 ms | 2.44 ms | 22.57 ms |\n| 1000 memories | 4.56 ms | 4.31 ms | 6.22 ms |\n\n## Two conftest comments asserted the belief being removed\n\n- `conftest.py:164` said `per-test rollback is the primary isolation`.\nIt isn't — the\nend-of-session `LIKE 'test-tenant-%'` sweep is what removes\nservice-layer writes.\n- `_reset_hooks`'s docstring said tests \"that use the `db` fixture\"\nbypass the app\n  lifespan, which described a minority even before this change.\n\n## Incidental, in blocks already being edited\n\n13 in-body imports of `_find_semantic_duplicate` made redundant by\nhoisting it; one\nunused `MagicMock`. And the benchmark's \"p99\" is now `max` — at 20\nsamples the\n99th-percentile index *is* the last element, so it was a max wearing a\npercentile's\nname.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/`: **4185 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed** — the 4183 baseline\nplus the 2 new\n  guard tests. Confirmed under random ordering, not just a pinned seed.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/`: **114 passed**, unchanged\n- mypy clean on both projects; ruff clean at all four CI scopes\n- `plugin/tools.json` and the broker OpenAPI regenerate byte-identical\n- `tests/` is outside CI's ruff scope, so no formatter could tidy up\nafter a bulk\nedit — the rewrite was AST-guided and every changed line was read.\nPer-file ruff\ncounts are unchanged everywhere except `test_p2_semantic_dedup.py`,\nwhich improves\n  from 6 findings to 1.\n\n## Deliberately not in this PR\n\n- **Making `tenant_id` unique per test** — the actual root fix, since\nthe shared\ntenant is the leftover-row generator. It looks like a one-line conftest\nchange and\nmeasured green in a scratch run, but it changes isolation semantics for\n72 tests\n  across 11 files and deserves its own PR and its own verification.\n- **`memories.supersedes_id` has no index**, so its `ON DELETE SET NULL`\nself-FK\nfires a full table scan per deleted row — measured 1.9 s to delete 1000\nrows from a\n28k-row table, growing linearly. That affects production deletes, not\njust tests.\n- **Three more tests that assert against their own simulation** rather\nthan the code\nthey name: `test_visibility.py:678` (benchmarks a local Python\nreimplementation and\nconcludes \"<1% overhead\" about the real SQL),\n`test_redistribute.py:125-255`, and\n`test_p0_3_recall_boost.py:225`. Same defect class as the benchmark\nfixed here.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"8da520d477285ab90ee5b9251e24f3038d870fc1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/8da520d477285ab90ee5b9251e24f3038d870fc1"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqekIjCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAlkAQAGtCJjqdjARPoJhr5mdi4Scm\nvllroaqOOdKZb243XskpDunj8Rlowjv4n8JOCB9y91iNgjtce2E3vtt7TBmBWC3H\nHGZeohjZ7tntnqPIT5b0fhQewXZ3lvNS/YWeEUqJeR2EQ0hosYvcmQDnHOPB5MYV\nmQUkkiKELNRo2CDS8RjmeucLtm9Z675q6OpzKs7kOrJlPpJfKsCSAAjjOXM7rMeA\nfuF96Az+sOa8JH57/7tidgdA1bMpZv7w8iwOKMdEbJqUydrHn5L6V3R9k0iWeDQ+\nZDZvVN2J1DMw62WRQOHqtnE0keeqInEuE14lqF4ohO6rCfSg0L2tmvZ4VYkGV1/X\nsEt6v8qooSZButzLykjHCMzcxtfSB2kvx/bRbVhQcga3xUhrgu0vG2ju62O4bddO\n+4vHOL9k1YRpBv5RJKnA9xK/dU3VEhcsCDR3JjgZo+x5eJetg2DHpXavrnffrYg3\n69umBvCdU7sF24W5kLPqOPlyl8ASl3kxl9RhkuqdFij8c5cjG19a5qr6J038OhVt\n35dQD7iKDV9y6fJSOWWFTliLedgzIHYNZH8pawv1G8b0+uBIXXcYPkYTM4TmM4Mn\nBXdRgR2++6LiBx5lU22BjDdpb8Jl2B/dOIZzKRWmCtR99rLXPO/7pRYgAiYJCURf\nWmbzWHtcGsJemHMerwvR\n=E5Eu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 8da520d477285ab90ee5b9251e24f3038d870fc1\nparent 8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786397219 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786397219 +0300\n\ntest: stop 107 tests advertising isolation they don't have (#749)\n\n## The signal that was wrong\n\n`tests/conftest.py`'s `db` fixture is a per-test session inside a\ntransaction that\nis **rolled back** at teardown. Requesting it reads as \"this test is\ntransactionally\nisolated.\"\n\nAlmost everything in `tests/` writes through the service layer instead,\nwhich commits\non its own connections — so that rollback never touches those rows.\n**107 test\nsignatures had drifted into requesting `db` and never using it**: a\nfalse isolation\nclaim, plus a connection, transaction and rollback bought for nothing.\n\nNothing is lost by dropping it. The autouse `_patch_storage_client`\n(`tests/conftest.py:185`) already depends on `_engine` and\n`_setup_schema`, so every\ntest gets schema setup either way.\n\n## 73 were direct; 34 were hidden\n\nFive helpers take `db` and never touch it — `_seed_governance`,\n`_seed_memories`, two\ndifferent `_insert_memory`, and `_insert_entity`. `helper(db, ...)`\nlooks like a use\nof the name while the parameter goes nowhere, so their callers had to\nkeep requesting\na session that was discarded. Those helpers and their **46 call sites**\nlose the\nargument too.\n\nWorth knowing for anyone repeating this: **seven different\n`_insert_memory` helpers\nexist across `tests/`, and some genuinely use `db`** (e.g.\n`test_redistribute.py:88`).\nMatching by bare name would have broken them, so this was scoped per\nfile.\n\n26 tests still request `db`, and all 26 genuinely use it.\n\n## A guard, so it cannot silently come back\n\n`tests/test_fixture_hygiene.py` asserts two things:\n\n1. no **helper** may take `db` without using it, and\n2. no **test** may request `db` without using it.\n\n(1) is what makes (2) sufficient — without it, forwarding to a helper\nthat drops the\nparameter reads as a use, which is exactly how 34 of the 107 stayed\nhidden from the\nfirst sweep. Verified by mutation: reintroducing a dead `db` on a test\ntrips (2),\nreintroducing one on a helper trips (1).\n\n## No speedup, and none claimed\n\nThe fixture costs **~0.111 ms per use**, so 73 removals is **~0.008 s**\nagainst a\n~210 s suite. This is hygiene, not performance. (The 258.7 s → 255.7 s I\nfirst\nobserved is noise, ~375× larger than the real saving.)\n\n## The concrete damage: the dedup benchmarks measured an empty table\n\n`tests/test_p2_semantic_dedup.py`'s benchmarks seeded via `db.add()` +\n`db.flush()` —\ninto the transaction that rolls back — while the query under test\nreached Postgres on\na separate connection. **Measured: seed 100, the query sees 0.**\n\nSo both `test_latency_100_memories` and `test_latency_1000_memories`\ntimed an empty\ntable, met their 10 ms and 50 ms budgets comfortably, and said nothing.\nThey were also\nusing the run's **shared** tenant, whose size is a running total of\nwhatever every\nother test left behind rather than the stated count.\n\nThey now commit their corpus under their own tenant, **assert it is\nactually visible\nbefore timing anything**, and clean up after. That assertion is\nload-bearing, not a\nsanity check — it is precisely what was missing. Real numbers, and they\nscale now:\n\n| corpus | mean | median | max |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 100 memories | 3.54 ms | 2.44 ms | 22.57 ms |\n| 1000 memories | 4.56 ms | 4.31 ms | 6.22 ms |\n\n## Two conftest comments asserted the belief being removed\n\n- `conftest.py:164` said `per-test rollback is the primary isolation`.\nIt isn't — the\nend-of-session `LIKE 'test-tenant-%'` sweep is what removes\nservice-layer writes.\n- `_reset_hooks`'s docstring said tests \"that use the `db` fixture\"\nbypass the app\n  lifespan, which described a minority even before this change.\n\n## Incidental, in blocks already being edited\n\n13 in-body imports of `_find_semantic_duplicate` made redundant by\nhoisting it; one\nunused `MagicMock`. And the benchmark's \"p99\" is now `max` — at 20\nsamples the\n99th-percentile index *is* the last element, so it was a max wearing a\npercentile's\nname.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/`: **4185 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed** — the 4183 baseline\nplus the 2 new\n  guard tests. Confirmed under random ordering, not just a pinned seed.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/`: **114 passed**, unchanged\n- mypy clean on both projects; ruff clean at all four CI scopes\n- `plugin/tools.json` and the broker OpenAPI regenerate byte-identical\n- `tests/` is outside CI's ruff scope, so no formatter could tidy up\nafter a bulk\nedit — the rewrite was AST-guided and every changed line was read.\nPer-file ruff\ncounts are unchanged everywhere except `test_p2_semantic_dedup.py`,\nwhich improves\n  from 6 findings to 1.\n\n## Deliberately not in this PR\n\n- **Making `tenant_id` unique per test** — the actual root fix, since\nthe shared\ntenant is the leftover-row generator. It looks like a one-line conftest\nchange and\nmeasured green in a scratch run, but it changes isolation semantics for\n72 tests\n  across 11 files and deserves its own PR and its own verification.\n- **`memories.supersedes_id` has no index**, so its `ON DELETE SET NULL`\nself-FK\nfires a full table scan per deleted row — measured 1.9 s to delete 1000\nrows from a\n28k-row table, growing linearly. That affects production deletes, not\njust tests.\n- **Three more tests that assert against their own simulation** rather\nthan the code\nthey name: `test_visibility.py:678` (benchmarks a local Python\nreimplementation and\nconcludes \"<1% overhead\" about the real SQL),\n`test_redistribute.py:125-255`, and\n`test_p0_3_recall_boost.py:225`. Same defect class as the benchmark\nfixed here.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-10T21:27:02Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/d93e641483b788b5e451d1ae67ffcf8de2f460cf/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4"}]},{"sha":"8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDhjZDJiNjBkMDQ0YmU1YjMyYmI5ODAwZTc0MTY3YTFlMjIwMWE1ZTQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-10T20:36:41Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-10T20:36:41Z"},"message":"ci: remove the Dependabot auto-merger revoked on 2026-08-09 (#748)\n\n## What this started as, and what it turned out to be\n\nThe task was a one-line comment fix: the header claimed *\"Majors arrive\nas separate, ungrouped PRs (different branch names)\"*, which is false —\n`dependabot.yml` defines five `*-majors` groups, so majors arrive\ngrouped.\n\nReading the file to fix that surfaced something bigger. **This repo\nstill carried the pre-revocation auto-merger.**\n\n| repo | job | behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| caura-ops | `flag-ready` | verifies CI, applies a label |\n| caura-memclaw-enterprise | `flag-ready` | verifies CI, applies a label\n|\n| **caura-memclaw (this repo)** | **`automerge`** | **mints an App\ntoken, `gh pr merge --squash`** |\n\nThat job was carve-out #2 of the never-auto-merge policy. The carve-out\nwas **revoked on 2026-08-09**; the two sibling repos were rewritten at\nthe time and this one was missed — so a policy that no longer exists\nstayed implemented, in the public repo.\n\n## It never merged anything — for two accidental reasons\n\n1. `permissions:` granted `contents`/`pull-requests`/`checks`/`statuses`\nbut **not `actions: read`**, the scope the rollup poll needs to resolve\ncheck runs. This is the same omission that silently no-op'd both\nsiblings for their entire lifetimes.\n2. The App was never added as a review-bypass actor on `main` —\n`bypass_pull_request_allowances` is empty — which the deleted header\nitself listed as a `PREREQUISITE`. The merge would have been refused\nregardless.\n\nEvidence, rather than inference: every merged Dependabot PR here was\nmerged **by hand**, including #649 whose branch\n`dependabot/uv/core-api/uv-minor-patch-bad095c31f` matched the gate\nexactly. Run\n[31391240657](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/actions/runs/31391240657)\nwent green against #743 today at 13:06, and #743 is still open.\n\nSo it was armed but jammed. Anyone \"fixing\" the permissions the way we\nfixed the siblings would have switched on auto-merging in a public repo.\n\n## Why deleted rather than rewritten to `flag-ready`\n\nThe label that job exists to write has **no reader here**:\n\n- `ci-verified` is absent from `.github/labels.yml`, which\n`labels-sync.yml` treats as the source of truth — a port needs a new\nlabel entry plus a sync, not just a workflow\n- the only workflow reading PR labels is `stale.yml`, and only\n`status/*`\n\nA ported version would write a label nothing consumes, and would have to\n**keep `pull_request_target`** to do it (Dependabot's `pull_request`\ntoken can't label). Deleting instead removes this repo's **last\n`pull_request_target` trigger** — `claude_code_review.yml` uses plain\n`pull_request` — which on a public repo is a real reduction in attack\nsurface. The backlog pressure the carve-out targeted is already handled\nstructurally: `dependabot.yml` caps each ecosystem at two PRs per run.\n\n## Verified safe to remove\n\n- `main`'s required status checks are exactly `[\"CI\",\"dco\"]` — this was\nnever a required check, so removing it cannot block PRs\n- nothing in the tree references the file, its job name, or the label\n- `DEPLOY_APP_ID` / `DEPLOY_APP_PRIVATE_KEY` remain in use by\n`release-please.yml`, so the org secrets are still needed\n- all remaining workflow YAML and `dependabot.yml` re-validated after\nthe change\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md:91` (\"Branch protection requires CI green, DCO check\ngreen, and ≥1 maintainer approval before merge\") becomes unconditionally\ntrue — the carve-out was its one exception\n\n## The three added lines\n\nWith the workflow gone, this repo would hold **no in-tree evidence**\nthat the carve-out ever existed or was revoked — which is precisely the\ndrift that let the broken copy survive here for weeks after the siblings\nwere fixed. The note sits in the `dependabot.yml` header, next to the\nexisting cross-repo line and directly above the `*-minor-patch` groups\nthe carve-out selected on, so the next person syncing from `caura-ops`\nsees it.\n\nI did not touch the logic. Your read was right that it was correct: the\nmajors groups genuinely `skipped` today (runs 31388948139 for the\nactions group, 31388881249 for `uv-majors`), because the allowlist\nexcludes them by name regardless of grouping.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"56cf4bfeff52cb1996b5c3757e4a14416e72e52b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/56cf4bfeff52cb1996b5c3757e4a14416e72e52b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqejZZCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAI+8QAGaXUDXS4ayV/TPxfeL8P1DX\n9gAqlmxwblW3rIUI+pdwV8D0va1mc74h/DQ97iGUTlIPPSeYA+XTfkPzxzPvl6zL\nQscGbb/8AUfEhqxOM6/HlHh9S4ssZfzblL9UTQ3y/idtuTY0Eisww1NeXXfmWCnd\nwtSba+3va9npO1+frmRUicRYapGGWMmyX+EmtAo2UXtoysEZekVy/yv255A4mWas\npWWASjS31PUhk3McZn8Ur9v2/hawJQe3QGtvym0aFRgzG+iGZWUp3Nsy65+S/eDp\nFZwUc47zM9MoePpzJrZZO8EQigRJG7jWjTM8m4ewKHVjC5oXR1WERvk8oLmIFaxx\nEdr4OVWbT0n4ecr8g15+sJC2glK23qyZowkJ+jJ3pSLLru47oHCI53GzLM8M/9vB\neLJNS2tL8Cz2VjWk2QV3MpCGGxtrkGPYlk6CQAYYwI40OnURoF8cPvHNNqaRpTK1\nWB3/FcyfNfBQ9CBUR4RevPdVCqN9KU/3Rxea6mhuIINq9cG+4/zkgeWUl4uD/fdw\nj3jHV8WJENUpNhQa17CTEGKKLj+AIcBtBxACJZHwCr1jRsTj4PzSoHak2fyG6fOB\nZGs/cfBYP72s4TpZu3jle4WXIfM98EqAngX4ZizCf1rfgM8RqiNnRa9z6QL8lVjL\nR5mOG09cGxQFlggH5ORq\n=okEH\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 56cf4bfeff52cb1996b5c3757e4a14416e72e52b\nparent bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786394201 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786394201 +0300\n\nci: remove the Dependabot auto-merger revoked on 2026-08-09 (#748)\n\n## What this started as, and what it turned out to be\n\nThe task was a one-line comment fix: the header claimed *\"Majors arrive\nas separate, ungrouped PRs (different branch names)\"*, which is false —\n`dependabot.yml` defines five `*-majors` groups, so majors arrive\ngrouped.\n\nReading the file to fix that surfaced something bigger. **This repo\nstill carried the pre-revocation auto-merger.**\n\n| repo | job | behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| caura-ops | `flag-ready` | verifies CI, applies a label |\n| caura-memclaw-enterprise | `flag-ready` | verifies CI, applies a label\n|\n| **caura-memclaw (this repo)** | **`automerge`** | **mints an App\ntoken, `gh pr merge --squash`** |\n\nThat job was carve-out #2 of the never-auto-merge policy. The carve-out\nwas **revoked on 2026-08-09**; the two sibling repos were rewritten at\nthe time and this one was missed — so a policy that no longer exists\nstayed implemented, in the public repo.\n\n## It never merged anything — for two accidental reasons\n\n1. `permissions:` granted `contents`/`pull-requests`/`checks`/`statuses`\nbut **not `actions: read`**, the scope the rollup poll needs to resolve\ncheck runs. This is the same omission that silently no-op'd both\nsiblings for their entire lifetimes.\n2. The App was never added as a review-bypass actor on `main` —\n`bypass_pull_request_allowances` is empty — which the deleted header\nitself listed as a `PREREQUISITE`. The merge would have been refused\nregardless.\n\nEvidence, rather than inference: every merged Dependabot PR here was\nmerged **by hand**, including #649 whose branch\n`dependabot/uv/core-api/uv-minor-patch-bad095c31f` matched the gate\nexactly. Run\n[31391240657](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/actions/runs/31391240657)\nwent green against #743 today at 13:06, and #743 is still open.\n\nSo it was armed but jammed. Anyone \"fixing\" the permissions the way we\nfixed the siblings would have switched on auto-merging in a public repo.\n\n## Why deleted rather than rewritten to `flag-ready`\n\nThe label that job exists to write has **no reader here**:\n\n- `ci-verified` is absent from `.github/labels.yml`, which\n`labels-sync.yml` treats as the source of truth — a port needs a new\nlabel entry plus a sync, not just a workflow\n- the only workflow reading PR labels is `stale.yml`, and only\n`status/*`\n\nA ported version would write a label nothing consumes, and would have to\n**keep `pull_request_target`** to do it (Dependabot's `pull_request`\ntoken can't label). Deleting instead removes this repo's **last\n`pull_request_target` trigger** — `claude_code_review.yml` uses plain\n`pull_request` — which on a public repo is a real reduction in attack\nsurface. The backlog pressure the carve-out targeted is already handled\nstructurally: `dependabot.yml` caps each ecosystem at two PRs per run.\n\n## Verified safe to remove\n\n- `main`'s required status checks are exactly `[\"CI\",\"dco\"]` — this was\nnever a required check, so removing it cannot block PRs\n- nothing in the tree references the file, its job name, or the label\n- `DEPLOY_APP_ID` / `DEPLOY_APP_PRIVATE_KEY` remain in use by\n`release-please.yml`, so the org secrets are still needed\n- all remaining workflow YAML and `dependabot.yml` re-validated after\nthe change\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md:91` (\"Branch protection requires CI green, DCO check\ngreen, and ≥1 maintainer approval before merge\") becomes unconditionally\ntrue — the carve-out was its one exception\n\n## The three added lines\n\nWith the workflow gone, this repo would hold **no in-tree evidence**\nthat the carve-out ever existed or was revoked — which is precisely the\ndrift that let the broken copy survive here for weeks after the siblings\nwere fixed. The note sits in the `dependabot.yml` header, next to the\nexisting cross-repo line and directly above the `*-minor-patch` groups\nthe carve-out selected on, so the next person syncing from `caura-ops`\nsees it.\n\nI did not touch the logic. Your read was right that it was correct: the\nmajors groups genuinely `skipped` today (runs 31388948139 for the\nactions group, 31388881249 for `uv-majors`), because the allowlist\nexcludes them by name regardless of grouping.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-10T20:36:45Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8cd2b60d044be5b32bb9800e74167a1e2201a5e4/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02"}]},{"sha":"bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGJkYzMyYzJhZmE2NjcxYzNmYmJjYmRmZDFkMDM5YTdmYjA5MWZhMDI","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-10T12:33:02Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-10T12:33:02Z"},"message":"chore(deps): drop the uv versioning-strategy that measurement showed is inert (#746)\n\n## What\n\nReverts the `versioning-strategy: increase-if-necessary` added to the\n`uv` entry in #740, and moves the durable rule to the file header.\n\n## Why — #740 was falsified by its own first real run\n\n#740 shipped with a stated test: the setting should kill the eight\nindividual widening PRs and leave lock-only PRs behind. The test ran,\nand it failed.\n\nThe timing matters, because it is easy to read the evidence backwards:\n\n| when | what |\n|---|---|\n| 01:41:47Z | scheduled Monday `uv` run produces #738 — **old config** |\n| 09:01:46Z | #740 merges |\n| 09:34:55Z | config-change re-run closes #738, opens #743 — **new\nconfig** |\n\nSo the scheduled run everyone was waiting on tested the config *before*\nthe change. The only run that exercised `increase-if-necessary` is the\none that produced #743, and #743's manifest edits are identical in kind\nto #738's:\n\n- `fastapi>=0.140.0,<1` → `>=0.141.1,<1` — 0.141.1 was already allowed\n- same shape for `alembic`, `cachetools`, `ruff`, `ddtrace`,\n`google-cloud-pubsub`\n- 56 requirement lines across the four services\n- the eight PRs it was meant to retire (#645, #646, #650, #691,\n#692-#695) were untouched, last updated 07-27 / 08-04\n\nGitHub's options reference *does* list `uv` among the ecosystems\nsupporting `versioning-strategy` (\"Supported by: `bundler`, `cargo`,\n`composer`, `helm`, `mix`, `npm`, `pip`, `pub`, and `uv`\"), so this is a\ngap in dependabot-core, not a mistake in the config. Reverting to the\ndefault restores honest behaviour: the file no longer claims to fix\nsomething it does not fix.\n\n## Why the rule moved to the header\n\nThe most valuable line in the old comment was the warning that the `pip`\nentry must never take this option — and it sat inside the `uv` entry, 36\nlines from the edit it exists to prevent. On a lock-less entry the\nsetting is worse than inert: dependabot-core skips the manifest edit\nwhen the new version already satisfies the range and no lockfile exists,\nso there is nothing left to change and **no PR is filed at all**. That\nis a silent loss of update coverage, and the warning now lives where\nboth entries can see it.\n\nThe header also records why `lockfile-only` is not the answer here: it\nis honoured, but it drops any update needing a manifest change, most\nspecs in the uv services are capped, and this repo is public — these\n`>=` floors are part of the contract with OSS consumers, so a raised\nfloor is not purely churn the way it is inside a private service.\n\nConstraint-widening PRs are now documented as a known, accepted cost\nrather than a solved problem.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Config is 7 lines **shorter** than before #740; the point-in-time\ncensus numbers and the nine PR references are deliberately kept in this\nPR body rather than the config, where nothing fails when they go stale.\n- No behaviour change beyond restoring the Dependabot default. All six\nentries and all ten groups verified intact after the edit.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"e423e72f613942c0470b2d1ac9fda7e380c42710","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/e423e72f613942c0470b2d1ac9fda7e380c42710"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqecT+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAtQsQAHM2yO4KdDv+fLVlmToO6h36\n4mVLngx7Jl9uqQJQH8lrb31/zd/arjkxT6i4KKShE0wHa+aH98sKGEtSFHhtWOE2\nJ/bnoCA6o2mPtQJmubnxtgxW5+eBuMtTgLWgwo2PcFCM3Z+Li74YuhQsJ8gtQjir\n0DA5VfRlRBPbTk+FBQvYUaesq42jFbbVPGkCKtjHbAQmXzTegOc6tPlLTgKpjl6v\niVAwazmTZY6x3aV/Y2f66KqQ0CPeeAqXsE0wHsOgQEObn9nYLTFUAGiS/cvrDPzb\nce5OOJrIcJy/k2IQzZxVw/cIZa2C/Xh9wp+GXZ0T8SF78tZOUwVZLTsfez5aVQDe\nZPyL3iIkv0DBqH/6zAwou74+1DQdwCLIsRCiobbjnoWJPZClxPfKMbVovlB62tND\n+7BW6tQ+YIMm82o62aj7hXwoMqOb2ry4h8OjGsfWHfChsojl5HDhs3SZW9RUmciJ\ni+hTO8j8XnaR+3ffJBKhqMnnBx8R+UTipU4c68W0xg/r9hNINfAWyfaGp6FHwq4d\nwEhEG+HjMHMyWGuUPNogU8HUaFYwba356KKJ/fFtXYIXXumzefF1q+xZIMacZPzO\n6eBdGzXvrfGWMmHALJW4mElePwYGxoxLUOv0afBscVQQwAqyRHXbw0dR5p2Yxyq6\nwABTRfrnDlmxw0R1vQdQ\n=CDO7\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree e423e72f613942c0470b2d1ac9fda7e380c42710\nparent 3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786365182 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786365182 +0300\n\nchore(deps): drop the uv versioning-strategy that measurement showed is inert (#746)\n\n## What\n\nReverts the `versioning-strategy: increase-if-necessary` added to the\n`uv` entry in #740, and moves the durable rule to the file header.\n\n## Why — #740 was falsified by its own first real run\n\n#740 shipped with a stated test: the setting should kill the eight\nindividual widening PRs and leave lock-only PRs behind. The test ran,\nand it failed.\n\nThe timing matters, because it is easy to read the evidence backwards:\n\n| when | what |\n|---|---|\n| 01:41:47Z | scheduled Monday `uv` run produces #738 — **old config** |\n| 09:01:46Z | #740 merges |\n| 09:34:55Z | config-change re-run closes #738, opens #743 — **new\nconfig** |\n\nSo the scheduled run everyone was waiting on tested the config *before*\nthe change. The only run that exercised `increase-if-necessary` is the\none that produced #743, and #743's manifest edits are identical in kind\nto #738's:\n\n- `fastapi>=0.140.0,<1` → `>=0.141.1,<1` — 0.141.1 was already allowed\n- same shape for `alembic`, `cachetools`, `ruff`, `ddtrace`,\n`google-cloud-pubsub`\n- 56 requirement lines across the four services\n- the eight PRs it was meant to retire (#645, #646, #650, #691,\n#692-#695) were untouched, last updated 07-27 / 08-04\n\nGitHub's options reference *does* list `uv` among the ecosystems\nsupporting `versioning-strategy` (\"Supported by: `bundler`, `cargo`,\n`composer`, `helm`, `mix`, `npm`, `pip`, `pub`, and `uv`\"), so this is a\ngap in dependabot-core, not a mistake in the config. Reverting to the\ndefault restores honest behaviour: the file no longer claims to fix\nsomething it does not fix.\n\n## Why the rule moved to the header\n\nThe most valuable line in the old comment was the warning that the `pip`\nentry must never take this option — and it sat inside the `uv` entry, 36\nlines from the edit it exists to prevent. On a lock-less entry the\nsetting is worse than inert: dependabot-core skips the manifest edit\nwhen the new version already satisfies the range and no lockfile exists,\nso there is nothing left to change and **no PR is filed at all**. That\nis a silent loss of update coverage, and the warning now lives where\nboth entries can see it.\n\nThe header also records why `lockfile-only` is not the answer here: it\nis honoured, but it drops any update needing a manifest change, most\nspecs in the uv services are capped, and this repo is public — these\n`>=` floors are part of the contract with OSS consumers, so a raised\nfloor is not purely churn the way it is inside a private service.\n\nConstraint-widening PRs are now documented as a known, accepted cost\nrather than a solved problem.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Config is 7 lines **shorter** than before #740; the point-in-time\ncensus numbers and the nine PR references are deliberately kept in this\nPR body rather than the config, where nothing fails when they go stale.\n- No behaviour change beyond restoring the Dependabot default. All six\nentries and all ten groups verified intact after the edit.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-10T12:33:05Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/bdc32c2afa6671c3fbbcbdfd1d039a7fb091fa02/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455"}]},{"sha":"3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDNiOTRhMDY5YjI1YTM2Y2M1NTYyNjRkZTZlZWRkOWZlY2E5MjQ0NTU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-10T12:18:11Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-10T12:18:11Z"},"message":"ci(review): allow a manual capture backfill, mirroring the shared pipeline's caller (#745)\n\n`claude-capture` only fires on a merge, so there was no way to exercise\nit on demand. That matters now for two reasons.\n\n## 1. It makes the credential verifiable\n\n`MEMCLAW_AGENTS_KEY` isn't set on this repo yet, so recall and capture\nare both dark. **Recall** is verifiable the moment it's added — it runs\non every review and logs whether it injected anything. **Capture**\nwasn't: confirming it needed a real merge that happened to carry a\ndeclined finding, which isn't something you can arrange in order to\ncheck a secret.\n\nThe shared pipeline's caller has had this dispatch for exactly that\nreason; this brings the local copy in line.\n\n## 2. Backfill\n\nCapture didn't exist here until #737, so every decline in this repo's\nhistory is unrecorded. #735, #737 and #744 each carry findings a\nmaintainer declined **with a stated reason** — precisely what the\nextractor looks for. One dispatch per PR recovers them.\n\n## Details\n\n`required: true` on the input, because the only failure mode of an empty\nvalue is a confusing one: the script would request `issues//comments`,\ntake the 404, warn, and exit 0 looking exactly like a PR with nothing to\ncapture.\n\n**Only `claude-capture` answers a dispatch**, and that falls out of the\nexisting gates rather than needing a new one — `pre-checks` is gated on\n`pull_request`, `claude-review` needs it to have succeeded, and\n`claude-retrigger` needs `issue_comment`. So a dispatch can't start a\nreview, which would otherwise be an expensive surprise given a backfill\nnames an already-merged PR.\n\n`PR_NUMBER` reads `github.event.pull_request.number ||\ninputs.pr_number`, in that order, so a merge never picks up a stale\ndispatch input.\n\n`actionlint` clean.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"520fef348228c88a405dfb1d48f7fc9b4af569bf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/520fef348228c88a405dfb1d48f7fc9b4af569bf"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqecGDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4dwQAKJpWT/8CoPEq3ig0DFG70oe\newGRGaB+3P5Z16Gf3Fh4va9gjkZV39MF1txDJs0HMLEBgYwJzd/7w4LAT5yC5lon\nfRea5PaKxpD4psaNRNld/XrnYoNEhGPCz07Dy/uL8R5XMb9lpFTLlUssNaAf+KoW\nv6UYKCbQQYIIUkxANnp9KO7tMZFHIRE10yLTxRxE22fUR5yzjzyMR7Y7S8Xvy+Ze\nd/GYWNhKej/VHFj79tI3kkIWukCBvX8UaljVr0tXF/8SWxhgRTUgIIFYpCEatSQs\nDf4B4FytdwLjl7SX/rTeJOJd9THeuvzA5JdUSr4vaHruD1A6frh9N2fg453M7f+u\nriyrN5hTJa3gtSIK4sXMWMiU5S4OFurFh5BAHFeO3KG5FzXnHZRX2M0zmCbiFHPS\neUPdRNkUpEZC7p2osQ8PYZIrrfeLIJ3c/swID1v30rHj6FGhviqCxb/cTLgg/xhR\nQniO3CYRZg0CDZX0HpYuwJjb78dbaMBKotPgAmKDcxLtHGvjh+aNBRON8iyodJdX\nWj6bI8i1WhpY2ziP+0/jW0RV0evHLQEhquhP0sce3lpQujzebKsT94xIaFC7YNWn\n/Mib3PIXIn8zowOqvfS9iBMY8DzVmIvBoDtWqJK2DVg6HOc5O2514J6UESePXCqO\nGQ6ySiqIcfQbR75goInC\n=3WEJ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 520fef348228c88a405dfb1d48f7fc9b4af569bf\nparent eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786364291 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786364291 +0300\n\nci(review): allow a manual capture backfill, mirroring the shared pipeline's caller (#745)\n\n`claude-capture` only fires on a merge, so there was no way to exercise\nit on demand. That matters now for two reasons.\n\n## 1. It makes the credential verifiable\n\n`MEMCLAW_AGENTS_KEY` isn't set on this repo yet, so recall and capture\nare both dark. **Recall** is verifiable the moment it's added — it runs\non every review and logs whether it injected anything. **Capture**\nwasn't: confirming it needed a real merge that happened to carry a\ndeclined finding, which isn't something you can arrange in order to\ncheck a secret.\n\nThe shared pipeline's caller has had this dispatch for exactly that\nreason; this brings the local copy in line.\n\n## 2. Backfill\n\nCapture didn't exist here until #737, so every decline in this repo's\nhistory is unrecorded. #735, #737 and #744 each carry findings a\nmaintainer declined **with a stated reason** — precisely what the\nextractor looks for. One dispatch per PR recovers them.\n\n## Details\n\n`required: true` on the input, because the only failure mode of an empty\nvalue is a confusing one: the script would request `issues//comments`,\ntake the 404, warn, and exit 0 looking exactly like a PR with nothing to\ncapture.\n\n**Only `claude-capture` answers a dispatch**, and that falls out of the\nexisting gates rather than needing a new one — `pre-checks` is gated on\n`pull_request`, `claude-review` needs it to have succeeded, and\n`claude-retrigger` needs `issue_comment`. So a dispatch can't start a\nreview, which would otherwise be an expensive surprise given a backfill\nnames an already-merged PR.\n\n`PR_NUMBER` reads `github.event.pull_request.number ||\ninputs.pr_number`, in that order, so a merge never picks up a stale\ndispatch input.\n\n`actionlint` clean.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-10T12:18:14Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/3b94a069b25a36cc556264de6eedd9feca924455/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584"}]},{"sha":"eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGViMDdjMjY4YzdkYWExN2RkNDZkYWQyMmE3ZGNiZGE0NDY5OWM1ODQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-10T11:27:35Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-10T11:27:35Z"},"message":"ci(review): name the reviewer at the top of the verdict, mirroring the shared pipeline (#744)\n\n[caura-ai/.github#34](https://github.com/caura-ai/.github/pull/34) moved\nreviewer attribution to the **first** line of a verdict rather than a\nfooter under what is often a long review, released in **v1.5.1**. This\nrepo posts its own verdict from its local copy, so without this it's the\none place a review looks different — and someone comparing a review here\nagainst one from a shared-pipeline repo would have to work out that the\n*formats* differ rather than the reviewers.\n\n```markdown\n> 🤖 Review by **Claude Code**\n\n## Summary\n…\n\n---\n*Reviewed by `claude-sonnet-5` · cost $0.60*\n```\n\nByte-identical to what the shared pipeline emits once its `AGENT_LABEL`\nresolves.\n\n## Three choices carried over, one changed\n\n**Hardcoded** rather than read from a variable, unlike upstream — this\ncopy runs one reviewer and is deliberately standalone, so a variable\nwith a single possible value is indirection without a second caller.\nSame reasoning that inlined recall here instead of adding a\n`review_lib.sh`.\n\n**A blockquote, not a heading** — a verdict opens with its own `##\nSummary`, and a second heading would compete with the review's structure\nrather than label it.\n\n**The header avoids the string `Reviewed by \\``** —\n`claude_pr_capture.sh` greps exactly that to decide a PR was reviewed,\nand the footer is what it should find. A header matching it too would\nmake that gate depend on which line survived thread truncation, and\ncapture keeps the **tail**. Verified against the rendered body: first\nline is the header with no gate string, footer carries it.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The drift checker **cannot** catch this one. It compares five\nbehavioural facts and the verdict *format* isn't among them, so this\nmirror rests on `CLAUDE.md` and on someone noticing. Worth remembering\nnext time the two copies diverge in something the checker doesn't\nmeasure.\n\n`shellcheck` and `bash -n` clean.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"a1863ed9eb9d1cc7b4102801706117a37954448b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/a1863ed9eb9d1cc7b4102801706117a37954448b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqebWnCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvfIQAAuZBe/IOET4aJA0gCrv/oVm\noUxEfPcawfv7fgnnCklWlGMmpb1AZf+e2A4cefv/3fM0RUF7zHW7Guxqpsw4py/B\na972Rn4IsKRnsW0UhmixesbeAIOjOuKN/cZETD//78cikA5pyOcNY4Z8F4v7uMsW\njnHirtZQfuPXZF4c6kdCY2k2LPXKDKeLgqczN0YCiLKR/sQDQ6L/4ofjejhIYeHV\n8l10djj0dJQrQZthrYhf3MIM8De26uCMBVsDlNw9yGH+Ni0CzMA9LnEis6+7Og+p\nTLf+n+Zog88agKWPNwnmCNagV9tQFijrSo+mtZI1WPXwUaySmYUak0zl5KuoUv2/\nm6ejqEbOoGc4FRfER50dHXp0eB1h11tnNYapyn0h2s433gpOc5Xp2jp40yCr66Bz\noQQ8EgBXBPE6/+hjhuMKno+DB2Ud9xITyPjMEOxXweV9vQwx1IiRvS2byyuxnVR1\nouxmp8Q64DJvSkmq7YvWDNx62NdfzVyASQdBxWz8VoF835RksgDKh9t21DFL1ktJ\n6suiXbL8y0U9KLqJnqPWAvxXgF9tORDP53rBqiZ/V/W3TzqntmX3NsUjBj6Lt2jV\nF3aZm0nqkAKDB6VzMQZAC5YcsqeF68IAnh2keOiOSv3aMPB5f3NJExNI5xSIQQhJ\nDHEUBv3GNm9cfPfZC7i7\n=Pzq/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree a1863ed9eb9d1cc7b4102801706117a37954448b\nparent b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786361255 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786361255 +0300\n\nci(review): name the reviewer at the top of the verdict, mirroring the shared pipeline (#744)\n\n[caura-ai/.github#34](https://github.com/caura-ai/.github/pull/34) moved\nreviewer attribution to the **first** line of a verdict rather than a\nfooter under what is often a long review, released in **v1.5.1**. This\nrepo posts its own verdict from its local copy, so without this it's the\none place a review looks different — and someone comparing a review here\nagainst one from a shared-pipeline repo would have to work out that the\n*formats* differ rather than the reviewers.\n\n```markdown\n> 🤖 Review by **Claude Code**\n\n## Summary\n…\n\n---\n*Reviewed by `claude-sonnet-5` · cost $0.60*\n```\n\nByte-identical to what the shared pipeline emits once its `AGENT_LABEL`\nresolves.\n\n## Three choices carried over, one changed\n\n**Hardcoded** rather than read from a variable, unlike upstream — this\ncopy runs one reviewer and is deliberately standalone, so a variable\nwith a single possible value is indirection without a second caller.\nSame reasoning that inlined recall here instead of adding a\n`review_lib.sh`.\n\n**A blockquote, not a heading** — a verdict opens with its own `##\nSummary`, and a second heading would compete with the review's structure\nrather than label it.\n\n**The header avoids the string `Reviewed by \\``** —\n`claude_pr_capture.sh` greps exactly that to decide a PR was reviewed,\nand the footer is what it should find. A header matching it too would\nmake that gate depend on which line survived thread truncation, and\ncapture keeps the **tail**. Verified against the rendered body: first\nline is the header with no gate string, footer carries it.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The drift checker **cannot** catch this one. It compares five\nbehavioural facts and the verdict *format* isn't among them, so this\nmirror rests on `CLAUDE.md` and on someone noticing. Worth remembering\nnext time the two copies diverge in something the checker doesn't\nmeasure.\n\n`shellcheck` and `bash -n` clean.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-10T11:27:37Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/eb07c268c7daa17dd46dad22a7dcbda44699c584/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4"}]},{"sha":"b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGI0YTg4N2QwZTI2NTE0OWU3YTY0MmRmYzE5ZWYzMGMyMDg5OTg5YjQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-10T10:56:43Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-10T10:56:43Z"},"message":"ci(review): give this repo's reviewer the shared code-review memory (#737)\n\nSix of the org's seven repos read learned guidance before reviewing and\nwrite a maintainer's declined findings back afterwards, so a finding\njudged wrong once stops being raised again. This repo was the exception\n— and not by decision: it keeps a **local copy** of the pipeline (public\nrepo, private `.github`, and a component in a private repository can\nonly be used by private repositories), and that copy predates the memory\nfeature entirely.\n\nThe practical cost: the reviewer here has been re-raising things already\nsettled, on the OSS repo where outside contributors see it.\n\n## Recall\n\nInlined into `claude_pr_review.sh` rather than sourced from a\n`review_lib.sh` — this copy is deliberately two standalone files, and a\nlibrary holding one function is structure without a second caller.\n\nSame recall the shared pipeline runs: the query is built from the\nchanged paths so guidance is relevant to *this* diff, recalled bullets\nare entity-escaped so one cannot close the `<review_guidance>` wrapper\nand escape into the prompt body, and the wrapper frames them as data.\nThe instructional half of that defence is soft, and the residual risk is\naccepted for a curated internal fleet — worth revisiting if it ever\ningests external content.\n\n## Capture\n\nNew script plus a job on merge. `merged == true`, not merely `closed`: a\nPR closed *without* merging carries no accepted judgement, and learning\nfrom an abandoned branch teaches the reviewer a decision nobody stood\nbehind.\n\nThe extractor runs with **every tool removed** and `--bare`, because a\ncomment thread on a public repo is attacker-influenced input exactly as\na diff is.\n\nNotes are tagged `· claude ·` even though this repo runs one reviewer —\nthe **fleet is shared** with repos that run two, and an untagged note\nfrom here would be the only one no per-reviewer filter could account\nfor. Attribution is a constant here rather than inferred from reviewer\nmarkers, which is what the shared pipeline needs and this doesn't.\n\n## Trigger changes\n\n`closed` had to join the trigger list for capture to see a merge, so\n`pre-checks` now excludes it explicitly — otherwise merging a PR would\nstart a fresh review of it. `claude-review` is additionally gated on\n`pre-checks` succeeding, so it can't fire on a merge either way.\n\n## It lands DARK, deliberately\n\n`MEMCLAW_AGENTS_KEY` is an **org** secret with **`private`** visibility,\nand a public repo cannot read it. So today it resolves to `''` here:\n\n- recall returns immediately, leaving the review byte-for-byte unchanged\n- capture logs `capture is dark (no MEMCLAW_AGENTS_KEY) — skipping` and\nexits 0\n\nTurning it on is **a repo-level secret and no code change**. Shipping it\ndark makes the config step independently verifiable rather than bundling\na behaviour change with a credential change.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Adding the key here means a live MemClaw write credential on a public\nrepo. Fork PRs never receive secrets, so a fork cannot reach it;\nsame-repo PRs mean push access already. That's the same posture\n`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` has here today.\n\n`actionlint`, `shellcheck`, `bash -n` clean. Verified the dark path\nactually logs rather than failing silently.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"4b552d9e7605d91c698f45372c2ff341f4c6e5ac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/4b552d9e7605d91c698f45372c2ff341f4c6e5ac"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqea5rCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxUwQACv3wGrYMMzlVsOxX7rfyUOE\n58DvT1ywCikYJ86odqVAIEgEvrWGM5Zm8KAEurIDRh6FSMwGM/f43RNJe8nBNKp9\nxqyNSf/gCf/rn9XfdSRpUddpQyKIkMuvXkAhQwCbdiGgYBGSbv5fIEeXI0BMnYQW\n7Yq0a9R1kBrqMZtTqqGqetekVsCykoA8AbG7PSkw0BqRpra//Yyktj49TBkN3PMK\nZ9EBPFXTiwUqU3hGfZZBwhJgso4NJ5II6xvHfN7/mNHTcD1dOcK8ii/pEoNJHYMu\nR+lR1OOhssj1dLQjpy4G5q9IMacFzbX3oKU/NRDUTBJL+OtJ4xzUbW7PPeMDP/5B\nAgcraF/K81f+dKHDIrKyHh5t/GpgQNANuuPHlAGlg331HZ9zRLarOZnOKycFJz07\nA8sUboM/tO2Cl/Bvn6XWriIuzaM7PV3PGVoTwI3Oi+s1bUPyB+H9zf51GnYsIvs4\nV2VziTnraua/mN4wN9/bDlhxh+sjgfqtC1GuTjqbUJcpZzSeUo1vPzRmJYFOM8cI\nwbilTvuqIINt/OgtiQvoVXx2r+uN8FKvbkJk1/vVnNhJIx/Kd/jq1boIOf8wlHym\nPxs/O031kJHVchRNxwbeE0lLrq3g0KLz6X4wcnzuI33RK/oCS7TYKhSyhhSuoMWe\nR/9Tq8J34ucirSsmPhvm\n=/EDA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 4b552d9e7605d91c698f45372c2ff341f4c6e5ac\nparent b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786359403 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786359403 +0300\n\nci(review): give this repo's reviewer the shared code-review memory (#737)\n\nSix of the org's seven repos read learned guidance before reviewing and\nwrite a maintainer's declined findings back afterwards, so a finding\njudged wrong once stops being raised again. This repo was the exception\n— and not by decision: it keeps a **local copy** of the pipeline (public\nrepo, private `.github`, and a component in a private repository can\nonly be used by private repositories), and that copy predates the memory\nfeature entirely.\n\nThe practical cost: the reviewer here has been re-raising things already\nsettled, on the OSS repo where outside contributors see it.\n\n## Recall\n\nInlined into `claude_pr_review.sh` rather than sourced from a\n`review_lib.sh` — this copy is deliberately two standalone files, and a\nlibrary holding one function is structure without a second caller.\n\nSame recall the shared pipeline runs: the query is built from the\nchanged paths so guidance is relevant to *this* diff, recalled bullets\nare entity-escaped so one cannot close the `<review_guidance>` wrapper\nand escape into the prompt body, and the wrapper frames them as data.\nThe instructional half of that defence is soft, and the residual risk is\naccepted for a curated internal fleet — worth revisiting if it ever\ningests external content.\n\n## Capture\n\nNew script plus a job on merge. `merged == true`, not merely `closed`: a\nPR closed *without* merging carries no accepted judgement, and learning\nfrom an abandoned branch teaches the reviewer a decision nobody stood\nbehind.\n\nThe extractor runs with **every tool removed** and `--bare`, because a\ncomment thread on a public repo is attacker-influenced input exactly as\na diff is.\n\nNotes are tagged `· claude ·` even though this repo runs one reviewer —\nthe **fleet is shared** with repos that run two, and an untagged note\nfrom here would be the only one no per-reviewer filter could account\nfor. Attribution is a constant here rather than inferred from reviewer\nmarkers, which is what the shared pipeline needs and this doesn't.\n\n## Trigger changes\n\n`closed` had to join the trigger list for capture to see a merge, so\n`pre-checks` now excludes it explicitly — otherwise merging a PR would\nstart a fresh review of it. `claude-review` is additionally gated on\n`pre-checks` succeeding, so it can't fire on a merge either way.\n\n## It lands DARK, deliberately\n\n`MEMCLAW_AGENTS_KEY` is an **org** secret with **`private`** visibility,\nand a public repo cannot read it. So today it resolves to `''` here:\n\n- recall returns immediately, leaving the review byte-for-byte unchanged\n- capture logs `capture is dark (no MEMCLAW_AGENTS_KEY) — skipping` and\nexits 0\n\nTurning it on is **a repo-level secret and no code change**. Shipping it\ndark makes the config step independently verifiable rather than bundling\na behaviour change with a credential change.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Adding the key here means a live MemClaw write credential on a public\nrepo. Fork PRs never receive secrets, so a fork cannot reach it;\nsame-repo PRs mean push access already. That's the same posture\n`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` has here today.\n\n`actionlint`, `shellcheck`, `bash -n` clean. Verified the dark path\nactually logs rather than failing silently.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-10T10:56:45Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b4a887d0e265149e7a642dfc19ef30c2089989b4/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e"}]},{"sha":"b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGI4NTNhOTc4ZTNkOTQ1NTk3MzI5ODY2M2U4NDA1NGZkODIwNmUyOWU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-10T09:13:25Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-10T09:13:25Z"},"message":"perf(search): halve ts_rank_cd renders in the scored-search SQL (#739)\n\n## What\n\n`fts_score` was `x / (1 + x)`, where `x` is the\n`ts_rank_cd(search_vector, plainto_tsquery(...))`\nexpression — not a bound value. SQLAlchemy inlines an expression at\nevery site that names it, so\nwriting it twice made Postgres evaluate the ranking function twice.\n\nThe algebraically identical form names it once:\n\n```\nx / (1 + x)  ==  1 - 1/(1 + x)\n```\n\nCompiling the real `memory_scored_search` statement: **`ts_rank_cd(` 18\nrenders → 9**, and\n`plainto_tsquery(` 27 → 18.\n\n## Measured\n\nOn a 31,446-memory corpus with genuine multi-term matches, scoring +\nordering + limiting, medians\nof 7 runs per query:\n\n| query | matching rows | two-render | one-render | delta |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `case never use` | 11,505 | 92.0 ms | 56.4 ms | **38.7%** |\n| `case never project` | 7,222 | 59.6 ms | 37.2 ms | **37.7%** |\n| `case never must` | 5,581 | 47.2 ms | 29.9 ms | **36.6%** |\n| `case never convent` | 4,016 | 34.6 ms | 22.2 ms | **36.0%** |\n| `case never requir` | 1,875 | 19.1 ms | 13.0 ms | **31.8%** |\n\nRead that as the gain on the **FTS scoring component**. The full search\nalso pays six pgvector\ndistance computations per row, so end-to-end is smaller. Prior attempts\nto measure this locally\nwere meaningless: the local corpus's realistic multi-term queries match\nzero rows, and its only\nmatching single term returns 3,397 rows that all score the identical\n0.375.\n\n## What this does NOT do\n\nIt does not get to one render, and the code no longer claims it does.\n`similarity` names\n`fts_score` in both branches of its CASE, `score` inlines `similarity`,\nand both are output columns\nof the scored CTE — four live references remain, times the UNION'd\nreserved branch.\n\nGetting to 1 means projecting `similarity`/`vec_sim` once in an inner\nderived table, and it **does**\nneed a real optimisation barrier: with references still live, Postgres\nflattens a plain subquery and\nre-substitutes. That is a separate change to the hot path with its own\nplan-shape review. (An\nearlier note in this thread claimed no barrier was ever needed — true of\nthe factor in isolation,\nfalse of the statement it composes into.)\n\n## Not bit-identical\n\nThe two forms disagree by at most one ULP — measured `max |a - b| =\n5.55e-17` over every matching\nrow of that corpus, not the `0.000e+00` recorded earlier. The old zero\nwas measured where every\nmatching row scored the same single value, so it had no rank variety to\nexpose the difference.\n5.55e-17 is ~4 orders of magnitude below the tightest tolerance anything\nhere asserts (`1e-12`) and\nfar below any score gap that could reorder results.\n\n## Tests\n\nTwo levels, because the factor being clean is not the same as the query\nbeing clean:\n\n- the saturating factor in isolation renders `ts_rank_cd` once, plus a\ncontrol proving the naive\nform really does render twice (so the count-of-one assertion can't pass\nvacuously);\n- **the assembled statement's render counts**, captured on the way to\n`session.execute` and\ncompiled — no DB. This is what catches someone adding a fifth reference\nto `fts_score`. Reverting\n  the fix fails it with `renders ts_rank_cd 18 times, expected 9`.\n\n`_rank_and_score` in the integration suite carried its own copy of the\nold expression while its\ndocstring claimed to use the production one; it now matches.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `core-storage-api/tests/`: **114 passed** (105 on `origin/main` + 9\nnew)\n- `tests/`: **4183 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed** — identical to the\n`a5a55f2` baseline\n- mypy clean on both projects; ruff check/format clean at all four CI\nscopes\n- `plugin/tools.json` and the broker OpenAPI regenerate byte-identical\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"1fad5772dada24c028bacb4af23f718cdce097fd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/1fad5772dada24c028bacb4af23f718cdce097fd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqeZY1CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAMKYQAF2QVy6U4VDyyynaH6cQ987h\n7dq/I2LS/cOMR6we/5vwucpD7d7BUJQXEUEbOapdEUhGPMu3IM4B+CYyNIJBLV2W\nT45LNj+w//LIe9gPHasFLmpJ65QKDgVRY5SKJOv/pHwJnycDESbQYRMLmCK3biLx\nPHB+ZfPevtaHPzh5Di6Y8yvJ95vagVCV1NwEg/jF0Acqa1CaXIIr4REbgIjWIzIq\nJYcMHDBiv/juC+6PToQMYUcrw1EsefgPTHDG5R3B8bWquX/+t0OKr3Rm/cNDPaDV\nETpm3I2CZRThiSoq8v83X+X4Ky31Aa/VTOBwbbDvMHSx5Qs0pfaHMwdlDCBRCg0h\neUnIBv1XlQuoF+DV67XYtk/oXlxpl2CiOpapGqjniLz0IE2OrvGf8I7dY++zi0PT\ncDm87fH9yYBD43oI5fm/ZMKTLwIs6mv8votLPX8OKalfhOPwqzK8+9wNmNuxhdEv\nVzSAHrJl6yr0WCiusBTUxV2T2uKdLpPeUSi2tdl51lsOxwl21gyHabV2KUDJrSGo\n5ZzKR9hLzrw+OodUPmkjgVUXgo+Ym4wTxKm/WVAXx8mZqLM75YwYfOsTnwqekgt1\n0c4SYRkypv7lmbv3aDWyPiSBXsoxA9BM6JxSEWbyU6wFPIipxoAfBmzhB+jO1p52\nNr6qGGbNmR9PXn+1uhuo\n=AOHd\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 1fad5772dada24c028bacb4af23f718cdce097fd\nparent 1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786353205 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786353205 +0300\n\nperf(search): halve ts_rank_cd renders in the scored-search SQL (#739)\n\n## What\n\n`fts_score` was `x / (1 + x)`, where `x` is the\n`ts_rank_cd(search_vector, plainto_tsquery(...))`\nexpression — not a bound value. SQLAlchemy inlines an expression at\nevery site that names it, so\nwriting it twice made Postgres evaluate the ranking function twice.\n\nThe algebraically identical form names it once:\n\n```\nx / (1 + x)  ==  1 - 1/(1 + x)\n```\n\nCompiling the real `memory_scored_search` statement: **`ts_rank_cd(` 18\nrenders → 9**, and\n`plainto_tsquery(` 27 → 18.\n\n## Measured\n\nOn a 31,446-memory corpus with genuine multi-term matches, scoring +\nordering + limiting, medians\nof 7 runs per query:\n\n| query | matching rows | two-render | one-render | delta |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `case never use` | 11,505 | 92.0 ms | 56.4 ms | **38.7%** |\n| `case never project` | 7,222 | 59.6 ms | 37.2 ms | **37.7%** |\n| `case never must` | 5,581 | 47.2 ms | 29.9 ms | **36.6%** |\n| `case never convent` | 4,016 | 34.6 ms | 22.2 ms | **36.0%** |\n| `case never requir` | 1,875 | 19.1 ms | 13.0 ms | **31.8%** |\n\nRead that as the gain on the **FTS scoring component**. The full search\nalso pays six pgvector\ndistance computations per row, so end-to-end is smaller. Prior attempts\nto measure this locally\nwere meaningless: the local corpus's realistic multi-term queries match\nzero rows, and its only\nmatching single term returns 3,397 rows that all score the identical\n0.375.\n\n## What this does NOT do\n\nIt does not get to one render, and the code no longer claims it does.\n`similarity` names\n`fts_score` in both branches of its CASE, `score` inlines `similarity`,\nand both are output columns\nof the scored CTE — four live references remain, times the UNION'd\nreserved branch.\n\nGetting to 1 means projecting `similarity`/`vec_sim` once in an inner\nderived table, and it **does**\nneed a real optimisation barrier: with references still live, Postgres\nflattens a plain subquery and\nre-substitutes. That is a separate change to the hot path with its own\nplan-shape review. (An\nearlier note in this thread claimed no barrier was ever needed — true of\nthe factor in isolation,\nfalse of the statement it composes into.)\n\n## Not bit-identical\n\nThe two forms disagree by at most one ULP — measured `max |a - b| =\n5.55e-17` over every matching\nrow of that corpus, not the `0.000e+00` recorded earlier. The old zero\nwas measured where every\nmatching row scored the same single value, so it had no rank variety to\nexpose the difference.\n5.55e-17 is ~4 orders of magnitude below the tightest tolerance anything\nhere asserts (`1e-12`) and\nfar below any score gap that could reorder results.\n\n## Tests\n\nTwo levels, because the factor being clean is not the same as the query\nbeing clean:\n\n- the saturating factor in isolation renders `ts_rank_cd` once, plus a\ncontrol proving the naive\nform really does render twice (so the count-of-one assertion can't pass\nvacuously);\n- **the assembled statement's render counts**, captured on the way to\n`session.execute` and\ncompiled — no DB. This is what catches someone adding a fifth reference\nto `fts_score`. Reverting\n  the fix fails it with `renders ts_rank_cd 18 times, expected 9`.\n\n`_rank_and_score` in the integration suite carried its own copy of the\nold expression while its\ndocstring claimed to use the production one; it now matches.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `core-storage-api/tests/`: **114 passed** (105 on `origin/main` + 9\nnew)\n- `tests/`: **4183 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed** — identical to the\n`a5a55f2` baseline\n- mypy clean on both projects; ruff check/format clean at all four CI\nscopes\n- `plugin/tools.json` and the broker OpenAPI regenerate byte-identical\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-10T09:13:28Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b853a978e3d9455973298663e84054fd8206e29e/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7"}]},{"sha":"1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDEwNTNhMmJiZTY2NmY3ODdjYTgyNTI0YjdiNjFjNThmZDg4ODYyYjc","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-10T09:01:46Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-10T09:01:46Z"},"message":"chore(deps): stop Dependabot widening uv requirements it doesn't need to (#740)\n\nSets `versioning-strategy: increase-if-necessary` on the **uv entry\nonly**, so a requirement in `pyproject.toml` is touched only when the\nnew version actually falls outside it.\n\n## Eight open PRs were doing nothing else\n\nThe default (`increase`) raises the floor on every release even when the\nrange already permits the new version:\n\n```\nuvicorn[standard]  >=0.51.0,<1  →  >=0.52.0,<1     for a version >=0.51.0,<1 already allowed\n```\n\n…once per service directory (#692, #693, #694, #695), plus #645, #646,\n#650, #691. **The `uv.lock` in all four directories was already being\nupdated correctly** — the manifest edit on top was pure churn.\n\nGrouping can't absorb them: `update-types` classifies the semver bump of\na *resolved version*, and a floor-raise on an already-satisfied range\nhas no such classification. That's precisely why `uv-minor-patch`\ncollapses the lock updates into one PR (#703, *\"across 4 directories\"*)\nwhile these arrive one at a time.\n\n## Why this setting is right here and wrong elsewhere\n\nThis is the same option I **rejected** for `caura-ops` two days ago, and\nthe distinction is the whole point:\n\n| | this repo's `uv` entry | `caura-ops` |\n|---|---|---|\n| requirement shape | **capped** — 22 of 30 in `core-api` have an upper\nbound | open-ended `>=X`, no cap |\n| is \"outside the range\" reachable? | **yes** — uvicorn 1.0 vs `<1`\nstill files a PR | never |\n| effect of this setting | de-duplicates churn | would have **silenced\nPython updates entirely** |\n| correct fix | this | commit a lock + switch ecosystem (done\n2026-08-09) |\n\n## Deliberately scoped\n\nThe **`pip` entry in this same file is untouched.** It has mixed\nopen-ended and capped requirements and no committed root lock, so it\nneeds the `caura-ops` treatment and would be actively harmed by this\nsetting. The file comment says exactly that, placed where someone would\nbe tempted to copy the line.\n\nVerified the value is valid against the schema and that it landed on the\n`uv` entry alone — `pip`, both `npm` entries, `github-actions` and\n`docker` all remain on the default.\n\n## Honest limit, and a cheap test\n\n**I could not confirm `increase-if-necessary`'s precise semantics from\nthe docs** — that section of the options reference truncated on three\nseparate fetches. This rests on the option name plus the observed\ncapped-vs-open behaviour across these eight PRs.\n\nIt's falsifiable next Monday: the scheduled run should produce\n**lock-only PRs with no `update X requirement from >=A to >=B` titles**.\nIf widenings still appear, my reading is wrong and this is a one-line\nrevert. I'd rather ship it with the test stated than assert it works.\n\n## What to expect\n\nThe eight open widening PRs become obsolete. Dependabot may supersede\nthem on the next run; if not, they can be closed. The remaining five\n`caura-memclaw` widenings (#633–#637) come from the `pip` entry and need\nthe separate lockfile change.\n\n@claude\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"40fd91513a7a990e9cfd63ddde2198405954b758","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/40fd91513a7a990e9cfd63ddde2198405954b758"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqeZN6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAACA4QACDSKOgf3ugz1pWYxxFKboX5\nRySDvA5fyR+6xTFGyoSDQnOXAmpjLoAllrf/XG90gGg5IlQWdeY8S+rxMJZ9+uAu\nRCvHJIsX9kSqbCedUMGP7nY97U7RA5xRtw77CuBUf/fUGmtfQauEroR4YfBR2mRL\nP+InVTUfFTfRRC2I6fjVPp1hx5sTYg0huR4hDH4ST7auEABho+2eQ5plRA5NCNIr\n+gTtpA05JiU4mdslQ6SFMq28N7f9IVimy0iCSnLoigWa8p1Y/hJIIfi0nFqUlbkK\nOdx9nrw+ZwO4qxkIsITXII9OQ3160N44/HQte+CK9r6qitXoFMUvtCE1wULYOegu\nuU3Jk9/pubdMCAoOm9QZsvMX1AmlGf5JsGSjl7rVS6YbIpZiFD5E7SWdoTOHX5pd\n0y8ejARfXwDQ+x0R7SUa4a73dRntiob9tBzRrquchdjhOPeyR+V+jnfIyG8MAa6r\nerbG2AirfxFcbsN7WXmWATFEyCrrhGytt0PZ/MNL0w+6fuqB9yn0z6EnTgkweHRS\nYMO1VwLloQ9a3yl2fR/8FLOZztFYSOTYEDLoYmvIKiG1UrIWyTFUe0BKnn4ZeTTC\nC/Hr5PerryHdzcLk/4FIvNEKAofX27rqY2KNCu9DYnSl7Mu2kRCBmH10UkP0/rI7\naE6QOO3GOfO9zV+1TEOx\n=Ivqn\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 40fd91513a7a990e9cfd63ddde2198405954b758\nparent a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786352506 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786352506 +0300\n\nchore(deps): stop Dependabot widening uv requirements it doesn't need to (#740)\n\nSets `versioning-strategy: increase-if-necessary` on the **uv entry\nonly**, so a requirement in `pyproject.toml` is touched only when the\nnew version actually falls outside it.\n\n## Eight open PRs were doing nothing else\n\nThe default (`increase`) raises the floor on every release even when the\nrange already permits the new version:\n\n```\nuvicorn[standard]  >=0.51.0,<1  →  >=0.52.0,<1     for a version >=0.51.0,<1 already allowed\n```\n\n…once per service directory (#692, #693, #694, #695), plus #645, #646,\n#650, #691. **The `uv.lock` in all four directories was already being\nupdated correctly** — the manifest edit on top was pure churn.\n\nGrouping can't absorb them: `update-types` classifies the semver bump of\na *resolved version*, and a floor-raise on an already-satisfied range\nhas no such classification. That's precisely why `uv-minor-patch`\ncollapses the lock updates into one PR (#703, *\"across 4 directories\"*)\nwhile these arrive one at a time.\n\n## Why this setting is right here and wrong elsewhere\n\nThis is the same option I **rejected** for `caura-ops` two days ago, and\nthe distinction is the whole point:\n\n| | this repo's `uv` entry | `caura-ops` |\n|---|---|---|\n| requirement shape | **capped** — 22 of 30 in `core-api` have an upper\nbound | open-ended `>=X`, no cap |\n| is \"outside the range\" reachable? | **yes** — uvicorn 1.0 vs `<1`\nstill files a PR | never |\n| effect of this setting | de-duplicates churn | would have **silenced\nPython updates entirely** |\n| correct fix | this | commit a lock + switch ecosystem (done\n2026-08-09) |\n\n## Deliberately scoped\n\nThe **`pip` entry in this same file is untouched.** It has mixed\nopen-ended and capped requirements and no committed root lock, so it\nneeds the `caura-ops` treatment and would be actively harmed by this\nsetting. The file comment says exactly that, placed where someone would\nbe tempted to copy the line.\n\nVerified the value is valid against the schema and that it landed on the\n`uv` entry alone — `pip`, both `npm` entries, `github-actions` and\n`docker` all remain on the default.\n\n## Honest limit, and a cheap test\n\n**I could not confirm `increase-if-necessary`'s precise semantics from\nthe docs** — that section of the options reference truncated on three\nseparate fetches. This rests on the option name plus the observed\ncapped-vs-open behaviour across these eight PRs.\n\nIt's falsifiable next Monday: the scheduled run should produce\n**lock-only PRs with no `update X requirement from >=A to >=B` titles**.\nIf widenings still appear, my reading is wrong and this is a one-line\nrevert. I'd rather ship it with the test stated than assert it works.\n\n## What to expect\n\nThe eight open widening PRs become obsolete. Dependabot may supersede\nthem on the next run; if not, they can be closed. The remaining five\n`caura-memclaw` widenings (#633–#637) come from the `pip` entry and need\nthe separate lockfile change.\n\n@claude\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-10T09:01:49Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/1053a2bbe666f787ca82524b7b61c58fd88862b7/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b"}]},{"sha":"a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGE1YTU1ZjJlNTI5YzZkMWJlZjY4Mjc2NWEzZjY2OWU5YzIwYzM4OGI","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-09T15:31:30Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-09T15:31:30Z"},"message":"refactor(search): derive SearchProfileUpdate from the knob table (#736)\n\nItem #2 of the post-thread list: close the last registration point for a\nsearch knob.\n\n## Why\n\n`SearchProfileUpdate` wrote out its nine fields and their `ge`/`le` by\nhand, duplicating `SEARCH_KNOBS`. That duplication had already produced\na real defect — `graph_max_hops` capped at 3 here while the table\nallowed 5, so a tenant-wide `default_profile` could hold a graph depth\nno per-agent profile could set. #730 settled the value; this removes the\nmechanism that produced it.\n\n## What\n\nThe model is built by `create_model` over the table, selected by a new\n**`agent_tunable`** flag on `SearchKnob`. `AGENT_TUNABLE_KEYS` derives\nfrom it exactly as the two SQL tuples already do.\n\nThat flag also records, in the one place it matters, **why the split is\n9 of 12**: `fts_rank_scale`, `candidate_pool_size` and `score_formula`\nare tenant-level A/B levers held at their global defaults, not per-agent\ntuning. Previously that was an undocumented omission a reader could\nplausibly \"fix\".\n\n## What stays hand-written, and why\n\n`memclaw_tune`'s MCP signature. It declares its knobs as **named\nparameters**, and that signature *is* the tool schema shipped to clients\nin `plugin/tools.json` — `create_model` cannot express it. So it becomes\nthe last copy, and a test pins it: both the parameter set **and the\nhuman-readable ranges in each description**, because a bound that moves\nin the table while the description still advertises the old one is a\nsilently wrong instruction to an agent, not merely stale prose.\n\nThe previous bounds test would be tautological against a derived model,\nso it is **retargeted rather than kept**: one cheap assertion that the\nderivation still yields a usable model (a botched comprehension would\nproduce the wrong field set or drop constraints entirely), and the real\nguard moved onto the MCP signature.\n\n## Verification\n\nMutation-verified in both directions:\n\n```\nwiden graph_max_hops in the table →\n  memclaw_tune documents graph_max_hops as '0-3.' but the knob table says (0, 5)\ndrop agent_tunable from top_k →\n  memclaw_tune exposes ['top_k'] differently from the knob table\n```\n\n`tests/` **4183**, `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, ruff clean at every CI\nscope. **`plugin/tools.json` and the broker OpenAPI both regenerate\nbyte-identical** — the derivation reproduces the previous model exactly,\nso no client-visible change.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"e83ee7e457f399bbc6dcc692f6f61be6403ae798","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/e83ee7e457f399bbc6dcc692f6f61be6403ae798"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqeJ1SCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsIMQAHNWXxvZFC10F2iJxABI4a5G\nXOII24Ch4AT+FVF7o9Zj77qI6A87rXDOJG7rhoWtuTtiI2poudGehM7yKaGWPjGE\njtca+ZdAbsAY8mAzcK+5HNMRvfjWNGpdUioRkT58SmaxFg+MVh//OxW7N9/UcHq9\ncRcV1jjtg/3CtfF84eiWIw1vh6pCl/AiJMKnt4Aj6U2X+nflERQfDGLN6SBCq67S\nrat809T0UH3YPb1brPLoYt9AGzIXraZWkEOILteWptJ1n842AdiA1VPjtHKMbZOl\nafC0RTNLlxCr2/sstLLl8aytq4UmFsB0QDefYYM/sTgOC5krsy91AEp7ZvFU0J2x\nablqA9ZBC1AcoQ/yCNdTicPeINtpRrantHiqkqB3UMNwZz+ekV687y3/z3m/M9nH\nGjwC6SzqohAMuWzor4/YJvDVxDyrWI/hQ47juTQpfxpYGIfDRyFMaiPGumj5Umvt\nOZhW6fGymushXEETiriO3NZpcgG6hbsbeKHSk1y8hCzlYJ6reQNyOK1m5ViixUkO\nB8oLJ8S7Y6P9f3kmH4ZGWv1igVD2oS5rUtm3bcPE2BrxMB25l/r8s51DJvL+nxzJ\nBFXl7K2Xhy+/xvwf14GSPmGS6+R8aVNOS7gyFvimwx5Qx8cB6mUfC43iRWxY5v51\nCHOQUPfkZs8i2JWMzUom\n=qBj8\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree e83ee7e457f399bbc6dcc692f6f61be6403ae798\nparent e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786289490 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786289490 +0300\n\nrefactor(search): derive SearchProfileUpdate from the knob table (#736)\n\nItem #2 of the post-thread list: close the last registration point for a\nsearch knob.\n\n## Why\n\n`SearchProfileUpdate` wrote out its nine fields and their `ge`/`le` by\nhand, duplicating `SEARCH_KNOBS`. That duplication had already produced\na real defect — `graph_max_hops` capped at 3 here while the table\nallowed 5, so a tenant-wide `default_profile` could hold a graph depth\nno per-agent profile could set. #730 settled the value; this removes the\nmechanism that produced it.\n\n## What\n\nThe model is built by `create_model` over the table, selected by a new\n**`agent_tunable`** flag on `SearchKnob`. `AGENT_TUNABLE_KEYS` derives\nfrom it exactly as the two SQL tuples already do.\n\nThat flag also records, in the one place it matters, **why the split is\n9 of 12**: `fts_rank_scale`, `candidate_pool_size` and `score_formula`\nare tenant-level A/B levers held at their global defaults, not per-agent\ntuning. Previously that was an undocumented omission a reader could\nplausibly \"fix\".\n\n## What stays hand-written, and why\n\n`memclaw_tune`'s MCP signature. It declares its knobs as **named\nparameters**, and that signature *is* the tool schema shipped to clients\nin `plugin/tools.json` — `create_model` cannot express it. So it becomes\nthe last copy, and a test pins it: both the parameter set **and the\nhuman-readable ranges in each description**, because a bound that moves\nin the table while the description still advertises the old one is a\nsilently wrong instruction to an agent, not merely stale prose.\n\nThe previous bounds test would be tautological against a derived model,\nso it is **retargeted rather than kept**: one cheap assertion that the\nderivation still yields a usable model (a botched comprehension would\nproduce the wrong field set or drop constraints entirely), and the real\nguard moved onto the MCP signature.\n\n## Verification\n\nMutation-verified in both directions:\n\n```\nwiden graph_max_hops in the table →\n  memclaw_tune documents graph_max_hops as '0-3.' but the knob table says (0, 5)\ndrop agent_tunable from top_k →\n  memclaw_tune exposes ['top_k'] differently from the knob table\n```\n\n`tests/` **4183**, `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, ruff clean at every CI\nscope. **`plugin/tools.json` and the broker OpenAPI both regenerate\nbyte-identical** — the derivation reproduces the previous model exactly,\nso no client-visible change.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-09T15:31:32Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a5a55f2e529c6d1bef682765a3f669e9c20c388b/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34"}]},{"sha":"e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGU3OGNkMjkzYjViYTU3MmM3Y2VhM2IwZWZhYzQ5ZDZhODA4ZDhiMzQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-09T12:22:58Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-09T12:22:58Z"},"message":"fix(search): ship the 034 backfill inside the image (#734)\n\n> Prerequisite for running the 034 backfill as a Cloud Run job. Found\nwhile setting that job up.\n\n## The problem\n\n`scripts/backfill_034_search_vector.py` landed in the repo's top-level\n`scripts/`, which **the storage Dockerfile does not copy** — it takes\n`common/` and `core-storage-api/src/` only:\n\n```\nCOPY common/ /app/common/\nCOPY core-storage-api/src/ /app/core-storage-api/src/\n```\n\nSo a Cloud Run job on the deployed image cannot reach it, and that job\nis the one way this is meant to run against staging and prod.\n\n## The fix\n\nMoved to `core_storage_api.scripts.backfill_034_search_vector`, next to\nthe existing `backfill_embeddings.py` and `preflight_012.py` — that\npackage already exists for exactly this, and both siblings are `python\n-m`-invocable one-offs.\n\nAlso switched from a `--dsn` argument to `get_engine()`, the way those\nsiblings do. A job then **inherits the VPC connector, service account\nand secret bindings of the deployment it runs in**, rather than needing\na DSN handed to it — which matters because AlloyDB is only reachable\nover that connector.\n\n```bash\ngcloud run jobs create staging-backfill-034 \\\n  --image <deployed core-storage-api image> \\\n  --command python \\\n  --args -m,core_storage_api.scripts.backfill_034_search_vector \\\n  --vpc-connector <same as service> --service-account <same> --set-secrets <same>\ngcloud run jobs execute staging-backfill-034 --wait\n```\n\n## Unchanged\n\nWindow bounds from the PK index with no predicate; the predicate applied\ninside the window on the write; `--from-id` to resume; `--dry-run` as\nthe only whole-table count; `--revert` for after a downgrade.\n\n## Verification\n\nExercised **as a job would invoke it** — `python -m\ncore_storage_api.scripts.backfill_034_search_vector` against a real\ndatabase, round-tripping forward (966 rows) → `--revert` → forward → `0\nneed rebuilding`.\n\n`tests/` 4182, `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, ruff clean at every CI\nscope — note the module is now under `core-storage-api/src/`, so unlike\nits previous home it is both `check`- and `format`-gated.\n\nThe byte-identical drift guard follows it to the new path. It survived\n`ruff format` rewrapping the file, which would have broken its regex\nsilently had the test not asserted the match itself — the same class of\ntrap as a string-replace mutation that quietly stops applying.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"0d6d9ca600644cf78c208a72de97fd956b0e1074","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/0d6d9ca600644cf78c208a72de97fd956b0e1074"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqeHEjCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAkaMQAF9clO2+BFDRID/012jJmWXq\nJ7IkDXoyFk75pSAD4hh/ZufX5TZXHHJBZDj64axv6UeeW0vT0d287eNZBbzIkBKZ\nhyQB4zUHtkJTQ6f5fXZ6oNVNMPc5ecmvxt95Y1lxrwPuFLHmu4x8qWfWjFR+ZRgM\nakrLccKknnG3s+oGDMDCUaSRlo64ITZf5Cy9pp1tndtHlI1O0ecBponSL3NlRrkP\nU7/nYatQyTwsnEFBl9U0n2Xrr61JIOdICXdRLpiGXiw21kXerjRDMcj0CJ56m2ww\n7iaNHlbXcldfjse5wKq1easgLXOGSvLggjIBLs9QlEhNECJuQyUJzL2/E03VUEIY\npz+ZVgnNA6kpb+aY4S/2IP9rqMEJ+HiBJqpESJKoqoG0osXHsfiPyrigmhvOJcC3\n2QPtUs9w1behcWxWMpbRIWquNoNcWnQMi85fhDBZEL9u7G9FYWM2Jv4S0tol+4jl\n9U4wBV+CNa5uQyVXBePEaepiPINliUUOv1h96r3kcAhG0/HyW5UU/iHGNPQsm2nO\nlNukHFQI1NRIQaG7xYiALsDGcifbmnzoh0l9mSh/ZIBE4Z2+vz75jEY9cDY9goVr\nQ1b/JJaNCk5BrckIaMvLYo9C0AUunFf8VDtKIywMIPtl6ePwfqw0LEmszm5ewq4H\njYVcVSBePnkdFMOehG53\n=kxp2\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 0d6d9ca600644cf78c208a72de97fd956b0e1074\nparent 6ab81585fd52de7c6e6befb6edeb7189c46fb46b\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786278178 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786278178 +0300\n\nfix(search): ship the 034 backfill inside the image (#734)\n\n> Prerequisite for running the 034 backfill as a Cloud Run job. Found\nwhile setting that job up.\n\n## The problem\n\n`scripts/backfill_034_search_vector.py` landed in the repo's top-level\n`scripts/`, which **the storage Dockerfile does not copy** — it takes\n`common/` and `core-storage-api/src/` only:\n\n```\nCOPY common/ /app/common/\nCOPY core-storage-api/src/ /app/core-storage-api/src/\n```\n\nSo a Cloud Run job on the deployed image cannot reach it, and that job\nis the one way this is meant to run against staging and prod.\n\n## The fix\n\nMoved to `core_storage_api.scripts.backfill_034_search_vector`, next to\nthe existing `backfill_embeddings.py` and `preflight_012.py` — that\npackage already exists for exactly this, and both siblings are `python\n-m`-invocable one-offs.\n\nAlso switched from a `--dsn` argument to `get_engine()`, the way those\nsiblings do. A job then **inherits the VPC connector, service account\nand secret bindings of the deployment it runs in**, rather than needing\na DSN handed to it — which matters because AlloyDB is only reachable\nover that connector.\n\n```bash\ngcloud run jobs create staging-backfill-034 \\\n  --image <deployed core-storage-api image> \\\n  --command python \\\n  --args -m,core_storage_api.scripts.backfill_034_search_vector \\\n  --vpc-connector <same as service> --service-account <same> --set-secrets <same>\ngcloud run jobs execute staging-backfill-034 --wait\n```\n\n## Unchanged\n\nWindow bounds from the PK index with no predicate; the predicate applied\ninside the window on the write; `--from-id` to resume; `--dry-run` as\nthe only whole-table count; `--revert` for after a downgrade.\n\n## Verification\n\nExercised **as a job would invoke it** — `python -m\ncore_storage_api.scripts.backfill_034_search_vector` against a real\ndatabase, round-tripping forward (966 rows) → `--revert` → forward → `0\nneed rebuilding`.\n\n`tests/` 4182, `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, ruff clean at every CI\nscope — note the module is now under `core-storage-api/src/`, so unlike\nits previous home it is both `check`- and `format`-gated.\n\nThe byte-identical drift guard follows it to the new path. It survived\n`ruff format` rewrapping the file, which would have broken its regex\nsilently had the test not asserted the match itself — the same class of\ntrap as a string-replace mutation that quietly stops applying.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-09T12:23:01Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e78cd293b5ba572c7cea3b0efac49d6a808d8b34/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.