ould not load (#735)","tree":{"sha":"96f00d1e4f2089fc1728ceb4d6b72a37531c14ac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/96f00d1e4f2089fc1728ceb4d6b72a37531c14ac"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/6ab81585fd52de7c6e6befb6edeb7189c46fb46b","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqeGeyCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0S8QAEp1v4urvqpwSGoXqa662af8\nicnMaxrzWI9Jx5gPHZYXTI5C3/ibmhOjYpIHNY+DQ8BqFGxHzSQO5w6b2ASHrh0a\ngiQlAlUlQXXKIvnNc1j/9q4/FVtMtFhH1EsbtX8Qbe0pvFWuK3axbfLDnk9tnG8z\nwNTjDj570iMt78hrDUqSA4tYBYMhwwsiyDiOeYnUIxA9hzjBpa4/0yMQbvoQ8Lay\nNOmff9TZfNgoOxqHlGM/aXAYQTk9ogdZHSIh2EdFYd284Hjtf5HwhnnZ1kjO2AWR\nRFbYFVeo6QAY/RNWowFeF6FZhWO/oaNedK+tz7iY+rE/D1EzZ4kco2THp6VXIoRB\nIgahtk66+DJJ/Qx6/wAIkeBjFbNWfFJEwAv0cZhJDv2onXNCdSbH1XLX5Ty+EIMZ\nVY1xkoZd7FlkZyjYTPkp8gnDS2e1u7dceuSkCAsPfnsfDMV9gqT4Dm9reHQ6eebf\nwT57yLLVavjNUtZ0i3lY3yQNf1EF12C0lNTbhpqNtZH9/f+pWW105nWbf7y3smq3\nEgoZDc8AoAYqcpONehT5AGfvHH6W8LEpTUdRs+0BagKFWCrbf2jVUIXWcUr9Ro1j\nZARcv/Q/SVpTauWUsv2wj1kaK76M+fCGiiaDG1Mvi+3DKEnqZ1DS+IY1WNWqQy5r\nzS9d3RBV+llWHo2x/hrM\n=yrgM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 96f00d1e4f2089fc1728ceb4d6b72a37531c14ac\nparent a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786275762 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786275762 +0300\n\nci: restore the local review workflow, modernised, after the shared pipeline could not load (#735)","verified_at":"2026-08-09T11:42:44Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6ab81585fd52de7c6e6befb6edeb7189c46fb46b","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/6ab81585fd52de7c6e6befb6edeb7189c46fb46b","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6ab81585fd52de7c6e6befb6edeb7189c46fb46b/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2"}]},{"sha":"a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGE2Yzg2YjJlOTViYmQxMzI2OWUyOThlZDUwZGY3YzMxNDY0MGJhZTI","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-08T21:15:47Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-08T21:15:47Z"},"message":"ci: adopt the org's shared review pipeline (#733)\n\nReplaces this repo's **297-line local copy** of the review pipeline with\na **70-line caller**. The skip rules, the org-membership gate, the\nreviewer scripts and the prompt now come from `caura-ai/.github`, which\nevery other repo in the org already uses. This was the last repo still\ncarrying its own copy.\n\n## Nothing bespoke had to be preserved\n\nVerified byte-identical to the shared defaults before switching:\n\n| local | shared default |\n|---|---|\n| `MAX_FILES: 30` | `max-files: 30` |\n| source-file `jq` filter | **byte-for-byte** the org default |\n| `@anthropic-ai/claude-code@2.1.159` | `cli-version` default |\n| org-membership gate | `author-gate: auto` → on for public repos |\n\nThe local `REVIEW_PROMPT` was an older copy of the shared template, so\ninheriting the current one is an upgrade rather than a compromise.\n\n## What this fixes\n\nThe local file-count call omitted `per_page`:\n\n```\nFILE_COUNT=$(gh api \"repos/${REPO}/pulls/${PR_NUMBER}/files\" --jq 'length')\n```\n\nGitHub's `/files` endpoint **defaults to 30**, so `FILE_COUNT` could\nnever exceed 30 and the `-gt 30` oversized-PR guard **has never once\nfired here**. The shared `pre-checks` passes `per_page=100`. This was a\nlive bug in all seven copies of the pipeline — this is the last one.\n\n## What this gains\n\n- **`claude-capture`** — findings a maintainer declined become review\nmemory on merge\n- **`/remember`** — record a curated note for future reviews\n- **bot-PR skip** for dependabot and renovate\n- the **maintained prompt** — the local copy still carried a `Fix All\nPrompts` section removed upstream in v1.3.0, which duplicated every\nper-issue prompt and cost 20–25% of each comment\n\n## `id-token: write`\n\nGranted even though this repo does **not** run the Gemini reviewer.\nGitHub validates a reusable workflow's permissions graph **statically at\nload time**, regardless of any callee job's `if:` — so withholding it\nmakes GitHub refuse to load the *entire* workflow, a `startup_failure`\nthat would take the Claude review down too. Harmless here: nothing\nrequests the token, and `mode=gemini-review` is refused outright on a\npublic repository.\n\n## Unchanged\n\nThe author gate behaves exactly as before — `auto` derives from\nrepository visibility, this repo is public, so PRs from anyone who isn't\na **public** org member are skipped. Note the long-standing operational\ncost, which has bitten this repo: `GITHUB_TOKEN` resolves only *public*\nmembers, so a member whose org membership is private is skipped silently\nbehind a green check.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"91cd868d033121c6a84cec00cec9fad5d489c60a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/91cd868d033121c6a84cec00cec9fad5d489c60a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqd5yDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAKFkQAC/TMxzXrpMonom2A7+W8j2f\nAy8AQUOndKQWAZFJPIoD1n7VFgn6uoCYbJyMlBvJ3zlh3+C3bdXaCoM7ZHrrCzhn\nLKOuXRc6PoMxCJP9OTYhz+3nekPo5LusQdly5WnHT8cgCQYps61nsRvhuDaZbc/g\nD1FdcJeUD5B76vPtDSaZSjYgUwMJJxxdUTipKGE15/+nI8n/a9qhqLeCwl5yJrJR\nbOtbWwMcpPSEJqRZuKg0ipMRCBP1dUy30fWe08Db+L3zFIaNWwis3Pk0+c/byXOf\nj7yo2fk+s6Cwc0l3e3AgVb8/yrj17FXpkMcoJjccgz8gaI+vppNaxQf9wSXt4aed\nTnJ1JsWjlS8UvAb5dcYSSzIluU7KoHeXvx0h9TjiEMBuGeYoKr2JQe87lelf1vjp\ngWFbJaLacfpZWTk/nPf7uHSLT2i5yok7c7+Jcrj5YXho1q1hYVy54+IKSM+gbBvb\n6upGMc4M5+00b48h/VBMyywPoTi9ZC+4AxKbPlj+o9zTyoaKQPt/Py1j/VICmRyT\n9uaPVDAZpbdleI2eP33r8b9/+NY4oYZ1/NYi9yw7bOmAiZVCzI/cQ7k1g9DDjIpT\n/Kp1UJFNH+g5AhKOL8vPTwl83Nd44N939VHqy2GaGYzeDgCSIvUgzyi9viUMbGQO\nE9+RdK2sA89hD0fRxZLI\n=+SaC\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 91cd868d033121c6a84cec00cec9fad5d489c60a\nparent 9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786223747 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786223747 +0300\n\nci: adopt the org's shared review pipeline (#733)\n\nReplaces this repo's **297-line local copy** of the review pipeline with\na **70-line caller**. The skip rules, the org-membership gate, the\nreviewer scripts and the prompt now come from `caura-ai/.github`, which\nevery other repo in the org already uses. This was the last repo still\ncarrying its own copy.\n\n## Nothing bespoke had to be preserved\n\nVerified byte-identical to the shared defaults before switching:\n\n| local | shared default |\n|---|---|\n| `MAX_FILES: 30` | `max-files: 30` |\n| source-file `jq` filter | **byte-for-byte** the org default |\n| `@anthropic-ai/claude-code@2.1.159` | `cli-version` default |\n| org-membership gate | `author-gate: auto` → on for public repos |\n\nThe local `REVIEW_PROMPT` was an older copy of the shared template, so\ninheriting the current one is an upgrade rather than a compromise.\n\n## What this fixes\n\nThe local file-count call omitted `per_page`:\n\n```\nFILE_COUNT=$(gh api \"repos/${REPO}/pulls/${PR_NUMBER}/files\" --jq 'length')\n```\n\nGitHub's `/files` endpoint **defaults to 30**, so `FILE_COUNT` could\nnever exceed 30 and the `-gt 30` oversized-PR guard **has never once\nfired here**. The shared `pre-checks` passes `per_page=100`. This was a\nlive bug in all seven copies of the pipeline — this is the last one.\n\n## What this gains\n\n- **`claude-capture`** — findings a maintainer declined become review\nmemory on merge\n- **`/remember`** — record a curated note for future reviews\n- **bot-PR skip** for dependabot and renovate\n- the **maintained prompt** — the local copy still carried a `Fix All\nPrompts` section removed upstream in v1.3.0, which duplicated every\nper-issue prompt and cost 20–25% of each comment\n\n## `id-token: write`\n\nGranted even though this repo does **not** run the Gemini reviewer.\nGitHub validates a reusable workflow's permissions graph **statically at\nload time**, regardless of any callee job's `if:` — so withholding it\nmakes GitHub refuse to load the *entire* workflow, a `startup_failure`\nthat would take the Claude review down too. Harmless here: nothing\nrequests the token, and `mode=gemini-review` is refused outright on a\npublic repository.\n\n## Unchanged\n\nThe author gate behaves exactly as before — `auto` derives from\nrepository visibility, this repo is public, so PRs from anyone who isn't\na **public** org member are skipped. Note the long-standing operational\ncost, which has bitten this repo: `GITHUB_TOKEN` resolves only *public*\nmembers, so a member whose org membership is private is skipped silently\nbehind a green check.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-08T21:15:49Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a6c86b2e95bbd13269e298ed50df7c314640bae2/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee"}]},{"sha":"9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDlmZTQ1NTdjMGIxMzhjMjE0OTM2YjVkNTMyYjNmNDM1MDQ1NmQ0ZWU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-08T20:56:29Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-08T20:56:29Z"},"message":"fix(search): move 034's backfill out of the startup path (#732)\n\n> **Fixes a blocked staging deploy caused by #731.** Staging was never\ndown — Cloud Run kept the previous revision serving — but the new\nstorage revision could not become healthy, so nothing rolled forward.\n\n## What happened\n\nMigration 034 backfilled `memories.search_vector` inside the alembic\nstartup hook. Every instance boots, serialises on the migration advisory\nlock, and Cloud Run's startup probe kills the container after **240 s**:\n\n```\n09:30:46  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …\n09:34:37  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …\n09:39:16  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …\n09:43:06  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …\n09:47:16  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …          ← never completes\nERROR: Container failed to become healthy. Startup probes timed out after 4m\n```\n\n**Committed batches were supposed to make this converge, and they\ndon't.** The keyset cursor lives in process memory, so every restart\nresumes from the top and re-evaluates the `IS DISTINCT FROM` predicate —\none `to_tsvector` per row — across an ever-growing prefix of rows it has\nalready rebuilt. The quadratic cost I removed from *within* a run came\nback *across* restarts, which is strictly worse: within a run it was\nmerely slow; across restarts it cannot finish.\n\nThat is the same failure mode 034's own docstring described and claimed\nto prevent. The batching addressed lock duration, which was never the\nbinding constraint — wall-clock against a fixed probe was.\n\n## The fix\n\n**034 is now DDL only — 0.28 s.** The backfill moves to\n`scripts/backfill_034_search_vector.py`, which is what the house\nconvention said in the first place: DDL in the migration, non-DDL\nbackfills in one-off scripts.\n\nThe script takes its window bounds from the **primary key index alone,\nwith no predicate**, so choosing the next slice is an index scan rather\nthan a scan that re-evaluates `to_tsvector` over rows already handled.\nThe predicate then applies *inside* the window, so untitled rows — whose\nvector 034 does not change — cost a comparison and no write. Resumable\nvia `--from-id`, prints its cursor, idempotent.\n\nVerified locally: 924 rows across 74 windows in **0.78 s**; a re-run\nreports `0 need rebuilding`.\n\n## Why a partial backfill is safe\n\nThis is what makes out-of-band viable at all, and it falls directly out\nof the equal-weight decision on #731: a row not yet rebuilt keeps its\ncontent-only vector and **scores exactly as it always did**, because\ncontent lexemes and their weight are identical either side of 034. It\nhas simply not gained title search yet. There is no window in which two\nrows are scored on different scales — which would not have been true of\nthe `setweight` A/B version.\n\n## Deploy sequence\n\n1. Merge → storage deploys, 034 applies instantly, new and updated rows\nindex their titles from the trigger.\n2. Run the script once against staging, then prod, when convenient.\n\nStaging's DB is already in exactly this state: the trigger applied (it\ncommits before the backfill), the backfill partial, alembic still at 033\nbecause the migration never completed. Re-running 034 is idempotent —\n`CREATE OR REPLACE` / `DROP … IF EXISTS`.\n\n## Verification\n\n`tests/` **4181**, `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, ruff clean at every CI\nscope, `--select F821` over `scripts/` clean.\n\nThe tsvector expression now exists in two places, so a test asserts they\nare **byte-identical** — a divergence would have the script write\nvectors the trigger would never produce, invisible until a search\nmisses. Mutation-verified: changing `coalesce(m.title, '')` to\n`coalesce(m.title, ' ')` in the script fails it with both expressions\nprinted.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"0091319bce9b3c6366c841445344b591bb233754","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/0091319bce9b3c6366c841445344b591bb233754"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqd5f9CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAARqUQACkMzQoJd4M19iKtzpmCwDVs\nf+CYrBkL8hYN6rM50S2HZUQ3bBHz34vyU1CKqpvox4rlLHByXoC1kOsx+cxpYrbT\nnrTybVgOwiw7vXqjm91wHJhT7OXhv6jUUzd//CBuGJ0KkJ9pM/FoAAE3/90vIsfW\nxtH3rRkGkuobVPKlb2jZmuyhJ3ozSQZurJT3HOCFTboyAoW8u/jcNLGBHZKlx88k\nMcLZ7qOUYGHA3cnpGAE9132CIGoZddMB0kLfB14i7PYwzMb5QwWXLjRnGzlGrIAD\nT7nmFDqIarR5qHj8VutczUB6PKV/1u9HnF34vGA2rLpthQOgrutivmB376eBvh3M\nr/jg8zQ0mow2Tdi1oS+S6VVI6Hjn0U7W0d+BfhZYwdYKV52OmaslQ5VgPwq37j2Z\nrHazxccLiA5Cjkxe/vClaT0liXNjZ7j561ETNKHiU4fgAhJb3FvhsEKclKb4t2g+\nxLDRlioGchWBjmnuB7eQ6NT5y0qyWXnpjJo6GWsAUxsCaykcX4SyqSvABth3ws4P\ny5pZAVIHnQc51lj3RlwNBLJVZew8hCZT4gfFSBxZ6JYxs49kSQVds9BpBAclgYMH\nZxSj3j9vzYzLK+iDNoHO6q6WC/s/Xe8RLBJ5jRR0sxBJrlXGJpaRdVL4Llr2b3zc\nhvihjpRj9AdN1ExFCMGD\n=bqvl\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 0091319bce9b3c6366c841445344b591bb233754\nparent 621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786222589 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786222589 +0300\n\nfix(search): move 034's backfill out of the startup path (#732)\n\n> **Fixes a blocked staging deploy caused by #731.** Staging was never\ndown — Cloud Run kept the previous revision serving — but the new\nstorage revision could not become healthy, so nothing rolled forward.\n\n## What happened\n\nMigration 034 backfilled `memories.search_vector` inside the alembic\nstartup hook. Every instance boots, serialises on the migration advisory\nlock, and Cloud Run's startup probe kills the container after **240 s**:\n\n```\n09:30:46  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …\n09:34:37  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …\n09:39:16  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …\n09:43:06  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …\n09:47:16  Running upgrade 033 -> 034 …          ← never completes\nERROR: Container failed to become healthy. Startup probes timed out after 4m\n```\n\n**Committed batches were supposed to make this converge, and they\ndon't.** The keyset cursor lives in process memory, so every restart\nresumes from the top and re-evaluates the `IS DISTINCT FROM` predicate —\none `to_tsvector` per row — across an ever-growing prefix of rows it has\nalready rebuilt. The quadratic cost I removed from *within* a run came\nback *across* restarts, which is strictly worse: within a run it was\nmerely slow; across restarts it cannot finish.\n\nThat is the same failure mode 034's own docstring described and claimed\nto prevent. The batching addressed lock duration, which was never the\nbinding constraint — wall-clock against a fixed probe was.\n\n## The fix\n\n**034 is now DDL only — 0.28 s.** The backfill moves to\n`scripts/backfill_034_search_vector.py`, which is what the house\nconvention said in the first place: DDL in the migration, non-DDL\nbackfills in one-off scripts.\n\nThe script takes its window bounds from the **primary key index alone,\nwith no predicate**, so choosing the next slice is an index scan rather\nthan a scan that re-evaluates `to_tsvector` over rows already handled.\nThe predicate then applies *inside* the window, so untitled rows — whose\nvector 034 does not change — cost a comparison and no write. Resumable\nvia `--from-id`, prints its cursor, idempotent.\n\nVerified locally: 924 rows across 74 windows in **0.78 s**; a re-run\nreports `0 need rebuilding`.\n\n## Why a partial backfill is safe\n\nThis is what makes out-of-band viable at all, and it falls directly out\nof the equal-weight decision on #731: a row not yet rebuilt keeps its\ncontent-only vector and **scores exactly as it always did**, because\ncontent lexemes and their weight are identical either side of 034. It\nhas simply not gained title search yet. There is no window in which two\nrows are scored on different scales — which would not have been true of\nthe `setweight` A/B version.\n\n## Deploy sequence\n\n1. Merge → storage deploys, 034 applies instantly, new and updated rows\nindex their titles from the trigger.\n2. Run the script once against staging, then prod, when convenient.\n\nStaging's DB is already in exactly this state: the trigger applied (it\ncommits before the backfill), the backfill partial, alembic still at 033\nbecause the migration never completed. Re-running 034 is idempotent —\n`CREATE OR REPLACE` / `DROP … IF EXISTS`.\n\n## Verification\n\n`tests/` **4181**, `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, ruff clean at every CI\nscope, `--select F821` over `scripts/` clean.\n\nThe tsvector expression now exists in two places, so a test asserts they\nare **byte-identical** — a divergence would have the script write\nvectors the trigger would never produce, invisible until a search\nmisses. Mutation-verified: changing `coalesce(m.title, '')` to\n`coalesce(m.title, ' ')` in the script fails it with both expressions\nprinted.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-08T20:56:32Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9fe4557c0b138c214936b5d532b3f4350456d4ee/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675"}]},{"sha":"621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDYyMWFmZWE3YzMyZTIwNDE3NWE4MDY5NGRlMmE1MzY0MTJjZTE2NzU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-08T09:15:47Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-08T09:15:47Z"},"message":"feat(search): index memories.title, at the same weight as content (#731)\n\n> Reworked from the `setweight` A/B split after Eldad's call: **same\nweight for title and content**. No calibration question remains, and the\ndiff shrank to one migration plus its tests.\n\n## The bug\n\n`memories.title` has **never been in the tsvector**. Migration 001\nbuilds `search_vector` from content alone, so a memory whose title\ncarries the distinguishing words — and enrichment writes a title on\n*every* enriched memory — cannot be found by FTS on those words at all.\n\n```\nquery \"pool sizing\", title \"Postgres connection pool sizing\",\nterms absent from content:      ts_rank_cd = 0.0000   ← unreachable, not ranked low\n```\n\n## What 034 does\n\n- tokenises title and content **together**, so every lexeme keeps the\nweight D it has always had\n- widens the trigger to **`UPDATE OF content, title`** — a title-only\nedit otherwise leaves a stale index, and that is the async-enrich path's\n*normal* case, not an edge one\n- backfills existing rows\n\n## Why one weight, not `setweight` A/B\n\nThe split multiplies every content rank by 4 (D=0.1 → B=0.4), which\nforces `FTS_RANK_SCALE` to be re-derived, and it moves the modal\n*enriched* row a long way — titles are LLM summaries that reuse the\ncontent's salient terms, so most rows match both fields and the weights\nadd:\n\n| | content-only | title-only | title-echo |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| today | 0.1000 | **0.0000** | 0.1000 |\n| title A / content B | 0.4000 | 1.0000 | 1.5429 |\n| **this migration** | **0.1000** | **0.1000** | 0.2250 |\n\nEqual weight keeps a content-only match at **exactly its historical\n0.1000**, so `FTS_RANK_SCALE` stays 6.0 and no bound, default or\ncalibration moves anywhere in the stack — that is why this touches three\nfiles instead of seven.\n\nThe only rows that score differently are ones that **could not be found\nat all** before, plus rows whose title repeats a content term (0.1 →\n0.225). That second effect isn't a field preference: it's the boost\n`ts_rank_cd` gives *any* repeated term, exactly as if the content had\nsaid the word twice.\n\nPreferring a title hit over a content hit would be a relevance claim,\nand nothing here can test it — `benchmark_blend_locomo.py`'s corpus is\nLoCoMo dialogue turns, which have no titles. So the benchmark this\nchange was expected to need cannot measure it, and the change is scoped\nto avoid needing it.\n\n## Backfill safety\n\nCommitted batches inside `autocommit_block`, the shape migration 012\nuses. A single-transaction rewrite would hold the startup advisory lock\npast Cloud Run's 240 s probe, get SIGKILLed and restart from zero — the\nnon-converging crash loop that took out six writer boots on 2026-06-16.\nResumable via the `IS DISTINCT FROM` predicate. Verified over 21k rows,\nround-tripped down and up, zero left unbackfilled.\n\nA `GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) STORED` column would make the stale-vector\nclass unrepresentable and produces a byte-identical vector — noted in\nthe migration, not done here, because converting needs DROP + ADD\nCOLUMN: a full table rewrite under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE plus a GIN rebuild.\nThe moment for it is a future migration already rewriting `memories`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/` **4180** vs a 4176 baseline on `origin/main` — exactly the 4\nnew tests. `core-storage-api/tests/` 105. Ruff clean at every CI scope.\n- Mutation-verified: **downgrading to 033** fails the title test with\n`scored 0.0` and the reindex test with `stale`.\n- Four tests: title is searchable at all; a title match scores **the\nsame** as a content match (this is what pins the no-preference\ndecision); content-only scoring is untouched at 0.1 raw / 0.375 scored;\na title-only UPDATE reindexes.\n- Three test sites that hand-wrote a content-only `search_vector` are\ndeleted in favour of the trigger — left alone they would have\noverwritten the title and silently un-tested the change.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"2cc86b07a483401b45965095d62bfaa0f54887ac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/2cc86b07a483401b45965095d62bfaa0f54887ac"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdvPDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAb0EQAKj2ciw8+16OYm/S6cXUjHWO\nGMje3gELVc2LFAbvPOxNJ8NY9et4z8MAfl93T/MyweWtj9yr+LN1KIQ5ZKCopQZ9\nc9sg0CHC5RWRVwmv2y/vuEzqIScJajr9GpEL5FivjDXfLqBSt3HN1uMZGZgMCy+r\nefLqgrxivy3qhR9Anr++ZmoefcrZLjPjoqmON3wg44fW+Fa3vjVVO0EfFm47mZF0\n4PtT7tqIlN12hxYWzbfBmDQpPksAuk6ESt0z7joyxosVwV2d6yVpm7D/qLgWOsqZ\n5Yvgj9Q+YsXivXMx98irJcUGXDaGM8hE4YihPvEgIacB3rGBseA19ZNCXkKLDxKi\nJY059vYyTZuYcoG3kac/9kz/JYff9dYMu+MffDsn04mg4y2CWSuN8qY+jIpZ8V1a\nJwPVaBlV2sNTewZcamnFBaW4wCxvia0jT6VwxTIKozrA5bYbtpTiAs3TjwM+Zx8r\nqvC2TA6UK9kFjw38N9P42rq9yl0gUOlzoa8cubJ8U+ZGUdUWt2YOxCtBIz3OPCAu\nTLxWIoYwKqo9vNY1FaRT/t7RXKqKr07Y5M/hRVsjKVIJ+5Ivnn3/vQbYLP2ykwzB\nrgxyQ3sDQmjDYJ+rFEkUGaqC+kV3t7ei5SdoBpwd9E0XTgLzJEuJ70HhxyDajANS\nK2hesyVr1F1zyMoTbMwc\n=IO4x\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 2cc86b07a483401b45965095d62bfaa0f54887ac\nparent 0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786180547 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786180547 +0300\n\nfeat(search): index memories.title, at the same weight as content (#731)\n\n> Reworked from the `setweight` A/B split after Eldad's call: **same\nweight for title and content**. No calibration question remains, and the\ndiff shrank to one migration plus its tests.\n\n## The bug\n\n`memories.title` has **never been in the tsvector**. Migration 001\nbuilds `search_vector` from content alone, so a memory whose title\ncarries the distinguishing words — and enrichment writes a title on\n*every* enriched memory — cannot be found by FTS on those words at all.\n\n```\nquery \"pool sizing\", title \"Postgres connection pool sizing\",\nterms absent from content:      ts_rank_cd = 0.0000   ← unreachable, not ranked low\n```\n\n## What 034 does\n\n- tokenises title and content **together**, so every lexeme keeps the\nweight D it has always had\n- widens the trigger to **`UPDATE OF content, title`** — a title-only\nedit otherwise leaves a stale index, and that is the async-enrich path's\n*normal* case, not an edge one\n- backfills existing rows\n\n## Why one weight, not `setweight` A/B\n\nThe split multiplies every content rank by 4 (D=0.1 → B=0.4), which\nforces `FTS_RANK_SCALE` to be re-derived, and it moves the modal\n*enriched* row a long way — titles are LLM summaries that reuse the\ncontent's salient terms, so most rows match both fields and the weights\nadd:\n\n| | content-only | title-only | title-echo |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| today | 0.1000 | **0.0000** | 0.1000 |\n| title A / content B | 0.4000 | 1.0000 | 1.5429 |\n| **this migration** | **0.1000** | **0.1000** | 0.2250 |\n\nEqual weight keeps a content-only match at **exactly its historical\n0.1000**, so `FTS_RANK_SCALE` stays 6.0 and no bound, default or\ncalibration moves anywhere in the stack — that is why this touches three\nfiles instead of seven.\n\nThe only rows that score differently are ones that **could not be found\nat all** before, plus rows whose title repeats a content term (0.1 →\n0.225). That second effect isn't a field preference: it's the boost\n`ts_rank_cd` gives *any* repeated term, exactly as if the content had\nsaid the word twice.\n\nPreferring a title hit over a content hit would be a relevance claim,\nand nothing here can test it — `benchmark_blend_locomo.py`'s corpus is\nLoCoMo dialogue turns, which have no titles. So the benchmark this\nchange was expected to need cannot measure it, and the change is scoped\nto avoid needing it.\n\n## Backfill safety\n\nCommitted batches inside `autocommit_block`, the shape migration 012\nuses. A single-transaction rewrite would hold the startup advisory lock\npast Cloud Run's 240 s probe, get SIGKILLed and restart from zero — the\nnon-converging crash loop that took out six writer boots on 2026-06-16.\nResumable via the `IS DISTINCT FROM` predicate. Verified over 21k rows,\nround-tripped down and up, zero left unbackfilled.\n\nA `GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) STORED` column would make the stale-vector\nclass unrepresentable and produces a byte-identical vector — noted in\nthe migration, not done here, because converting needs DROP + ADD\nCOLUMN: a full table rewrite under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE plus a GIN rebuild.\nThe moment for it is a future migration already rewriting `memories`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/` **4180** vs a 4176 baseline on `origin/main` — exactly the 4\nnew tests. `core-storage-api/tests/` 105. Ruff clean at every CI scope.\n- Mutation-verified: **downgrading to 033** fails the title test with\n`scored 0.0` and the reindex test with `stale`.\n- Four tests: title is searchable at all; a title match scores **the\nsame** as a content match (this is what pins the no-preference\ndecision); content-only scoring is untouched at 0.1 raw / 0.375 scored;\na title-only UPDATE reindexes.\n- Three test sites that hand-wrote a content-only `search_vector` are\ndeleted in favour of the trigger — left alone they would have\noverwritten the title and silently un-tested the change.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-08T09:15:49Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/621afea7c32e204175a80694de2a536412ce1675/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43"}]},{"sha":"0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDBmOGQ0YzhhZTFiM2UxNWFkZDU3MmQ5MmIwODg4ZjA1MDYzYzlmNDM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-07T12:59:16Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-07T12:59:16Z"},"message":"fix(search): settle graph_max_hops at 3 across every surface (#730)\n\nCloses the divergence #728 flagged and left open pending a decision.\n**Eldad chose 3.**\n\n## The inconsistency\n\n| surface | ceiling |\n|---|---|\n| `SEARCH_KNOBS` (`common/constants.py`) | **5** |\n| `SearchProfileUpdate` (`schemas.py:550`) | 3 |\n| `memclaw_tune` (`mcp_server.py:1340`) | 3 |\n\nSame knob, two ceilings. Since `_validate_default_search_profile`\nderives its bounds from the table, a tenant-wide `default_profile` could\nbe written with a graph-expansion depth **no per-agent profile was\nallowed to set**.\n\n## Why 3 rather than 5\n\nDepth drives graph-expansion cost, so this is a performance ceiling, not\na formality. Two of the three surfaces already said 3, and raising the\nother two would have widened a public request schema on the strength of\na bound nobody appears to have chosen deliberately — `(0, 5)` was\ninherited from the old `_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES` table.\n\n**Nothing depended on 4–5:** `GRAPH_MAX_HOPS` defaults to **2**, every\n`graph_max_hops` in the test suite is 2, and the agent ingress has\nalways refused above 3. A stored tenant default above 3 would now clamp\nto 3 on read (via the existing warning path) and 422 on the next write —\nand the only way to have created one is the A47 tenant-default path,\nwhich is admin-only and recent.\n\n`plugin/tools.json` needed no change: it is generated from the MCP\nregistry, already described the knob as `\"0-3\"`, and regenerates\nbyte-identical (verified — no drift).\n\n## Two tests, so it cannot drift again\n\n- **`test_agent_tunable_bounds_match_the_knob_table`** — every\n`SearchProfileUpdate` field's `ge`/`le` must equal\n`SEARCH_KNOBS[name].bounds`. It asserts the **bounds**, not just the\nnames: matching names with mismatched limits is precisely what hid here.\nThis test was impossible to write before this change, because it would\nhave failed.\n- **`test_the_three_ab_knobs_are_not_agent_tunable`** — records that the\n9-of-12 split is deliberate and names the three A/B knobs\n(`fts_rank_scale`, `candidate_pool_size`, `score_formula`) that stay off\nthe agent ingress, so the omission doesn't get \"fixed\" by someone\nreading it as an oversight.\n\nVerified by mutation — restoring `(0, 5)`:\n\n```\nAssertionError: graph_max_hops: SearchProfileUpdate allows (0, 3) but\nSEARCH_KNOBS declares (0, 5) — the tenant-default path and the agent path\nwould accept different values for the same knob\n```\n\n## Verification\n\n`tests/` **4176 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed (baseline on\n`origin/main` is 4174 → +2 is exactly this PR's tests).\n`core-storage-api/tests/` 105, unchanged. Ruff clean at all of CI's\nexact scopes including `common/`; `export_tool_specs.py` regenerates\n`plugin/tools.json` with no diff.\n\n## Follow-up this unblocks\n\n`SearchProfileUpdate` and `memclaw_tune` still write their bounds by\nhand in three places — they now merely *agree*. Deriving them from\n`SEARCH_KNOBS` (`pydantic.create_model` plus an `agent_tunable` flag) is\nthe remaining step, and the first test above is what makes that refactor\nsafe to attempt.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"6c45f3017d34fde3ef397ee99581870d24ee9742","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/6c45f3017d34fde3ef397ee99581870d24ee9742"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqddakCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5MwQAFmHHqxLX6bufe7FuQWmntzd\nlYmJVESyRsQql7quM9/82GNWUSMj3/IGpQN6LSirV+XW9MmI2/kQyQeGDGaQSpXc\n3uC5LTK3MdlvAGeMJKXtsk4j8C67aAd4ecuC1Oyybrc+iy6+IvoPGJVa7kMUm5Ma\nmpQ7tSnVwYOGOCFezPPCiv93uU3nOEyMqSWFBbxiKm4jfdSKfoEjBOUVK/58OTvZ\ni3MzIhryOpWBXKvyursOXOPVCrL7hCxaj/5U3m6lRFxvq6rmylcy1G+FfmQc7REP\n4SnTGZ5XBYIN74K+/ZurDjNEaK2Az+ppeZPh7frXCTZ5cVMvJiDSumvCgEVUx9Oq\nBU5U1suJJgVbad3cws/vaEmU9PpSvHh4uNU2ebpBrMO2ecvF4TsHIjkN/Bw5IHAi\nGEXpVe8KIQ/7mkl5dEFn5IbGDX4rA0/QLn8W2EVKLYT/DFaQrd6zNym2ZJs907he\n0vkpqYDAGiO2uOsmEJnHo5YEIL17sx26vXbFUV1QnuQvcTJy7q8vHQLrTeSbGDdK\noqKOU3FmqRatzZNhKD3/FeYUURdG5HrbWYEdFCVTUGdm0v3eBgI+pEa4Ksk1br6V\nnVsejCDpR2VgNss3rDyZpgnZkQj/YYOEZlEErQ1587zKQhfSC5jWMz7eMaAlKcyt\nu7LFc7V/nIkSN+hzi6+R\n=BJ+B\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 6c45f3017d34fde3ef397ee99581870d24ee9742\nparent 724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786107556 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786107556 +0300\n\nfix(search): settle graph_max_hops at 3 across every surface (#730)\n\nCloses the divergence #728 flagged and left open pending a decision.\n**Eldad chose 3.**\n\n## The inconsistency\n\n| surface | ceiling |\n|---|---|\n| `SEARCH_KNOBS` (`common/constants.py`) | **5** |\n| `SearchProfileUpdate` (`schemas.py:550`) | 3 |\n| `memclaw_tune` (`mcp_server.py:1340`) | 3 |\n\nSame knob, two ceilings. Since `_validate_default_search_profile`\nderives its bounds from the table, a tenant-wide `default_profile` could\nbe written with a graph-expansion depth **no per-agent profile was\nallowed to set**.\n\n## Why 3 rather than 5\n\nDepth drives graph-expansion cost, so this is a performance ceiling, not\na formality. Two of the three surfaces already said 3, and raising the\nother two would have widened a public request schema on the strength of\na bound nobody appears to have chosen deliberately — `(0, 5)` was\ninherited from the old `_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES` table.\n\n**Nothing depended on 4–5:** `GRAPH_MAX_HOPS` defaults to **2**, every\n`graph_max_hops` in the test suite is 2, and the agent ingress has\nalways refused above 3. A stored tenant default above 3 would now clamp\nto 3 on read (via the existing warning path) and 422 on the next write —\nand the only way to have created one is the A47 tenant-default path,\nwhich is admin-only and recent.\n\n`plugin/tools.json` needed no change: it is generated from the MCP\nregistry, already described the knob as `\"0-3\"`, and regenerates\nbyte-identical (verified — no drift).\n\n## Two tests, so it cannot drift again\n\n- **`test_agent_tunable_bounds_match_the_knob_table`** — every\n`SearchProfileUpdate` field's `ge`/`le` must equal\n`SEARCH_KNOBS[name].bounds`. It asserts the **bounds**, not just the\nnames: matching names with mismatched limits is precisely what hid here.\nThis test was impossible to write before this change, because it would\nhave failed.\n- **`test_the_three_ab_knobs_are_not_agent_tunable`** — records that the\n9-of-12 split is deliberate and names the three A/B knobs\n(`fts_rank_scale`, `candidate_pool_size`, `score_formula`) that stay off\nthe agent ingress, so the omission doesn't get \"fixed\" by someone\nreading it as an oversight.\n\nVerified by mutation — restoring `(0, 5)`:\n\n```\nAssertionError: graph_max_hops: SearchProfileUpdate allows (0, 3) but\nSEARCH_KNOBS declares (0, 5) — the tenant-default path and the agent path\nwould accept different values for the same knob\n```\n\n## Verification\n\n`tests/` **4176 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed (baseline on\n`origin/main` is 4174 → +2 is exactly this PR's tests).\n`core-storage-api/tests/` 105, unchanged. Ruff clean at all of CI's\nexact scopes including `common/`; `export_tool_specs.py` regenerates\n`plugin/tools.json` with no diff.\n\n## Follow-up this unblocks\n\n`SearchProfileUpdate` and `memclaw_tune` still write their bounds by\nhand in three places — they now merely *agree*. Deriving them from\n`SEARCH_KNOBS` (`pydantic.create_model` plus an `agent_tunable` flag) is\nthe remaining step, and the first test above is what makes that refactor\nsafe to attempt.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-07T12:59:19Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/0f8d4c8ae1b3e15add572d92b0888f05063c9f43/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac"}]},{"sha":"724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDcyNGY0ZTg3ODQ1OGU2MDI0ZDdiMDYxYzFmNTVmOTU5YzgyNzhlYWM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-07T09:15:00Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-07T09:15:00Z"},"message":"refactor(search): one resolver for both search paths, and A47 reaches legacy (#729)\n\nThe last structural item from the #723 → #725 → #727 → #728 thread.\nBranched off `main` after #728 merged, so **not** a stacked PR.\n\n## The bug\n\nThe knob resolution ladder — agent profile → tenant-wide default (A47) →\nglobal constant — was written out **twice**: in `ResolveSearchProfile`\nand inline in `_search_memories_legacy`. The copies had diverged: only\nthe pipeline merged `tenant_config.default_search_profile`.\n\nSo a tenant-wide search default applied on the active path alone, and\nthe per-tenant `fts_rank_scale = 1.0` revert **documented for #687** did\nnothing on the path you would flip to in an emergency. Measured, with\nthe legacy path resolving its own ladder:\n\n```\n[tenant_default-legacy]  storage saw {'fts_rank_scale': 6.0, 'candidate_pool_size': 0, 'score_formula': 0}\n                         where the tenant asked for {3.0, 25, 1}\n```\n\n## The change\n\n`resolve_search_params()` is the single ladder; both callers use it. The\nlegacy path drops from twelve resolved locals to two and projects its\nwire payload straight off the resolved dict. The pipeline step becomes\nthe `ctx.data` adapter it always was — 72 lines to 27 — and keeps its\nclass, because the runner emits per-step `step_ms` and resolving inline\nwould delete `resolve_search_profile` from the prod latency breakdown.\n\nBecause a knob is now resolved in one place,\n`test_resolve_search_params_emits_every_declared_knob` covers **both**\npaths at once.\n\n## Scoped to resolution — this does NOT make the paths interchangeable\n\nI want to be explicit, because my first draft of the docstring claimed\nthe rollback lever now \"reproduces\" ranking and that is **false in the\nother direction**. `ClassifyQuery` writes per-strategy\n`search_param_overrides` that `ExecuteScoredSearch` merges over the\nresolved dict — TEMPORAL and RECENT_CONTEXT both force `freshness_floor`\nand `freshness_decay_days` — and that is pipeline-only. So on a query\nwith a temporal hint the **pipeline overrides a tenant's freshness knobs\nwhere legacy honours them**: a second divergence, opposite in direction\nto the one fixed here.\n\nThe remaining divergences are enumerated in the handoff. The one to know\nabout: **`readable_tenant_ids` is not forwarded to the legacy path at\nall** — the dispatcher's call omits it and the signature lacks it — so\ncross-tenant reads silently narrow to the home tenant there. Also\nlegacy-only-missing: the hard `date_range` filter, the `ENTITY_LOOKUP`\nshort-circuit, rerank, STM injection, and `TrackRecalls`' A26\n`caller_agent_id` gate (legacy increments unconditionally and\nsynchronously).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/` **4174 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed. Baseline\nmeasured on a detached `origin/main` checkout: **4172** → +2 is exactly\nthe new source axis.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, unchanged. Ruff clean at all of CI's\nexact scopes; broker OpenAPI regenerated with no diff.\n- The A47 fix rides the existing both-paths delivery test as a third\nsource axis (`defaults` / `agent` / `tenant_default`) rather than a\nseparate test — which covers all three quiet knobs through the tenant\nrung instead of the one knob my first version checked. Verified by\nmutation, above. The knob-set test still fails with the key named when a\nknob is dropped from the resolver.\n\n`/simplify` found the false equivalence claim above, folded my\nstandalone test into the existing one, and cut a war story I had told at\nfour sites down to one. It also confirmed the module-level hoist of\n`validate_search_profile` creates no cycle (`organization_settings`\nimports nothing from `core_api.services`) and costs 1.37 ms of a 539 ms\nimport.\n\n## Follow-ups\n\n- Seven sibling `resolve_config` imports in `memory_service.py` are\nstill function-local; this change's module-level import is the proof\nthey were never cycle-avoidance. Left alone to keep the diff readable.\n- Still the next duplication worth collapsing: `SearchProfileUpdate` and\nthe `memclaw_tune` signature enumerate their own knob subset with their\nown bounds, and `graph_max_hops` has already drifted — `(0, 5)` in\n`SEARCH_KNOBS` vs `le=3` there.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"afe226421ebbf78a1a391fb6bf0cb00e2bb15856","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/afe226421ebbf78a1a391fb6bf0cb00e2bb15856"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdaIUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAhZkQAIZnECWCy3J1SBBuw5qBZ84K\nAWQcgsydg2Qw2OSqG/fTlc6Ucjo3ItHaCkfaAucrquYfIG8Xbwv7b1lLXI9Zier4\nnkZ5RvLE8qhdv5gNXTz5D2xKYtC47SsM8/6mmEYOsmd/hHk6kYzJvqNGmE/7ESGG\n/ZVLJowwEhxqhpuATDufOAnas+vTtQ5Y4kYgJktnr+36UFYPIb39b9zBW6Kh6tXu\n8NcgGSLQak+ubz+kyh9rELJUZfFcwR7KA6wgpPwsYShmkatKT9Ku6PBOErFgCJn3\nuaz2yb3Tnx6GiH2WG7z+4tzjO/a4OQbkTL5rMU1G3m21ADGNEklXqxsXTO7cof+2\nwDgbJ4IFllIJBI0awlpmIkeMcEc5FYy4HWh4honp7+9rbd4OH2yKu97Kh/Bm8lj5\nyAYaaXp/FcWD6y3Jk+8Rkf3lqf9twulbWz2xbvmE60uNcCBz0rhij3kbvOEjjwwS\nITJNtIEAd4czWeCeKKBN30KwkqU/4XllzQ53d4Ws0CxRWtrufgsjjEoA87S97Aho\nDfzUNlc5cLB0FjszsffldiMtu2+3E6DkHW1HB0Pp/MEpr8l2J/PvVv2qkauCgMpD\nDImVEuLbdcWFoHjnMK5sddrRXJq+kPNkereoryPGXz3Rb8bEr6wNJ8dmzTfeoiBS\n8bcDzoufBsTfuRJBDw3M\n=6tTi\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree afe226421ebbf78a1a391fb6bf0cb00e2bb15856\nparent ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786094100 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786094100 +0300\n\nrefactor(search): one resolver for both search paths, and A47 reaches legacy (#729)\n\nThe last structural item from the #723 → #725 → #727 → #728 thread.\nBranched off `main` after #728 merged, so **not** a stacked PR.\n\n## The bug\n\nThe knob resolution ladder — agent profile → tenant-wide default (A47) →\nglobal constant — was written out **twice**: in `ResolveSearchProfile`\nand inline in `_search_memories_legacy`. The copies had diverged: only\nthe pipeline merged `tenant_config.default_search_profile`.\n\nSo a tenant-wide search default applied on the active path alone, and\nthe per-tenant `fts_rank_scale = 1.0` revert **documented for #687** did\nnothing on the path you would flip to in an emergency. Measured, with\nthe legacy path resolving its own ladder:\n\n```\n[tenant_default-legacy]  storage saw {'fts_rank_scale': 6.0, 'candidate_pool_size': 0, 'score_formula': 0}\n                         where the tenant asked for {3.0, 25, 1}\n```\n\n## The change\n\n`resolve_search_params()` is the single ladder; both callers use it. The\nlegacy path drops from twelve resolved locals to two and projects its\nwire payload straight off the resolved dict. The pipeline step becomes\nthe `ctx.data` adapter it always was — 72 lines to 27 — and keeps its\nclass, because the runner emits per-step `step_ms` and resolving inline\nwould delete `resolve_search_profile` from the prod latency breakdown.\n\nBecause a knob is now resolved in one place,\n`test_resolve_search_params_emits_every_declared_knob` covers **both**\npaths at once.\n\n## Scoped to resolution — this does NOT make the paths interchangeable\n\nI want to be explicit, because my first draft of the docstring claimed\nthe rollback lever now \"reproduces\" ranking and that is **false in the\nother direction**. `ClassifyQuery` writes per-strategy\n`search_param_overrides` that `ExecuteScoredSearch` merges over the\nresolved dict — TEMPORAL and RECENT_CONTEXT both force `freshness_floor`\nand `freshness_decay_days` — and that is pipeline-only. So on a query\nwith a temporal hint the **pipeline overrides a tenant's freshness knobs\nwhere legacy honours them**: a second divergence, opposite in direction\nto the one fixed here.\n\nThe remaining divergences are enumerated in the handoff. The one to know\nabout: **`readable_tenant_ids` is not forwarded to the legacy path at\nall** — the dispatcher's call omits it and the signature lacks it — so\ncross-tenant reads silently narrow to the home tenant there. Also\nlegacy-only-missing: the hard `date_range` filter, the `ENTITY_LOOKUP`\nshort-circuit, rerank, STM injection, and `TrackRecalls`' A26\n`caller_agent_id` gate (legacy increments unconditionally and\nsynchronously).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/` **4174 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed. Baseline\nmeasured on a detached `origin/main` checkout: **4172** → +2 is exactly\nthe new source axis.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, unchanged. Ruff clean at all of CI's\nexact scopes; broker OpenAPI regenerated with no diff.\n- The A47 fix rides the existing both-paths delivery test as a third\nsource axis (`defaults` / `agent` / `tenant_default`) rather than a\nseparate test — which covers all three quiet knobs through the tenant\nrung instead of the one knob my first version checked. Verified by\nmutation, above. The knob-set test still fails with the key named when a\nknob is dropped from the resolver.\n\n`/simplify` found the false equivalence claim above, folded my\nstandalone test into the existing one, and cut a war story I had told at\nfour sites down to one. It also confirmed the module-level hoist of\n`validate_search_profile` creates no cycle (`organization_settings`\nimports nothing from `core_api.services`) and costs 1.37 ms of a 539 ms\nimport.\n\n## Follow-ups\n\n- Seven sibling `resolve_config` imports in `memory_service.py` are\nstill function-local; this change's module-level import is the proof\nthey were never cycle-avoidance. Left alone to keep the diff readable.\n- Still the next duplication worth collapsing: `SearchProfileUpdate` and\nthe `memclaw_tune` signature enumerate their own knob subset with their\nown bounds, and `graph_max_hops` has already drifted — `(0, 5)` in\n`SEARCH_KNOBS` vs `le=3` there.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-07T09:15:03Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/724f4e878458e6024d7b061c1f55f959c8278eac/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2"}]},{"sha":"ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGFlNTZkMDhhZmZiN2I5NDJlNTIxNjY3NTJkMWUxMDc3ZmJjYzFlZjI","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-06T14:20:13Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-06T14:20:13Z"},"message":"refactor(search): one knob table instead of four registration points (#728)\n\nThe last item from the #723 → #725 → #727 thread. A search tuning knob\nhad to be registered in **four** places, and every omission was silent\nin a different way:\n\n| forgotten | symptom |\n|---|---|\n| a search-path builder | the knob applies on one path only (#723) |\n| the wire-contract tuple | a core-api-local key reaches the SQL —\n`top_k` became the candidate-window LIMIT (#725) |\n| `_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES` | **accepted unvalidated and unclamped** on\nthe agent path, while the tenant-default path rejects it as unknown |\n\n## One table\n\n`common/constants.py` now holds `SEARCH_KNOBS` — per knob: type, bounds,\nand whether it crosses the wire. Everything else derives:\n\n- `SQL_SCORING_PARAM_KEYS` = knobs flagged `sql`\n- `SQL_SCORING_REQUIRED_KEYS` = knobs flagged `sql_required` (storage's\nindexed reads)\n- `_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES` — **deleted**; both validators read the table\ndirectly\n\nIts third `default` slot went with it: `None` for all twelve entries, so\nthe branch consuming it was unreachable. The log line that said *\"using\ndefault\"* now says *\"dropping key\"*, which is what actually happens.\n\n## Why the table carries no defaults\n\nThree of them aren't constants — `fts_weight` is query-adaptive, `top_k`\nand `min_similarity` fall back to the caller's request. A `default`\ncolumn would need a sentinel and the resolvers would stay hand-written\nregardless, which is precisely the shape of the dead slot just removed.\nSo the resolver is pinned by a test instead.\n\n## Tests: two in, two out\n\n- **`test_resolve_search_profile_emits_every_declared_knob`** — asserts\nthe resolver emits exactly the declared set. This is what makes a\nforgotten resolver loud, and it is the only *direct* cover for the three\nknobs that never reach the wire (`top_k`, `min_similarity`,\n`graph_max_hops`).\n- **`test_sql_flags_match_how_storage_reads_each_knob`** — pins `sql` /\n`sql_required` against storage's actual reads, indexed vs `.get`.\n\nThe two source-scan tests #727 added are **deleted, not weakened**: they\nasserted the tuples against storage, and the tuples are now derived from\nthe flags this test pins. Same property, one place.\n\nEach verified by mutation, with the offending key named every time:\n\n```\ndeclare a knob, skip the resolver  → declared but not resolved: ['brand_new_knob']\nflip sql_required → sql            → indexed-but-not-required: ['similarity_blend']\ndrop a knob from the resolver      → declared but not resolved: ['graph_max_hops']\n```\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/` **4172 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed — identical\nto the `origin/main` baseline I measured on a detached checkout, as\nexpected: two tests added, two superseded ones removed.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, unchanged. Ruff clean at all of CI's\nexact scopes including `common/`; broker OpenAPI regenerated with no\ndiff.\n\n## Not addressed — now stated rather than implied\n\n`SearchProfileUpdate` (`schemas.py:540`) and the `memclaw_tune` MCP\nsignature still enumerate their own subset with their own bounds, and\n**they have already drifted**: `graph_max_hops` is `(0, 5)` in the table\nand `ge=0, le=3` there, so the same knob accepts 5 from a tenant default\nand rejects it from the REST ingress. That predates this change.\nDeriving those from the table would alter a public request schema and\nneeds a decision on which bound is correct, so I have flagged it in the\ntable's own comment rather than silently picking one.\n\nAlso still open: the two resolvers remain separate, and\n`_search_memories_legacy` never merges the A47\n`tenant_config.default_search_profile`, so tenant-wide search defaults\nreach the pipeline path only. Extracting one shared resolver is the\nlargest remaining win here and would let the new resolver test pin both\npaths at once.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"68c522816f7017e0f99a39b2b4851263d6573152","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/68c522816f7017e0f99a39b2b4851263d6573152"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdJgdCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvRwQAHxSinkZignEy24rrqufPPqq\nim8WA7u7YiZfADdAF935NLwAyqvZxtSWFR1WxogdjAxJLF1+Nct3XaHlcr3YEGge\nT9YGAOGJkZSNp0pO2HilJ5vnped8Z/+fOupZ/8KLcLRlPSQ4CBz48OEdd8Fq3R8P\nJb6+Bt6GwAoC6FLbWeJ7s7KqoyTmI4Q0AcI8xWC324J0ckwXcOT8BlBwB6NSTNs2\n4klcXRlxjAO1+6cd3KDYEFNEsNLnIJHBIjXyH0ZvWyi3NzzWcKpkbPdDl6s0/c0m\niEshN8YI+CGtjqrSn299gHK+s/lyhgRYEIN2N0PrFoJSU7kpPU8d8lESrNvgrsw6\nBh/k/63azytHNy8QWT1p9IE4tFV5pOk1sLcFTqlp/YamlkukzOfoqUjONWV/RC1m\nFVBO//pHacBBGD1jl5+Sgpln7O9KkBRcKU/EKW2bTWbYVhsOd73GhY/vQpTb3cEc\nSuuFHaQ/oFupWssotxJx3uGUmlD6X7Dod5vbRr6ebTFdz8VdmRYNY4pzPg2PvsE5\nbtTazNb87zrFV6bsFQ/09Iwf+kigIRWuYdwrJIg/bJT/XiBFA9BJSFylCFTPlRla\nL8fSfsFjcy8+eD9eS4B2BT5t2jW533pDWs5+kuSBCJOPHLvP0w/meNUFQqiooJKW\n9Y5eI0zXYAzglOzmqeWd\n=5FFJ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 68c522816f7017e0f99a39b2b4851263d6573152\nparent 048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786026013 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786026013 +0300\n\nrefactor(search): one knob table instead of four registration points (#728)\n\nThe last item from the #723 → #725 → #727 thread. A search tuning knob\nhad to be registered in **four** places, and every omission was silent\nin a different way:\n\n| forgotten | symptom |\n|---|---|\n| a search-path builder | the knob applies on one path only (#723) |\n| the wire-contract tuple | a core-api-local key reaches the SQL —\n`top_k` became the candidate-window LIMIT (#725) |\n| `_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES` | **accepted unvalidated and unclamped** on\nthe agent path, while the tenant-default path rejects it as unknown |\n\n## One table\n\n`common/constants.py` now holds `SEARCH_KNOBS` — per knob: type, bounds,\nand whether it crosses the wire. Everything else derives:\n\n- `SQL_SCORING_PARAM_KEYS` = knobs flagged `sql`\n- `SQL_SCORING_REQUIRED_KEYS` = knobs flagged `sql_required` (storage's\nindexed reads)\n- `_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES` — **deleted**; both validators read the table\ndirectly\n\nIts third `default` slot went with it: `None` for all twelve entries, so\nthe branch consuming it was unreachable. The log line that said *\"using\ndefault\"* now says *\"dropping key\"*, which is what actually happens.\n\n## Why the table carries no defaults\n\nThree of them aren't constants — `fts_weight` is query-adaptive, `top_k`\nand `min_similarity` fall back to the caller's request. A `default`\ncolumn would need a sentinel and the resolvers would stay hand-written\nregardless, which is precisely the shape of the dead slot just removed.\nSo the resolver is pinned by a test instead.\n\n## Tests: two in, two out\n\n- **`test_resolve_search_profile_emits_every_declared_knob`** — asserts\nthe resolver emits exactly the declared set. This is what makes a\nforgotten resolver loud, and it is the only *direct* cover for the three\nknobs that never reach the wire (`top_k`, `min_similarity`,\n`graph_max_hops`).\n- **`test_sql_flags_match_how_storage_reads_each_knob`** — pins `sql` /\n`sql_required` against storage's actual reads, indexed vs `.get`.\n\nThe two source-scan tests #727 added are **deleted, not weakened**: they\nasserted the tuples against storage, and the tuples are now derived from\nthe flags this test pins. Same property, one place.\n\nEach verified by mutation, with the offending key named every time:\n\n```\ndeclare a knob, skip the resolver  → declared but not resolved: ['brand_new_knob']\nflip sql_required → sql            → indexed-but-not-required: ['similarity_blend']\ndrop a knob from the resolver      → declared but not resolved: ['graph_max_hops']\n```\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tests/` **4172 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed — identical\nto the `origin/main` baseline I measured on a detached checkout, as\nexpected: two tests added, two superseded ones removed.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` 105, unchanged. Ruff clean at all of CI's\nexact scopes including `common/`; broker OpenAPI regenerated with no\ndiff.\n\n## Not addressed — now stated rather than implied\n\n`SearchProfileUpdate` (`schemas.py:540`) and the `memclaw_tune` MCP\nsignature still enumerate their own subset with their own bounds, and\n**they have already drifted**: `graph_max_hops` is `(0, 5)` in the table\nand `ge=0, le=3` there, so the same knob accepts 5 from a tenant default\nand rejects it from the REST ingress. That predates this change.\nDeriving those from the table would alter a public request schema and\nneeds a decision on which bound is correct, so I have flagged it in the\ntable's own comment rather than silently picking one.\n\nAlso still open: the two resolvers remain separate, and\n`_search_memories_legacy` never merges the A47\n`tenant_config.default_search_profile`, so tenant-wide search defaults\nreach the pipeline path only. Extracting one shared resolver is the\nlargest remaining win here and would let the new resolver test pin both\npaths at once.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-06T14:20:16Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ae56d08affb7b942e52166752d1e1077fbcc1ef2/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56"}]},{"sha":"048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDA0OGUyZTE5ZjZjNzlmMTk0NWZjZjRmZjJmMzg4OWQ5M2JlMGZhNTY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-06T11:58:32Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-06T11:58:32Z"},"message":"fix(embedding): key degraded-provider stats per backend, not per process (#726)\n\nCloses the limitation documented in #721 and carried through #724.\n\n## The masking, one level up\n\n`_EmbeddingStats` was a single module-level object shared by every call\npath **and every tenant**. One process holds an embedding provider per\n`(api_key, model, base_url, send_dimensions, truncate_to_dim)` — the\nregistry caches up to 32, explicitly because *\"the same api_key can host\nmultiple simultaneous providers, e.g. real OpenAI for one tenant and a\nlocal TEI sidecar\"*.\n\nSo a healthy backend's success cleared a broken backend's streak, and\nthe broken one never reached the reporting threshold.\n\nThat is precisely the masking the bulk streak was added to fix, moved up\na level:\n\n| | masked by |\n|---|---|\n| #721 (fixed) | single-embed successes hiding a broken **bulk path** |\n| **this** | one tenant's healthy backend hiding **another tenant's**\nbroken one |\n\n## The change\n\nStats are keyed on the backend — `provider_name` + `model`, both already\non the `EmbeddingProvider` protocol. When provider construction fails\nthere is no object to take an identity from, so the resolved provider\n**name** is the scope; every tenant misconfiguring the same provider\nthen shares one streak, which is right.\n\nThe map is bounded at 256 with clear-on-overflow — same shape and same\nreasoning as `common/ranking`'s `_PERMANENT_LOGGED_MAX`. Ranking reached\nthis conclusion first for its log-once dedup, and its note on why\nclearing must be per-backend rather than wholesale applies verbatim.\n\n`_run_with_retry` now takes the backend's stats rather than reaching for\na global; that's what let the single-embed paths be keyed at all.\n\n## The test that matters\n\nDrives a broken backend and a healthy one **alternately** — three\nfailures, each separated by a success on the *other* backend — and\nasserts the broken streak survives and still reports.\n\nVerified it **fails when every scope is collapsed to one**, i.e. against\nthe old shared object. That's the point of it.\n\n## A helper that immediately earned itself\n\nTwo existing tests changed shape, not intent: they reset\n`_stats_by_scope` instead of swapping a global (matching the\n`_reset_misconfig_dedup` idiom already in the file), and assert through\n`_only_stats()`, which fails loudly if a test touches two backends\nwithout meaning to.\n\nIt caught one straight away. `test_bulk_success_rearms_the_bulk_report`\nused a bare stand-in for the \"recovered\" provider, which now keys as a\n**different backend** — so the recovery would have landed on a fresh\nstreak and the test would have passed while proving nothing. Its double\nsubclasses the real fake now, and the comment says why that matters here\nbeyond protocol conformance.\n\n## Checks\n\n`ruff check common/ core-api/src/` · `ruff format --check --isolated` —\nclean.\n\nFull `pytest tests/ -m \"not benchmark\"`: **4098 passed**, and I compared\nagainst a **same-environment** pristine baseline rather than an older\nnumber — 39 failures on both, with `comm` confirming **zero\nintroduced**. (The 39 vs the 26 quoted on earlier PRs is a\nfreshly-created local Postgres, identical before and after.) `uv.lock`\nuntouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"413753c83684a8d8ec9079992ddb86a661340c27","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/413753c83684a8d8ec9079992ddb86a661340c27"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdHboCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAVncQAA9HCku9fcw7+RF7UoGNR1I7\n0oM7j9j01hOHVrvkrkMyiwnrm6ac4e5h5g7+OUdqyJaOc9wPckus/1QKhjAnRDh9\nh1jaQzG21ar6Rx1zEqjJgQ0GftV9dH1CcUIJ5V8xFoMFJbnMbU5OXmLWAKWvIq+R\nCMwIFZOdpoHljR1H33nAEAnrIi782Q0sDJ1c5MHKUHyl6WwmhgRBLOurG8n2oeLI\nkS7NLPNbP3tiDMI4lBbChn2+rXLqv8bpzf/drwrtKpdESPWSTIlIUy996QxPvsmf\nB3MckIMyZ2I3RbQRo1L+/IqUe2vPW1pYTvmL43tlAsvZS7a2pkJGv66/FN9cFO6+\nBBKHJv38icNZRUWUTPfz/HoGyJeE1pRVXZsP0+sTIpsDfIp9m/7m2cVfypFIJpXJ\nYATZJ11S0Eyq52iqz82HFunMhHmogIDKyAb7IQWm7zVpRipPXjgusbjs3JPy8FVP\nmprwLViJIKTUAtT0CjmGYduZ3RD82sAXw5Zo7rK/sel1OinQhjz7O7JxDVskft9A\nEuLqs9/HjJjbdDJLiQPEtikEO3/ECjPNHi6gvN+EvDG2ELraO8gn3uoeONHiZwZf\nPiN1dg/BLaiaDhvfN74pOlWYvsz3XvOBuicL+qIIL/kwl2T2VVg/BfooXbzBofvh\nlH0V/vgvZ+EW3km5AZaK\n=Au6f\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 413753c83684a8d8ec9079992ddb86a661340c27\nparent 6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786017512 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786017512 +0300\n\nfix(embedding): key degraded-provider stats per backend, not per process (#726)\n\nCloses the limitation documented in #721 and carried through #724.\n\n## The masking, one level up\n\n`_EmbeddingStats` was a single module-level object shared by every call\npath **and every tenant**. One process holds an embedding provider per\n`(api_key, model, base_url, send_dimensions, truncate_to_dim)` — the\nregistry caches up to 32, explicitly because *\"the same api_key can host\nmultiple simultaneous providers, e.g. real OpenAI for one tenant and a\nlocal TEI sidecar\"*.\n\nSo a healthy backend's success cleared a broken backend's streak, and\nthe broken one never reached the reporting threshold.\n\nThat is precisely the masking the bulk streak was added to fix, moved up\na level:\n\n| | masked by |\n|---|---|\n| #721 (fixed) | single-embed successes hiding a broken **bulk path** |\n| **this** | one tenant's healthy backend hiding **another tenant's**\nbroken one |\n\n## The change\n\nStats are keyed on the backend — `provider_name` + `model`, both already\non the `EmbeddingProvider` protocol. When provider construction fails\nthere is no object to take an identity from, so the resolved provider\n**name** is the scope; every tenant misconfiguring the same provider\nthen shares one streak, which is right.\n\nThe map is bounded at 256 with clear-on-overflow — same shape and same\nreasoning as `common/ranking`'s `_PERMANENT_LOGGED_MAX`. Ranking reached\nthis conclusion first for its log-once dedup, and its note on why\nclearing must be per-backend rather than wholesale applies verbatim.\n\n`_run_with_retry` now takes the backend's stats rather than reaching for\na global; that's what let the single-embed paths be keyed at all.\n\n## The test that matters\n\nDrives a broken backend and a healthy one **alternately** — three\nfailures, each separated by a success on the *other* backend — and\nasserts the broken streak survives and still reports.\n\nVerified it **fails when every scope is collapsed to one**, i.e. against\nthe old shared object. That's the point of it.\n\n## A helper that immediately earned itself\n\nTwo existing tests changed shape, not intent: they reset\n`_stats_by_scope` instead of swapping a global (matching the\n`_reset_misconfig_dedup` idiom already in the file), and assert through\n`_only_stats()`, which fails loudly if a test touches two backends\nwithout meaning to.\n\nIt caught one straight away. `test_bulk_success_rearms_the_bulk_report`\nused a bare stand-in for the \"recovered\" provider, which now keys as a\n**different backend** — so the recovery would have landed on a fresh\nstreak and the test would have passed while proving nothing. Its double\nsubclasses the real fake now, and the comment says why that matters here\nbeyond protocol conformance.\n\n## Checks\n\n`ruff check common/ core-api/src/` · `ruff format --check --isolated` —\nclean.\n\nFull `pytest tests/ -m \"not benchmark\"`: **4098 passed**, and I compared\nagainst a **same-environment** pristine baseline rather than an older\nnumber — 39 failures on both, with `comm` confirming **zero\nintroduced**. (The 39 vs the 26 quoted on earlier PRs is a\nfreshly-created local Postgres, identical before and after.) `uv.lock`\nuntouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-06T11:58:34Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/048e2e19f6c79f1945fcf4ff2f3889d93be0fa56/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93"}]},{"sha":"6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDY0ODNhMzlhMTE4NTdkNGNhZTI4MDdkMmU4YzA2NDQ5NTFlNjlmOTM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-06T11:53:24Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-06T11:53:24Z"},"message":"fix(search): declare the scored-search wire contract once, in common/ (#727)\n\nThe follow-up #725 named. `search_params` crosses core-api →\ncore-storage-api as a bare dict, and which keys belong in it was written\ndown **nowhere** — which is why the set has now been wrong in both\ndirections:\n\n- keys the SQL **needed** went missing on one path —\n`candidate_pool_size`, `score_formula` (#723);\n- keys the SQL **never reads** travelled anyway, and one of them\n(`top_k`) collided with a same-named request parameter and silently\nbecame the candidate-window LIMIT, defeating the overfetch on the active\npath (#725).\n\n## The contract, declared once\n\n`common/constants.py` now holds `SQL_SCORING_PARAM_KEYS` (the nine keys\n`memory_scored_search` reads) and `SQL_SCORING_REQUIRED_KEYS` (the six\nit reads positionally).\n\n**Why `common/` and not `core_api/constants.py`** — I had it in core-api\nfirst, on the reasoning that both consumers live there. That was wrong,\nand the comment I wrote to justify it (\"storage cannot import this\") was\nfactually false: `common/constants.py` is documented as *\"DB-query\nconstants shared between core-api and core-storage-api\"*, and\n`postgres_service.py:47` already imports twelve names from it,\n**including ones used inside `memory_scored_search` itself**. What\ndrifts here is the key set against the SQL — a two-service concern — and\n`common/` is where this repo puts those. It is also not in enterprise's\n`vendored_files_manifest.json` (only 9 files are), so there is **no\nre-vendor cost**.\n\n## Both builders project through it\n\nNeither hand-lists the set now, so a core-api-local knob cannot reach\nthe SQL by accident:\n\n- **Pipeline** projects out of a genuine superset —\n`ctx.data[\"search_params\"]` also carries `top_k`, `min_similarity` and\n`graph_max_hops`, which `ClassifyQuery` and `PostFilterResults` read and\nstorage does not. Projecting *at the boundary* rather than removing them\nat the source keeps them available to those steps.\n- **Legacy** projects strictly (`_resolved_scoring[k]`, no `if k in`) —\nit resolves every knob a few lines above, so a key declared and not\nresolved is a mistake worth a `KeyError`, not a silent omission at the\nSQL.\n\n## The route now enforces what #723 only described\n\n#723's comment said a payload missing the positionally-read keys is\nmalformed and should 4xx at the edge — but the guard only checked the\ndict was non-empty, so an *incomplete* one still reached the SQL and\nsurfaced as a `KeyError` 500 from inside the session. It is now a 422\nnaming exactly which keys are missing.\n\n## Verification\n\nEvery new test watched failing for its own reason:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| pipeline sends the whole dict | `surplus=['graph_max_hops',\n'min_similarity', 'top_k']` |\n| drop `candidate_pool_size` from the declaration | `storage reads but\ncore-api does not declare: ['candidate_pool_size']` |\n| remove the route's required-key check | partial-payload 422 test fails\n|\n\n- `tests/` **4170 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed. Baseline\nmeasured on a detached `origin/main` checkout: **4166** → +4 is exactly\nthis PR's cases.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` **105** (baseline 104 → +1).\n- Ruff clean at all of CI's exact scopes, including `ruff check common/`\nand the `--isolated` vendored-file check; `F821` over `scripts/` clean;\nbroker OpenAPI regenerated with no diff.\n\n### On the source-scan test\n\n`test_sql_scoring_param_keys_matches_what_storage_reads` is what makes\nthis a *single* registration point rather than a fourth list to\nmaintain: both services import the tuple, but nothing **derives**\nstorage's reads from it, since the SQL builder names each key inline —\nwhich is the readable way to write it.\n\nIt round-trips the storage source through `ast.unparse` rather than\ngrepping it. That is not incidental: the storage source discusses these\nkeys in prose as well as reading them, and my first version — a regex\nover the raw text — scored `top_k` as a read, out of the comment\nexplaining why #725 **stopped** reading it. `ast.unparse` drops\ncomments, so what is left is only code.\n\n## What this does not fix\n\nAdding a knob end-to-end still touches `_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES`\n(`organization_settings.py:1086`), and forgetting it is the one step no\ntest catches: `validate_search_profile` copies unknown keys through\nunvalidated and unclamped on the agent-profile path, while a tenant\ndefault 422s. Collapsing the remaining lists into one knob table\n(default + type + range + `sql: bool`) is the real end state, but it\nrestructures both resolvers and the settings-validation table, so it is\nnot this change.\n\nAlso still open and separate: `_search_memories_legacy` never merges the\nA47 `tenant_config.default_search_profile`, so a rollback to the legacy\npath silently drops tenant-wide search defaults — a divergence in the\n*values* resolved, not the keys delivered.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"98e58c07bbcabb630c5debe923e07d056471c708","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/98e58c07bbcabb630c5debe923e07d056471c708"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdHW0CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAKxEQAHP4umtuOm6uBwrn8i05dzhB\n0kWefN5Xslew+Y9TK/9SgsIwrtkc2GtMFqiU17zel4l9AQKso0HGQOI1naeZrdVJ\n/Xh6REvKrgV3bGBhwcKKaYEcvYji+et2CitZWAVXhEt4uePmKtnnJD+rUTzqZ5mO\nzEFmkHeo2ws6+zV8OkD43RDyghik2o63BfjXhvK0+sqXQ6xNhGHvarGT+Ko7QRI0\nYxJHFgg4wTFwWTsDA6tsEiMFq/TOn5ZCFE0fpTRO7IVq6n86YGow4hvLlkYoqNgJ\nVq0mHcdWtRPyVHiDfSdGYUedeW3w19PfPnDMpK08ooHdjS59qxuyi70xsVpaJ74T\nrrI5R+9oQ4PYbFTGlO7BfsVYh4PC/LARSksa0n1V5oE/6XRLEddgBo1ORMOXb3UX\npYmUN+6bbcYJ1vzcL9uWq9Ekvy4tMlyfj/F0uh0Aek6V2Ktbodek12GoFCevpKIP\nis/XLmhDIK7JCzDHStrfYWxT+vEC6yR15l2LIjQ0JfptHO/ddhK1u5Dhthmav3KJ\nCmQ1lYdzYMOmBLVujhqqn97Bf3hTnFcDLxLso2mR59OY09bMb21jmKN3+/svJT59\nS0FqYjh8ouSzMlD6kwIqPXE1sC1LwFFa0O4VXi32RW8HqO2xmsibWXfgmmNhKf1Q\nawlQvImZq3jL0GFU8maF\n=TSdv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 98e58c07bbcabb630c5debe923e07d056471c708\nparent f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786017204 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786017204 +0300\n\nfix(search): declare the scored-search wire contract once, in common/ (#727)\n\nThe follow-up #725 named. `search_params` crosses core-api →\ncore-storage-api as a bare dict, and which keys belong in it was written\ndown **nowhere** — which is why the set has now been wrong in both\ndirections:\n\n- keys the SQL **needed** went missing on one path —\n`candidate_pool_size`, `score_formula` (#723);\n- keys the SQL **never reads** travelled anyway, and one of them\n(`top_k`) collided with a same-named request parameter and silently\nbecame the candidate-window LIMIT, defeating the overfetch on the active\npath (#725).\n\n## The contract, declared once\n\n`common/constants.py` now holds `SQL_SCORING_PARAM_KEYS` (the nine keys\n`memory_scored_search` reads) and `SQL_SCORING_REQUIRED_KEYS` (the six\nit reads positionally).\n\n**Why `common/` and not `core_api/constants.py`** — I had it in core-api\nfirst, on the reasoning that both consumers live there. That was wrong,\nand the comment I wrote to justify it (\"storage cannot import this\") was\nfactually false: `common/constants.py` is documented as *\"DB-query\nconstants shared between core-api and core-storage-api\"*, and\n`postgres_service.py:47` already imports twelve names from it,\n**including ones used inside `memory_scored_search` itself**. What\ndrifts here is the key set against the SQL — a two-service concern — and\n`common/` is where this repo puts those. It is also not in enterprise's\n`vendored_files_manifest.json` (only 9 files are), so there is **no\nre-vendor cost**.\n\n## Both builders project through it\n\nNeither hand-lists the set now, so a core-api-local knob cannot reach\nthe SQL by accident:\n\n- **Pipeline** projects out of a genuine superset —\n`ctx.data[\"search_params\"]` also carries `top_k`, `min_similarity` and\n`graph_max_hops`, which `ClassifyQuery` and `PostFilterResults` read and\nstorage does not. Projecting *at the boundary* rather than removing them\nat the source keeps them available to those steps.\n- **Legacy** projects strictly (`_resolved_scoring[k]`, no `if k in`) —\nit resolves every knob a few lines above, so a key declared and not\nresolved is a mistake worth a `KeyError`, not a silent omission at the\nSQL.\n\n## The route now enforces what #723 only described\n\n#723's comment said a payload missing the positionally-read keys is\nmalformed and should 4xx at the edge — but the guard only checked the\ndict was non-empty, so an *incomplete* one still reached the SQL and\nsurfaced as a `KeyError` 500 from inside the session. It is now a 422\nnaming exactly which keys are missing.\n\n## Verification\n\nEvery new test watched failing for its own reason:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| pipeline sends the whole dict | `surplus=['graph_max_hops',\n'min_similarity', 'top_k']` |\n| drop `candidate_pool_size` from the declaration | `storage reads but\ncore-api does not declare: ['candidate_pool_size']` |\n| remove the route's required-key check | partial-payload 422 test fails\n|\n\n- `tests/` **4170 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed. Baseline\nmeasured on a detached `origin/main` checkout: **4166** → +4 is exactly\nthis PR's cases.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` **105** (baseline 104 → +1).\n- Ruff clean at all of CI's exact scopes, including `ruff check common/`\nand the `--isolated` vendored-file check; `F821` over `scripts/` clean;\nbroker OpenAPI regenerated with no diff.\n\n### On the source-scan test\n\n`test_sql_scoring_param_keys_matches_what_storage_reads` is what makes\nthis a *single* registration point rather than a fourth list to\nmaintain: both services import the tuple, but nothing **derives**\nstorage's reads from it, since the SQL builder names each key inline —\nwhich is the readable way to write it.\n\nIt round-trips the storage source through `ast.unparse` rather than\ngrepping it. That is not incidental: the storage source discusses these\nkeys in prose as well as reading them, and my first version — a regex\nover the raw text — scored `top_k` as a read, out of the comment\nexplaining why #725 **stopped** reading it. `ast.unparse` drops\ncomments, so what is left is only code.\n\n## What this does not fix\n\nAdding a knob end-to-end still touches `_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES`\n(`organization_settings.py:1086`), and forgetting it is the one step no\ntest catches: `validate_search_profile` copies unknown keys through\nunvalidated and unclamped on the agent-profile path, while a tenant\ndefault 422s. Collapsing the remaining lists into one knob table\n(default + type + range + `sql: bool`) is the real end state, but it\nrestructures both resolvers and the settings-validation table, so it is\nnot this change.\n\nAlso still open and separate: `_search_memories_legacy` never merges the\nA47 `tenant_config.default_search_profile`, so a rollback to the legacy\npath silently drops tenant-wide search defaults — a divergence in the\n*values* resolved, not the keys delivered.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-06T11:53:27Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6483a39a11857d4cae2807d2e8c0644951e69f93/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec"}]},{"sha":"f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGY5NDU3MmEyNjg3MTEwYzgyYTQzMzY4YzkyYjQ2Y2NmNjhlMWI2ZWM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-06T09:45:23Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-06T09:45:23Z"},"message":"fix(search): let SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR reach the SQL LIMIT (#725)\n\nFollow-up to #723, which found this. `claude-review` on that PR\nindependently flagged it at **High** severity.\n\n## The bug\n\n`SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR` was **inert on the pipeline search path** —\nthe active one (`_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH = True`).\n\nThree pieces, each correct in isolation:\n\n1. `resolve_search_profile` puts the caller's **unmultiplied** `top_k`\ninto `search_params`.\n2. `ExecuteScoredSearch` multiplies only a **local** variable, then\nsends that as the top-level `top_k`.\n3. `postgres_service` read the LIMIT as `sp.get(\"top_k\", top_k)` —\npreferring the nested one.\n\nMeasured with a spy, caller `top_k=3`, factor 2:\n\n```\n[pipeline] body top_k=6  search_params top_k=3     => SQL LIMIT=3   ✗\n[legacy]   body top_k=6  search_params top_k=None  => SQL LIMIT=6   ✓\n```\n\nSo `PostFilterResults` ran with none of the headroom its own comment\nsays the overfetch provides. The #700 FTS reservation does **not**\ncompensate — it adds a separately capped `union` branch, it does not\nwiden the main `LIMIT`, which is also why\n`test_fts_only_row_survives_a_saturated_candidate_window` passes either\nway.\n\n### What it cost users\n\nThe candidate window is ordered by `score`, which blends `weight` into\n`similarity` — so rows **below** the cosine floor can legitimately fill\nthe whole window and then be discarded by the `min_similarity`\npost-filter, with nothing behind them to backfill.\n\nThe new test builds exactly that: 3 rows at cosine 0.28 / weight 1.0\noutranking 4 rows at cosine 0.45 / weight 0.0. Before this change the\npipeline path returned **0 of 3** requested results. After: **3**.\n\n## The fix, at both ends\n\n- **`ExecuteScoredSearch`** no longer sends `top_k` inside\n`search_params`. It stays in `ctx.data` — `ClassifyQuery` (`:73`,\n`:199`) and the `_ALLOWED_OVERRIDES` merge both read it — so this is a\nprojection at the boundary, not a removal at the source.\n- **`postgres_service`** takes the LIMIT from the `top_k` parameter\nonly. This is the end that actually makes it correct, and it is\nskew-safe in the useful direction: an older core-api that still nests a\n`top_k` now gets the wider window it always meant to ask for.\n\nEither end alone fixes all four deploy-skew combinations. The strip is\nthere so the payload says what we mean, not for skew.\n\nAlso restores diagnostic mode's `max(top_k, 50)`, defeated by the same\nshadowing — though that mode turns out to be **half-wired** and\nunreachable (`SearchRequest.diagnostic` is declared but never read by a\nroute; nothing writes `diagnostic_results`). Noted in a comment for\nwhoever reconnects it, including that the branch skips `final_top_k` so\n`TrackRecalls` would bump `recall_count` for all 50 rows.\n\n## Why no ranking benchmark, unlike #722\n\n**This change cannot reorder results — it can only append.** The\nprevious candidates were the top-N by score; the wider set is a\nsuperset, and every row that joins ranks *below* all of them, so it\ncannot displace anything. After filtering and trimming, the old results\nare retained in the same order and the new ones fill positions the\npost-filter had emptied.\n\nThat is categorically different from #722, which changed the score\nitself and therefore reordered everything. My handoff had said this\nwanted a LoCoMo run; on inspection `benchmark_blend_locomo.py`\nexplicitly holds the candidate LIMIT out of scope (\"Those are held out\non purpose\"), so it is the wrong instrument. The evidence here is the\nappend-only argument plus the full suite: **4162 passed** with every\nexisting ranking assertion (freshness ordering, expired-lower,\nrecall-boost decay, blend) unchanged.\n\n`TrackRecalls` reads the **trimmed** `filtered_rows`, so `recall_count`\nbumps — and the A26 feedback loop — are unaffected on every reachable\npath.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Three tests, each parametrised over both paths, each watched failing\n**on the pipeline path only** before the fix, and re-verified as still\nfailing after the `/simplify` restructure: payload delivery, the SQL\n`LIMIT` actually applied (counts rows storage returned), and the\nuser-visible starvation above. Legacy passes throughout — it was already\ncorrect.\n- `tests/` **4162 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed. Baseline\nmeasured on a detached `origin/main` checkout: **4156** → +6 is exactly\nthis PR's cases.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` 104, unchanged. Ruff clean at all of CI's\nexact scopes; `F821` over `scripts/` clean; broker OpenAPI regenerated\nwith no diff.\n\n`/simplify`: applied 6 findings — most usefully, a comment #723 left in\n`_search_memories_legacy` that described the old storage read and called\nthis fix \"pending\", and the `_top_k` local, which named the LIMIT as if\nit came from `search_params` like every other `_`-prefixed local in that\nblock. Both were exactly the misreading this change exists to remove.\n\n## Follow-up, deliberately not here\n\nStripping one key does not make the nested dict scoring-knobs-only:\n`min_similarity` and `graph_max_hops` are core-api-local too and still\nride along (storage reads neither). The generalisation is to project an\nexplicit allowlist shared with the legacy builder — which would also let\n`test_scoring_knobs_reach_the_sql_on_both_search_paths` assert set\n*equality* instead of per-key presence. Left out because several tests\nconstruct partial `search_params`, so a strict projection needs its own\npass.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"2bea569d2ba86d7b8f9fdcc8d50bc8bc31b38fbf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/2bea569d2ba86d7b8f9fdcc8d50bc8bc31b38fbf"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdFezCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsbAQAJ+kw3uP80DDHjxMJGfoEloL\n+72IXzc4seUAoYN8ga2rEZWbFCewHkfyLTJ5DO/ALQnEobO4ZdXpu8vKyD4wODni\n0+ml2FE4q5rSkY5nambi+B+fwmw9+Rqp00Bue/RgAJtG4BF4rz9RVsdT6LYEATJH\nEcF/zELu6CP3MPbS9Khg+qbCRkQgqiLMRE0YSbvPLnwq/Kikg+Vrv6+BKYUVb/Br\nHiN1V92942mIw6vfP1tyHvjrLJoJjikJej02uY+R/pFmYwuYUp2jyuIAOE6ZmLcS\nN5SYQ8bEO828yH4rO9LzQunCCgHD2I5D7MuqmkyOtJkAI49OLB5dFurYg/heHdrC\nSxaSuj1JpQ1E4akQeHRIk5yp6Z5LLElDnm60U52xgoBOae4Dnh7Ght6z8JbA65E9\n/mMxinrutDVkfk1I+c5PSPEWK/pURJz/0BJK7s0k+IBQQyBJpFTBQL1fPYnNfdFB\n77AQp7IhgPH1HOOk1aTnQ7SBPLDCvZHbjU4rl5VvWh9a3vWgiRHJSIZfM5EooEv6\nljuNFQ++gHYP9XdgaacWiM1c7i+LRc27WLBexxEqhMY9w0QRGyduPz1SaGh4OfpB\nTe1QeTDlduZRp0Q4f4rXrusJmjBL4uvKCnpFzXQjl64gc6rDs3DduH1AmAHU5uWG\n1VQKlcJ+S2+HWqcD2R02\n=6ld/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 2bea569d2ba86d7b8f9fdcc8d50bc8bc31b38fbf\nparent 052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786009523 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786009523 +0300\n\nfix(search): let SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR reach the SQL LIMIT (#725)\n\nFollow-up to #723, which found this. `claude-review` on that PR\nindependently flagged it at **High** severity.\n\n## The bug\n\n`SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR` was **inert on the pipeline search path** —\nthe active one (`_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH = True`).\n\nThree pieces, each correct in isolation:\n\n1. `resolve_search_profile` puts the caller's **unmultiplied** `top_k`\ninto `search_params`.\n2. `ExecuteScoredSearch` multiplies only a **local** variable, then\nsends that as the top-level `top_k`.\n3. `postgres_service` read the LIMIT as `sp.get(\"top_k\", top_k)` —\npreferring the nested one.\n\nMeasured with a spy, caller `top_k=3`, factor 2:\n\n```\n[pipeline] body top_k=6  search_params top_k=3     => SQL LIMIT=3   ✗\n[legacy]   body top_k=6  search_params top_k=None  => SQL LIMIT=6   ✓\n```\n\nSo `PostFilterResults` ran with none of the headroom its own comment\nsays the overfetch provides. The #700 FTS reservation does **not**\ncompensate — it adds a separately capped `union` branch, it does not\nwiden the main `LIMIT`, which is also why\n`test_fts_only_row_survives_a_saturated_candidate_window` passes either\nway.\n\n### What it cost users\n\nThe candidate window is ordered by `score`, which blends `weight` into\n`similarity` — so rows **below** the cosine floor can legitimately fill\nthe whole window and then be discarded by the `min_similarity`\npost-filter, with nothing behind them to backfill.\n\nThe new test builds exactly that: 3 rows at cosine 0.28 / weight 1.0\noutranking 4 rows at cosine 0.45 / weight 0.0. Before this change the\npipeline path returned **0 of 3** requested results. After: **3**.\n\n## The fix, at both ends\n\n- **`ExecuteScoredSearch`** no longer sends `top_k` inside\n`search_params`. It stays in `ctx.data` — `ClassifyQuery` (`:73`,\n`:199`) and the `_ALLOWED_OVERRIDES` merge both read it — so this is a\nprojection at the boundary, not a removal at the source.\n- **`postgres_service`** takes the LIMIT from the `top_k` parameter\nonly. This is the end that actually makes it correct, and it is\nskew-safe in the useful direction: an older core-api that still nests a\n`top_k` now gets the wider window it always meant to ask for.\n\nEither end alone fixes all four deploy-skew combinations. The strip is\nthere so the payload says what we mean, not for skew.\n\nAlso restores diagnostic mode's `max(top_k, 50)`, defeated by the same\nshadowing — though that mode turns out to be **half-wired** and\nunreachable (`SearchRequest.diagnostic` is declared but never read by a\nroute; nothing writes `diagnostic_results`). Noted in a comment for\nwhoever reconnects it, including that the branch skips `final_top_k` so\n`TrackRecalls` would bump `recall_count` for all 50 rows.\n\n## Why no ranking benchmark, unlike #722\n\n**This change cannot reorder results — it can only append.** The\nprevious candidates were the top-N by score; the wider set is a\nsuperset, and every row that joins ranks *below* all of them, so it\ncannot displace anything. After filtering and trimming, the old results\nare retained in the same order and the new ones fill positions the\npost-filter had emptied.\n\nThat is categorically different from #722, which changed the score\nitself and therefore reordered everything. My handoff had said this\nwanted a LoCoMo run; on inspection `benchmark_blend_locomo.py`\nexplicitly holds the candidate LIMIT out of scope (\"Those are held out\non purpose\"), so it is the wrong instrument. The evidence here is the\nappend-only argument plus the full suite: **4162 passed** with every\nexisting ranking assertion (freshness ordering, expired-lower,\nrecall-boost decay, blend) unchanged.\n\n`TrackRecalls` reads the **trimmed** `filtered_rows`, so `recall_count`\nbumps — and the A26 feedback loop — are unaffected on every reachable\npath.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Three tests, each parametrised over both paths, each watched failing\n**on the pipeline path only** before the fix, and re-verified as still\nfailing after the `/simplify` restructure: payload delivery, the SQL\n`LIMIT` actually applied (counts rows storage returned), and the\nuser-visible starvation above. Legacy passes throughout — it was already\ncorrect.\n- `tests/` **4162 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed. Baseline\nmeasured on a detached `origin/main` checkout: **4156** → +6 is exactly\nthis PR's cases.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` 104, unchanged. Ruff clean at all of CI's\nexact scopes; `F821` over `scripts/` clean; broker OpenAPI regenerated\nwith no diff.\n\n`/simplify`: applied 6 findings — most usefully, a comment #723 left in\n`_search_memories_legacy` that described the old storage read and called\nthis fix \"pending\", and the `_top_k` local, which named the LIMIT as if\nit came from `search_params` like every other `_`-prefixed local in that\nblock. Both were exactly the misreading this change exists to remove.\n\n## Follow-up, deliberately not here\n\nStripping one key does not make the nested dict scoring-knobs-only:\n`min_similarity` and `graph_max_hops` are core-api-local too and still\nride along (storage reads neither). The generalisation is to project an\nexplicit allowlist shared with the legacy builder — which would also let\n`test_scoring_knobs_reach_the_sql_on_both_search_paths` assert set\n*equality* instead of per-key presence. Left out because several tests\nconstruct partial `search_params`, so a strict projection needs its own\npass.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-06T09:45:26Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/f94572a2687110c82a43368c92b46ccf68e1b6ec/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0"}]},{"sha":"052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDA1MmQ3MDA3NTVlOTdhNjAxMmIxMjdhOWEwYjU4MjE2ZGRmYTY1YzA","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-06T07:24:13Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-06T07:24:13Z"},"message":"fix(embedding): let bulk callers pass their budget so a slow provider is attributable (#724)\n\nThe last of the follow-ups from #721.\n\n## The problem, and why it was structural\n\nCallers enforce their embed deadlines by **cancelling** the call. A\n`CancelledError` says only \"time ran out\" — not which layer ate it. The\nprovider call itself was unbounded, so a slow backend surfaced as the\ncaller's anonymous timeout with nothing naming the embed.\n\nAt the strong-embed budget that isn't just untidy:\n\n```\nBULK_STRONG_EMBED_TIMEOUT_SECONDS   8s   ← the caller's budget\nOPENAI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS     25s   ← what ONE provider request may take\n```\n\nThat budget could **never** be the cap that fired. The module already\napplies the opposite principle to its own concurrency gate —\n`EMBEDDING_GATE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` sits *\"deliberately BELOW the callers'\ndeadlines … so the gate could attribute the failure\"* — the provider\ncall was simply never brought under it.\n\n## The change\n\n`get_embeddings_batch` takes an optional keyword `budget_s`. Given it,\nthe provider call is bounded at `budget_s - EMBEDDING_BUDGET_MARGIN_S`,\nso the inner cap fires first and raises `TimeoutError` **from inside the\nembed layer**. Omitting it keeps the previous behaviour — right for the\ntwo auto-chunk call sites that have no deadline of their own to sit\nunder.\n\nOne margin constant rather than a subtraction per call site, so the\nordering survives an operator retuning the budgets.\n\nThis **narrows the window rather than closing it**: a caller can still\ncancel for its own reasons, so the `BaseException` catch that counts\nthose stays.\n\n## Cost worth naming\n\nSeven test doubles for `get_embeddings_batch` needed updating, and\n**four tests failed loudly until they did**. A keyword-only argument on\na widely-mocked function has real blast radius even when production\ncallers are unaffected. I updated the doubles to mirror the real\nsignature rather than loosening them to `**kwargs`, so the next\nsignature change fails the same way instead of silently passing.\n\n## On the test\n\nThe first version of the attribution test **passed with the fix\nreverted** — both caps raise `TimeoutError`, so the exception type can't\ntell them apart. It was worthless and I caught it by reverting.\n\nIt now asserts on **elapsed time**: an inner budget 25x tighter than the\ncaller's deadline, failing if the caller's is what stopped it.\n\n```\nwith fix:     0.87s   ✅\nwithout fix:  5.70s   ❌  \"took 5.70s, which means the caller's 5s deadline was what stopped it\"\n```\n\nA second test pins that omitting `budget_s` imposes no cap of its own.\n\n## Context from the load test\n\nWorth recording since it sizes the margin: driving staging TEI from\ninside the VPC at the gate's own max concurrency (16), a 100-item\nwrite's chunked round trips measured **p95 0.394s, worst 0.428s**. A 1s\nmargin is generous against that without meaningfully shortening the\nuseful budget.\n\nThat same run answered #721's open load-test question, and refutes the\npremise of its Medium finding — chunking does **not** cost ~4x hold\ntime, because per-request latency scales with request size:\n\n| concurrency | unchunked p95 | chunked p95 | ratio |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 0.106s | 0.174s | 1.64x |\n| 4 | 0.264s | 0.333s | 1.26x |\n| 8 | 0.543s | 0.567s | 1.04x |\n| **16** | 0.988s | **0.926s** | **0.94x** |\n\nAt the concurrency the gate actually permits, chunking is *faster* —\nsmaller requests interleave better across instances. Zero errors at\nevery level. Staging TEI's shape (min 2 / max 3 / concurrency 10) is\nidentical to prod's, so this is a faithful proxy.\n\n## Checks\n\n`ruff check common/ core-api/src/` · `ruff format --check` — clean.\nFull `pytest tests/ -m \"not benchmark\"`: **4108 passed**; 26 failures +\n8 errors byte-identical to pristine `main` (no local Postgres). Zero\nregressions. `uv.lock` untouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"dda19f810ae4c6e3ba4e6198882dec4a86bfa4a8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/dda19f810ae4c6e3ba4e6198882dec4a86bfa4a8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdDadCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAzaAQAEGNHY0p/Xu6YZVP5KFnsNbO\nsc1DqCq2CAZxUepFYpS3NJ8472UILRA56dC6Q2oC/Jgvx0HJfSTCr6q6lSZTJJmP\nXP5FO++syT2KLqcGLNsxp370/sfdfo6fwx2yfsxa8ZCUJ1em5kJ1YNDHpooJLRtU\nNwYFksm5KLLpD3xMXNjHzn3HOVkJo29eOur9w+6h7JTpWkr0AAltCVu+DRa9k6by\nPB8udav+9mX2CvqhIMOmW+rIaGxrxNta8jGg/DfW/UAOsmpwIkV1/jeUs7tauPbK\nN7CVLG4z6eQUdvGNGbEpYYDZknaQGo81uF3SJslv7AX+LSl4SFYIbqO3svPq/8aE\nCojb6TqM+g74rIXbH5xqwTt+w7ydU0AtKKXuB2mpkuXDWA8WQg51XK1IZL7T6+Jq\nXEoHhHFYU2bo9ygHhf7lDUInES9K4uQQJY7RYk/EGIyqTXEkDyUNvMF2avJU9Tj5\nC7Vi93D3h/2YC4jpcjHAxVmIOMRxupiLtJnJAmNjxQxZlGD20w4t+s1JtZOvGqOr\nHrMDJoMVzW0muwo8TP543ULhKSuXd3Z/W3fU3HEKTTw6ftYgEIyP31wiet0yYnUD\nqpa8DOLE91f2KX3qW/UwcvS61cb3hLygB20ep8hh2kQCnG+FouMohx1abL8rXx5D\n4AwQzzjK7h+Vh+B5EHPw\n=BoeT\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree dda19f810ae4c6e3ba4e6198882dec4a86bfa4a8\nparent 9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1786001053 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786001053 +0300\n\nfix(embedding): let bulk callers pass their budget so a slow provider is attributable (#724)\n\nThe last of the follow-ups from #721.\n\n## The problem, and why it was structural\n\nCallers enforce their embed deadlines by **cancelling** the call. A\n`CancelledError` says only \"time ran out\" — not which layer ate it. The\nprovider call itself was unbounded, so a slow backend surfaced as the\ncaller's anonymous timeout with nothing naming the embed.\n\nAt the strong-embed budget that isn't just untidy:\n\n```\nBULK_STRONG_EMBED_TIMEOUT_SECONDS   8s   ← the caller's budget\nOPENAI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS     25s   ← what ONE provider request may take\n```\n\nThat budget could **never** be the cap that fired. The module already\napplies the opposite principle to its own concurrency gate —\n`EMBEDDING_GATE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` sits *\"deliberately BELOW the callers'\ndeadlines … so the gate could attribute the failure\"* — the provider\ncall was simply never brought under it.\n\n## The change\n\n`get_embeddings_batch` takes an optional keyword `budget_s`. Given it,\nthe provider call is bounded at `budget_s - EMBEDDING_BUDGET_MARGIN_S`,\nso the inner cap fires first and raises `TimeoutError` **from inside the\nembed layer**. Omitting it keeps the previous behaviour — right for the\ntwo auto-chunk call sites that have no deadline of their own to sit\nunder.\n\nOne margin constant rather than a subtraction per call site, so the\nordering survives an operator retuning the budgets.\n\nThis **narrows the window rather than closing it**: a caller can still\ncancel for its own reasons, so the `BaseException` catch that counts\nthose stays.\n\n## Cost worth naming\n\nSeven test doubles for `get_embeddings_batch` needed updating, and\n**four tests failed loudly until they did**. A keyword-only argument on\na widely-mocked function has real blast radius even when production\ncallers are unaffected. I updated the doubles to mirror the real\nsignature rather than loosening them to `**kwargs`, so the next\nsignature change fails the same way instead of silently passing.\n\n## On the test\n\nThe first version of the attribution test **passed with the fix\nreverted** — both caps raise `TimeoutError`, so the exception type can't\ntell them apart. It was worthless and I caught it by reverting.\n\nIt now asserts on **elapsed time**: an inner budget 25x tighter than the\ncaller's deadline, failing if the caller's is what stopped it.\n\n```\nwith fix:     0.87s   ✅\nwithout fix:  5.70s   ❌  \"took 5.70s, which means the caller's 5s deadline was what stopped it\"\n```\n\nA second test pins that omitting `budget_s` imposes no cap of its own.\n\n## Context from the load test\n\nWorth recording since it sizes the margin: driving staging TEI from\ninside the VPC at the gate's own max concurrency (16), a 100-item\nwrite's chunked round trips measured **p95 0.394s, worst 0.428s**. A 1s\nmargin is generous against that without meaningfully shortening the\nuseful budget.\n\nThat same run answered #721's open load-test question, and refutes the\npremise of its Medium finding — chunking does **not** cost ~4x hold\ntime, because per-request latency scales with request size:\n\n| concurrency | unchunked p95 | chunked p95 | ratio |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 0.106s | 0.174s | 1.64x |\n| 4 | 0.264s | 0.333s | 1.26x |\n| 8 | 0.543s | 0.567s | 1.04x |\n| **16** | 0.988s | **0.926s** | **0.94x** |\n\nAt the concurrency the gate actually permits, chunking is *faster* —\nsmaller requests interleave better across instances. Zero errors at\nevery level. Staging TEI's shape (min 2 / max 3 / concurrency 10) is\nidentical to prod's, so this is a faithful proxy.\n\n## Checks\n\n`ruff check common/ core-api/src/` · `ruff format --check` — clean.\nFull `pytest tests/ -m \"not benchmark\"`: **4108 passed**; 26 failures +\n8 errors byte-identical to pristine `main` (no local Postgres). Zero\nregressions. `uv.lock` untouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-06T07:24:15Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/052d700755e97a6012b127a9a0b58216ddfa65c0/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8"}]},{"sha":"9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDk1OTBjNGNlODI2ZDljNDdmMzFkMDU5NTM0NTVkNjkwOWRiNzlkYjg","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-05T21:43:54Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-05T21:43:54Z"},"message":"fix(search): deliver every scoring knob to the SQL on both search paths (#723)\n\n## The bug\n\n`candidate_pool_size` (A49) and `score_formula` (A50) applied on the\n**pipeline search path only**.\n\nThree places had to agree, and each was invisible to the other two:\n\n| | builds | sent both keys? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `resolve_search_profile` | pipeline's nested `search_params` | ✅ |\n| `_search_memories_legacy` | its own payload, keys **flat** | ❌ |\n| storage `/scored-search` route | rebuilt `search_params` from a\nhardcoded **allowlist** when no nested dict was present | ❌ |\n\nSo on the legacy path both features silently vanished, and the\ndocumented `_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH = False` rollback lever\n(`memory_service.py`) reverted two ranking features as a side effect.\n#722 was the third time that allowlist bit — and a test caught it, not\nreview, because the allowlist was the one registration point **no\nclient-boundary assertion can see**: the nesting happens server-side.\n\n## The fix\n\nNot \"add the two names to the allowlist\". `_search_memories_legacy` was\nthe only non-test flat-key sender left in the repo — every storage test\nalready posts nested `search_params` — so it now sends nested like the\npipeline. That makes the allowlist dead code:\n\n- **deleted** `_SEARCH_PARAM_KEYS` and the flat rebuild;\n- the route now **requires** `search_params`, via a new `_require_dict`\nin the shared `routers/_validation.py` (422). Fail-closed rather than\ndefaulting, because `memory_scored_search` reads six of those keys with\n**indexed** access — so a payload without them is malformed, and 4xx at\nthe edge beats the KeyError 500 it would otherwise raise from inside the\nsession.\n\nThree registration points become two.\n\n### `top_k` is deliberately not nested\n\nStorage reads the SQL LIMIT as `search_params.get(\"top_k\", top_k)`, so a\n`top_k` in the nested dict **shadows** the overfetched body-level value.\nNesting it would have silently narrowed the legacy candidate window from\n`top_k * SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR` to `top_k`. Measured, `top_k=3`:\n\n```\n[legacy]   body top_k=6  search_params top_k=None  => SQL LIMIT=6   ✅ overfetch intact\n```\n\n## Second instance of the same class, found on the way\n\nThe legacy payload sent the per-memory boost factors as the **value of\n`boosted_memory_ids`** and never sent `memory_boost_factor` at all. The\nSQL gates the entire entity boost on `if boosted_memory_ids and\nmemory_boost_factor` (`postgres_service.py:1342`), so it saw a falsy\nfactor map and **skipped the whole stack** — graph expansion and hop\nboosts were computed on every legacy search and then discarded at the\nstorage boundary. Measured before the fix:\n\n```\n[pipeline] ids={...} factors={UUID(...): 1.5}  => entity_boost_applies=True\n[legacy]   ids={...} factors=None              => entity_boost_applies=False\n```\n\n`tests/test_entity_retrieval_flag.py` asserted the buggy shape\n(`boosted_memory_ids == {str(mid): 1.3}`) at the client boundary, which\nis why it passed while the SQL skipped the boost. Updated to assert both\nkeys.\n\n## Verification\n\nTests are parametrised over **both** paths and assert against the dict\n`PostgresService.memory_scored_search` actually receives — not the\nclient boundary, which cannot see this.\n\nEvery test was watched **failing on the legacy path for the stated\nreason** before the fix (3 quiet knobs at defaults and at tuned values,\nthe boost pair, the route guard); pipeline cases pass throughout, since\nthat path was never broken.\n\n- `tests/` — **4144 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed. Baseline\nmeasured on a detached `origin/main` checkout: **4140** → +4 is exactly\nthis PR's new cases.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` — **104 passed** (baseline 103 → +1).\n- `ruff check` / `ruff format --check` clean at all of CI's exact\nscopes, plus `--select F821` over `scripts/`.\n- `core-api/openapi.broker.json` regenerated: **no diff**.\n\n`/simplify` run: 9 findings applied (reuse the shared `_require_dict`\nrather than an 8th inline guard; consolidated a rationale that had been\nduplicated across 4 files; merged 8 integration cases into 4; corrected\na comment that overclaimed which keys it covered). 4 skipped as\nout-of-diff or behaviour-affecting — see \"Deliberately not in this PR\".\n\n## Deliberately not in this PR\n\n1. **`SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR` is inert on the pipeline path** — a live\ndefect this PR's `top_k` reasoning uncovered. `resolve_search_profile`\nputs the un-overfetched `top_k` in `search_params`;\n`execute_scored_search` overfetches only the body-level value; storage\nprefers the nested one. Measured: `caller top_k=3, factor=2 → SQL\nLIMIT=3`, so `PostFilterResults` has none of the headroom its comment\nsays it has. Widening it changes recall on every query, so it wants its\nown benchmark and review — same treatment #722 got.\n2. **Extracting one shared `search_params` builder.** Two hand-built\ndicts remain, and they have *already* drifted: the A47\n`tenant_config.default_search_profile` merge exists only in\n`resolve_search_profile`, so a per-tenant default never reaches the\nlegacy path. Restructuring both builders is outside this diff and\nbehaviour-affecting.\n3. **Adopting the dead `ScoredSearchRequest` schema** (`schemas.py:260`)\n— it already declares this endpoint's contract, including\n`search_params` as required, and has zero references. Adopting it is the\ndeeper fix for the guard, but it changes the 422 body shape and touches\nevery field parse in a hot handler.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"348fa98909910396db06ecc80e279667d6748509","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/348fa98909910396db06ecc80e279667d6748509"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqc66aCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAK/sQAFxkZdLzzLXq7lMjX0s8lIUC\nHj5aR6UrkEgI8TtHx+qSfOP8JlsddCJeNsFYp03bVg8JMx3GubqTy0lztiEQnHNj\njxqRMZIWF21vbiTxPo9mK5xdn98kHboTo9ke21MLApuQ196XdxzhfyRWZVP8Y1i7\nbPCNwax4bjFJi1R0unDPuy5feraBnBZLTNxf+45lCElPbxa/udRpDoDFv4ZU7qHa\nfR08mDLZiwLc2n9X2kwQSZ3AN99k8J+JDpBUSfzA9RtzJMkuM0PZ9P5IhIeq2SxY\nGDSBNFb5WzKL9fnxClTmjaEFtIkO8K05NVZIcrhlBVYK7ueG4NI7CTOWRoVjrW3e\nQKlCnf97JyR50V7JdBFnJ5+pZKgQSkflmvPAujum/H2qCg0OFGYpGgC9Rqxw4OoL\nlxNGlXUQs8thUFUSU0W/PapcZE/ZtlPNFKn5ocmP05Ws2pVgQ/FppuhCZCHMR2A8\nCB0Z61KjMjfC+hQLDg1xbZ8ATPYn7mZCcNQE9bnq3s9gJRd+WgnGX6ReU8zkM7fY\nm/Vvwm3+lzN0R9lTn/+W5kkiQzd2y38T2r0z/a44ZZq9t4eu9RhPEP1SIOlPVEml\n5sjc06wNlIXFRlS7/PuBS3Mg3zD5VEyifKp4ds6Q4HUwWsybQnJqPOz0QzyRpbfX\n2cQpXVhgSe2Kvzrcolj/\n=Qquc\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 348fa98909910396db06ecc80e279667d6748509\nparent d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785966234 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785966234 +0300\n\nfix(search): deliver every scoring knob to the SQL on both search paths (#723)\n\n## The bug\n\n`candidate_pool_size` (A49) and `score_formula` (A50) applied on the\n**pipeline search path only**.\n\nThree places had to agree, and each was invisible to the other two:\n\n| | builds | sent both keys? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `resolve_search_profile` | pipeline's nested `search_params` | ✅ |\n| `_search_memories_legacy` | its own payload, keys **flat** | ❌ |\n| storage `/scored-search` route | rebuilt `search_params` from a\nhardcoded **allowlist** when no nested dict was present | ❌ |\n\nSo on the legacy path both features silently vanished, and the\ndocumented `_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH = False` rollback lever\n(`memory_service.py`) reverted two ranking features as a side effect.\n#722 was the third time that allowlist bit — and a test caught it, not\nreview, because the allowlist was the one registration point **no\nclient-boundary assertion can see**: the nesting happens server-side.\n\n## The fix\n\nNot \"add the two names to the allowlist\". `_search_memories_legacy` was\nthe only non-test flat-key sender left in the repo — every storage test\nalready posts nested `search_params` — so it now sends nested like the\npipeline. That makes the allowlist dead code:\n\n- **deleted** `_SEARCH_PARAM_KEYS` and the flat rebuild;\n- the route now **requires** `search_params`, via a new `_require_dict`\nin the shared `routers/_validation.py` (422). Fail-closed rather than\ndefaulting, because `memory_scored_search` reads six of those keys with\n**indexed** access — so a payload without them is malformed, and 4xx at\nthe edge beats the KeyError 500 it would otherwise raise from inside the\nsession.\n\nThree registration points become two.\n\n### `top_k` is deliberately not nested\n\nStorage reads the SQL LIMIT as `search_params.get(\"top_k\", top_k)`, so a\n`top_k` in the nested dict **shadows** the overfetched body-level value.\nNesting it would have silently narrowed the legacy candidate window from\n`top_k * SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR` to `top_k`. Measured, `top_k=3`:\n\n```\n[legacy]   body top_k=6  search_params top_k=None  => SQL LIMIT=6   ✅ overfetch intact\n```\n\n## Second instance of the same class, found on the way\n\nThe legacy payload sent the per-memory boost factors as the **value of\n`boosted_memory_ids`** and never sent `memory_boost_factor` at all. The\nSQL gates the entire entity boost on `if boosted_memory_ids and\nmemory_boost_factor` (`postgres_service.py:1342`), so it saw a falsy\nfactor map and **skipped the whole stack** — graph expansion and hop\nboosts were computed on every legacy search and then discarded at the\nstorage boundary. Measured before the fix:\n\n```\n[pipeline] ids={...} factors={UUID(...): 1.5}  => entity_boost_applies=True\n[legacy]   ids={...} factors=None              => entity_boost_applies=False\n```\n\n`tests/test_entity_retrieval_flag.py` asserted the buggy shape\n(`boosted_memory_ids == {str(mid): 1.3}`) at the client boundary, which\nis why it passed while the SQL skipped the boost. Updated to assert both\nkeys.\n\n## Verification\n\nTests are parametrised over **both** paths and assert against the dict\n`PostgresService.memory_scored_search` actually receives — not the\nclient boundary, which cannot see this.\n\nEvery test was watched **failing on the legacy path for the stated\nreason** before the fix (3 quiet knobs at defaults and at tuned values,\nthe boost pair, the route guard); pipeline cases pass throughout, since\nthat path was never broken.\n\n- `tests/` — **4144 passed**, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 xpassed. Baseline\nmeasured on a detached `origin/main` checkout: **4140** → +4 is exactly\nthis PR's new cases.\n- `core-storage-api/tests/` — **104 passed** (baseline 103 → +1).\n- `ruff check` / `ruff format --check` clean at all of CI's exact\nscopes, plus `--select F821` over `scripts/`.\n- `core-api/openapi.broker.json` regenerated: **no diff**.\n\n`/simplify` run: 9 findings applied (reuse the shared `_require_dict`\nrather than an 8th inline guard; consolidated a rationale that had been\nduplicated across 4 files; merged 8 integration cases into 4; corrected\na comment that overclaimed which keys it covered). 4 skipped as\nout-of-diff or behaviour-affecting — see \"Deliberately not in this PR\".\n\n## Deliberately not in this PR\n\n1. **`SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR` is inert on the pipeline path** — a live\ndefect this PR's `top_k` reasoning uncovered. `resolve_search_profile`\nputs the un-overfetched `top_k` in `search_params`;\n`execute_scored_search` overfetches only the body-level value; storage\nprefers the nested one. Measured: `caller top_k=3, factor=2 → SQL\nLIMIT=3`, so `PostFilterResults` has none of the headroom its comment\nsays it has. Widening it changes recall on every query, so it wants its\nown benchmark and review — same treatment #722 got.\n2. **Extracting one shared `search_params` builder.** Two hand-built\ndicts remain, and they have *already* drifted: the A47\n`tenant_config.default_search_profile` merge exists only in\n`resolve_search_profile`, so a per-tenant default never reaches the\nlegacy path. Restructuring both builders is outside this diff and\nbehaviour-affecting.\n3. **Adopting the dead `ScoredSearchRequest` schema** (`schemas.py:260`)\n— it already declares this endpoint's contract, including\n`search_params` as required, and has zero references. Adopting it is the\ndeeper fix for the guard, but it changes the 422 body shape and touches\nevery field parse in a hot handler.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-05T21:43:56Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9590c4ce826d9c47f31d05953455d6909db79db8/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315"}]},{"sha":"d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGQ4MTJhOWRiNzkwM2ZlNTMxYjRkMjQ2Y2RhNzE1M2IxOTgxNzUzMTU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-05T17:05:34Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-05T17:05:34Z"},"message":"fix(embedding): chunk bulk embeds to the backend's batch cap, and report bulk-only outages (#721)\n\n## The premise changed once I checked prod\n\nThe task was \"make the embedding fallback audible — one warning when a\nbulk embed fails and cascades to per-item, so this can never again be\ninvisible.\"\n\n**The fallback was already audible.** `memory_service.py:2080` logs at\nERROR with a full traceback, and the traceback ends in:\n\n```\nopenai.APIStatusError: Error code: 413 - {'message': 'batch size 50 > maximum allowed batch size 32', 'code': 413, 'type': 'Validation'}\n```\n\n87 of those in prod over 30 days, structured JSON, with `dd.trace_id`.\nThe batch size, the cap, and the diagnosis were all already in our own\nlogs. Nobody was reading them. So adding a second log line would have\nbeen the one thing guaranteed not to help.\n\nChecking the codebase turned up what was actually missing.\n\n## Finding 1 — the real fix is chunking, and the sibling module already\nshipped it\n\n`common/ranking` hit **the same 413, from the same sidecar**, and fixed\nit in #709: `RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH` (default 32, explicitly \"TEI's own\n`--max-client-batch-size` default\") chunks the candidate pool so the cap\n*cannot* reject it. `tests/test_ranking_remote.py` even asserts against\n`batch size 51 > maximum allowed batch size 32`.\n\nThe embedding provider had no equivalent.\n`OpenAIEmbeddingProvider.embed_batch` sent `input=texts` verbatim, and\ncallers send up to `BULK_MAX_ITEMS` (100) or\n`EMBEDDING_REEMBED_BATCH_SIZE` (50).\n\nSo this adds `EMBEDDING_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH` (default 32) and chunks inside\nthe provider. **This makes the bug impossible rather than merely\naudible** — and it fixes it for every deployment, not just the one where\nsomeone remembered the server flag.\n\nThat includes the shipped OSS stack: `docker-compose.yml` starts TEI\nwith only `--model-id` and `--auto-truncate`, so **any bulk write over\n32 items 413s today** for anyone following `docs/local-embedder.md`.\n\nSequential, not the reranker's concurrent `gather` — `_call_gated` holds\none `EMBEDDING_MAX_CONCURRENCY` slot for the whole call, so fanning out\ninside it would multiply real provider concurrency by the chunk count\nand defeat a cap that exists because of the 2026-07-27 pool exhaustion.\nBulk callers arm 30s where the reranker had 0.5s, so there's no need.\n\n## Finding 2 — the aggregate trip-wire was structurally unable to fire\n\n`_EmbeddingStats` is process-wide and shared by every call path.\ncore-api serves search constantly, so every successful *query* embed\nresets `consecutive_failures`. A 100%-broken bulk path therefore never\naccumulates a streak.\n\nVerified against prod: `\"Embedding service degraded\"` never fired\n**once** in 30 days, while staging fired it repeatedly for an unrelated\ncondition.\n\nFixed with a bulk-only streak that only a bulk success clears, reported\nat WARNING with the batch size. What it adds over the existing ERROR is\nthe **consecutive count** — \"N failures with no bulk success between\"\ndistinguishes systematically-broken from flaky, which a stream of\nindependent tracebacks cannot. It is explicitly *not* the per-occurrence\ndetector; that's the existing ERROR plus the Datadog monitor in\nenterprise#980.\n\n## Scope note\n\nChunking is wider than the task as written. I judged it in scope because\nthe goal was \"this can never again be invisible\" and chunking means it\nnever happens — and because leaving OSS without the fix its own sibling\nmodule already has is the kind of asymmetry that bites the next person.\nSay the word and I'll split it.\n\n## Known limitation, documented in code\n\nThe streak is not keyed on the provider, so in a multi-tenant process\none tenant's healthy bulk success clears another's broken backend — the\nsame masking, one level down. Keying it properly means keying all of\n`_stats` (ranking's `_permanent_scope` is the model), which is a wider\nchange. The docstring says so rather than implying a guarantee the code\ndoesn't give.\n\n## Tests\n\nFour new tests, **each verified to fail without its fix**:\n\n| Test | Claim |\n|---|---|\n| `test_embed_batch_splits_requests_above_max_batch` | 50 texts (the\nincident's exact size) → `[32, 18]`, nothing over the cap |\n| `test_embed_batch_at_or_below_cap_makes_one_request` | fast path\npreserved — chunking costs nothing at ≤32 |\n| `test_embed_batch_preserves_input_order_across_chunks` | `result[i]`\nstill belongs to `texts[i]` (callers zip positionally) |\n| `test_bulk_failure_reports_even_while_single_embeds_succeed` |\ninterleaves a successful single embed after every bulk failure — the\nshape that defeated the shared streak — and asserts\n`consecutive_failures == 0` as a *precondition* so the test can't\nsilently stop exercising the bug |\n\nAlso reviewed and applied: dropped a dead `batch_size` fixture\nparameter, and `_CappedProvider` now subclasses the real\n`FakeEmbeddingProvider` so the stand-in actually satisfies the\n`EmbeddingProvider` protocol.\n\n## Corrections made during review\n\nTwo comments I'd written asserted things that turned out to be false,\nand are fixed:\n- \"keeps log volume off the traffic curve … bounded number of times\" —\nit's a 10x reduction, linear not bounded.\n- A justification citing Vertex behaviour — there is no Vertex embedding\nprovider; `_platform.py` rejects it outright.\n\n## Checks\n\n`ruff check common/` (discovery, no `--config`) · `ruff check\ncore-api/src/` · `ruff format --check --isolated` — clean.\nFull `pytest tests/ -m \"not benchmark\"`: **4090 passed**, and the 26\nfailures + 8 errors are byte-identical to pristine `main` (no local\nPostgres). Zero regressions. `core-api/uv.lock` untouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"908c053626be3f968ee1fbe0cfa7d687bda7242a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/908c053626be3f968ee1fbe0cfa7d687bda7242a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqc21eCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA138QALAFy+ybrxESaVekWrO1obBF\nF9G206w+BqdGth6Is1++jKxH7PwJKG8Yxr0EItfuxwBVOSSpmWvZdu/SWX+2U/fY\nlTG1eb9M6ngK2R3ZCEetCU4sL/VKSBGSVzufilhz3woAQvO4yoZIkE2WgIzXGpep\nje1V+NVH8AhScZ5SP5IIru+x6D/BuK5w1my6nimlVaBkTt+GZEI+4lqqi0w/lXT6\no3qmSrlqWIggZrj/qjjFhevMV4v/iZqhQTw3XxAJwPbnb6JygYWr1XLGfH0CgzuR\nvBrfoFbzqfD81UHeRHZ3eyo8+r0XiqfFTrg9eZ1ifLl1BYZfqjKybHpDy0iT0o/2\nZIrDUqPe6Nt3fsUyrvNZ9tLp0pLI1ioXrdyrplQqrAqC+qStkQcbW/BuT1SeoA13\n53OUdqqCmIenEgaZd2rEA8C3YPMQh0wX2U8lga43wqF7792ir1dx5D/ChVDoT19d\nQzlVlzRng+QxtCp0VChgjFcpG4aKadD3HG6no/Ayy8tCGH7k/eYGfXVtQEM7Npbd\nJB9BGEghQM/UNKoMS0uhlT2SWp0DjpYANDyNBqVrgb45ItIFxgWo+zT3q4Uovpl2\nu4Wm2ZoqUo9FI8aCK8bVltQDgaMWGwka1g4hPFFNql4NiOJrXOe0ofA1fKK2UmNP\nQSkNWTZnGasF8jLzwgHc\n=gv2w\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 908c053626be3f968ee1fbe0cfa7d687bda7242a\nparent 4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785949534 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785949534 +0300\n\nfix(embedding): chunk bulk embeds to the backend's batch cap, and report bulk-only outages (#721)\n\n## The premise changed once I checked prod\n\nThe task was \"make the embedding fallback audible — one warning when a\nbulk embed fails and cascades to per-item, so this can never again be\ninvisible.\"\n\n**The fallback was already audible.** `memory_service.py:2080` logs at\nERROR with a full traceback, and the traceback ends in:\n\n```\nopenai.APIStatusError: Error code: 413 - {'message': 'batch size 50 > maximum allowed batch size 32', 'code': 413, 'type': 'Validation'}\n```\n\n87 of those in prod over 30 days, structured JSON, with `dd.trace_id`.\nThe batch size, the cap, and the diagnosis were all already in our own\nlogs. Nobody was reading them. So adding a second log line would have\nbeen the one thing guaranteed not to help.\n\nChecking the codebase turned up what was actually missing.\n\n## Finding 1 — the real fix is chunking, and the sibling module already\nshipped it\n\n`common/ranking` hit **the same 413, from the same sidecar**, and fixed\nit in #709: `RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH` (default 32, explicitly \"TEI's own\n`--max-client-batch-size` default\") chunks the candidate pool so the cap\n*cannot* reject it. `tests/test_ranking_remote.py` even asserts against\n`batch size 51 > maximum allowed batch size 32`.\n\nThe embedding provider had no equivalent.\n`OpenAIEmbeddingProvider.embed_batch` sent `input=texts` verbatim, and\ncallers send up to `BULK_MAX_ITEMS` (100) or\n`EMBEDDING_REEMBED_BATCH_SIZE` (50).\n\nSo this adds `EMBEDDING_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH` (default 32) and chunks inside\nthe provider. **This makes the bug impossible rather than merely\naudible** — and it fixes it for every deployment, not just the one where\nsomeone remembered the server flag.\n\nThat includes the shipped OSS stack: `docker-compose.yml` starts TEI\nwith only `--model-id` and `--auto-truncate`, so **any bulk write over\n32 items 413s today** for anyone following `docs/local-embedder.md`.\n\nSequential, not the reranker's concurrent `gather` — `_call_gated` holds\none `EMBEDDING_MAX_CONCURRENCY` slot for the whole call, so fanning out\ninside it would multiply real provider concurrency by the chunk count\nand defeat a cap that exists because of the 2026-07-27 pool exhaustion.\nBulk callers arm 30s where the reranker had 0.5s, so there's no need.\n\n## Finding 2 — the aggregate trip-wire was structurally unable to fire\n\n`_EmbeddingStats` is process-wide and shared by every call path.\ncore-api serves search constantly, so every successful *query* embed\nresets `consecutive_failures`. A 100%-broken bulk path therefore never\naccumulates a streak.\n\nVerified against prod: `\"Embedding service degraded\"` never fired\n**once** in 30 days, while staging fired it repeatedly for an unrelated\ncondition.\n\nFixed with a bulk-only streak that only a bulk success clears, reported\nat WARNING with the batch size. What it adds over the existing ERROR is\nthe **consecutive count** — \"N failures with no bulk success between\"\ndistinguishes systematically-broken from flaky, which a stream of\nindependent tracebacks cannot. It is explicitly *not* the per-occurrence\ndetector; that's the existing ERROR plus the Datadog monitor in\nenterprise#980.\n\n## Scope note\n\nChunking is wider than the task as written. I judged it in scope because\nthe goal was \"this can never again be invisible\" and chunking means it\nnever happens — and because leaving OSS without the fix its own sibling\nmodule already has is the kind of asymmetry that bites the next person.\nSay the word and I'll split it.\n\n## Known limitation, documented in code\n\nThe streak is not keyed on the provider, so in a multi-tenant process\none tenant's healthy bulk success clears another's broken backend — the\nsame masking, one level down. Keying it properly means keying all of\n`_stats` (ranking's `_permanent_scope` is the model), which is a wider\nchange. The docstring says so rather than implying a guarantee the code\ndoesn't give.\n\n## Tests\n\nFour new tests, **each verified to fail without its fix**:\n\n| Test | Claim |\n|---|---|\n| `test_embed_batch_splits_requests_above_max_batch` | 50 texts (the\nincident's exact size) → `[32, 18]`, nothing over the cap |\n| `test_embed_batch_at_or_below_cap_makes_one_request` | fast path\npreserved — chunking costs nothing at ≤32 |\n| `test_embed_batch_preserves_input_order_across_chunks` | `result[i]`\nstill belongs to `texts[i]` (callers zip positionally) |\n| `test_bulk_failure_reports_even_while_single_embeds_succeed` |\ninterleaves a successful single embed after every bulk failure — the\nshape that defeated the shared streak — and asserts\n`consecutive_failures == 0` as a *precondition* so the test can't\nsilently stop exercising the bug |\n\nAlso reviewed and applied: dropped a dead `batch_size` fixture\nparameter, and `_CappedProvider` now subclasses the real\n`FakeEmbeddingProvider` so the stand-in actually satisfies the\n`EmbeddingProvider` protocol.\n\n## Corrections made during review\n\nTwo comments I'd written asserted things that turned out to be false,\nand are fixed:\n- \"keeps log volume off the traffic curve … bounded number of times\" —\nit's a 10x reduction, linear not bounded.\n- A justification citing Vertex behaviour — there is no Vertex embedding\nprovider; `_platform.py` rejects it outright.\n\n## Checks\n\n`ruff check common/` (discovery, no `--config`) · `ruff check\ncore-api/src/` · `ruff format --check --isolated` — clean.\nFull `pytest tests/ -m \"not benchmark\"`: **4090 passed**, and the 26\nfailures + 8 errors are byte-identical to pristine `main` (no local\nPostgres). Zero regressions. `core-api/uv.lock` untouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-05T17:05:36Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/d812a9db7903fe531b4d246cda7153b198175315/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066"}]},{"sha":"4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDRmMGViMmIxMzk0MjIwNmM3YmFhMzNkNWIzZWVjMjU2ZTU4MWYwNjY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-05T16:14:59Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-05T16:14:59Z"},"message":"fix(search): put fts_score on the cosine scale (#687) (#722)\n\nCloses the deeper half of #687. #700 made an unembedded FTS-matching row\n*reachable*; it could not make a lexical match **rank** like one.\n\n## The defect\n\n`fts_score = ts_rank_cd/(1+ts_rank_cd)` saturated a modal single-term\nmatch to **0.0909**, while cosine on the same corpus measures\n**0.35–0.39**. Blended as if comparable, the `FTS_WEIGHT = 0.3` this\nrepo declares delivered an **effective ~0.09** — keyword relevance\nstructurally under-weighted on *every* query, not just the\ndeferred-embed ones #687 was filed about.\n\nRoot cause: migration 001 builds `search_vector` as a bare\n`to_tsvector('english', content)` with no `setweight`, so every lexeme\nis weight D = 0.1.\n\n## The fix, and why k=6 is derived rather than picked\n\n`(k*r)/(1+k*r)` with `FTS_RANK_SCALE = 6.0`. Solving `k*0.1/(1+k*0.1) ≈\n0.37` lands a modal match in the cosine range, bringing the effective\nweight to **0.292** against the nominal **0.300**. `FTS_WEIGHT` remains\nthe *preference* between signals; this is the *unit conversion* that\nmakes the preference mean anything.\n\n## Measured before shipping\n\nVia #714's harness — full LoCoMo, **n=1531**, `FTS_WEIGHT=0.3`. `*` =\npaired bootstrap CI excludes zero:\n\n| k | overall recall@5 | fts-matched-only recall@5 | matched-only MRR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | +0.0000 | +0.0000 | +0.0000 |\n| 3 | +0.0035\\* | +0.0211\\* | +0.0080\\* |\n| **6** | **+0.0042\\*** | **+0.0250\\*** | **+0.0185\\*** |\n| 10 | +0.0075\\* | +0.0445\\* | +0.0357\\* |\n| 20 | +0.0113\\* | +0.0672\\* | +0.0692\\* |\n\nRecall improves at every `k > 1`, significantly on all five metrics,\nwith **zero queries regressing** on recall. The sweep rises\nmonotonically across the whole range, so it confirms direction but\n**does not locate an optimum** — 6 is the derivation's answer, not the\nsweep's, and that's recorded beside the constant so nobody bumps it to\n20 citing the benchmark.\n\nTwo honesty caveats a reviewer should hold me to: the calibration is\n**at the mode**, not distributional — `ts_rank_cd`'s cover-density\npenalty isn't a constant factor, so a spread-out multi-term match still\nlands below the band (arguably correct, but \"effective ≈ nominal\"\ndescribes the typical query). And LoCoMo questions are paraphrases, so\nonly ~17% lexically match anything; the matched-only column is the\neffect on queries the change can reach.\n\n## It had to go in four places — the fourth is the interesting one\n\n- `resolve_search_profile` — the pipeline's `search_params`\n- `_search_memories_legacy` — its own, sent **flat** rather than nested\n- **`_SEARCH_PARAM_KEYS` in the storage router** — an allowlist that\nrebuilds `search_params` from flat keys and **silently drops anything\nunnamed**. Adding the key to both core-api builders was *not enough*:\nthe legacy path kept the old ranking until it was named here. **The test\ncaught that, not review.**\n- `postgres_service`, which reads it and defaults to **1.0** when absent\n— so a storage revision deployed ahead of core-api keeps today's ranking\nrather than changing it. Deploys aren't atomic across the two services\nand the safe direction for the gap is \"unchanged\".\n\nThreaded as a per-tenant knob rather than a literal, matching\n`candidate_pool_size` / `score_formula`: `fts_rank_scale` joins\n`_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES` bounded `(1.0, 20.0)`. Floor is 1.0 — the\npre-#687 formula — so a tenant can revert but can't weaken keyword\nrelevance below where it's always been.\n\n## Hot-path cost: nil, verified not assumed\n\nThe compiled SQL renders `ts_rank_cd` **18 times before and after** —\nthe scale multiplies an existing expression object rather than\nduplicating the call. `k` binds once and the SQL text is byte-identical\nfor any `k`, so one plan and one prepared statement regardless of\nper-tenant override. EXPLAIN ANALYZE over 4,795 candidate rows: **36.1\nms scaled vs 37.1 ms unscaled**.\n\n## Verification\n\n| | passed | xfailed | xpassed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 4117 | 1 | 0 |\n| this branch | 4124 | 1 | 0 |\n\n+7 is exactly the added tests, with **no existing test's behaviour\nchanged** — the signal that matters when a change reprices every query.\n\nEach assertion confirmed load-bearing by mutation:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| revert the scale to 1.0 | cosine-scale test fails (`0.3 <= 0.0909`) |\n| drop the key from the pipeline builder | **pipeline** case fails only\n|\n| remove it from the storage allowlist | **legacy** case fails only |\n| widen the bounds floor to 0.0 | bounds test fails (`DID NOT RAISE`) |\n\n## Also corrects #714\n\nThat PR merged one commit earlier and describes `k=1` as \"today's\nformula\" in `BENCHMARKS.md` and three places in the harness — false the\nmoment this lands. Fixed, and the harness's `--k`/`--fts-weight`\ndefaults now come from `core_api.constants` rather than hand-mirrored\nliterals, since the constant's instruction to re-derive it was otherwise\nsilently unenforceable.\n\n## Deliberately out of scope, recorded for follow-up\n\n- **`setweight` is not an alternative.** Measured: uniform `setweight`\nis exactly a constant multiply capped at ×10 (ladder ×1/×2/×4/×10), and\n`ts_rank_cd(weights[])` rejects weights > 1.0 — so **no configuration\nlands in the target band** (needs ×6). A **title/content** weight split\nwould change *relative* ranking and is a genuine opportunity, needing\nits own benchmark.\n- `_SEARCH_PARAM_KEYS` is *also* missing `candidate_pool_size` and\n`score_formula`, so the legacy rollback lever already reverts two other\nranking features. Own diff.\n- `fts_score` renders `ts_rank_cd` twice per row (~14% of the query),\nbut the naive subquery hoist gets flattened by the planner; a real fix\nneeds an optimization barrier and its own plan-shape review.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"22c5fbf2b76b9d1b44728f6086bbd00f44b78b77","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/22c5fbf2b76b9d1b44728f6086bbd00f44b78b77"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqc2GDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAACJgQAEe6zakHbXqI1r6ZQ9OjFYd4\n5nck9Kzihc4GOPKaN0CBmszdQao5xIhSgIQcwPef5R6fgliiIaMeQnvtHA4cCLnJ\nVefZFeDvtAyVbR6aHuniRjQeW6brqIyzJtPJekoWDMP1EuvXkqRrRnpSGJLVvzzq\nk4U5Oxh12uVDwggHd2SiNhlFCbGGQms5PdfN5gy6oUkkT5zGf1NqZOBShssy9Ox4\nYadpGG4t+1Y9et1TregHMN2V0UUZTjf269ja6Vy4w1Ygd2AAd66sKU3qC4YNTqWV\nTmaJgA0IrgsCe6v2hqSJ43z90wBNAlXFntu2TKRWzkh6Fn97OJG7zwQYAo/FLfmw\nFIb7RZpy57S83XxOXhJsMwplbYSLIw9H62zaPRU3J6+ZjSUqiiFP3cD1soLv5T84\n+dK6HZ8tlKHHNqiiRpgELSNb0bfDHotMIsgW/t45UNTGBpXrZ06GQfkgKla3iNGw\nEP10q0Q0xn8K3aei21OFUMhcdv5RrvuAvdhC6+D6PfU/XWS+BXGnVAKLPngrD2BE\nn20BZmynros41BWfz5k87kkpbGQdBkpRDBdjqajDAEWkBNM2UODBllmnJP8WuU3K\nIBdhxYQWpGY0BwgfJjlquL4J/2N4qQy2pf1cIKGIHQ+GfvfBx4W0SHoI8DMihu8n\n8pK2ETujg20bLd1oLSQA\n=VsBU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 22c5fbf2b76b9d1b44728f6086bbd00f44b78b77\nparent f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785946499 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785946499 +0300\n\nfix(search): put fts_score on the cosine scale (#687) (#722)\n\nCloses the deeper half of #687. #700 made an unembedded FTS-matching row\n*reachable*; it could not make a lexical match **rank** like one.\n\n## The defect\n\n`fts_score = ts_rank_cd/(1+ts_rank_cd)` saturated a modal single-term\nmatch to **0.0909**, while cosine on the same corpus measures\n**0.35–0.39**. Blended as if comparable, the `FTS_WEIGHT = 0.3` this\nrepo declares delivered an **effective ~0.09** — keyword relevance\nstructurally under-weighted on *every* query, not just the\ndeferred-embed ones #687 was filed about.\n\nRoot cause: migration 001 builds `search_vector` as a bare\n`to_tsvector('english', content)` with no `setweight`, so every lexeme\nis weight D = 0.1.\n\n## The fix, and why k=6 is derived rather than picked\n\n`(k*r)/(1+k*r)` with `FTS_RANK_SCALE = 6.0`. Solving `k*0.1/(1+k*0.1) ≈\n0.37` lands a modal match in the cosine range, bringing the effective\nweight to **0.292** against the nominal **0.300**. `FTS_WEIGHT` remains\nthe *preference* between signals; this is the *unit conversion* that\nmakes the preference mean anything.\n\n## Measured before shipping\n\nVia #714's harness — full LoCoMo, **n=1531**, `FTS_WEIGHT=0.3`. `*` =\npaired bootstrap CI excludes zero:\n\n| k | overall recall@5 | fts-matched-only recall@5 | matched-only MRR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | +0.0000 | +0.0000 | +0.0000 |\n| 3 | +0.0035\\* | +0.0211\\* | +0.0080\\* |\n| **6** | **+0.0042\\*** | **+0.0250\\*** | **+0.0185\\*** |\n| 10 | +0.0075\\* | +0.0445\\* | +0.0357\\* |\n| 20 | +0.0113\\* | +0.0672\\* | +0.0692\\* |\n\nRecall improves at every `k > 1`, significantly on all five metrics,\nwith **zero queries regressing** on recall. The sweep rises\nmonotonically across the whole range, so it confirms direction but\n**does not locate an optimum** — 6 is the derivation's answer, not the\nsweep's, and that's recorded beside the constant so nobody bumps it to\n20 citing the benchmark.\n\nTwo honesty caveats a reviewer should hold me to: the calibration is\n**at the mode**, not distributional — `ts_rank_cd`'s cover-density\npenalty isn't a constant factor, so a spread-out multi-term match still\nlands below the band (arguably correct, but \"effective ≈ nominal\"\ndescribes the typical query). And LoCoMo questions are paraphrases, so\nonly ~17% lexically match anything; the matched-only column is the\neffect on queries the change can reach.\n\n## It had to go in four places — the fourth is the interesting one\n\n- `resolve_search_profile` — the pipeline's `search_params`\n- `_search_memories_legacy` — its own, sent **flat** rather than nested\n- **`_SEARCH_PARAM_KEYS` in the storage router** — an allowlist that\nrebuilds `search_params` from flat keys and **silently drops anything\nunnamed**. Adding the key to both core-api builders was *not enough*:\nthe legacy path kept the old ranking until it was named here. **The test\ncaught that, not review.**\n- `postgres_service`, which reads it and defaults to **1.0** when absent\n— so a storage revision deployed ahead of core-api keeps today's ranking\nrather than changing it. Deploys aren't atomic across the two services\nand the safe direction for the gap is \"unchanged\".\n\nThreaded as a per-tenant knob rather than a literal, matching\n`candidate_pool_size` / `score_formula`: `fts_rank_scale` joins\n`_SEARCH_PROFILE_RULES` bounded `(1.0, 20.0)`. Floor is 1.0 — the\npre-#687 formula — so a tenant can revert but can't weaken keyword\nrelevance below where it's always been.\n\n## Hot-path cost: nil, verified not assumed\n\nThe compiled SQL renders `ts_rank_cd` **18 times before and after** —\nthe scale multiplies an existing expression object rather than\nduplicating the call. `k` binds once and the SQL text is byte-identical\nfor any `k`, so one plan and one prepared statement regardless of\nper-tenant override. EXPLAIN ANALYZE over 4,795 candidate rows: **36.1\nms scaled vs 37.1 ms unscaled**.\n\n## Verification\n\n| | passed | xfailed | xpassed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 4117 | 1 | 0 |\n| this branch | 4124 | 1 | 0 |\n\n+7 is exactly the added tests, with **no existing test's behaviour\nchanged** — the signal that matters when a change reprices every query.\n\nEach assertion confirmed load-bearing by mutation:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| revert the scale to 1.0 | cosine-scale test fails (`0.3 <= 0.0909`) |\n| drop the key from the pipeline builder | **pipeline** case fails only\n|\n| remove it from the storage allowlist | **legacy** case fails only |\n| widen the bounds floor to 0.0 | bounds test fails (`DID NOT RAISE`) |\n\n## Also corrects #714\n\nThat PR merged one commit earlier and describes `k=1` as \"today's\nformula\" in `BENCHMARKS.md` and three places in the harness — false the\nmoment this lands. Fixed, and the harness's `--k`/`--fts-weight`\ndefaults now come from `core_api.constants` rather than hand-mirrored\nliterals, since the constant's instruction to re-derive it was otherwise\nsilently unenforceable.\n\n## Deliberately out of scope, recorded for follow-up\n\n- **`setweight` is not an alternative.** Measured: uniform `setweight`\nis exactly a constant multiply capped at ×10 (ladder ×1/×2/×4/×10), and\n`ts_rank_cd(weights[])` rejects weights > 1.0 — so **no configuration\nlands in the target band** (needs ×6). A **title/content** weight split\nwould change *relative* ranking and is a genuine opportunity, needing\nits own benchmark.\n- `_SEARCH_PARAM_KEYS` is *also* missing `candidate_pool_size` and\n`score_formula`, so the legacy rollback lever already reverts two other\nranking features. Own diff.\n- `fts_score` renders `ts_rank_cd` twice per row (~14% of the query),\nbut the naive subquery hoist gets flattened by the planner; a real fix\nneeds an optimization barrier and its own plan-shape review.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-05T16:15:03Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4f0eb2b13942206c7baa33d5b3eec256e581f066/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9"}]},{"sha":"f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGY0MjgwZDczNzc0ZDYwN2FlZjJjOWNjMjE0Zjc4NzkyZDBkMjhkZjk","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-05T13:34:56Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-05T13:34:56Z"},"message":"feat(bench): first-stage blend A/B on LoCoMo, and share the harness plumbing (#714)\n\nExtends #712 one stage earlier, so the `fts_score` scale question from\n[HANDOFF-2026-08-04](HANDOFF-2026-08-04-fts-score-scale-normalisation.md)\nis finally measurable — **and measured**.\n\n## Why #712 couldn't answer it\n\n#712 holds a cosine-selected pool fixed and A/Bs a reranker's ordering,\nso recall is constant *by construction*. Correct for judging a reranker;\nwrong for a first-stage change, which moves **which rows are\nretrieved**. Verified rather than assumed: that script contains no\nreference to `fts`, `fts_score`, `FTS_WEIGHT`, `similarity_blend` or\n`ts_rank_cd`.\n\n## What this measures\n\nThe keyword half of the production blend, `similarity = (1 - FTS_WEIGHT)\n* vec_sim + FTS_WEIGHT * fts_score`, by rescaling `ts_rank_cd` before\nsaturation (`--k`; `k=1` is today's `r/(1+r)`), ranking the **whole\ncorpus** in both arms so recall can move. Truth is each QA item's\n`evidence` turn ids. Same paired bootstrap CI + sign test as #712.\n\n## The numbers\n\nFull LoCoMo, **n=1531** scorable questions (FTS matching on 257),\nMiniLM-L6-v2, `FTS_WEIGHT=0.3`. Delta vs today; `*` = bootstrap CI\nexcludes zero.\n\n| k | overall recall@5 | fts-matched-only recall@5 | matched-only MRR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | +0.0000 | +0.0000 | +0.0000 |\n| 3 | +0.0035\\* | +0.0211\\* | +0.0080\\* |\n| 6 | +0.0042\\* | +0.0250\\* | +0.0185\\* |\n| 10 | +0.0075\\* | +0.0445\\* | +0.0357\\* |\n| 20 | +0.0113\\* | +0.0672\\* | +0.0692\\* |\n\n**Recall does not move the wrong way.** It improves at every `k > 1`,\nsignificantly on all five metrics, with **zero** queries getting worse\non recall@5/@10/@20.\n\n## Three limits I don't want quoted away\n\n- **It does not locate an optimum.** The effect rises monotonically\nacross the entire range tested — so this says more keyword weight helps\n*on LoCoMo*, not that `k=20` is right. Picking a production `k` needs an\nargument this benchmark doesn't supply.\n- **Weak instrument for this particular change.** LoCoMo questions are\nparaphrases, so only ~17% lexically match any turn; the other 83% are a\nmathematical no-op that dilutes the overall delta. Hence the separate\n`fts-matched-only` block. Production queries skewing more keyword-shaped\nwould show a larger effect — which is what `_adaptive_fts_weight`\nalready assumes.\n- **Blend in isolation.** No freshness decay, weight blending, recall\nboost, or candidate LIMIT. Not the end-to-end pipeline delta.\n\nAll three are in `BENCHMARKS.md` too, so the numbers aren't quotable\nwithout them.\n\n## Design decisions worth a reviewer's eye\n\n- **Real Postgres for `ts_rank_cd`.** Reimplementing Postgres FTS in\nPython would measure a different function, and that function's output\nscale *is* the question. This costs #712's stdlib-only property, so it's\nconfined to a `TEMP` table that never touches application tables, and\nmirrors production exactly (bare `to_tsvector('english', content)` per\nmigration 001, `plainto_tsquery('english', q)`).\n- **`k=1` asserts a delta of exactly zero** on every metric *and*\nsubgroup — the treatment formula *is* the baseline there, so any\nmovement means the harness is manufacturing differences. Passing.\n- **Index breaks score ties**, so both arms order equal-scoring docs\nidentically and a tie can't masquerade as a win.\n\n## The refactor, and how it was verified\n\nShared plumbing moved to `scripts/_locomo_bench.py` (loader, embed\nclient, nDCG/MRR/recall, paired stats) rather than copied.\n`benchmark_rerank_locomo.py` imports it and **loses 144 lines**,\nincluding its own inline bootstrap.\n\nI can't run that harness here — the TEI reranker has no arm64 image,\nwhich the repo's own compose overlay notes. So I verified the refactor\nby **equivalence** instead: same synthetic scored rows, same seed,\nthrough `main`'s `report()` and the rewired one →\n\n```\nstdout identical: True\nreturned dict identical: True\n```\n\nNo test imports either script, and `scripts/` is outside CI's ruff\nscope. Both harnesses parse, `--help`, and reject bad `--k` /\n`--fts-weight`.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"822d5edd424639fe2f0b8444e2d5a78ef9fbcc69","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/822d5edd424639fe2f0b8444e2d5a78ef9fbcc69"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqczwACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvQEQAAQBe+FX5J5nCjTlD773jLTI\n4Lkx1myQ4+LSFnum5bAtUSAbMVhIeBu4buxFE42QyvxGaUTUkiqMvigmTbm/Nfaw\nckxaq1OsAYilAF2xQ8ZP0sHxaohkjwf/B4ndH+41EdxjyvCFfxVax1x2CyMWr1Sx\nMF6MdlERn3D6UKqi4f9BDGnc+aCHW3B2sSqyYFO7rB2dMIgyIpi5qJeG8zeVVuJN\nPFbVeug2X8V5zjZ3g8worUE244sLZpTRVhVcC+feicnyGGJz1AHWvo25RJaKQIr5\njZaa5EhduEntJwnjLtBLt8Pe7O8ZxD7ut2TycOnBoSocrkX4Vg0QlP8tS/57ptbc\n9Bjm5LisSjfXfAPBpC64/CG4GhpMMT3KD/hLYXqsjmZ0vS6eZMFMU7V/cTHm0toq\n0YEbsor1idMACKaMZPPy0JRa8h4AyNZ0LHCSBbCL5KJlWK0C8QzeLpMDsWknefBx\nESqbkDIAE+rZGFHzkuMFxvLu8L53vJ4dUEA/h1TIy800spXdl8qq0hRtIfLCyOga\nVDXWp8OWsdo+5CL+m+yW06hftbYTqKcr+Bjro0OLonwItBjHmYcHL1cD/SDnbVxm\nyHg31cwgftzMzIPin1+SRcrcsuJ52uOSSKOL2SvcIofqf9m72vIj0C8Kmn1dduEQ\nEdJdCXKV0L4aeAJUiXNi\n=LnFB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 822d5edd424639fe2f0b8444e2d5a78ef9fbcc69\nparent ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785936896 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785936896 +0300\n\nfeat(bench): first-stage blend A/B on LoCoMo, and share the harness plumbing (#714)\n\nExtends #712 one stage earlier, so the `fts_score` scale question from\n[HANDOFF-2026-08-04](HANDOFF-2026-08-04-fts-score-scale-normalisation.md)\nis finally measurable — **and measured**.\n\n## Why #712 couldn't answer it\n\n#712 holds a cosine-selected pool fixed and A/Bs a reranker's ordering,\nso recall is constant *by construction*. Correct for judging a reranker;\nwrong for a first-stage change, which moves **which rows are\nretrieved**. Verified rather than assumed: that script contains no\nreference to `fts`, `fts_score`, `FTS_WEIGHT`, `similarity_blend` or\n`ts_rank_cd`.\n\n## What this measures\n\nThe keyword half of the production blend, `similarity = (1 - FTS_WEIGHT)\n* vec_sim + FTS_WEIGHT * fts_score`, by rescaling `ts_rank_cd` before\nsaturation (`--k`; `k=1` is today's `r/(1+r)`), ranking the **whole\ncorpus** in both arms so recall can move. Truth is each QA item's\n`evidence` turn ids. Same paired bootstrap CI + sign test as #712.\n\n## The numbers\n\nFull LoCoMo, **n=1531** scorable questions (FTS matching on 257),\nMiniLM-L6-v2, `FTS_WEIGHT=0.3`. Delta vs today; `*` = bootstrap CI\nexcludes zero.\n\n| k | overall recall@5 | fts-matched-only recall@5 | matched-only MRR |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | +0.0000 | +0.0000 | +0.0000 |\n| 3 | +0.0035\\* | +0.0211\\* | +0.0080\\* |\n| 6 | +0.0042\\* | +0.0250\\* | +0.0185\\* |\n| 10 | +0.0075\\* | +0.0445\\* | +0.0357\\* |\n| 20 | +0.0113\\* | +0.0672\\* | +0.0692\\* |\n\n**Recall does not move the wrong way.** It improves at every `k > 1`,\nsignificantly on all five metrics, with **zero** queries getting worse\non recall@5/@10/@20.\n\n## Three limits I don't want quoted away\n\n- **It does not locate an optimum.** The effect rises monotonically\nacross the entire range tested — so this says more keyword weight helps\n*on LoCoMo*, not that `k=20` is right. Picking a production `k` needs an\nargument this benchmark doesn't supply.\n- **Weak instrument for this particular change.** LoCoMo questions are\nparaphrases, so only ~17% lexically match any turn; the other 83% are a\nmathematical no-op that dilutes the overall delta. Hence the separate\n`fts-matched-only` block. Production queries skewing more keyword-shaped\nwould show a larger effect — which is what `_adaptive_fts_weight`\nalready assumes.\n- **Blend in isolation.** No freshness decay, weight blending, recall\nboost, or candidate LIMIT. Not the end-to-end pipeline delta.\n\nAll three are in `BENCHMARKS.md` too, so the numbers aren't quotable\nwithout them.\n\n## Design decisions worth a reviewer's eye\n\n- **Real Postgres for `ts_rank_cd`.** Reimplementing Postgres FTS in\nPython would measure a different function, and that function's output\nscale *is* the question. This costs #712's stdlib-only property, so it's\nconfined to a `TEMP` table that never touches application tables, and\nmirrors production exactly (bare `to_tsvector('english', content)` per\nmigration 001, `plainto_tsquery('english', q)`).\n- **`k=1` asserts a delta of exactly zero** on every metric *and*\nsubgroup — the treatment formula *is* the baseline there, so any\nmovement means the harness is manufacturing differences. Passing.\n- **Index breaks score ties**, so both arms order equal-scoring docs\nidentically and a tie can't masquerade as a win.\n\n## The refactor, and how it was verified\n\nShared plumbing moved to `scripts/_locomo_bench.py` (loader, embed\nclient, nDCG/MRR/recall, paired stats) rather than copied.\n`benchmark_rerank_locomo.py` imports it and **loses 144 lines**,\nincluding its own inline bootstrap.\n\nI can't run that harness here — the TEI reranker has no arm64 image,\nwhich the repo's own compose overlay notes. So I verified the refactor\nby **equivalence** instead: same synthetic scored rows, same seed,\nthrough `main`'s `report()` and the rewired one →\n\n```\nstdout identical: True\nreturned dict identical: True\n```\n\nNo test imports either script, and `scripts/` is outside CI's ruff\nscope. Both harnesses parse, `--help`, and reject bad `--k` /\n`--fts-weight`.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-05T13:34:59Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/f4280d73774d607aef2c9cc214f78792d0d28df9/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56"}]},{"sha":"ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGNjZWMxZTZkMzgwOTg2NDBmNmQ2YjQyNzJiYzYwODQ2NzQ1OTJhNTY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-05T08:54:45Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-05T08:54:45Z"},"message":"test(keystones): give each keystone test its own tenant (#713)\n\nFixes the test-isolation bug I hit while running suites for #710 and\nflagged on that PR.\n\n## What was wrong\n\nAll 17 tests in `tests/test_api_keystones.py` shared tenant `default`.\nKeystones are `documents` rows written through the API's **own committed\ntransaction**, so the per-test session rollback never reaches them —\nevery run left rules behind. Measured: **7 per full-suite run**,\nunbounded.\n\nThat isn't merely untidy, because the listing is capped at\n`KEYSTONE_MAX_RESULTS` (50) and ordered `weight DESC, updated_at DESC`.\nOnce 50+ rules outrank a freshly-written one, the new rule isn't in the\nresponse at all and `test_set_then_list_round_trip` fails — **including\nin isolation**, which makes it read as a code regression with nothing in\nthe diff to explain it. That's exactly how it presented: 197 rows under\n`default`, a test failing alone, mid-way through unrelated work.\n\nCI never sees it (fresh DB per run), which is why it went unnoticed.\n\n## The fix\n\n- **Each test gets its own tenant** via `get_test_auth(_ks_tenant())`.\nThe helper already accepted an override and merely defaulted to\n`\"default\"`; nothing about these tests needs the shared tenant. The\n`test-tenant-` prefix is load-bearing — it's what the teardown sweeps\non.\n- **`documents` added to the teardown's table list**, so those rows are\ncleaned rather than diluted. Without it the new per-test tenants would\nstill pile up, just harmlessly.\n\n## Two assertions that had let this hide\n\n- `test_list_keystones_empty` asserted only `isinstance(resp.json(),\nlist)` — true of a tenant holding 197 rules — while its docstring\nclaimed *\"a tenant with no keystones\"*. Now `== []`, which the per-test\ntenant makes honest.\n- The round-trip test now asserts the response wasn't truncated.\nTruncation and \"my rule is missing\" are different failures; without this\nthe first presents as the second.\n\n## Reproduction — and two attempts that didn't work\n\nWorth recording, because the naive reproductions pass and would have\n\"confirmed\" a wrong fix:\n\n| attempt | result |\n|---|---|\n| 70 rows at the same weight | **passes** — equal weights order\nnewest-first, so the new rule still lands inside the cap |\n| 55 rows at a *lower* weight | **passes** — they sort below it and\nchange nothing |\n| 55 rows at weight 100 (what `\"high\"` stores as) | **fails** on the\noriginal assertion |\n\nThe third is what the full suite's mix of `weight=\"high\"` writers\nproduces. Against that poisoned database: old code fails, fixed code\npasses 17/17.\n\n## Verification\n\n| | residual keystone rows after one full suite run | suite |\n|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 7 | 4117 passed / 1 xfailed / 0 xpassed |\n| this branch | **0** | 4117 passed / 1 xfailed / 0 xpassed |\n\nIdentical counts, so no test's behaviour changed. Root `tests/` isn't in\nCI's ruff scope, so nothing was reformatted.\n\nI skipped `/simplify` here: a mechanical call-site substitution plus one\nteardown entry, no new logic or control flow, and every property\nasserted above was confirmed by the reproduction rather than by reading.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"99c84b3a0e70eedeeedb4ff3fdf972730fbf5ae6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/99c84b3a0e70eedeeedb4ff3fdf972730fbf5ae6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcvpVCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAod4QAFc2VgZlqo3KhC9C9JaokotF\nAGKiYIcheD5PqpJVpLBeGPgncGmgcAi88IrCaFWYlehRKWIyS7k3Sot9esBv477x\nhPpiOsXPi6/i5qkWL8MicEKRXEci9doIPyoiADC6o8ZAINFskWycGR15+03AUA2R\nL2YXigV/+zrmXEuCNc+juAOp46vHsc14tzDDhbr8M4GMyeJc4gFr8utIuvEC73ZO\nFS82jQK3hgu0J06B3UrcFENnwSRW1rizDc3nKxMKqrUY5F0qblbnDT6tfN4NcuDy\nLIJbDBFEsayYnsBeIxUynK/MNpeaKnzYxcRohc5f0W2VHt8EN3+//kfurA4Oq4kf\ntUf2Sp7NDtC+ne1KXsFJH7hW/Gnc09tIt4iJJjtBLct8OUXx9uPwAM15BgkZmnWB\nIzDfSADFQm7NLFBDfmIfqCsRBPdaiLtrO97YkhAStt/Y7UPLGI0Rk+xIWMs+OLjE\nPv+Ntd73Sp2OreLU3hUDXgKrcYELcABKc43j3H/fSWAbUZYP0QaVc7CIPqre6jvO\nZovJLrNyYD6Rjm4tcDV0zr/9agggrnVKgdI0pcKAxUzQzVCV/Al5xCIQM75TXPA0\n1sfZtMOTNfsmb1kLP+8YvYqdxu6Qpn3SKZF4DuzkocZ+7oe+pOWF+sBQPqKW1/mT\nYK4bjqGP+kvaWOeHNn6B\n=uOCv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 99c84b3a0e70eedeeedb4ff3fdf972730fbf5ae6\nparent 9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785920085 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785920085 +0300\n\ntest(keystones): give each keystone test its own tenant (#713)\n\nFixes the test-isolation bug I hit while running suites for #710 and\nflagged on that PR.\n\n## What was wrong\n\nAll 17 tests in `tests/test_api_keystones.py` shared tenant `default`.\nKeystones are `documents` rows written through the API's **own committed\ntransaction**, so the per-test session rollback never reaches them —\nevery run left rules behind. Measured: **7 per full-suite run**,\nunbounded.\n\nThat isn't merely untidy, because the listing is capped at\n`KEYSTONE_MAX_RESULTS` (50) and ordered `weight DESC, updated_at DESC`.\nOnce 50+ rules outrank a freshly-written one, the new rule isn't in the\nresponse at all and `test_set_then_list_round_trip` fails — **including\nin isolation**, which makes it read as a code regression with nothing in\nthe diff to explain it. That's exactly how it presented: 197 rows under\n`default`, a test failing alone, mid-way through unrelated work.\n\nCI never sees it (fresh DB per run), which is why it went unnoticed.\n\n## The fix\n\n- **Each test gets its own tenant** via `get_test_auth(_ks_tenant())`.\nThe helper already accepted an override and merely defaulted to\n`\"default\"`; nothing about these tests needs the shared tenant. The\n`test-tenant-` prefix is load-bearing — it's what the teardown sweeps\non.\n- **`documents` added to the teardown's table list**, so those rows are\ncleaned rather than diluted. Without it the new per-test tenants would\nstill pile up, just harmlessly.\n\n## Two assertions that had let this hide\n\n- `test_list_keystones_empty` asserted only `isinstance(resp.json(),\nlist)` — true of a tenant holding 197 rules — while its docstring\nclaimed *\"a tenant with no keystones\"*. Now `== []`, which the per-test\ntenant makes honest.\n- The round-trip test now asserts the response wasn't truncated.\nTruncation and \"my rule is missing\" are different failures; without this\nthe first presents as the second.\n\n## Reproduction — and two attempts that didn't work\n\nWorth recording, because the naive reproductions pass and would have\n\"confirmed\" a wrong fix:\n\n| attempt | result |\n|---|---|\n| 70 rows at the same weight | **passes** — equal weights order\nnewest-first, so the new rule still lands inside the cap |\n| 55 rows at a *lower* weight | **passes** — they sort below it and\nchange nothing |\n| 55 rows at weight 100 (what `\"high\"` stores as) | **fails** on the\noriginal assertion |\n\nThe third is what the full suite's mix of `weight=\"high\"` writers\nproduces. Against that poisoned database: old code fails, fixed code\npasses 17/17.\n\n## Verification\n\n| | residual keystone rows after one full suite run | suite |\n|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 7 | 4117 passed / 1 xfailed / 0 xpassed |\n| this branch | **0** | 4117 passed / 1 xfailed / 0 xpassed |\n\nIdentical counts, so no test's behaviour changed. Root `tests/` isn't in\nCI's ruff scope, so nothing was reformatted.\n\nI skipped `/simplify` here: a mechanical call-site substitution plus one\nteardown entry, no new logic or control flow, and every property\nasserted above was confirmed by the reproduction rather than by reading.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-05T08:54:47Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ccec1e6d38098640f6d6b4272bc6084674592a56/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4"}]},{"sha":"9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDkzNjllNmU4Y2E5ZjY5YTdjYzQwZGFmOTFjYjg4YWQ5Y2UzZmEzZDQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-05T07:42:36Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-05T07:42:36Z"},"message":"feat(bench): LoCoMo rerank A/B harness (#712)\n\n`BENCHMARKS.md` names LoCoMo/LongMemEval as the instrument we publish\nagainst, but ships **no runner** — so every rerank question so far has\nbeen answered by throwaway scripts. This commits the one for reranking,\nand updates that section to say what is and isn't bundled.\n\n## What it measures\n\nOne question: **does the cross-encoder order results better than\nfirst-stage similarity alone?** It scores the *same* candidate pool two\nways —\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| baseline | embedding cosine, top `--pool` (what first-stage ranks by)\n|\n| treatment | that same pool, reordered by `POST {rerank-url}/rerank` |\n| truth | each QA item's `evidence` turn ids, binary relevance |\n\nBecause both arms score one pool, **recall@pool is identical by\nconstruction** — asserted at runtime — so any difference is ordering,\nwhich is all a reranker does. Reports nDCG@5/@10, MRR, P@5 with\npaired-bootstrap CIs and an exact sign test, overall and per LoCoMo\ncategory.\n\nStdlib only, no cloud dependencies — runs against `docker compose` as\nreadily as a deployed stack.\n\n## Design decisions worth knowing\n\n- **Per conversation, never pooled.** Each LoCoMo conversation is one\nagent's memory and a question only concerns its own. Pooling all ten\nwould measure a task the product never performs.\n- **Category 5 excluded** (adversarial/unanswerable) — no evidence turn\nexists to retrieve, so those items measure answer *abstention*, not\nranking.\n- **Queries where no evidence reaches the pool are skipped and counted,\nnot scored as zero.** First-stage failed outright there; a reranker\ncannot invent the row. Scoring them would add a shared zero to both arms\nand dilute the comparison. The skip count prints so the\nconditional-vs-unconditional distinction stays visible — on the run\nbelow it's 400/1531, i.e. the headline +0.29 is conditional on retrieval\nsucceeding; unconditional it's ≈ +0.215.\n- **Speakers kept in the turn text** — who said something is part of\nwhat makes a turn the right answer to *\"when did X do Y\"*.\n\n## Why commit a harness at all\n\nA first attempt at this A/B used a ~55-document fixture and returned a\n**null result**: nDCG@5 +0.008, CI spanning zero, 8 queries better / 9\nworse. That was a **ceiling effect** — cosine alone already scored\n0.85–0.97 there, so there was nothing left to win — and it would have\nbeen easy to misread as *\"reranking doesn't help\"* and drop the feature.\n\nOn LoCoMo, where 600-turn conversations put first-stage at 0.50, the\nsame reranker against the same sidecar is worth **+0.29 nDCG@5**. Corpus\nsize decided the answer, not the reranker. That's the argument for\ncommitting a harness with a real corpus rather than leaving this to\nfixtures.\n\n## Verified\n\nAgainst a live TEI `bge-m3` embedder and MiniLM reranker — 10\nconversations, 5,882 turns, 1,531 questions, **n=1,131 scorable**:\n\n| metric | first-stage | reranked | Δ | 95% CI | better/worse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nDCG@5 | 0.5019 | 0.7938 | **+0.2920** | [+0.269, +0.315] | 648 / 96 |\n| nDCG@10 | 0.5598 | 0.8159 | +0.2561 | [+0.236, +0.276] | 675 / 112 |\n| MRR | 0.4927 | 0.7807 | +0.2880 | [+0.266, +0.311] | 647 / 117 |\n| P@5 | 0.1496 | 0.2055 | +0.0559 | [+0.049, +0.063] | 325 / 39 |\n\nMulti-hop gains most (**+0.3812**) from the worst baseline (0.328) — the\npattern you'd hope for. Open-domain is the weakest evidence (n=41; its\nnDCG@5 sign test doesn't reject at p=0.12 even though the CI excludes\nzero).\n\nThat table was produced by running **this exact file**, unmodified,\nfetched from the branch — not by the prototype it replaces (which\nreported +0.2915 on the same setup).\n\nAlso unit-checked offline: metric helpers against hand-computed values\n(including nDCG's ideal-capped-at-k case and order sensitivity), and\n`report()` confirmed to flag a synthetic true effect while **not**\nflagging a null one. `ruff check` + `format` clean.\n\n## Caveat stated in the module docstring, the README section, and here\n\nThis **isolates the reranker**: the baseline is pure cosine, whereas the\nreal first-stage also applies freshness decay, weight blending and\nrecall boost. It answers *\"is the cross-encoder worth its latency\"* —\nnot *\"what is the end-to-end pipeline delta\"*.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"9973f09b513dccf48e1b518452c9fc53312f7d7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/9973f09b513dccf48e1b518452c9fc53312f7d7d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqculsCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0O8QADniqiR7XqJQ7p6btGpHqLn/\njJVeIT/EBV28+qpatl4CdWI3dEDI/pX7aT2tFjjJN8ICkv/LW2KzSfdBqZwqWsDT\n5xwCl2sLpPoJvbgihF8J5BLveOivHEYsDgD+Znm9fFjAzxs/7ls8cG4ysbNdQMlQ\nPRDlpk9B2OKR5RGKWlI9nPYq7b3stV6HvyjFrjfmxTTST3z9CrFnG5THqt/OPXDV\nhU4bFk4bQ4RnvAPWNvFAtVrCfW/IJG79lv30UcSFAjlgweVrB1agowU/oE3g6/9N\n9sCL0RL1qKNiHrCnHY0VJ3NYbOdrjoPvhKoflhIDyPY9m9+mQ4gQbQnX8HOyH+ms\n9kmSk2b++yGs3Yvh5IyrhvxSoeh2VwhFHJ2lLPGFy4O+G2MGq9jxl2UsDL+ekMTB\nI+0f1Kn6MWEwjRbudfUztGK2NEe39JhMb652KCvtUEzbxHXER0G1vXdLD2CTxGOq\nqAKuswIWiMNyqGniYJOVw4A4SHVWraSGtyWUdZE9nprsTbTx2oNvslqbGSvdGWYQ\nPiw7fA+kwh6Dh8wwS2os8Lx09mYax4ospYRn+e5tiBlSbbVM210Pg11dozdZG2LU\nMdgpj0rNDlwnD5UTVCOa/lIJtXADEr6CUW9skJzVdUKGobWiW2YRfj3HaMldGi1z\nHhsagbRXhm8iN8b41XEH\n=789j\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 9973f09b513dccf48e1b518452c9fc53312f7d7d\nparent 6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785915756 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785915756 +0300\n\nfeat(bench): LoCoMo rerank A/B harness (#712)\n\n`BENCHMARKS.md` names LoCoMo/LongMemEval as the instrument we publish\nagainst, but ships **no runner** — so every rerank question so far has\nbeen answered by throwaway scripts. This commits the one for reranking,\nand updates that section to say what is and isn't bundled.\n\n## What it measures\n\nOne question: **does the cross-encoder order results better than\nfirst-stage similarity alone?** It scores the *same* candidate pool two\nways —\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| baseline | embedding cosine, top `--pool` (what first-stage ranks by)\n|\n| treatment | that same pool, reordered by `POST {rerank-url}/rerank` |\n| truth | each QA item's `evidence` turn ids, binary relevance |\n\nBecause both arms score one pool, **recall@pool is identical by\nconstruction** — asserted at runtime — so any difference is ordering,\nwhich is all a reranker does. Reports nDCG@5/@10, MRR, P@5 with\npaired-bootstrap CIs and an exact sign test, overall and per LoCoMo\ncategory.\n\nStdlib only, no cloud dependencies — runs against `docker compose` as\nreadily as a deployed stack.\n\n## Design decisions worth knowing\n\n- **Per conversation, never pooled.** Each LoCoMo conversation is one\nagent's memory and a question only concerns its own. Pooling all ten\nwould measure a task the product never performs.\n- **Category 5 excluded** (adversarial/unanswerable) — no evidence turn\nexists to retrieve, so those items measure answer *abstention*, not\nranking.\n- **Queries where no evidence reaches the pool are skipped and counted,\nnot scored as zero.** First-stage failed outright there; a reranker\ncannot invent the row. Scoring them would add a shared zero to both arms\nand dilute the comparison. The skip count prints so the\nconditional-vs-unconditional distinction stays visible — on the run\nbelow it's 400/1531, i.e. the headline +0.29 is conditional on retrieval\nsucceeding; unconditional it's ≈ +0.215.\n- **Speakers kept in the turn text** — who said something is part of\nwhat makes a turn the right answer to *\"when did X do Y\"*.\n\n## Why commit a harness at all\n\nA first attempt at this A/B used a ~55-document fixture and returned a\n**null result**: nDCG@5 +0.008, CI spanning zero, 8 queries better / 9\nworse. That was a **ceiling effect** — cosine alone already scored\n0.85–0.97 there, so there was nothing left to win — and it would have\nbeen easy to misread as *\"reranking doesn't help\"* and drop the feature.\n\nOn LoCoMo, where 600-turn conversations put first-stage at 0.50, the\nsame reranker against the same sidecar is worth **+0.29 nDCG@5**. Corpus\nsize decided the answer, not the reranker. That's the argument for\ncommitting a harness with a real corpus rather than leaving this to\nfixtures.\n\n## Verified\n\nAgainst a live TEI `bge-m3` embedder and MiniLM reranker — 10\nconversations, 5,882 turns, 1,531 questions, **n=1,131 scorable**:\n\n| metric | first-stage | reranked | Δ | 95% CI | better/worse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| nDCG@5 | 0.5019 | 0.7938 | **+0.2920** | [+0.269, +0.315] | 648 / 96 |\n| nDCG@10 | 0.5598 | 0.8159 | +0.2561 | [+0.236, +0.276] | 675 / 112 |\n| MRR | 0.4927 | 0.7807 | +0.2880 | [+0.266, +0.311] | 647 / 117 |\n| P@5 | 0.1496 | 0.2055 | +0.0559 | [+0.049, +0.063] | 325 / 39 |\n\nMulti-hop gains most (**+0.3812**) from the worst baseline (0.328) — the\npattern you'd hope for. Open-domain is the weakest evidence (n=41; its\nnDCG@5 sign test doesn't reject at p=0.12 even though the CI excludes\nzero).\n\nThat table was produced by running **this exact file**, unmodified,\nfetched from the branch — not by the prototype it replaces (which\nreported +0.2915 on the same setup).\n\nAlso unit-checked offline: metric helpers against hand-computed values\n(including nDCG's ideal-capped-at-k case and order sensitivity), and\n`report()` confirmed to flag a synthetic true effect while **not**\nflagging a null one. `ruff check` + `format` clean.\n\n## Caveat stated in the module docstring, the README section, and here\n\nThis **isolates the reranker**: the baseline is pure cosine, whereas the\nreal first-stage also applies freshness decay, weight blending and\nrecall boost. It answers *\"is the cross-encoder worth its latency\"* —\nnot *\"what is the end-to-end pipeline delta\"*.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-05T07:42:38Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9369e6e8ca9f69a7cc40daf91cb88ad9ce3fa3d4/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06"}]},{"sha":"6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDZhYjhmMmQyNmRmZjkyMjI3MDUzZWM1YzQ5NzBjZmJmNjkwNGViMDY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-05T07:10:23Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-05T07:10:23Z"},"message":"feat(write): honour per-item write_mode on the bulk path (#710)\n\nCloud half of adding a read-your-own-write opt-out to the **bulk** write\nsurface. Prerequisite for the broker change that follows — the broker\nwrites via `/api/v1/memories/bulk`, and that endpoint had no\n`write_mode` concept at all, so broker-side passthrough alone would have\nshipped a field the cloud silently drops.\n\n## What was missing\n\n`write_mode` existed only on `MemoryCreate`. Neither `BulkMemoryItem`\nnor `BulkMemoryCreate` carried it, and the bulk path gated inline\nembedding purely on `settings.inline_embedding` — `_resolve_write_mode`\nnever ran for bulk. On a deferring deployment, *every* bulk-written\nmemory waited for the background backfill before becoming semantically\nsearchable, with no opt-out.\n\n`write_mode=\"strong\"` on an item now embeds that item inline.\n\n**Per item, not per batch**, deliberately: callers coalesce writes from\nunrelated sources into one request — the broker's durable queue does\nexactly this — so a batch-level flag would let one caller's opt-in put a\nprovider call on the request path for everyone else's items, or be lost\namong them. `tests/test_bulk_write_mode.py` pins the mixed-batch case at\n`[False, True, False]`.\n\n## Scope boundaries — stated in the field description, not left to be\ndiscovered\n\n- **Embedding only.** Single-write `strong` also enriches inline; here\nenrichment defers either way. Honouring the enrich half would put N\nsemaphored LLM calls on a fan-in request — a much larger commitment than\none batched embed.\n- **Not routed through `_resolve_write_mode`.** That also consults\n`tenant_config.default_write_mode` and escalates `_STRONG_TYPES`, which\nwould add a request-path provider call for callers who never asked. Only\nan explicit per-item opt-in counts. *(Worth a reviewer's opinion:\nclosing that asymmetry is defensible, but it's a latency change for\nstrong-defaulting tenants that nobody requested, so I left it and\ndocumented it.)*\n- **`str`, not `Literal`.** This model deliberately avoids schema\nconstraints that reject a whole batch over one item's field, so an\nunrecognised value (`\"stm\"` copied from a single-write payload) degrades\nto fast instead of 422-ing every sibling. A `Literal` here would have\nfought that documented policy.\n\n## Two failure modes handled rather than inherited\n\n**A failed opt-in embed does not fail the request.** Those rows were\ngoing to defer anyway, so falling through to the background re-embed\ncosts only the latency they'd have had — where raising would let one\nopt-in item take down a batch of items that never asked for inline\nembedding. Inline deployments keep the pre-existing contract: the embed\nis how every row there gets its vector, so a timeout is still 504 and\nother errors still propagate.\n\n**The opt-in embed gets its own tighter deadline** —\n`BULK_STRONG_EMBED_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 8.0` instead of borrowing the\nrequired path's 30s. A required embed can justify 30s because the\nalternative is a 504; an opportunistic one would spend it and then land\non the outcome it already accepted for free, with the latency charged to\nthe whole batch. The 8s is bounded on both sides and both bounds matter:\nit must stay **above** `EMBEDDING_GATE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (5.0), which\nsits deliberately below callers' deadlines so a saturated gate reads as\nattributable backpressure rather than an anonymous timeout. A unit test\npins both bounds.\n\nNo double work: an inline-embedded item is excluded from `reembed_batch`\nby the existing `embeddings[orig_idx] is None` gate and reaches\ncontradiction detection with its vector in hand.\n\n## One thing I built, then deliberately removed\n\nI had this also setting `metadata.embedding_pending` on deferred bulk\nrows, for parity with the single path. I dropped it after checking what\nclears it: **nothing does, on this path.** core-worker clears the flag\nvia `metadata_patch` (its docstring: that exists \"so a\nread-after-success returns clean state instead of confusingly claiming\nthe row is still pending\"), but bulk backfills in-process through\n`_reembed_memories_bulk` → core-api's `update_embedding`, which sends\nonly `{embedding, tenant_id}`.\n\nEvery deferred bulk row would have claimed to be pending forever,\nincluding after it was embedded and searchable — worse than no signal at\nall, given #706 documented that flag as the read-your-own-write\nindicator and `plugin/tools.json` tells agents not to re-write on it.\n`BulkItemResult` returns no metadata either, so it bought nothing at\nwrite time. Making it honest means teaching `update_embedding` to patch\nmetadata, which is its own change.\n\nAlso corrects two comments this change falsified: `insights_service`'s\nnote that \"`BulkMemoryItem` carries no `write_mode` field\", and the\n`MemoryOut.metadata` note I added in #706, which described single-write\n`strong` in terms that don't hold for bulk.\n\n## Verification\n\nFull suite against local pgvector with `alembic upgrade head`:\n\n| | passed | xfailed | xpassed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 4088 | 1 | 0 |\n| this branch | 4096 | 1 | 0 |\n\n+8 is exactly the added tests, with **no change to any existing test's\nbehaviour** — worth stating, since this touches a write path much of the\nsuite exercises.\n\nEvery new assertion confirmed load-bearing by mutation, inverting rather\nthan deleting:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| drop the strong subset | opt-in + mixed-batch cases fail |\n| make the opportunistic embed fatal | fallback case fails |\n| remove the timeout→504 mapping | inline-deployment case fails |\n| raise the opt-in budget to the required cap | bounds test fails\n(`assert 30.0 < 30.0`) |\n\n`ruff check` and `ruff format --check` clean, run separately and from\ninside `core-api`; `mypy` clean on 190 files.\n\n## Known follow-ups, not in scope here\n\n- A `strong` item that turns out to be a **content duplicate** still\npays a discarded embed — the embed runs before the hash-dedup lookup.\nPre-existing ordering for inline deployments, newly reachable on\ndeferring ones, and the fix is a reorder of an existing hot path.\n- The MCP `memclaw_write` batch branch doesn't forward a top-level\n`write_mode` into items. The param is accurately documented \"(single\nonly)\", so nothing is misleading today, but per-item mode isn't\nreachable from that surface.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"1a699d90a34731e1157fba80fe13605c7ab20d1b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/1a699d90a34731e1157fba80fe13605c7ab20d1b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcuHfCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAH78QAEl2KxCaWC9hicHiaRN4rIA2\nn+D9vrUM/4QYgA4FUi3sW6S2YD9yuf0D7kaSnPS4APPPbcvdaTdQycyAAMVzPVzD\nxKCc8MHo2H+aEQGSxeym1lW0rh8hUGYxiFvhUqoknPkT+Uu46O56XT9RjhtWWauv\nRrOQFSWrBDe2myqJM4i4HHWGI+UqkoSmS8o2LgrlVhojmV84pkxo0cidi3h6f7TQ\n0tB0yCDhb2SWvkl6cFmclkOAWLEsNpq+12LkdivPtKUj2YawR+PXhwFpQXfoMEtN\nNp8We0bwW9ehd9jaLI3hBCm/YbqcfOUBEb4v6Eb8vPcuLM5/xWPzaezNkG02qx7R\nl/jluR1juV5eq3t4z/UDbwOM9HYdu8fC0g8BOUmsB7+N9buQObORxUxIY+/XTeSU\nAA95jJXhrLHkKzclikdmzGEXaJW5H9WoTqXoPtJVeqCfzcsBVOa+12PanssFn16a\nUlZy45Gv9HnuT5XvdLf33SHF2q+r1+HF5BqWC1c3/h0dh/3dHyBWLfYjxf9QiOSq\nnByNr1mbw5Hq0VZn9ZDsX2P4YlJr6zHgtxFQFXLDAihe2uRXZd9UyTEVVZobJUFQ\nQK5S+CYyppNXkOxJ0ERUjdpmFnku//RqjXSE15Q+iyo59WGRlLdbqusvBf9IzBjf\n2mB5jbp77bWynW3Vg6EB\n=/NZv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 1a699d90a34731e1157fba80fe13605c7ab20d1b\nparent c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785913823 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785913823 +0300\n\nfeat(write): honour per-item write_mode on the bulk path (#710)\n\nCloud half of adding a read-your-own-write opt-out to the **bulk** write\nsurface. Prerequisite for the broker change that follows — the broker\nwrites via `/api/v1/memories/bulk`, and that endpoint had no\n`write_mode` concept at all, so broker-side passthrough alone would have\nshipped a field the cloud silently drops.\n\n## What was missing\n\n`write_mode` existed only on `MemoryCreate`. Neither `BulkMemoryItem`\nnor `BulkMemoryCreate` carried it, and the bulk path gated inline\nembedding purely on `settings.inline_embedding` — `_resolve_write_mode`\nnever ran for bulk. On a deferring deployment, *every* bulk-written\nmemory waited for the background backfill before becoming semantically\nsearchable, with no opt-out.\n\n`write_mode=\"strong\"` on an item now embeds that item inline.\n\n**Per item, not per batch**, deliberately: callers coalesce writes from\nunrelated sources into one request — the broker's durable queue does\nexactly this — so a batch-level flag would let one caller's opt-in put a\nprovider call on the request path for everyone else's items, or be lost\namong them. `tests/test_bulk_write_mode.py` pins the mixed-batch case at\n`[False, True, False]`.\n\n## Scope boundaries — stated in the field description, not left to be\ndiscovered\n\n- **Embedding only.** Single-write `strong` also enriches inline; here\nenrichment defers either way. Honouring the enrich half would put N\nsemaphored LLM calls on a fan-in request — a much larger commitment than\none batched embed.\n- **Not routed through `_resolve_write_mode`.** That also consults\n`tenant_config.default_write_mode` and escalates `_STRONG_TYPES`, which\nwould add a request-path provider call for callers who never asked. Only\nan explicit per-item opt-in counts. *(Worth a reviewer's opinion:\nclosing that asymmetry is defensible, but it's a latency change for\nstrong-defaulting tenants that nobody requested, so I left it and\ndocumented it.)*\n- **`str`, not `Literal`.** This model deliberately avoids schema\nconstraints that reject a whole batch over one item's field, so an\nunrecognised value (`\"stm\"` copied from a single-write payload) degrades\nto fast instead of 422-ing every sibling. A `Literal` here would have\nfought that documented policy.\n\n## Two failure modes handled rather than inherited\n\n**A failed opt-in embed does not fail the request.** Those rows were\ngoing to defer anyway, so falling through to the background re-embed\ncosts only the latency they'd have had — where raising would let one\nopt-in item take down a batch of items that never asked for inline\nembedding. Inline deployments keep the pre-existing contract: the embed\nis how every row there gets its vector, so a timeout is still 504 and\nother errors still propagate.\n\n**The opt-in embed gets its own tighter deadline** —\n`BULK_STRONG_EMBED_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 8.0` instead of borrowing the\nrequired path's 30s. A required embed can justify 30s because the\nalternative is a 504; an opportunistic one would spend it and then land\non the outcome it already accepted for free, with the latency charged to\nthe whole batch. The 8s is bounded on both sides and both bounds matter:\nit must stay **above** `EMBEDDING_GATE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (5.0), which\nsits deliberately below callers' deadlines so a saturated gate reads as\nattributable backpressure rather than an anonymous timeout. A unit test\npins both bounds.\n\nNo double work: an inline-embedded item is excluded from `reembed_batch`\nby the existing `embeddings[orig_idx] is None` gate and reaches\ncontradiction detection with its vector in hand.\n\n## One thing I built, then deliberately removed\n\nI had this also setting `metadata.embedding_pending` on deferred bulk\nrows, for parity with the single path. I dropped it after checking what\nclears it: **nothing does, on this path.** core-worker clears the flag\nvia `metadata_patch` (its docstring: that exists \"so a\nread-after-success returns clean state instead of confusingly claiming\nthe row is still pending\"), but bulk backfills in-process through\n`_reembed_memories_bulk` → core-api's `update_embedding`, which sends\nonly `{embedding, tenant_id}`.\n\nEvery deferred bulk row would have claimed to be pending forever,\nincluding after it was embedded and searchable — worse than no signal at\nall, given #706 documented that flag as the read-your-own-write\nindicator and `plugin/tools.json` tells agents not to re-write on it.\n`BulkItemResult` returns no metadata either, so it bought nothing at\nwrite time. Making it honest means teaching `update_embedding` to patch\nmetadata, which is its own change.\n\nAlso corrects two comments this change falsified: `insights_service`'s\nnote that \"`BulkMemoryItem` carries no `write_mode` field\", and the\n`MemoryOut.metadata` note I added in #706, which described single-write\n`strong` in terms that don't hold for bulk.\n\n## Verification\n\nFull suite against local pgvector with `alembic upgrade head`:\n\n| | passed | xfailed | xpassed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 4088 | 1 | 0 |\n| this branch | 4096 | 1 | 0 |\n\n+8 is exactly the added tests, with **no change to any existing test's\nbehaviour** — worth stating, since this touches a write path much of the\nsuite exercises.\n\nEvery new assertion confirmed load-bearing by mutation, inverting rather\nthan deleting:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| drop the strong subset | opt-in + mixed-batch cases fail |\n| make the opportunistic embed fatal | fallback case fails |\n| remove the timeout→504 mapping | inline-deployment case fails |\n| raise the opt-in budget to the required cap | bounds test fails\n(`assert 30.0 < 30.0`) |\n\n`ruff check` and `ruff format --check` clean, run separately and from\ninside `core-api`; `mypy` clean on 190 files.\n\n## Known follow-ups, not in scope here\n\n- A `strong` item that turns out to be a **content duplicate** still\npays a discarded embed — the embed runs before the hash-dedup lookup.\nPre-existing ordering for inline deployments, newly reachable on\ndeferring ones, and the fix is a reorder of an existing hot path.\n- The MCP `memclaw_write` batch branch doesn't forward a top-level\n`write_mode` into items. The param is accurately documented \"(single\nonly)\", so nothing is misleading today, but per-item mode isn't\nreachable from that surface.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-05T07:10:25Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6ab8f2d26dff92227053ec5c4970cfbf6904eb06/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f"}]},{"sha":"c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGM5ODc2NDkxMDY2YzhlNzc3OGE5M2E1YjA1NDAyMDMzMTY3NmZjMmY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-04T14:11:59Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-04T14:11:59Z"},"message":"fix(rerank): chunk the remote candidate pool so the sidecar's cap can't reject it (#709)\n\nCloses #704. Completes the pair with #708: that made a batch-cap\nrejection **loud**, this makes it **not happen**.\n\n## The problem\n\n`RemoteRanker.rank()` posted the whole candidate pool as ONE `/rerank`\nrequest, up to `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT` (50). A TEI reranker admits **32**\nper request unless started with `--max-client-batch-size`, so the\n**default pair was broken out of the box**: every search returning more\nthan 32 candidates was rejected and silently served first-stage order.\n\nAn operator could fix it by raising the sidecar's cap — but nothing in\nthe library enforced the coupling, and stock-vs-stock didn't work.\n\n## The change\n\nThe pool is split into chunks of **`RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH`** (new,\ndefault **32** = TEI's own default) and issued **concurrently**. The two\nsettings are independent from here: `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT` is a\nquality/latency choice, `RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH` is purely what the\nsidecar accepts per call.\n\n### Why splitting is invisible in the result\n\nA cross-encoder scores each `(query, text)` pair **independently** — a\ncandidate's score doesn't depend on what shares its batch — so\nchunk-then-merge returns exactly what one large call would. Not an\napproximation.\n\nThat property is load-bearing and does **not** hold for a listwise\nreranker that scores a set jointly. The docstring says so, because\nputting one behind this provider would silently change rankings.\n\n### Two deliberate design points\n\n**Concurrent, not sequential.** The service layer wraps the whole call\nin one `RANK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` deadline (0.5s) while a warm 50-candidate\nrerank already measures ~285ms — sequential chunks would blow it,\nconverting a silent batch-cap rejection into a silent *timeout*: same\nsymptom, harder to diagnose. `gather(return_exceptions=True)` so every\nchunk is awaited before any is inspected; a bare `gather` propagates the\nfirst failure and leaves siblings running unobserved.\n\nTo be precise about what this buys: it removes **client-side\nserialisation**, not server-side compute. One replica still scores the\nsame number of pairs, so budget against roughly the unchunked figure\nplus a round-trip, not N× it.\n\n**Any chunk failing fails the whole rank.** Scoring the failed chunk's\ncandidates `0.0` would bury real results at the bottom and call it\nsuccess — worse than not reranking. A `PermanentRankError` is surfaced\nover a transient sibling: a cap set too high is permanent regardless of\nwhat blip another chunk hit, and misreporting it transient would spend\nthe retry budget re-earning the same 413.\n\n## Blast radius is smaller than it looks\n\nThe default `top_k` of 5 with 2× overfetch yields a **10-candidate\npool** → single-request fast path → byte-for-byte identical on the wire\nto before. Splitting begins around `top_k >= 17`.\n\n**Operational note:** a deployment already running its sidecar with\n`--max-client-batch-size 64` should set `RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH=64` to\nkeep single-request behaviour at every `top_k`.\n\nAlso corrected: #708's 413 hint advised lowering `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT`,\nwhich is no longer the remedy since pool size no longer determines\nrequest size. It now names `RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH` and its live value.\n\n## Verification\n\nEach mechanism was reverted individually to confirm the matching test\nactually guards it:\n\n| Reverted | Result |\n|---|---|\n| chunking entirely | all new tests fail |\n| chunked but **sequential** | the split/order test fails on its\npeak-in-flight assertion — **and only that one** |\n| `raise failures[0]` instead of the permanent-wins scan | the\npermanent-over-transient test fails |\n\nThat last one matters: in an earlier draft the test was **vacuous** —\n`gather` preserves order and chunk 0 happened to hold the 413, so the\nnaive implementation satisfied it. The handler now keys the status off\nchunk *size*, making the short tail chunk the permanent one. Removing\nthat shared-counter handler also fixed a one-off flake: **45 runs of the\nfile under deliberate CPU contention, zero failures.**\n\n`ruff check common/` + gated scopes clean, format clean, `mypy` clean\n(190 files), `bandit` clean, no `uv.lock` drift.\n\n## Not in scope\n\n`RANK_MODEL` still defaults to `cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2`,\nwhich hf.co answers with a 307 to the canonical `…MiniLM-L6-v2`\n(established in caura-memclaw-enterprise#972). Harmless today, worth a\none-line follow-up rather than widening this PR.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"d132fb8fc35f41e99d627312edd442cea2ab00a8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/d132fb8fc35f41e99d627312edd442cea2ab00a8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcfMvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAduEQAK7/L3a50ZtYNiZd7DWgfOZy\nYkqcJcwrH11TY3KATeP7GQAlVXtO00j+dtexdkWiDv68jze8OR/e30WI8xi42K6t\npS+9vrM88el9rzDqavXzPtoNpwPMRRsCZ/mRai8n/I68cv5sK2TkZHtXCBK8eXW7\n6dvI71vFQqxtJGv72wLRPQ3qvQgvUUIpS2sh8l/dPuYwIA2tOdTAXF+EyVqHPUpy\nQ7LfZ0w7fm6+16+nAVnNDQAkzIDYPD1/AVY0wwlsu+/hrvkGPpQwfrdm9e5NEGXc\n8gTBi3GRaJhIE+0ElSyk4RLSufNfoJjUAU3klDTssOjUgwf/YdgbeyHYn0yhTYmv\nEnPVUBA4rFpf1VtSd3erhHLWaJve9xegDxU6nAMjUXw5HYSirZQIomsqzrrlS1KV\nl4E629iMzAHgi3UIh9CadxHoQ8VzO2pbqR0tkmbK4PXr4ZWm2C3WdKPApaM69Tey\ngpjreIqgSfMte47SidgG/WlzACC8f2EY5ZaUxkzlXDZ+rC+sXHAY5/YBIJhj1Y61\ntG5cvBEGwXmZ/UyPOZjPFh/rAqEm2fbgRlgBopfod79koq8T/32h3y4plIAMqnMq\nzxywYhSXLjzDcp7kA+gdZVZzvyVfjbGoKPhJbpPUoZ80KuvPM9UEF3/qJQ7rAikD\nG/zAz8UjTeVNVZoleFd5\n=G2bq\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree d132fb8fc35f41e99d627312edd442cea2ab00a8\nparent ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785852719 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785852719 +0300\n\nfix(rerank): chunk the remote candidate pool so the sidecar's cap can't reject it (#709)\n\nCloses #704. Completes the pair with #708: that made a batch-cap\nrejection **loud**, this makes it **not happen**.\n\n## The problem\n\n`RemoteRanker.rank()` posted the whole candidate pool as ONE `/rerank`\nrequest, up to `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT` (50). A TEI reranker admits **32**\nper request unless started with `--max-client-batch-size`, so the\n**default pair was broken out of the box**: every search returning more\nthan 32 candidates was rejected and silently served first-stage order.\n\nAn operator could fix it by raising the sidecar's cap — but nothing in\nthe library enforced the coupling, and stock-vs-stock didn't work.\n\n## The change\n\nThe pool is split into chunks of **`RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH`** (new,\ndefault **32** = TEI's own default) and issued **concurrently**. The two\nsettings are independent from here: `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT` is a\nquality/latency choice, `RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH` is purely what the\nsidecar accepts per call.\n\n### Why splitting is invisible in the result\n\nA cross-encoder scores each `(query, text)` pair **independently** — a\ncandidate's score doesn't depend on what shares its batch — so\nchunk-then-merge returns exactly what one large call would. Not an\napproximation.\n\nThat property is load-bearing and does **not** hold for a listwise\nreranker that scores a set jointly. The docstring says so, because\nputting one behind this provider would silently change rankings.\n\n### Two deliberate design points\n\n**Concurrent, not sequential.** The service layer wraps the whole call\nin one `RANK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` deadline (0.5s) while a warm 50-candidate\nrerank already measures ~285ms — sequential chunks would blow it,\nconverting a silent batch-cap rejection into a silent *timeout*: same\nsymptom, harder to diagnose. `gather(return_exceptions=True)` so every\nchunk is awaited before any is inspected; a bare `gather` propagates the\nfirst failure and leaves siblings running unobserved.\n\nTo be precise about what this buys: it removes **client-side\nserialisation**, not server-side compute. One replica still scores the\nsame number of pairs, so budget against roughly the unchunked figure\nplus a round-trip, not N× it.\n\n**Any chunk failing fails the whole rank.** Scoring the failed chunk's\ncandidates `0.0` would bury real results at the bottom and call it\nsuccess — worse than not reranking. A `PermanentRankError` is surfaced\nover a transient sibling: a cap set too high is permanent regardless of\nwhat blip another chunk hit, and misreporting it transient would spend\nthe retry budget re-earning the same 413.\n\n## Blast radius is smaller than it looks\n\nThe default `top_k` of 5 with 2× overfetch yields a **10-candidate\npool** → single-request fast path → byte-for-byte identical on the wire\nto before. Splitting begins around `top_k >= 17`.\n\n**Operational note:** a deployment already running its sidecar with\n`--max-client-batch-size 64` should set `RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH=64` to\nkeep single-request behaviour at every `top_k`.\n\nAlso corrected: #708's 413 hint advised lowering `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT`,\nwhich is no longer the remedy since pool size no longer determines\nrequest size. It now names `RANK_REMOTE_MAX_BATCH` and its live value.\n\n## Verification\n\nEach mechanism was reverted individually to confirm the matching test\nactually guards it:\n\n| Reverted | Result |\n|---|---|\n| chunking entirely | all new tests fail |\n| chunked but **sequential** | the split/order test fails on its\npeak-in-flight assertion — **and only that one** |\n| `raise failures[0]` instead of the permanent-wins scan | the\npermanent-over-transient test fails |\n\nThat last one matters: in an earlier draft the test was **vacuous** —\n`gather` preserves order and chunk 0 happened to hold the 413, so the\nnaive implementation satisfied it. The handler now keys the status off\nchunk *size*, making the short tail chunk the permanent one. Removing\nthat shared-counter handler also fixed a one-off flake: **45 runs of the\nfile under deliberate CPU contention, zero failures.**\n\n`ruff check common/` + gated scopes clean, format clean, `mypy` clean\n(190 files), `bandit` clean, no `uv.lock` drift.\n\n## Not in scope\n\n`RANK_MODEL` still defaults to `cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2`,\nwhich hf.co answers with a 307 to the canonical `…MiniLM-L6-v2`\n(established in caura-memclaw-enterprise#972). Harmless today, worth a\none-line follow-up rather than widening this PR.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-04T14:12:03Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c9876491066c8e7778a93a5b054020331676fc2f/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199"}]},{"sha":"ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGNlM2ZjNTE0ZTRiZDViZmZmZTI1MzZjNzdkYzlmYWFjMmZjN2ExOTk","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-04T12:50:59Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-04T12:50:59Z"},"message":"fix(rerank): distinguish permanent config faults from transient failures (#708)\n\nCloses #704 (the classification half; chunking stays open — see Scope\nbelow).\n\n## The problem\n\nReranking degrades to first-stage order on any failure, by design —\nrecall must never break. That posture is unchanged here. What was\nmissing is the distinction between the two *reasons* it can fail, which\nwant opposite handling.\n\nA permanent, configuration-class fault was reported exactly like a\ntransient one:\n\n```\nWARNING  Ranking attempt 1/1 failed\nERROR    Ranking failed after 1 attempt(s); keeping first-stage order\n```\n\nNo status code, no response body, no endpoint. So a misconfiguration was\nindistinguishable from load, and the actual cause never reached the log.\n\n**The failure that motivated this:** a TEI reranker admits **32** texts\nper request by default, but core-api sends up to `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT`\n(**50**) in ONE call — so every search with a larger pool came back\n`413`. `raise_for_status()` turned that into a generic exception, the\nbroad handler swallowed it, and the search quietly served first-stage\norder. Recall never broke, so it read as *\"reranking isn't helping\"*. It\ntook hours and the sidecar's own logs to find — twice.\n\n## The change\n\nNew `common/ranking/errors.py` with `PermanentRankError`. Providers,\nwhich know their own transport, classify; `_service.py` stays\ntransport-agnostic and only reacts to the type (it already dispatched on\n`TimeoutError`, so no new concept there). **Anything not explicitly\nclassified stays transient**, so a new failure mode can never silently\nstop retrying.\n\n- **`remote.py`** — 4xx except `408`/`429` is permanent; those two are\nabout *when* the call happened, not what it contained. 5xx and network\nerrors keep the existing retry path untouched. A `200` whose body isn't\na ranked list is also permanent: that means `RANK_BASE_URL` points at\nsomething that doesn't speak `/rerank`. The message carries endpoint,\nstatus, candidate count and the service's own words, plus a specific\nhint for 413 — the batch-cap coupling isn't guessable from the status\ncode.\n- **`local.py`** — a missing `sentence-transformers` is permanent by\nconstruction. The default image ships without torch, so it's the likely\nresult of flipping `RANK_PROVIDER=local`, and it was being retried at\nwarning level.\n- **`.env.example`** — documents the `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT` ↔\nsidecar-cap constraint where the value is actually set, so it's\npreventive rather than post-mortem.\n\n### Log-once, not log-per-search\n\nA permanent fault recurs on every search by definition, so the full\nERROR fires **once per condition** and repeats drop to DEBUG — reusing\nthe log-once posture this module already applies to provider\nmisconfiguration. Otherwise ERROR volume would track traffic rather than\nthe fault, and alerting on ERROR rate would page on QPS.\n\nThe dedup key comes from the exception (`\"remote:413\"`), deliberately\n**not** the message — that embeds a per-request response body and would\ndefeat the dedup. A success clears it, so a fixed-then-regressed sidecar\nreports again. `record_failure()` still fires every occurrence, so the\ndegraded trip-wire keeps counting.\n\n### One thing worth knowing\n\nThe retry-skip is a **no-op at the shipped default** of\n`RANK_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=1` — one attempt happens either way. For a default\ndeployment the deliverable is the ERROR level and the actionable detail.\nThe tests raise the budget so the distinction is observable at all;\nthere's a comment saying so, because \"simplifying\" it back to the\ndefault would turn every budget assertion into a tautology.\n\n## Scope\n\nClassification only. **Chunking** the candidate pool to the sidecar's\ncap — which would make the batch-cap fault structurally impossible —\nstays open in #704. The two are complementary rather than alternatives:\n`401`/`403`, a wrong endpoint, and the wrong-shape case are permanent\nregardless of chunking. Chunking arguably *depends* on this, since the\nsidecar's cap isn't discoverable from config and would otherwise have to\nbe learned from a 413.\n\n## Verification\n\n23 tests pass. The meaningful check — reverting just the two providers,\nleaving the service layer in place:\n\n```\n5 failed, 18 passed\n  FAILED test_413_raises_permanent_error_with_actionable_detail\n  FAILED test_permanent_failure_is_not_retried_and_logs_error\n  FAILED test_200_that_is_not_a_ranked_list_is_permanent\n  FAILED test_permanent_error_logs_once_then_debug_until_next_success\n  FAILED test_local_provider_missing_dependency_is_permanent\n```\n\nExactly the 5 classification tests, while the transient ones — 503, 429\nand 408 each still spending the full budget — keep passing. They assert\n*preserved* behaviour rather than restating the change.\n\n`ruff check common/` + the gated scopes clean, `ruff format` clean,\n`mypy` clean (189 source files).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"10e2e5b66f115b765916cef49956814e90841c42","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/10e2e5b66f115b765916cef49956814e90841c42"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqceAzCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4gEQAKBVgXB7JlzvJDuBOFhiHJA9\nAkrCTKGYNrbEyvEJBkM88X9whSrPxS+41Dmkr51xNWBqEz5kMXXoofrTy/DthrcX\nwuel0kI9zmHbo/Vxabwn+rzJAzDSThRPKRqmlYEd4H1i6jukj10frmu38WYZ0q4j\n8CC+rppcgD3IbjwUKoKxWht4dggKwt84GQ1Z43l04MbJiOLbNrNfm+Sq/gp8FCxZ\n0XfmTY2/gTOQ+3QRwjFiYIIyOL+jxseQx+iM55Shdmi6sVv/2zYqoPN8D9jucwim\nkfXqePIEWiqWhvzl93DQvxKYZdnabvK0hE1mo0N88FiEwdky4M/c/+PGUsEU0WW7\nPnBGHao/jl3D/v8ilo5n1NeElYpo1obnmFMbf+eNTMEPtm/VgxrwFLS6h8u6PQLn\nDrpZM3wy9nFiFWna3RbxUVxJxY3AXismJPTtbHJVlBMcjrw8XXEPadmuRLFON/Rc\nJRgzzR52holuRLEdy19DrgdjnpNq+irnV3OqKZ6cD8iMSAt1pHNCgg//2zQ2mIsB\nFA1nOQflbg8kt6xeZnMgGS9aydYGZoRRKUBogazxSpDY7J+qAwcHW7GASvQtYJ0m\nzRuxCSbsfE290Te2r8KgYqUalHh/AB203BibhF3g4AR+M464KEE5OzmJRVlvFNUW\n2mTQACvmkqAYHf3OXuO0\n=+fB0\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 10e2e5b66f115b765916cef49956814e90841c42\nparent 4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785847859 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785847859 +0300\n\nfix(rerank): distinguish permanent config faults from transient failures (#708)\n\nCloses #704 (the classification half; chunking stays open — see Scope\nbelow).\n\n## The problem\n\nReranking degrades to first-stage order on any failure, by design —\nrecall must never break. That posture is unchanged here. What was\nmissing is the distinction between the two *reasons* it can fail, which\nwant opposite handling.\n\nA permanent, configuration-class fault was reported exactly like a\ntransient one:\n\n```\nWARNING  Ranking attempt 1/1 failed\nERROR    Ranking failed after 1 attempt(s); keeping first-stage order\n```\n\nNo status code, no response body, no endpoint. So a misconfiguration was\nindistinguishable from load, and the actual cause never reached the log.\n\n**The failure that motivated this:** a TEI reranker admits **32** texts\nper request by default, but core-api sends up to `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT`\n(**50**) in ONE call — so every search with a larger pool came back\n`413`. `raise_for_status()` turned that into a generic exception, the\nbroad handler swallowed it, and the search quietly served first-stage\norder. Recall never broke, so it read as *\"reranking isn't helping\"*. It\ntook hours and the sidecar's own logs to find — twice.\n\n## The change\n\nNew `common/ranking/errors.py` with `PermanentRankError`. Providers,\nwhich know their own transport, classify; `_service.py` stays\ntransport-agnostic and only reacts to the type (it already dispatched on\n`TimeoutError`, so no new concept there). **Anything not explicitly\nclassified stays transient**, so a new failure mode can never silently\nstop retrying.\n\n- **`remote.py`** — 4xx except `408`/`429` is permanent; those two are\nabout *when* the call happened, not what it contained. 5xx and network\nerrors keep the existing retry path untouched. A `200` whose body isn't\na ranked list is also permanent: that means `RANK_BASE_URL` points at\nsomething that doesn't speak `/rerank`. The message carries endpoint,\nstatus, candidate count and the service's own words, plus a specific\nhint for 413 — the batch-cap coupling isn't guessable from the status\ncode.\n- **`local.py`** — a missing `sentence-transformers` is permanent by\nconstruction. The default image ships without torch, so it's the likely\nresult of flipping `RANK_PROVIDER=local`, and it was being retried at\nwarning level.\n- **`.env.example`** — documents the `RANK_CANDIDATE_LIMIT` ↔\nsidecar-cap constraint where the value is actually set, so it's\npreventive rather than post-mortem.\n\n### Log-once, not log-per-search\n\nA permanent fault recurs on every search by definition, so the full\nERROR fires **once per condition** and repeats drop to DEBUG — reusing\nthe log-once posture this module already applies to provider\nmisconfiguration. Otherwise ERROR volume would track traffic rather than\nthe fault, and alerting on ERROR rate would page on QPS.\n\nThe dedup key comes from the exception (`\"remote:413\"`), deliberately\n**not** the message — that embeds a per-request response body and would\ndefeat the dedup. A success clears it, so a fixed-then-regressed sidecar\nreports again. `record_failure()` still fires every occurrence, so the\ndegraded trip-wire keeps counting.\n\n### One thing worth knowing\n\nThe retry-skip is a **no-op at the shipped default** of\n`RANK_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=1` — one attempt happens either way. For a default\ndeployment the deliverable is the ERROR level and the actionable detail.\nThe tests raise the budget so the distinction is observable at all;\nthere's a comment saying so, because \"simplifying\" it back to the\ndefault would turn every budget assertion into a tautology.\n\n## Scope\n\nClassification only. **Chunking** the candidate pool to the sidecar's\ncap — which would make the batch-cap fault structurally impossible —\nstays open in #704. The two are complementary rather than alternatives:\n`401`/`403`, a wrong endpoint, and the wrong-shape case are permanent\nregardless of chunking. Chunking arguably *depends* on this, since the\nsidecar's cap isn't discoverable from config and would otherwise have to\nbe learned from a 413.\n\n## Verification\n\n23 tests pass. The meaningful check — reverting just the two providers,\nleaving the service layer in place:\n\n```\n5 failed, 18 passed\n  FAILED test_413_raises_permanent_error_with_actionable_detail\n  FAILED test_permanent_failure_is_not_retried_and_logs_error\n  FAILED test_200_that_is_not_a_ranked_list_is_permanent\n  FAILED test_permanent_error_logs_once_then_debug_until_next_success\n  FAILED test_local_provider_missing_dependency_is_permanent\n```\n\nExactly the 5 classification tests, while the transient ones — 503, 429\nand 408 each still spending the full budget — keep passing. They assert\n*preserved* behaviour rather than restating the change.\n\n`ruff check common/` + the gated scopes clean, `ruff format` clean,\n`mypy` clean (189 source files).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-04T12:51:01Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/ce3fc514e4bd5bfffe2536c77dc9faac2fc7a199/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079"}]},{"sha":"4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDRhZTk3MmI5ZTI1MDJlMDI1MDVlMDM2Y2Y0YzZiOTNlMGU3ZTMwNzk","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-04T12:25:17Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-04T12:25:17Z"},"message":"fix(search): reserve candidate and result slots for FTS-only rows (#687) (#700)\n\nFixes #687 — option 2 of the three we weighed, plus removal of #699's\n`xfail(strict=True)` marker.\n\n## Why the exemptions never fired\n\nCAURA-594 admits a NULL-embedding row when it FTS-matches; CAURA-679\nscores it on `fts_score` alone so the blend can't bury it. Neither helps\nit survive the two places that cut by **rank**:\n\n1. storage's candidate `LIMIT` (`top_k * SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR`)\n2. the head slice trimming to the caller's `top_k`\n\n`fts_score` is `ts_rank_cd/(1+ts_rank_cd)` — single digits of a percent\nfor one term — against embedded rows scoring far higher. Measured on\nprod: **791 memories, embedded rows 0.35–0.39, the FTS-only row 0, a\n10-row window.** The row was gone before the cosine-gate exemptions\n(pipeline's since CAURA-679, legacy's since #697) could run.\n\n**No constant floor fixes this:** `min_similarity` is 0.3, *below* the\nmeasured embedded noise floor.\n\n## The change\n\nA small capped allowance in all three places — guaranteeing\n*discoverable*, not *ranked highly*:\n\n- **`postgres_service`** — a dedicated candidate branch for `embedding\nIS NULL AND search_vector @@ query`, ordered by FTS relevance and\nseparately capped, unioned into the scored CTE. Rows enter at their own\ntrue (low) score; the outer `ORDER BY` still places them last.\n- **`post_filter_results`** and **`_search_memories_legacy`** — promote\nat most `FTS_ONLY_RESERVED_RESULTS`, and only when the head contains\nnone already, displacing the same number of weakest results.\n\nBlast radius is bounded twice: by the caps, and because NULL-embedding\nrows exist only until the backfill lands (~seconds on prod).\n\n## The bug this nearly shipped with\n\nMy first version unioned the two selects directly. That let SQLAlchemy\nhoist the main branch's `ORDER BY … LIMIT` to the compound statement,\n**changing the candidate set for embedded rows** — two unrelated\ngraph-fanout tests silently flipped xfail → xpass. Wrapping each operand\nin its own subquery fixes it, and I only caught it by diffing the\nxfail/xpass profile against a clean tree. Worth knowing if anyone\ntouches this union again.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Full suite (mine) | **4057 passed, 1 xfailed, 3 xpassed** |\n| Full suite (clean `main` baseline) | 4054 passed, 2 xfailed, 3 xpassed\n|\n| Delta | exactly the 3 added tests + #699's marker removed; **same 3\npre-existing xpasses** |\n| Both halves load-bearing | setting either constant to 0 reproduces the\nbug |\n| Both search paths | saturated test parameterised over\n`_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH`; both pass |\n| Reservation bounds | unit test: promotes at most the cap, no-ops when\nthe head already has one, untouched for ordinary result sets, result\nlength unchanged |\n| mypy | clean — core-api (189 files) and core-storage-api (74) |\n| ruff check / format | clean on every file touched; verified against\nin-place baselines, since comparing via `/tmp` copies misreads isort |\n\nRun against local pgvector 0.8.6 with `alembic upgrade head` (the\nfixtures use `Base.metadata.create_all`, so without alembic the\nmigration-001 `search_vector` trigger is absent and any FTS test fails\nfor the wrong reason).\n\n## Not done here\n\n**Normalising `fts_score` onto the cosine scale** — option 1. It's the\ndeeper fix and would likely improve keyword recall generally, but it\nreprices every query and belongs behind a benchmark run rather than my\njudgement. Worth its own issue.\n\nAlso worth a follow-up: nothing tells a caller their just-written memory\nisn't searchable yet. `embedding_pending` is already in the write\nresponse metadata, but undocumented — the cheap half of option 3.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"fb579dfeb67b5def36ad7b2d3bec9df0b6fd0c46","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/fb579dfeb67b5def36ad7b2d3bec9df0b6fd0c46"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcdotCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/hUQACjei1s/x0aoQUNF7BniEyok\neExwI10xEmjwCV1d/URlGn83awvaD6za/YanE1rNq/92qe4ICkxyrnjSRJevnHWk\n/1PxBHFxFc7YUIAnR80K6UzoaT2WDzX9KzYqUJz2d0Ec7LJXnxz/qBg+ljOCKneG\nyOgRbo9Ww+kQLWNCywr4S/d8I0M4btAk/OK6kWbvSzq7sAiLBWcI0MsLN9c9j95j\nnv308GnwsPN8b+YwmOIAwx+R38McTPmT+lvuS+nhIMgvztnAJEZ68T9IzxA1vRgE\nrznbnfHd5zY9FUhP7VbTkbXGtvKnnTg1Nrd3qxk7u+w7uJV/elqt0EYYX6+FuPQp\nKTiWsxBEO6j3HZWYnGmmvrmvL3ia7NCd+K3F/XN0GIsM2fPnhjDQgJIVqapWtMs0\nBls80i0Y/IJ14mLmkTfVTmKGCJ0dN6VpxPhnUKkopC9ovl7REs8HN8Qv2aRgTE7t\nkCGi/oeuaAp5I1Zn/XvwPuu6mWKqaijTpOJxWHsFjwKbG6mdlLqvMuelC9yGsimK\nc/lTmjJpjCtcU1EaX9TPKvtqZD0oKqWRKDanzHh66TrEvQc0YzwNv8Qp2yQveaw+\nO6UpGFyye63C92LUDVM/Kd40vPNleT1QHNcy1Sw7tqrb/JCa+NNulEluCP17JqWo\noIXMfM3a2y7sLRM1CQ7w\n=5tr8\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree fb579dfeb67b5def36ad7b2d3bec9df0b6fd0c46\nparent 187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785846317 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785846317 +0300\n\nfix(search): reserve candidate and result slots for FTS-only rows (#687) (#700)\n\nFixes #687 — option 2 of the three we weighed, plus removal of #699's\n`xfail(strict=True)` marker.\n\n## Why the exemptions never fired\n\nCAURA-594 admits a NULL-embedding row when it FTS-matches; CAURA-679\nscores it on `fts_score` alone so the blend can't bury it. Neither helps\nit survive the two places that cut by **rank**:\n\n1. storage's candidate `LIMIT` (`top_k * SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR`)\n2. the head slice trimming to the caller's `top_k`\n\n`fts_score` is `ts_rank_cd/(1+ts_rank_cd)` — single digits of a percent\nfor one term — against embedded rows scoring far higher. Measured on\nprod: **791 memories, embedded rows 0.35–0.39, the FTS-only row 0, a\n10-row window.** The row was gone before the cosine-gate exemptions\n(pipeline's since CAURA-679, legacy's since #697) could run.\n\n**No constant floor fixes this:** `min_similarity` is 0.3, *below* the\nmeasured embedded noise floor.\n\n## The change\n\nA small capped allowance in all three places — guaranteeing\n*discoverable*, not *ranked highly*:\n\n- **`postgres_service`** — a dedicated candidate branch for `embedding\nIS NULL AND search_vector @@ query`, ordered by FTS relevance and\nseparately capped, unioned into the scored CTE. Rows enter at their own\ntrue (low) score; the outer `ORDER BY` still places them last.\n- **`post_filter_results`** and **`_search_memories_legacy`** — promote\nat most `FTS_ONLY_RESERVED_RESULTS`, and only when the head contains\nnone already, displacing the same number of weakest results.\n\nBlast radius is bounded twice: by the caps, and because NULL-embedding\nrows exist only until the backfill lands (~seconds on prod).\n\n## The bug this nearly shipped with\n\nMy first version unioned the two selects directly. That let SQLAlchemy\nhoist the main branch's `ORDER BY … LIMIT` to the compound statement,\n**changing the candidate set for embedded rows** — two unrelated\ngraph-fanout tests silently flipped xfail → xpass. Wrapping each operand\nin its own subquery fixes it, and I only caught it by diffing the\nxfail/xpass profile against a clean tree. Worth knowing if anyone\ntouches this union again.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Full suite (mine) | **4057 passed, 1 xfailed, 3 xpassed** |\n| Full suite (clean `main` baseline) | 4054 passed, 2 xfailed, 3 xpassed\n|\n| Delta | exactly the 3 added tests + #699's marker removed; **same 3\npre-existing xpasses** |\n| Both halves load-bearing | setting either constant to 0 reproduces the\nbug |\n| Both search paths | saturated test parameterised over\n`_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH`; both pass |\n| Reservation bounds | unit test: promotes at most the cap, no-ops when\nthe head already has one, untouched for ordinary result sets, result\nlength unchanged |\n| mypy | clean — core-api (189 files) and core-storage-api (74) |\n| ruff check / format | clean on every file touched; verified against\nin-place baselines, since comparing via `/tmp` copies misreads isort |\n\nRun against local pgvector 0.8.6 with `alembic upgrade head` (the\nfixtures use `Base.metadata.create_all`, so without alembic the\nmigration-001 `search_vector` trigger is absent and any FTS test fails\nfor the wrong reason).\n\n## Not done here\n\n**Normalising `fts_score` onto the cosine scale** — option 1. It's the\ndeeper fix and would likely improve keyword recall generally, but it\nreprices every query and belongs behind a benchmark run rather than my\njudgement. Worth its own issue.\n\nAlso worth a follow-up: nothing tells a caller their just-written memory\nisn't searchable yet. `embedding_pending` is already in the write\nresponse metadata, but undocumented — the cheap half of option 3.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-04T12:25:19Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4ae972b9e2502e02505e036cf4c6b93e0e7e3079/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483"}]},{"sha":"187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDE4N2I1YjU0NjVkNzY1OTYyNGUxM2Y0NTVlNjYyYzlmY2U2N2M0ODM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-04T11:15:59Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-04T11:15:59Z"},"message":"docs(write): document embedding_pending and the strong-mode opt-out (#706)\n\n## Why\n\nA write returns `metadata.embedding_pending: true` when the row was\nstored without an embedding — but nothing documented it, so a caller had\nno way to know its memory isn't semantically searchable yet, or what to\ndo about it. The failure mode is quiet and expensive: the search comes\nback empty, the agent concludes the write failed, and writes it again.\n\n## What's actually true\n\nVerified end to end with a real write before documenting any of it,\nrather than reading it off the code:\n\n| deployment | `write_mode` | response `metadata` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `deployment_mode=inline` (OSS local) | `fast` | `{enrichment_pending:\nTrue}` |\n| embedding deferred (SaaS-like) | `fast` | `{enrichment_pending: True,\nembedding_pending: True}` |\n| embedding deferred | `strong` | `{}` |\n\nThree things follow, and all three are now stated:\n\n- The flag means the memory **is** reachable by keyword and via `GET\n/memories`, but won't match a *semantic* search until `core-worker`\nbackfills — **~15–20s on production, over 10 minutes on staging**, whose\nbackfill is slower.\n- `write_mode=\"strong\"` embeds inline regardless of deployment mode, and\nis the supported read-your-own-write opt-out. It costs the embedding\nprovider call on the request path — exactly what fast mode's sub-2s p99\nvisibility SLA exists to avoid — so it's a per-write choice, not a\ndefault to flip.\n- **`fast` does not unconditionally defer.** It follows the deployment's\nembedding mode, so a deployment that embeds inline (the OSS local\ndefault) never sets the flag at all. Worth stating precisely, since the\nobvious summary of this feature is wrong.\n\n## Where\n\n- `MemoryOut.metadata` — both pending flags, what each implies, and the\nopt-out.\n- `MemoryCreate.write_mode` — promoted from a bare `#` comment to a\n`Field` description, so it now appears in the OpenAPI schema.\n- The **`memclaw_write` ToolSpec description**, which is the source of\ntruth for MCP `tools/list`. Worth flagging for reviewers: the handler\ndocstring in `mcp_server.py` is *not* what agents read — editing only\nthat would look right and change nothing. The docstring now says so, and\ncarries the maintainer-facing detail.\n- README's async-write note, which said \"for a moment\" — off by a factor\nof ~30 on prod, and far more on staging.\n\nTool-surface tokens are paid on every agent call, so the agent-facing\nwording is deliberately compact: **4885 → 4959** cl100k for\n`tools/list`, against the 5200 ceiling in `test_mcp_token_budget.py`.\nThe regenerated fixtures and `plugin/tools.json` diff only in\n`memclaw_write`'s description and its `write_mode` param — no incidental\nchurn.\n\n## Also: the documented regeneration path didn't exist\n\n`tests/fixtures/README.md` has pointed at `capture_baselines.py` for a\nwhile, but the script was missing — so refreshing a baseline meant\nhand-rolling the capture and matching its serialisation by trial\n(`indent=2`, `ensure_ascii=False`, trailing newline; anything else\nrewrites all three files as a whole-file diff). Added it, and it prints\nthe token count so a budget regression surfaces locally instead of in\nCI. Also corrected that README's stale **5000**-token ceiling to point\nat the constant (it's 5200), and noted that `plugin/tools.json` has a\nseparate generator.\n\n## Verification\n\nFull suite **4084 passed / 2 xfailed / 0 xpassed** — identical to\n`main`, no test delta. `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` clean, run\nseparately and from inside `core-api`; `mypy` clean.\n\nI skipped `/simplify` here: the change is documentation wording plus one\ndev-tooling script, with no behavioural logic. Every property asserted\nin the new prose was confirmed by the probe above rather than by\nreading.\n\n## Follow-up\n\nThe broker's `memory_save` tool description needs the same note; it\nlives in the private `memclawd` repo, so it's a separate PR.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"a920a0b9fb84c39ab89cbb40b309f16c45a738bb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/a920a0b9fb84c39ab89cbb40b309f16c45a738bb"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqccnwCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmUwQABmoOMy9ouvd1TG6o0VUvbwS\nyBMRGFoLfxBN4f4+UsN7bXP89KemLqPzZ/qlI3ChOdqVSXqDoqV2prsYzqhdwDXX\ngmjYUMbe3O2DKCtCFr46+kjyHLV6PAyXWMM6tIJePXlXVw4gHQzREEiGhxEd6eoE\n4+n6VaBhI7ZdM4kaMwkfj8V3YknZ/ry6putOUh0/u0Zkll7ckJ/FobFfcqyR9B4E\n92EtJeUG0kTiXx+LFTHry0igNS7uMZhAaJTnzbtH8Uk9+A9eM5AZxJC5hh6gi/JC\nc7U79BRrMpMmqYSH2oaUe4rOD3HA5MmjuMXr4w/ZgP8KHOcUiI7AzbbUsHyvoK4X\nRynM423rKRBW2nTHpwAO0gRCOMcx8nnj9Yv0N/hKQaXcier47Rio0zEGeRmUdkAj\nWliFZtY3kdS4xXgq00GbXW7gYlMk91eLAunvwDADSgowaP7wSjd0Rq+Z7W094SWi\nSYLkNSOUSzcUdr9YfxcVUFDZPMCJ4USUMMU+9h+3dl1Xk7PMvYXly/58W7BOZgV+\nZmvA7Fg4mIBUm0UjB5EnN7d/PzUgN7I1T/EEbrR2orXBv0GJFWxn+t/QKSMfF1Fd\nDdY5OxYtf6y5nc4sY4Ypu5/zSUrDnW+5BuNwbYBJXNv7k8cOw0YWownZ/02rAGEm\nhJITRuhR71HpTn1TXtH3\n=pxz5\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree a920a0b9fb84c39ab89cbb40b309f16c45a738bb\nparent 8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785842159 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785842159 +0300\n\ndocs(write): document embedding_pending and the strong-mode opt-out (#706)\n\n## Why\n\nA write returns `metadata.embedding_pending: true` when the row was\nstored without an embedding — but nothing documented it, so a caller had\nno way to know its memory isn't semantically searchable yet, or what to\ndo about it. The failure mode is quiet and expensive: the search comes\nback empty, the agent concludes the write failed, and writes it again.\n\n## What's actually true\n\nVerified end to end with a real write before documenting any of it,\nrather than reading it off the code:\n\n| deployment | `write_mode` | response `metadata` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `deployment_mode=inline` (OSS local) | `fast` | `{enrichment_pending:\nTrue}` |\n| embedding deferred (SaaS-like) | `fast` | `{enrichment_pending: True,\nembedding_pending: True}` |\n| embedding deferred | `strong` | `{}` |\n\nThree things follow, and all three are now stated:\n\n- The flag means the memory **is** reachable by keyword and via `GET\n/memories`, but won't match a *semantic* search until `core-worker`\nbackfills — **~15–20s on production, over 10 minutes on staging**, whose\nbackfill is slower.\n- `write_mode=\"strong\"` embeds inline regardless of deployment mode, and\nis the supported read-your-own-write opt-out. It costs the embedding\nprovider call on the request path — exactly what fast mode's sub-2s p99\nvisibility SLA exists to avoid — so it's a per-write choice, not a\ndefault to flip.\n- **`fast` does not unconditionally defer.** It follows the deployment's\nembedding mode, so a deployment that embeds inline (the OSS local\ndefault) never sets the flag at all. Worth stating precisely, since the\nobvious summary of this feature is wrong.\n\n## Where\n\n- `MemoryOut.metadata` — both pending flags, what each implies, and the\nopt-out.\n- `MemoryCreate.write_mode` — promoted from a bare `#` comment to a\n`Field` description, so it now appears in the OpenAPI schema.\n- The **`memclaw_write` ToolSpec description**, which is the source of\ntruth for MCP `tools/list`. Worth flagging for reviewers: the handler\ndocstring in `mcp_server.py` is *not* what agents read — editing only\nthat would look right and change nothing. The docstring now says so, and\ncarries the maintainer-facing detail.\n- README's async-write note, which said \"for a moment\" — off by a factor\nof ~30 on prod, and far more on staging.\n\nTool-surface tokens are paid on every agent call, so the agent-facing\nwording is deliberately compact: **4885 → 4959** cl100k for\n`tools/list`, against the 5200 ceiling in `test_mcp_token_budget.py`.\nThe regenerated fixtures and `plugin/tools.json` diff only in\n`memclaw_write`'s description and its `write_mode` param — no incidental\nchurn.\n\n## Also: the documented regeneration path didn't exist\n\n`tests/fixtures/README.md` has pointed at `capture_baselines.py` for a\nwhile, but the script was missing — so refreshing a baseline meant\nhand-rolling the capture and matching its serialisation by trial\n(`indent=2`, `ensure_ascii=False`, trailing newline; anything else\nrewrites all three files as a whole-file diff). Added it, and it prints\nthe token count so a budget regression surfaces locally instead of in\nCI. Also corrected that README's stale **5000**-token ceiling to point\nat the constant (it's 5200), and noted that `plugin/tools.json` has a\nseparate generator.\n\n## Verification\n\nFull suite **4084 passed / 2 xfailed / 0 xpassed** — identical to\n`main`, no test delta. `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` clean, run\nseparately and from inside `core-api`; `mypy` clean.\n\nI skipped `/simplify` here: the change is documentation wording plus one\ndev-tooling script, with no behavioural logic. Every property asserted\nin the new prose was confirmed by the probe above rather than by\nreading.\n\n## Follow-up\n\nThe broker's `memory_save` tool description needs the same note; it\nlives in the private `memclawd` repo, so it's a separate PR.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-04T11:16:02Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/187b5b5465d7659624e13f455e662c9fce67c483/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915"}]},{"sha":"8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDgzNTEwNzdlMmM4NjE4NWY0MzdkMjk3OWMzNTY3YTQxNjgyNzA5MTU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-04T10:35:40Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-04T10:35:40Z"},"message":"test(search): make three flaky xfail/xpass search tests deterministic (#705)\n\n## What\n\nThree integration tests carried `xfail(reason=\"Fake embeddings produce\nlow similarity — search returns empty\")` and **xpassed** in a full run.\nThis makes them deterministic and removes the last xpass from the suite.\n\nThey matter because the xfail/xpass profile is the signal that caught\n#700's near-miss — where a bare `union()` silently changed the candidate\nset for embedded rows, invisible to every test written for it and\nvisible only as an xpass count of 3 vs 1 against clean `main`. Noise in\nthat signal is expensive.\n\n**The stated reason was a misdiagnosis in both cases.**\n\n## `tests/test_p3_3_graph_fanout.py` — wrong fixture, not low similarity\n\nThese wrote setup through the `db` fixture, which opens a transaction\nand rolls it back at teardown — per its own docstring,\n*\"session.commit() flushes data without committing the outer\ntransaction\"*. `search_memories` runs on a **separate connection**, so\nit never saw those rows. Storage logged `row_count: 0`: they failed at\nthe SQL layer, nowhere near the similarity gate.\n\nThey xpassed in a full run only because `assert len(results) >= 1` was\nsatisfied by **other tests' committed rows** — `tenant_id` is a single\nsession-wide constant and 13 files commit into it via `sc`. So even when\ngreen, they asserted nothing about fan-out.\n\nNow they write via `sc` — the fixture documented for precisely this\n(\"use this instead of `db.add()` when the data needs to be visible …\ne.g. search\") — using the bulk insert and bulk link helpers, and assert\non **their own memory ids** instead of a bare count.\n\nThey also get a tenant of their own. A unique query token is **not**\nsufficient isolation, and this is measured rather than assumed:\n`fake_embedding` is a bag-of-words hash, not a near-orthogonal one, and\nan unrelated committed row (`\"User prefers dark mode in the editor\"`)\nscores cosine **0.3341** against a random token — above the 0.3\n`min_similarity` gate. A foreign row can still take a top_k slot.\n\n## `tests/test_visibility.py` — simply a stale marker\n\nAlready wrote via `sc`, and xpasses in isolation on a clean database\n(3/3 runs). Nothing flaky; the marker was obsolete. Dropped.\n\n## So it cannot recur silently\n\n- **`xfail_strict = true`** in `pytest.ini`. An xfail that starts\npassing is now a failure rather than a green XPASS nobody reads.\nVerified by probe — re-adding a stale marker turns that test `FAILED`.\nBoth remaining markers were checked against the setting: one is already\n`strict=True`, and the other (dedup-vs-visibility) genuinely xfails,\nreproducibly and in isolation. **This is the one change here with\nsuite-wide reach — flagging it explicitly; happy to drop it if you'd\nrather it landed on its own.**\n- **Corrected the `tenant_id` docstring**, which claimed *\"Unique tenant\nID per test module\"* while returning one session-wide constant. That is\nthe misconception these tests were built on, and 70 tests across 11\nfiles read it.\n\n## Verification\n\nBoth rewritten assertions confirmed load-bearing by mutation —\ninverting, not deleting: linking 4 of 5 memories fails the set-equality\ncheck; searching at `top_k=3` fails the top_k assertion. Restored, both\ngreen.\n\nI also confirmed the graph-boost path these tests exist to cover\n**does** fire, rather than assuming it — `entities.search_vector` is\npopulated by migration 001's trigger (`'populartopic…':1`), and\n`fts_search_entities` matches the entity, so the boost and its\n`GRAPH_MAX_BOOSTED_MEMORIES` cap are genuinely exercised. The helper\ndocstring records that this depends on the DB having been migrated:\n`Base.metadata.create_all` alone leaves the column NULL and would skip\nthe boost silently. The raw `UPDATE entities SET search_vector` that\ncame out was redundant with that trigger.\n\nFull suite against local pgvector with `alembic upgrade head`:\n\n| | passed | xfailed | xpassed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 4081 | 2 | 3 |\n| this branch | 4084 | 2 | **0** |\n\nThe +3 are these three tests now passing for real.\n`core-storage-api/tests/` 103 passed. Root `tests/` is not in CI's ruff\nscope, so nothing was reformatted there.\n\n## Noted, deliberately not in scope\n\n- `tests/test_p2_semantic_dedup.py:374` has the same\n`db`-write-then-storage-read trap; its \"100 memories\" / \"1000 memories\"\nlatency labels measure whatever is committed in the shared tenant. It is\n`@pytest.mark.benchmark` and CI excludes benchmarks, so it misleads\nlocal numbers only.\n- 64 of 128 tests that request `db` never use it, which makes them\n*look* transactionally isolated while committing through the service\nlayer. That is the leftover-row generator behind the old xpasses. A\nsuite-wide cleanup is a bigger change than this fix.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"7a51133c03daaff6a46461e3fb79d1737736d9a5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/7a51133c03daaff6a46461e3fb79d1737736d9a5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqccB8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA1sEQACFTFP32n5CvgL4NIcoEUaea\nXtmzUhRO4lh6MIkWNDjLsJG1LjbIc7rxCY6zQ7HuwM7tl+rrO5Em9EDNnu0wmeN3\nPV7WxzAy5B2aC0WODrHJpw+uzRKvJrEdQ0VASoYu8fQmjGfmRe8AcLtANdFpl5oS\nbIaSUyjBKDkRg+oULMQDf0qdw5GAGaMv1Xd5jQbpcH/bSY7iAHMhNGUFb5+fy04I\nhNrmclLoppmBHaYkk12xLIzKuXoK5YGBEDSAIIFZRqjlfNaoTnYZJmy8eQPZQlyx\nHGBLRMPTmd79occiXXVumO70984xvI2qgkpncxyY6JkLSFIi4ctaJD4nMXR3m20A\nil6oAgndTtIGHfNvFok6H9xN2XjjMlsIA8IXVhcbMBPCuhi4C6Z/SPUsGUdimiEX\nmf6TkVrfzj3RSnrdo4nG0VEE0gFU1CBCO5OJ1+uV5izsbPbTie4yvGlaWIvj8JPJ\n6n7xdkoa4toT7ll1mUvllvUA5gfA1FXRj7Ol00VDSp6BD86TWTTmUn3c6h1axsM1\nlzVFwmFIsj8c1ZxTeSf61HkAWALZ2dqWcmKWDFUA9fRw7rzqpjO5kB7H5xd3gpWD\n8jSa6VTk/TKjNLvRXgPq3ZbtKkV9vivfRhlN1asD6xXBXYSB2cJ1krcQD1AZqafh\nyu+/QSsLyvR7ubSFMJRO\n=zyKA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 7a51133c03daaff6a46461e3fb79d1737736d9a5\nparent a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785839740 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785839740 +0300\n\ntest(search): make three flaky xfail/xpass search tests deterministic (#705)\n\n## What\n\nThree integration tests carried `xfail(reason=\"Fake embeddings produce\nlow similarity — search returns empty\")` and **xpassed** in a full run.\nThis makes them deterministic and removes the last xpass from the suite.\n\nThey matter because the xfail/xpass profile is the signal that caught\n#700's near-miss — where a bare `union()` silently changed the candidate\nset for embedded rows, invisible to every test written for it and\nvisible only as an xpass count of 3 vs 1 against clean `main`. Noise in\nthat signal is expensive.\n\n**The stated reason was a misdiagnosis in both cases.**\n\n## `tests/test_p3_3_graph_fanout.py` — wrong fixture, not low similarity\n\nThese wrote setup through the `db` fixture, which opens a transaction\nand rolls it back at teardown — per its own docstring,\n*\"session.commit() flushes data without committing the outer\ntransaction\"*. `search_memories` runs on a **separate connection**, so\nit never saw those rows. Storage logged `row_count: 0`: they failed at\nthe SQL layer, nowhere near the similarity gate.\n\nThey xpassed in a full run only because `assert len(results) >= 1` was\nsatisfied by **other tests' committed rows** — `tenant_id` is a single\nsession-wide constant and 13 files commit into it via `sc`. So even when\ngreen, they asserted nothing about fan-out.\n\nNow they write via `sc` — the fixture documented for precisely this\n(\"use this instead of `db.add()` when the data needs to be visible …\ne.g. search\") — using the bulk insert and bulk link helpers, and assert\non **their own memory ids** instead of a bare count.\n\nThey also get a tenant of their own. A unique query token is **not**\nsufficient isolation, and this is measured rather than assumed:\n`fake_embedding` is a bag-of-words hash, not a near-orthogonal one, and\nan unrelated committed row (`\"User prefers dark mode in the editor\"`)\nscores cosine **0.3341** against a random token — above the 0.3\n`min_similarity` gate. A foreign row can still take a top_k slot.\n\n## `tests/test_visibility.py` — simply a stale marker\n\nAlready wrote via `sc`, and xpasses in isolation on a clean database\n(3/3 runs). Nothing flaky; the marker was obsolete. Dropped.\n\n## So it cannot recur silently\n\n- **`xfail_strict = true`** in `pytest.ini`. An xfail that starts\npassing is now a failure rather than a green XPASS nobody reads.\nVerified by probe — re-adding a stale marker turns that test `FAILED`.\nBoth remaining markers were checked against the setting: one is already\n`strict=True`, and the other (dedup-vs-visibility) genuinely xfails,\nreproducibly and in isolation. **This is the one change here with\nsuite-wide reach — flagging it explicitly; happy to drop it if you'd\nrather it landed on its own.**\n- **Corrected the `tenant_id` docstring**, which claimed *\"Unique tenant\nID per test module\"* while returning one session-wide constant. That is\nthe misconception these tests were built on, and 70 tests across 11\nfiles read it.\n\n## Verification\n\nBoth rewritten assertions confirmed load-bearing by mutation —\ninverting, not deleting: linking 4 of 5 memories fails the set-equality\ncheck; searching at `top_k=3` fails the top_k assertion. Restored, both\ngreen.\n\nI also confirmed the graph-boost path these tests exist to cover\n**does** fire, rather than assuming it — `entities.search_vector` is\npopulated by migration 001's trigger (`'populartopic…':1`), and\n`fts_search_entities` matches the entity, so the boost and its\n`GRAPH_MAX_BOOSTED_MEMORIES` cap are genuinely exercised. The helper\ndocstring records that this depends on the DB having been migrated:\n`Base.metadata.create_all` alone leaves the column NULL and would skip\nthe boost silently. The raw `UPDATE entities SET search_vector` that\ncame out was redundant with that trigger.\n\nFull suite against local pgvector with `alembic upgrade head`:\n\n| | passed | xfailed | xpassed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` | 4081 | 2 | 3 |\n| this branch | 4084 | 2 | **0** |\n\nThe +3 are these three tests now passing for real.\n`core-storage-api/tests/` 103 passed. Root `tests/` is not in CI's ruff\nscope, so nothing was reformatted there.\n\n## Noted, deliberately not in scope\n\n- `tests/test_p2_semantic_dedup.py:374` has the same\n`db`-write-then-storage-read trap; its \"100 memories\" / \"1000 memories\"\nlatency labels measure whatever is committed in the shared tenant. It is\n`@pytest.mark.benchmark` and CI excludes benchmarks, so it misleads\nlocal numbers only.\n- 64 of 128 tests that request `db` never use it, which makes them\n*look* transactionally isolated while committing through the service\nlayer. That is the leftover-row generator behind the old xpasses. A\nsuite-wide cleanup is a bigger change than this fix.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-04T10:35:43Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8351077e2c86185f437d2979c3567a4168270915/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e"}]},{"sha":"a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGEzOGM5NGNjYjg0ZjI2NGM1NjA4ZGJkNzM2MGI2OGU4MjA5NzQ3N2U","commit":{"author":{"name":"caura-deploy-bot[bot]","email":"265395343+caura-deploy-bot[bot]@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-04T08:45:44Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-04T08:45:44Z"},"message":"chore: release main (#672)\n\n:robot: I have created a release *beep* *boop*\n---\n\n\n<details><summary>backend: 2.27.0</summary>\n\n##\n[2.27.0](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/compare/backend-v2.26.0...backend-v2.27.0)\n(2026-08-04)\n\n\n### Features\n\n* **insights:** clarity contract — findings about the work, not the\nmachinery ([#698](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/698))\n([6cc0f67](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32))\n* **insights:** filter input noise — opaque payloads, title-dedup,\ndiscover window\n([#681](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/681))\n([7b5bf64](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f))\n* **interviewer-cli:** install/uninstall to schedule the cron in one\ncommand ([#674](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/674))\n([9122f08](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86))\n* **search:** org flag to disable query-time entity/graph retrieval\n([#677](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/677))\n([2b52546](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5))\n* **search:** pluggable reranking component (MiniLM cross-encoder),\nships dark\n([#669](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/669))\n([e18dd95](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71))\n* **search:** remote rerank provider (sidecar) + opt-in local build\n([#680](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/680))\n([72b453e](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd))\n\n\n### Bug Fixes\n\n* **ci:** pin common/'s ruff contract and lint-gate it\n([#685](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/685))\n([abfabdb](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955))\n* **common:** converge common/ruff.toml with enterprise's stricter\nconfig ([#696](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/696))\n([3518d4d](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73))\n* **insights:** declare numpy so discover keeps clustering\n([#679](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/679))\n([443fd0c](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a))\n* **search:** keep FTS-only rows past the legacy cosine gate\n([#687](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/687))\n([#697](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/697))\n([9d9003d](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746))\n\n\n### Documentation\n\n* **ci:** document that the two claude-review entry points don't share\ngates ([#686](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/686))\n([8d77b49](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751))\n* **review:** record the measured membership values on the review gate\n([#676](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/676))\n([81fbbc6](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7))\n</details>\n\n---\nThis PR was generated with [Release\nPlease](https://github.com/googleapis/release-please). See\n[documentation](https://github.com/googleapis/release-please#release-please).\n\nSigned-off-by: release-please[bot] <release-please[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: caura-deploy-bot[bot] <265395343+caura-deploy-bot[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"8262bed34270566812ec9f19be971c51411b32a1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/8262bed34270566812ec9f19be971c51411b32a1"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcaa4CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAs6wQABNU9HaUjoRHGwI6UVRpO972\nA0CMgLiYWfhftYSLWkgEDm9mTKrlI6Gj1KfH1Q+fdCwJ7Wssy5brL+qVUuytqbaZ\nrT+Euql6Dcw3BWC7pnbfGNOtkamFlZoWXkVhZuXITQij6zIwmFI5+Ck560qvXsVa\nGWB78xT3fcaCubduK7lAwE+laIFtcwjzH925TkCWzRI/ZYGBFPp9Bgxp7H8ppWy+\nCsXJa+aYZdjKqxf2EM+X0btq5iNajYobGVqz0cNSCDVU5MO9EQGOSt0GvwMhHTMm\nE8qO6Ut5RMoXYOqdHnpjd4Q6wDN7aMvhPVGWdve9LMI9EDkNGoE+VlB5NhnvY30P\nU89X/tgqWm10hsOdCENov2ntU34xgoo5PJ5tzv5oEk5+Xv8mv0YUNa75S4KV+1w9\nmAqknsjZUP5xM2J4qhn8l9HYbntyMROn1VGycV71yhGNNlAPQ5Si4SSeLsgEWGmK\naelY2ljZmsyXC7Z7UdZ5FXmQW8uckDpaNHq1oNqU5Kb+wo527jROmV45WQIEyiz6\nY/uXP8sIpzAkwCcjU3vAtGQKDLulv6VY0UKX27jBjSAtaiwFFj7cIhbr1Sq6yj/8\nYQ2oufXku8UgWJJ1kz33fvnFYnbF19c1iy6KcBvOobo9ARbqPohDQ/o3LGdDoKEV\nM0tbKM+LKN8Xnun5IB0O\n=AT9G\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 8262bed34270566812ec9f19be971c51411b32a1\nparent 6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32\nauthor caura-deploy-bot[bot] <265395343+caura-deploy-bot[bot]@users.noreply.github.com> 1785833144 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785833144 +0300\n\nchore: release main (#672)\n\n:robot: I have created a release *beep* *boop*\n---\n\n\n<details><summary>backend: 2.27.0</summary>\n\n##\n[2.27.0](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/compare/backend-v2.26.0...backend-v2.27.0)\n(2026-08-04)\n\n\n### Features\n\n* **insights:** clarity contract — findings about the work, not the\nmachinery ([#698](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/698))\n([6cc0f67](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32))\n* **insights:** filter input noise — opaque payloads, title-dedup,\ndiscover window\n([#681](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/681))\n([7b5bf64](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f))\n* **interviewer-cli:** install/uninstall to schedule the cron in one\ncommand ([#674](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/674))\n([9122f08](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86))\n* **search:** org flag to disable query-time entity/graph retrieval\n([#677](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/677))\n([2b52546](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5))\n* **search:** pluggable reranking component (MiniLM cross-encoder),\nships dark\n([#669](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/669))\n([e18dd95](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71))\n* **search:** remote rerank provider (sidecar) + opt-in local build\n([#680](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/680))\n([72b453e](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd))\n\n\n### Bug Fixes\n\n* **ci:** pin common/'s ruff contract and lint-gate it\n([#685](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/685))\n([abfabdb](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955))\n* **common:** converge common/ruff.toml with enterprise's stricter\nconfig ([#696](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/696))\n([3518d4d](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73))\n* **insights:** declare numpy so discover keeps clustering\n([#679](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/679))\n([443fd0c](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a))\n* **search:** keep FTS-only rows past the legacy cosine gate\n([#687](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/687))\n([#697](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/697))\n([9d9003d](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746))\n\n\n### Documentation\n\n* **ci:** document that the two claude-review entry points don't share\ngates ([#686](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/686))\n([8d77b49](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751))\n* **review:** record the measured membership values on the review gate\n([#676](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/issues/676))\n([81fbbc6](https://github.com/caura-ai/caura-memclaw/commit/81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7))\n</details>\n\n---\nThis PR was generated with [Release\nPlease](https://github.com/googleapis/release-please). See\n[documentation](https://github.com/googleapis/release-please#release-please).\n\nSigned-off-by: release-please[bot] <release-please[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: caura-deploy-bot[bot] <265395343+caura-deploy-bot[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-04T08:45:46Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/a38c94ccb84f264c5608dbd7360b68e82097477e/comments","author":{"login":"caura-deploy-bot[bot]","id":265395343,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOD9Gcjw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/229147182?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/caura-deploy-bot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/caura-deploy-bot%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32"}]},{"sha":"6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDZjYzBmNjc0NTJiZDg3OTc1OTMxOTVlNTMwMzU0YmZkOTY1MjRjMzI","commit":{"author":{"name":"ran-taig","email":"ran@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-04T05:43:02Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-04T05:43:02Z"},"message":"feat(insights): clarity contract — findings about the work, not the machinery (#698)\n\n## Problem\n\nFollow-up to the eToro insights quality analysis (#679, #681 fixed the\ninput side; this fixes the **output** side). Persisted insights are\nfrequently unreadable or meta — they discuss the methodology of\nproducing insights rather than any insight:\n\n- **80% of nightly discover findings** contain clustering-machinery\nlanguage, e.g. a real persisted \"insight\": *\"Cluster 5 shows\ninconsistent confidence (std=0.22)… Recommendation: Re-cluster Cluster 5\nusing a finer label set.\"* That is k-means QA, not intelligence.\n- **67% of discover recommendations target the memory system** (\"merge\nCluster 0\", \"add verification memories\") rather than anything an\noperator can act on.\n- Content is a single run-on paragraph — `[Insight/discover] Cluster 5:\n{title-truncated-mid-wor}: {description} Recommendation: …` — with\nprompt-local references (\"Memory 5\", \"memories 1, 8, 18\") that mean\nnothing after the run.\n\nRoot cause is the prompts: the persona was a *\"memory analyst\nspecializing in knowledge topology\"* asked *\"are any clusters\nsurprising?\"*. The LLM answered exactly what it was asked.\n\n## The clarity contract (\"front-page test\")\n\nA persisted finding must read like a line a human would put in a status\nreport.\n\n| Piece | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Prompts re-aimed** (6 modes) | Persona → *operations analyst reading\nthe team's work records*. Clusters/weights/repeat-counts are a **lens,\nnever the subject**. World modes (discover/patterns) forbid machinery\nsubjects and bookkeeping actions, with a bad→good example pair; hygiene\nmodes (contradictions/failures/stale/divergence) keep records as\nlegitimate subject but must name the disputed fact in world terms and\nresolve it concretely. *\"An empty findings list is a valid answer.\"* |\n| **Structured schema** | `headline` / `what_happened` /\n`why_it_matters` / `recommended_action`. Legacy answers\n(title/description/recommendation) accepted as fallbacks; legacy keys\n**mirrored** onto every finding so existing consumers keep working\nunchanged. |\n| **Clean renderer** | Headline first (enrichment derives the memory\ntitle from it), then short labeled lines. Method/provenance (`focus,\nscope, memories_analyzed, clustered, gate_rejected`) moves to\n`metadata.method` — transparent and auditable, out of the text. |\n| **Sharpness gate + self-repair** | Deterministic post-LLM regex checks\n(no extra LLM call on the happy path): machinery subjects & bookkeeping\nactions rejected in world modes, prompt-local numbering rejected\neverywhere. Violations are quoted back to the LLM in **one** repair\nretry; still-violating findings are dropped and counted as\n`gate_rejected` in the result — the ongoing per-run quality metric. |\n\n**Before → after (real finding):**\n> ❌ `[Insight/discover] Cluster 3: Periodic self-health checks &\nheartbeat monitoring: … Low std (≈0.08) suggests a stable monitoring\nroutine… Recommendation: Merge with Cluster 0 into a unified workflow.`\n\n> ✅ `Health monitoring runs as two disconnected loops`\n> `What happened: Heartbeat checks and cleanup escalation fire\nindependently across 6 agents; a disk-full event on Jul 28 alerted twice\nwith no shared cooldown.`\n> `Why it matters: Duplicate alerts and no shared cooldown.`\n> `Action: Unify triggers and cooldowns in the monitoring workflow\nconfig.`\n\n## Compatibility\n\n- No storage/API-shape changes: findings carry strictly more fields;\n`gate_rejected` is additive; metadata keeps\n`recommendation`/`confidence` (plus new `headline`, `method`).\n- Old-style LLM answers still sanitize correctly (legacy fallback),\nproven by the pre-existing stub tests passing unmodified.\n- Nothing parses the old `[Insight/` content prefix (verified across\nrepo).\n\n## Tests\n\n12 new: sanitize (new schema, legacy fallback, mirrors), gate (machinery\nsubject, bookkeeping action, prompt-numbering, hygiene-mode allowance,\nclean pass), persist renderer + `metadata.method`, repair loop\n(violation → exactly one repair call quoting the offender;\nstill-violating → dropped + counted). All six prompt templates\nsmoke-tested for `.format()` safety against brace-containing content.\n**47/47** in the service suite, **87/87** across the three insights\nsuites; `ruff` format+lint clean.\n\n## Out of scope (deliberate)\n\n- Machine-actionable resolutions for hygiene modes (`{action:\ntransition, target_id, …}`) — second PR as planned.\n- Offline A/B against a real night's prod input — validation step before\nthe eToro cutover.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3149a39e918c5f1b2dfe1d79b46461f91d60e1cd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/3149a39e918c5f1b2dfe1d79b46461f91d60e1cd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcXvmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmHwQAAZjArNw0YKFLTJgXmkgiHKg\nB3FIcaPLuiH+8S3IQAyiZENdSIFJsPCRjrH/9kszBovkZzT+ijMlyyKDzuDbYtMi\nvFUosZucpwXRLS/YRTI+XXhUHENaLvCkJT9rUxSkahjLn8Hv6S7EXVcrSzhYaJ0i\np3FA0CbF4fQ01jVb0Xzf7S6YYkBITaDD9gnJxZmDfykdpxE8hB3vmNFqVrvswAV/\n82moGMxaa1++pd2nniT9VptYIjch2lSa9Muk4Mr6mse+iRibpmcJHrG9TqlPFET0\n7Aaa2yOAxQANPwOW8eGgkczAmyVBWzCLPSXq9Z4Aqk9J6z+qP8zC6pnT/HfI5r84\n5CMTfA+Xl5VI28Bl4PofHbxLKTGVy3GpyfpzQq0K9ATlpptD59dIrJZN3TvdvTY0\n6bldgFYOSHLkRpueWT8Fg8F79b9jxTe243+7EFM2OzFmRboN6Wk4gYtfDBZQ4KqQ\nVLlLX45GrTcksCfXL0gvqA2s2V1+EORv3lsAmAu7GZYqEPqCIyOWD/8DtUS8N+M6\n1VXTQMb4amcTj8E5+QmcIzKd6w0Ui6M6ttrGDGDuS9fUqe5TDxwwPQqv4YfBppeG\nxOhz2JPdCgC8uyoOkl0ZUyFfe9FXJxLm8bFrVWNbMwNmHYL6SFPCSW/CwWUkemQ/\nD+VFe/QtpHtSQz93S4lY\n=fyO0\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 3149a39e918c5f1b2dfe1d79b46461f91d60e1cd\nparent 4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451\nauthor ran-taig <ran@caura.ai> 1785822182 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785822182 +0300\n\nfeat(insights): clarity contract — findings about the work, not the machinery (#698)\n\n## Problem\n\nFollow-up to the eToro insights quality analysis (#679, #681 fixed the\ninput side; this fixes the **output** side). Persisted insights are\nfrequently unreadable or meta — they discuss the methodology of\nproducing insights rather than any insight:\n\n- **80% of nightly discover findings** contain clustering-machinery\nlanguage, e.g. a real persisted \"insight\": *\"Cluster 5 shows\ninconsistent confidence (std=0.22)… Recommendation: Re-cluster Cluster 5\nusing a finer label set.\"* That is k-means QA, not intelligence.\n- **67% of discover recommendations target the memory system** (\"merge\nCluster 0\", \"add verification memories\") rather than anything an\noperator can act on.\n- Content is a single run-on paragraph — `[Insight/discover] Cluster 5:\n{title-truncated-mid-wor}: {description} Recommendation: …` — with\nprompt-local references (\"Memory 5\", \"memories 1, 8, 18\") that mean\nnothing after the run.\n\nRoot cause is the prompts: the persona was a *\"memory analyst\nspecializing in knowledge topology\"* asked *\"are any clusters\nsurprising?\"*. The LLM answered exactly what it was asked.\n\n## The clarity contract (\"front-page test\")\n\nA persisted finding must read like a line a human would put in a status\nreport.\n\n| Piece | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Prompts re-aimed** (6 modes) | Persona → *operations analyst reading\nthe team's work records*. Clusters/weights/repeat-counts are a **lens,\nnever the subject**. World modes (discover/patterns) forbid machinery\nsubjects and bookkeeping actions, with a bad→good example pair; hygiene\nmodes (contradictions/failures/stale/divergence) keep records as\nlegitimate subject but must name the disputed fact in world terms and\nresolve it concretely. *\"An empty findings list is a valid answer.\"* |\n| **Structured schema** | `headline` / `what_happened` /\n`why_it_matters` / `recommended_action`. Legacy answers\n(title/description/recommendation) accepted as fallbacks; legacy keys\n**mirrored** onto every finding so existing consumers keep working\nunchanged. |\n| **Clean renderer** | Headline first (enrichment derives the memory\ntitle from it), then short labeled lines. Method/provenance (`focus,\nscope, memories_analyzed, clustered, gate_rejected`) moves to\n`metadata.method` — transparent and auditable, out of the text. |\n| **Sharpness gate + self-repair** | Deterministic post-LLM regex checks\n(no extra LLM call on the happy path): machinery subjects & bookkeeping\nactions rejected in world modes, prompt-local numbering rejected\neverywhere. Violations are quoted back to the LLM in **one** repair\nretry; still-violating findings are dropped and counted as\n`gate_rejected` in the result — the ongoing per-run quality metric. |\n\n**Before → after (real finding):**\n> ❌ `[Insight/discover] Cluster 3: Periodic self-health checks &\nheartbeat monitoring: … Low std (≈0.08) suggests a stable monitoring\nroutine… Recommendation: Merge with Cluster 0 into a unified workflow.`\n\n> ✅ `Health monitoring runs as two disconnected loops`\n> `What happened: Heartbeat checks and cleanup escalation fire\nindependently across 6 agents; a disk-full event on Jul 28 alerted twice\nwith no shared cooldown.`\n> `Why it matters: Duplicate alerts and no shared cooldown.`\n> `Action: Unify triggers and cooldowns in the monitoring workflow\nconfig.`\n\n## Compatibility\n\n- No storage/API-shape changes: findings carry strictly more fields;\n`gate_rejected` is additive; metadata keeps\n`recommendation`/`confidence` (plus new `headline`, `method`).\n- Old-style LLM answers still sanitize correctly (legacy fallback),\nproven by the pre-existing stub tests passing unmodified.\n- Nothing parses the old `[Insight/` content prefix (verified across\nrepo).\n\n## Tests\n\n12 new: sanitize (new schema, legacy fallback, mirrors), gate (machinery\nsubject, bookkeeping action, prompt-numbering, hygiene-mode allowance,\nclean pass), persist renderer + `metadata.method`, repair loop\n(violation → exactly one repair call quoting the offender;\nstill-violating → dropped + counted). All six prompt templates\nsmoke-tested for `.format()` safety against brace-containing content.\n**47/47** in the service suite, **87/87** across the three insights\nsuites; `ruff` format+lint clean.\n\n## Out of scope (deliberate)\n\n- Machine-actionable resolutions for hygiene modes (`{action:\ntransition, target_id, …}`) — second PR as planned.\n- Offline A/B against a real night's prod input — validation step before\nthe eToro cutover.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-04T05:43:05Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/6cc0f67452bd8797593195e530354bfd96524c32/comments","author":{"login":"ran-taig","id":254242316,"node_id":"U_kgDODyduDA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/254242316?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig","html_url":"https://github.com/ran-taig","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451"}]},{"sha":"4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDRlODg1YzEyYjQ0NDA2ZmUxMDNlYTQ4NWU4OWIxMGIyNjdlODc0NTE","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-03T10:01:50Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-03T10:01:50Z"},"message":"ci(review): default the review model to Sonnet 5 (#701)\n\nBumps this repo's inlined reviewer from `claude-sonnet-4-6` to\n`claude-sonnet-5`, the\ndefault the org's shared pipeline has used for both review and\ndeclined-finding capture\nsince `caura-ai/.github@v1`.\n\n`caura-memclaw` is the last repo still on 4.6, and the highest-volume\nreviewer in the org\n— 211 of 292 sampled reviews ran here.\n\n### Why the default is bumped in place rather than inherited\n\nThis repo cannot adopt the shared workflow that would have carried the\nnew default in.\nGitHub refuses to let a **public** repo consume a reusable workflow or\ncomposite action\nfrom a **private** one, which is what blocked #668. The inlined script\nis therefore\npermanent here until `caura-ai/.github` goes public, so the default is\nset locally.\n\n### Cost\n\nSame tier as 4.6, newer model, and currently cheaper: Sonnet 5 is at an\nintroductory\n$2/$10 per MTok against 4.6's $3/$15. The intro rate ends 2026-08-31,\nafter which both\nlist at $3/$15 — so this is never a price increase.\n\n### Verification\n\n- `bash -n` and `shellcheck` clean.\n- Confirmed `claude --print --model claude-sonnet-5 --output-format\njson` returns a result\nwith `total_cost_usd` populated, so the cost footer and the job-summary\ntoken table keep\nworking. The `MAX_BUDGET_USD` ceiling is model-independent and\nunaffected.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2318e599b4e49614bdcf96e29043753b7d9db378","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/2318e599b4e49614bdcf96e29043753b7d9db378"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcGcOCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA71cQAEFOa2ugbAvKO1koaO1Yw/ne\nJFSRnwjJhSP6diHp+H96lzQgaxZHYAOdKuI7kOn/74RjTBeGspqNb+SOkWsy7ZT8\nBqJszMadg4YMr6AUCikNocxPqAWJzeJSmdWSFpqt/39Zx75CdXpcsxQTjLRtWRlv\n+2mJ8RUwoxfg33BrQKpcoD1Q8ry4GBmj1hdUCdOysQQHx2fZlc9ix5Mcd4npwhhY\ntnN5gMk0uRIRVqDzQS13d+NTEA9MqgqoNyov4SGQhwaOi7JBN2Y87sURQgap7A07\n1c0Z1xfLeHQk0cpehYuKw6brktA3oEXfw7Q4XrnceAQ2YKFV2trZtkBtioocJu2S\nUb8IAJE2ouWd2UtPv1oymZdDyowNql4ZOnF8OkuzfLf/rqyLPRxV+fwfsjABVFWE\nYWL3gXO3oUpch7xSl/irI3Ko4CCvE1t8UGBrNasyyxjotE97zrImKSg1w5ZmCImm\n7MH/xaw/WPIXKSDL6UevuessFZ+GcvsAa6399NfWBde7MWksHjRip6dlq46fU2Uq\nPOywo2Y1C7DswnlDIHo+9XcKsJuacUAvnfn7xtdYCfFHjd28NNN2uiie5/K7TbWW\nY/1WHkzB20WEmS0wEbB17rsGCxkioadzrUKLogIrykMS+n6KkIotzCYWrjsaG4zl\nhrDA4NkO79bY+cVscsOX\n=4rdu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 2318e599b4e49614bdcf96e29043753b7d9db378\nparent e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785751310 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785751310 +0300\n\nci(review): default the review model to Sonnet 5 (#701)\n\nBumps this repo's inlined reviewer from `claude-sonnet-4-6` to\n`claude-sonnet-5`, the\ndefault the org's shared pipeline has used for both review and\ndeclined-finding capture\nsince `caura-ai/.github@v1`.\n\n`caura-memclaw` is the last repo still on 4.6, and the highest-volume\nreviewer in the org\n— 211 of 292 sampled reviews ran here.\n\n### Why the default is bumped in place rather than inherited\n\nThis repo cannot adopt the shared workflow that would have carried the\nnew default in.\nGitHub refuses to let a **public** repo consume a reusable workflow or\ncomposite action\nfrom a **private** one, which is what blocked #668. The inlined script\nis therefore\npermanent here until `caura-ai/.github` goes public, so the default is\nset locally.\n\n### Cost\n\nSame tier as 4.6, newer model, and currently cheaper: Sonnet 5 is at an\nintroductory\n$2/$10 per MTok against 4.6's $3/$15. The intro rate ends 2026-08-31,\nafter which both\nlist at $3/$15 — so this is never a price increase.\n\n### Verification\n\n- `bash -n` and `shellcheck` clean.\n- Confirmed `claude --print --model claude-sonnet-5 --output-format\njson` returns a result\nwith `total_cost_usd` populated, so the cost footer and the job-summary\ntoken table keep\nworking. The `MAX_BUDGET_USD` ceiling is model-independent and\nunaffected.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-03T10:01:51Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/4e885c12b44406fe103ea485e89b10b267e87451/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7"}]},{"sha":"e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGU4MmJhZGU2ZjBlYThiMmMwN2IwNWYzNzNjNmNmOTdhNDcxZDFmYTc","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-03T08:14:28Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-03T08:14:28Z"},"message":"test(search): pin the FTS-only candidate-window bug (#687) (#699)\n\nReproduces #687 locally. **Marked `xfail(strict=True)` rather than\nfixed** — both viable fixes change core ranking, and I'd rather you pick\nthe tradeoff than have me guess it. Reasoning below.\n\n## The mechanism\n\nThe scored search fetches `top_k * SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR` candidates\nordered by score. A NULL-embedding row scores on `fts_score` alone,\nwhile embedded rows near the query score far higher — so it never\nsurvives the `LIMIT`.\n\n`PostFilterResults` exempts NULL-embedding rows from the cosine gate,\nand #697 added the same exemption to the legacy post-filter. **Both run\ndownstream of the cut**, so the mechanism CAURA-594/CAURA-679 built for\nexactly this window is unreachable.\n\nMeasured on prod:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| tenant size | 791 memories |\n| embedded row scores | 0.35 – 0.39 |\n| FTS-only row score | **0** (reported) |\n| candidate window | 10 (`top_k=5` × factor 2) |\n| agent `search_profile` | **empty** — every value from constants |\n\nSo this is code, not configuration.\n\n## Why every existing test missed it\n\nThey run in tenants of a handful of rows, where a 10-candidate window\nexcludes nothing — **including the test I added in #697**. That's the\nhonest lesson: that test pinned the contract at the right layer but in\nconditions where the bug cannot appear.\n\nThe reproduction controls everything except the window: competitors\ncarry the *query's own* embedding, so they legitimately outrank on\ncosine, and their content omits the token, so the subject row is the\n**only** FTS match and **is** exempt from the cosine gate. It fails\nwith:\n\n> FTS-only row cut by the candidate window (#687): it is the ONLY row\nwhose content contains 'zqxjvbn…', its search_vector FTS-matches, and it\nis exempt from the cosine gate — but 10 higher-cosine rows fill the\ntop_k(5) * SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR(2) window first. search returned 5\nrow(s), none of them the subject.\n\n## Why I didn't fix it here\n\n**The cheap fix doesn't work.** Flooring the FTS-only similarity to\n`min_similarity` fails on the numbers: min is **0.3**, the measured\nembedded noise floor is **0.35–0.39**. The row still loses. There is no\nconstant that works, because `ts_rank_cd/(1+ts_rank_cd)` is not on a\ncomparable scale to cosine — a row whose entire content *is* the query\nscores ~0.05.\n\nThat leaves two real options:\n\n1. **Normalise `fts_score` onto the cosine scale.** Principled, and\nfixes the root scale mismatch — but it reprices *every* query. This repo\nmeasures such choices (`SIMILARITY_BLEND` cites a LoCoMo sweep), so it\nwants a benchmark run, not a judgement call.\n2. **Reserve candidate and result slots for FTS-only rows.** Blast\nradius bounded to the transient deferred window, and arguably what users\nwant (read-your-own-write) — but it spans the SQL, the pipeline\npost-filter, *and* the legacy path, and it displaces one result during\nthe window.\n\nShipping either unbenchmarked into the path that defines the product's\nrecall quality seemed like the wrong call to make alone.\n\n`strict=True` so the marker cannot rot: the moment a fix lands, this\nfails loudly and forces its removal.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `tests/pipeline/` + `tests/test_integration_search.py` | 120 passed, 1\nxfailed |\n| Test genuinely reproduces | fails with the message above when the\nmarker is removed |\n| `mypy src/` (core-api) | no issues, 189 files |\n| `ruff check` / `format --check` | 1 pre-existing issue each, identical\nto `origin/main`; all reformat hunks are ≤ line 448, my addition starts\nat 492 |\n\nRun against local pgvector 0.8.6 with `alembic upgrade head`, mirroring\nCI — which matters here: the fixtures build the schema with\n`Base.metadata.create_all`, so without alembic the `search_vector`\ntrigger from migration 001 is absent and any FTS test fails for the\nwrong reason.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"e890d190b1fdf5c1186a56c85e829c9ddf1b4d89","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/e890d190b1fdf5c1186a56c85e829c9ddf1b4d89"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcE3kCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAATV0QAHWC365DpTOHl7dgK7oSKXFq\nf/zVMivC+4yj0PGQxORT9xCoJpL/0l5iUSiTPaXkIFSZVZFZ8tVgZwKIVlyCnIAn\nwC4QH/H6cNvhWK/ordIxdM/uHHcE/BnJWqdVAF8A1kXn4yc9DfjlWyW7OOXzMPo5\nt60YYPVcu69XJ+l6Itb7qPd37DKWEdrxN/UHaecZGxgJJUm+USEC5y7H7bfAJOE1\nazXThOo2/FgVGhyTTAP9n7WKqDpByt3khwQkqwQdnqE7/S4mG2nQ56FJm9am1CWp\nX31EpQctLOgm3qRNCcPwpukOqNacnuLsLroRNIZso1lMAnidTiAyBKVym+fCIhl4\n0xkdVBF1ajFuHCHkK/LZ/lCdXGPqDPdF98tGfi7EJOjPzsOl+Bn5bOcxMAjiio99\nZdtH6RfZby/txiB7mYI2PhjbAzByvR+tO6LjWLFxAMRMs6cgvGXQKnfrXf4UU2kG\nKE3oHM3aA/1TNuulWDBv7djiy1x5Kh6yhjYttBFLUSLEnV9vn+3k4SoSdsyxM927\n2I/BdhdboMcw/9h1qvT77NYqaSo0QN19JhnfN6lU5gBRmriL/C1amLepPY7Whij9\nIXV6h0VWokuomBQQ1r3mSjEHDe1NBc3tqG4QgQd9De8GgP3vVziCgnnRVpjzfkdE\nJUBvHhWinfM0XZ9uSyZg\n=3eAD\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree e890d190b1fdf5c1186a56c85e829c9ddf1b4d89\nparent 3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785744868 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785744868 +0300\n\ntest(search): pin the FTS-only candidate-window bug (#687) (#699)\n\nReproduces #687 locally. **Marked `xfail(strict=True)` rather than\nfixed** — both viable fixes change core ranking, and I'd rather you pick\nthe tradeoff than have me guess it. Reasoning below.\n\n## The mechanism\n\nThe scored search fetches `top_k * SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR` candidates\nordered by score. A NULL-embedding row scores on `fts_score` alone,\nwhile embedded rows near the query score far higher — so it never\nsurvives the `LIMIT`.\n\n`PostFilterResults` exempts NULL-embedding rows from the cosine gate,\nand #697 added the same exemption to the legacy post-filter. **Both run\ndownstream of the cut**, so the mechanism CAURA-594/CAURA-679 built for\nexactly this window is unreachable.\n\nMeasured on prod:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| tenant size | 791 memories |\n| embedded row scores | 0.35 – 0.39 |\n| FTS-only row score | **0** (reported) |\n| candidate window | 10 (`top_k=5` × factor 2) |\n| agent `search_profile` | **empty** — every value from constants |\n\nSo this is code, not configuration.\n\n## Why every existing test missed it\n\nThey run in tenants of a handful of rows, where a 10-candidate window\nexcludes nothing — **including the test I added in #697**. That's the\nhonest lesson: that test pinned the contract at the right layer but in\nconditions where the bug cannot appear.\n\nThe reproduction controls everything except the window: competitors\ncarry the *query's own* embedding, so they legitimately outrank on\ncosine, and their content omits the token, so the subject row is the\n**only** FTS match and **is** exempt from the cosine gate. It fails\nwith:\n\n> FTS-only row cut by the candidate window (#687): it is the ONLY row\nwhose content contains 'zqxjvbn…', its search_vector FTS-matches, and it\nis exempt from the cosine gate — but 10 higher-cosine rows fill the\ntop_k(5) * SEARCH_OVERFETCH_FACTOR(2) window first. search returned 5\nrow(s), none of them the subject.\n\n## Why I didn't fix it here\n\n**The cheap fix doesn't work.** Flooring the FTS-only similarity to\n`min_similarity` fails on the numbers: min is **0.3**, the measured\nembedded noise floor is **0.35–0.39**. The row still loses. There is no\nconstant that works, because `ts_rank_cd/(1+ts_rank_cd)` is not on a\ncomparable scale to cosine — a row whose entire content *is* the query\nscores ~0.05.\n\nThat leaves two real options:\n\n1. **Normalise `fts_score` onto the cosine scale.** Principled, and\nfixes the root scale mismatch — but it reprices *every* query. This repo\nmeasures such choices (`SIMILARITY_BLEND` cites a LoCoMo sweep), so it\nwants a benchmark run, not a judgement call.\n2. **Reserve candidate and result slots for FTS-only rows.** Blast\nradius bounded to the transient deferred window, and arguably what users\nwant (read-your-own-write) — but it spans the SQL, the pipeline\npost-filter, *and* the legacy path, and it displaces one result during\nthe window.\n\nShipping either unbenchmarked into the path that defines the product's\nrecall quality seemed like the wrong call to make alone.\n\n`strict=True` so the marker cannot rot: the moment a fix lands, this\nfails loudly and forces its removal.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `tests/pipeline/` + `tests/test_integration_search.py` | 120 passed, 1\nxfailed |\n| Test genuinely reproduces | fails with the message above when the\nmarker is removed |\n| `mypy src/` (core-api) | no issues, 189 files |\n| `ruff check` / `format --check` | 1 pre-existing issue each, identical\nto `origin/main`; all reformat hunks are ≤ line 448, my addition starts\nat 492 |\n\nRun against local pgvector 0.8.6 with `alembic upgrade head`, mirroring\nCI — which matters here: the fixtures build the schema with\n`Base.metadata.create_all`, so without alembic the `search_vector`\ntrigger from migration 001 is absent and any FTS test fails for the\nwrong reason.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-03T08:14:29Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e82bade6f0ea8b2c07b05f373c6cf97a471d1fa7/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73"}]},{"sha":"3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDM1MThkNGQ4ZDZhYWZhOTgzZTA0MmU3NjEwNDYyM2M2YmZkMmViNzM","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-03T07:38:45Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-03T07:38:45Z"},"message":"fix(common): converge common/ruff.toml with enterprise's stricter config (#696)\n\nFollow-up to #685. **Land this before caura-memclaw-enterprise's\ncompanion PR** — that repo's drift checker compares against OSS `main`,\nso its PR stays red until this merges.\n\n## Why\n\n#685 pinned `common/`'s ruff contract by mirroring core-api's rules. It\nthen emerged that **enterprise had already solved the same problem\nindependently and earlier**, in `e6d12110` (\"deps: take the ruff 0.15.22\n→ 0.16.0 bump\", #898) — same root cause, same fix shape. Its config even\nquantifies the expansion: **59 → 413 default rules**.\n\nThe two configs disagreed, and enterprise's is stricter. It keeps a\nshort global `ignore` and uses targeted per-file-ignores *with a written\nreason for each*, where OSS globally ignored 18 more rules:\n\n`ASYNC109, B007, B023, B905, E402, E701, PTH103/111/120,\nRUF005/006/034/059, SIM103/105/108, UP042, UP047`\n\n`select` was already identical on both sides.\n\n## What this does\n\nAdopts enterprise's shape as the shared config, so **one file governs\n`common/` in both repos** and enterprise can classify it `identical` in\nits vendoring manifest — after which the drift checker guarantees they\ncannot diverge again.\n\nTwo deliberate details:\n\n- **`target-version = \"py312\"`, not enterprise's `py313`.** `common/`\nruns on py312 here and py313 there, so the shared target must be the\n**lower** bound, or pyupgrade suggests rewrites that break this repo.\n- **The per-file-ignores are the union across both trees**, which are\n*not* the same file set — enterprise's `common/` also contains\n`email_service.py` and `license/*`. An entry with no matching file is\ninert, so each repo carries a few patterns that only matter in the\nother. That is the price of a single shared file, and beats two configs\ndrifting apart. Called out here because it will look odd in review.\n\n## Consequences under the newly-enforced rules\n\n- **Restored the two `noqa: E402` that #685 removed** as `RUF100`\n\"unused\". They were unused only relative to OSS's looser ignore list —\n`E402` is enforced under the shared config, so both late imports need\nthem back. Removing them was right for the config at the time and wrong\nfor this one.\n- **Dropped the two `# noqa: B027`** #685 added to\n`EventBus.start`/`stop`. The shared config per-file-ignores\n`events/base.py` with the same reasoning, which is the cleaner\nexpression of it — and enterprise had already written that entry.\n- **`SIM108` in `_parse_temporal`: applied the ternary.** Both branches\nare simple assignments to the same name with no interleaved comments, so\nit is behaviour-identical. Ruff marks `SIM108` unsafe in general, so I\napplied and read this by hand rather than trusting `--fix`. OSS\npreviously ignored `SIM108` globally; fixing it is the honest way to\nmeet the stricter rule instead of re-adding the ignore and partially\nundoing the convergence.\n- **`UP042`/`UP047`/`ASYNC109` are per-file-ignored with reasons, not\nfixed.** They would rewrite base classes and public generic signatures\non widely imported shared types — its own deliberate decision, not\nsomething to bundle into a tooling change. That is the call enterprise\nalready made, in the same words.\n\n## Follow-up left deliberately\n\nEnterprise's `structlog_config.py` entry notes that\n`SIM105`/`PTH120`/`PTH103` are real findings whose fix belongs upstream\n*here*. Doing that would let the entry disappear, but it is a code\nchange rather than a tooling one, so it is out of scope.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `ruff check common/` clean; all pre-existing ruff scopes clean;\nvendored `--isolated` gate still passes.\n- `common/ruff.toml` verified **byte-identical** to the enterprise copy\nvia `cmp`.\n- **4068 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 3 xpassed, 0 failed** — including\n176 targeted temporal/enrichment tests, since `_parse_temporal` changed.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"e8768b21ec3e1f70e45c2e7e4714b66e5d27c5bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/e8768b21ec3e1f70e45c2e7e4714b66e5d27c5bc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcEWFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAT6cQAJNCdNhkCT+TiPpa1J8kLhFb\nJ/o3a27KOi8rcuv9c268WdIKcBJ2034VtYfKtbrIhwignFu5/DzurzrEjsgP9MN8\npm8j6sy4grX6xVhszJJjHAuYaTF+42OMKyVW+D38U3fSuE8gIz8Syws66JIiD6Yk\nqcRJQQCtkSG2MIdWRSeJAYHbSG51VkDXwaO31PdzamicW6R+rIhaxH2aKCSW4Vga\nQkAjTjP+2hn2qb44/uC8rlB8JrOR0r9BKHH0+XOY9BRAREVZi6Vol7qndDk+iKdE\nn8RWdOol4I4GLYXkDnO3G0Zy6E5Jremd6XYJ1TB4KNACLr18D7Mk9FcMs0iDGWbP\nU3v+6jcbq742lvH7riE112oKPKEWigugAQwkLpNLqB64bO3rR8WDms1WfSGbbIyk\nNcIXepmDBu/zareG8P+3NTo++YAlbQNnTnR82HBsoh0cJE69h40DjAX719yIEvh0\nFhUMypi1cmjhAgo3oRVYgTTjRtGLx4rxwDn4+cYHIMxDaUXM4TSe9acyo+FiPQAW\nCaPCKxpxySwrh9rdyA/orrXGPyjJHj2AYzINSdACoV71X/w14fSrSQPb1vSuTRfQ\nLqbdsdPa0WyruRyRum/S2NTpZqOpy4APtVQq7Uh3ChFSQA4Ay4KYdx8c5yFlCOPF\nW3xCDKQLjtNjueKGBMRN\n=Raxs\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree e8768b21ec3e1f70e45c2e7e4714b66e5d27c5bc\nparent 9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785742725 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785742725 +0300\n\nfix(common): converge common/ruff.toml with enterprise's stricter config (#696)\n\nFollow-up to #685. **Land this before caura-memclaw-enterprise's\ncompanion PR** — that repo's drift checker compares against OSS `main`,\nso its PR stays red until this merges.\n\n## Why\n\n#685 pinned `common/`'s ruff contract by mirroring core-api's rules. It\nthen emerged that **enterprise had already solved the same problem\nindependently and earlier**, in `e6d12110` (\"deps: take the ruff 0.15.22\n→ 0.16.0 bump\", #898) — same root cause, same fix shape. Its config even\nquantifies the expansion: **59 → 413 default rules**.\n\nThe two configs disagreed, and enterprise's is stricter. It keeps a\nshort global `ignore` and uses targeted per-file-ignores *with a written\nreason for each*, where OSS globally ignored 18 more rules:\n\n`ASYNC109, B007, B023, B905, E402, E701, PTH103/111/120,\nRUF005/006/034/059, SIM103/105/108, UP042, UP047`\n\n`select` was already identical on both sides.\n\n## What this does\n\nAdopts enterprise's shape as the shared config, so **one file governs\n`common/` in both repos** and enterprise can classify it `identical` in\nits vendoring manifest — after which the drift checker guarantees they\ncannot diverge again.\n\nTwo deliberate details:\n\n- **`target-version = \"py312\"`, not enterprise's `py313`.** `common/`\nruns on py312 here and py313 there, so the shared target must be the\n**lower** bound, or pyupgrade suggests rewrites that break this repo.\n- **The per-file-ignores are the union across both trees**, which are\n*not* the same file set — enterprise's `common/` also contains\n`email_service.py` and `license/*`. An entry with no matching file is\ninert, so each repo carries a few patterns that only matter in the\nother. That is the price of a single shared file, and beats two configs\ndrifting apart. Called out here because it will look odd in review.\n\n## Consequences under the newly-enforced rules\n\n- **Restored the two `noqa: E402` that #685 removed** as `RUF100`\n\"unused\". They were unused only relative to OSS's looser ignore list —\n`E402` is enforced under the shared config, so both late imports need\nthem back. Removing them was right for the config at the time and wrong\nfor this one.\n- **Dropped the two `# noqa: B027`** #685 added to\n`EventBus.start`/`stop`. The shared config per-file-ignores\n`events/base.py` with the same reasoning, which is the cleaner\nexpression of it — and enterprise had already written that entry.\n- **`SIM108` in `_parse_temporal`: applied the ternary.** Both branches\nare simple assignments to the same name with no interleaved comments, so\nit is behaviour-identical. Ruff marks `SIM108` unsafe in general, so I\napplied and read this by hand rather than trusting `--fix`. OSS\npreviously ignored `SIM108` globally; fixing it is the honest way to\nmeet the stricter rule instead of re-adding the ignore and partially\nundoing the convergence.\n- **`UP042`/`UP047`/`ASYNC109` are per-file-ignored with reasons, not\nfixed.** They would rewrite base classes and public generic signatures\non widely imported shared types — its own deliberate decision, not\nsomething to bundle into a tooling change. That is the call enterprise\nalready made, in the same words.\n\n## Follow-up left deliberately\n\nEnterprise's `structlog_config.py` entry notes that\n`SIM105`/`PTH120`/`PTH103` are real findings whose fix belongs upstream\n*here*. Doing that would let the entry disappear, but it is a code\nchange rather than a tooling one, so it is out of scope.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `ruff check common/` clean; all pre-existing ruff scopes clean;\nvendored `--isolated` gate still passes.\n- `common/ruff.toml` verified **byte-identical** to the enterprise copy\nvia `cmp`.\n- **4068 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 3 xpassed, 0 failed** — including\n176 targeted temporal/enrichment tests, since `_parse_temporal` changed.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-03T07:38:46Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/3518d4d8d6aafa983e042e76104623c6bfd2eb73/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746"}]},{"sha":"9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDlkOTAwM2RjMWNhMDIxOTdlOTVjYjY4MmFlNTQyZmI5N2MyYWY3NDY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-03T07:27:14Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-03T07:27:14Z"},"message":"fix(search): keep FTS-only rows past the legacy cosine gate (#687) (#697)\n\nCloses the coverage gap behind #687, and fixes a real bug the new test\nfound on its first run.\n\n## The contract\n\nUnder `deployment_mode=deferred` a memory is stored with `embedding IS\nNULL` and `metadata.embedding_pending=True`; a worker fills the vector\nseconds (prod) to minutes (staging) later. Three mechanisms exist so the\nrow stays discoverable in that window:\n\n1. CAURA-594's admission predicate — `or_(embedding IS NOT NULL,\nfts_guard)`\n2. CAURA-679's FTS-only `similarity` for NULL-embedding rows\n3. the pipeline post-filter exempting them from the cosine gate\n\n**The legacy search path never got #3.**\n\n## The bug\n\n```python\nrows = [r for r in rows\n        if r.get(\"vec_sim\") is None or float(r[\"vec_sim\"]) >= _min_similarity]\n```\n\nThe comment above it justified the `is None` branch as defensive,\nbecause \"the scored_search SQL currently enforces `Memory.embedding IS\nNOT NULL`\". **CAURA-594 ended that invariant.** And as the same comment\nconceded, the storage layer coerces a NULL-embedding row's cosine to\n`0.0` rather than NULL — so the defensive branch never fires for those\nrows and `0.0 >= threshold` drops them.\n\nThe guard documented its own defeat. It says it is there *\"so that\nFTS-only rows aren't silently gated out by the cosine threshold if\neither invariant ever changes\"* — and that is exactly what happened.\n\nFix: add the `has_embedding is False` exemption so the legacy path\ngenuinely mirrors the pipeline post-filter it claims to mirror.\n`has_embedding` is the authoritative NULL signal precisely because\n`vec_sim == 0.0` is ambiguous with a genuinely orthogonal embedding.\n\n## The missing test\n\nCoverage of this contract stopped at the storage layer.\n`core-storage-api/tests/test_integration.py` pins it well — but it POSTs\n`embedding=None` **straight to core-storage-api, bypassing core-api**.\nAnd `grep -l embedding_pending` across the suites hits only\nwrite-pipeline tests: **nothing paired a pending embedding with a\nrecall.** That is the hole #687 shipped through.\n\nThe new test writes through the real core-api path with the embedding\ndeferred, then asserts recall returns the row. It is parameterised over\nboth search implementations because `search_memories` dispatches on\n`_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH` — covering only the default would have left the\nother path unpinned. **That parameterisation is what found the bug:**\n`[pipeline]` passed and `[legacy]` failed on the very first run, with\nthe log showing `path=scored-search row_count: 1` — the SQL returned the\nrow and the legacy path threw it away.\n\nIts preconditions assert the row really has a NULL embedding and a\npopulated `search_vector` that FTS-matches the query, so it cannot pass\nvacuously against an embedded row, and cannot fail misleadingly on a\nschema built by `Base.metadata.create_all` without migration 001's\n`search_vector` trigger (the fixtures do exactly that; CI's `alembic\nupgrade head` is what supplies it).\n\n## Verification\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `tests/pipeline/` + `tests/test_integration_search.py` | **120\npassed** |\n| Inverting the new clause | `[legacy]` fails again, nothing else — the\ntest earns its keep |\n| `mypy src/` (core-api) | no issues in 189 files |\n| `ruff check` (both files) | clean |\n| `ruff format --check` | source clean; the test file's pre-existing\ndrift is at lines 67–290, untouched by this change |\n\nRun against a local pgvector 0.8.6 with `alembic upgrade head`,\nmirroring CI.\n\n## What this does not fix\n\n**#687's production symptom is on the pipeline path**\n(`_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH = True`), and that path *passes* the new test\nunder every shape I could reproduce locally: bare, the full MCP-recall\nargument set (`caller_agent_id`, `fleet_ids`, `recall_boost`,\n`graph_expand`, `entity_retrieval`), and against 25 competing embedded\nrows — the crowding scenario CAURA-679's own comment warns about,\nverified non-vacuous by asserting the competitors really embedded.\n\nSo the remaining divergence there is environmental — `tenant_config`,\n`search_profile`, or the rank provider — not pipeline logic. That\nnarrowing is itself a result, and it stays tracked in #687. This PR\nfixes the defect that *is* in code and installs the test that will catch\nthe next one.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"ad19046011b3291b6e53933308010d109e171c28","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/ad19046011b3291b6e53933308010d109e171c28"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcELSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0isQACKHnFnWepbeLJ1UJUd1E4H0\nTNlPYGOY5bXq/lJ9HgcAlBx6mQBzzSRc0bbnT/rx2NUEmyQK1NeX2rVF70FsOLoR\n/4LmBL5oMjPOmARqr/3lh3a7pXU2JSpLzsxxS7HdXeiLObIefHjW0t0kQ0B3zB+6\nUo9aMJW+EeaYpPkVO7PZWiIvRUVQJX5hCz3dVnlsdMEG+1r8A2K3gOM92O22fylw\nlIYjudfBnlDNvSpN6mlEOAAJ/3VVxQXZflmWpmr3jfXguorNkHvDH6ZDlsPVuFW6\n+weAqWrmgcBbL6PJNji+TphE5NxoddglSe8OPnXmCT1twY0BrUFBXkCfCYoJTvnC\nzb1eKabAYLUJtzUiQvjICr4ZnyJf+C1bIShxEGxj2S/eThTy6Bwy2cLgBgT9EV0K\nZFzClkjRxWdMZnLv6AkhZL3YQf3fo6T8rf6oBGKjyNhKRbW3VmTRjN3TIP7BIMWv\nDKbk5nOpQXuiYi4EQChtneSZou/mlDuNgiEEEWmZXs/CP4QzQoe+fE1SE7g4gwfv\nF30pfQYfi9TmxDR3Lnvyqm7ECCb5CCddly5Ms51AY5o4okNGHLal6QlQMS4E07y8\n8sjsgkQIi+kJDWZkWU3EwmmKgFlmfegXGkfX6NaCuS/TRUi+6N0IK3I8StHw5QcY\ni5EHlGBd7RJPMoSv/U1q\n=6Txw\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree ad19046011b3291b6e53933308010d109e171c28\nparent 7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785742034 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785742034 +0300\n\nfix(search): keep FTS-only rows past the legacy cosine gate (#687) (#697)\n\nCloses the coverage gap behind #687, and fixes a real bug the new test\nfound on its first run.\n\n## The contract\n\nUnder `deployment_mode=deferred` a memory is stored with `embedding IS\nNULL` and `metadata.embedding_pending=True`; a worker fills the vector\nseconds (prod) to minutes (staging) later. Three mechanisms exist so the\nrow stays discoverable in that window:\n\n1. CAURA-594's admission predicate — `or_(embedding IS NOT NULL,\nfts_guard)`\n2. CAURA-679's FTS-only `similarity` for NULL-embedding rows\n3. the pipeline post-filter exempting them from the cosine gate\n\n**The legacy search path never got #3.**\n\n## The bug\n\n```python\nrows = [r for r in rows\n        if r.get(\"vec_sim\") is None or float(r[\"vec_sim\"]) >= _min_similarity]\n```\n\nThe comment above it justified the `is None` branch as defensive,\nbecause \"the scored_search SQL currently enforces `Memory.embedding IS\nNOT NULL`\". **CAURA-594 ended that invariant.** And as the same comment\nconceded, the storage layer coerces a NULL-embedding row's cosine to\n`0.0` rather than NULL — so the defensive branch never fires for those\nrows and `0.0 >= threshold` drops them.\n\nThe guard documented its own defeat. It says it is there *\"so that\nFTS-only rows aren't silently gated out by the cosine threshold if\neither invariant ever changes\"* — and that is exactly what happened.\n\nFix: add the `has_embedding is False` exemption so the legacy path\ngenuinely mirrors the pipeline post-filter it claims to mirror.\n`has_embedding` is the authoritative NULL signal precisely because\n`vec_sim == 0.0` is ambiguous with a genuinely orthogonal embedding.\n\n## The missing test\n\nCoverage of this contract stopped at the storage layer.\n`core-storage-api/tests/test_integration.py` pins it well — but it POSTs\n`embedding=None` **straight to core-storage-api, bypassing core-api**.\nAnd `grep -l embedding_pending` across the suites hits only\nwrite-pipeline tests: **nothing paired a pending embedding with a\nrecall.** That is the hole #687 shipped through.\n\nThe new test writes through the real core-api path with the embedding\ndeferred, then asserts recall returns the row. It is parameterised over\nboth search implementations because `search_memories` dispatches on\n`_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH` — covering only the default would have left the\nother path unpinned. **That parameterisation is what found the bug:**\n`[pipeline]` passed and `[legacy]` failed on the very first run, with\nthe log showing `path=scored-search row_count: 1` — the SQL returned the\nrow and the legacy path threw it away.\n\nIts preconditions assert the row really has a NULL embedding and a\npopulated `search_vector` that FTS-matches the query, so it cannot pass\nvacuously against an embedded row, and cannot fail misleadingly on a\nschema built by `Base.metadata.create_all` without migration 001's\n`search_vector` trigger (the fixtures do exactly that; CI's `alembic\nupgrade head` is what supplies it).\n\n## Verification\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `tests/pipeline/` + `tests/test_integration_search.py` | **120\npassed** |\n| Inverting the new clause | `[legacy]` fails again, nothing else — the\ntest earns its keep |\n| `mypy src/` (core-api) | no issues in 189 files |\n| `ruff check` (both files) | clean |\n| `ruff format --check` | source clean; the test file's pre-existing\ndrift is at lines 67–290, untouched by this change |\n\nRun against a local pgvector 0.8.6 with `alembic upgrade head`,\nmirroring CI.\n\n## What this does not fix\n\n**#687's production symptom is on the pipeline path**\n(`_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH = True`), and that path *passes* the new test\nunder every shape I could reproduce locally: bare, the full MCP-recall\nargument set (`caller_agent_id`, `fleet_ids`, `recall_boost`,\n`graph_expand`, `entity_retrieval`), and against 25 competing embedded\nrows — the crowding scenario CAURA-679's own comment warns about,\nverified non-vacuous by asserting the competitors really embedded.\n\nSo the remaining divergence there is environmental — `tenant_config`,\n`search_profile`, or the rank provider — not pipeline logic. That\nnarrowing is itself a result, and it stays tracked in #687. This PR\nfixes the defect that *is* in code and installs the test that will catch\nthe next one.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-03T07:27:15Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9d9003dc1ca02197e95cb682ae542fb97c2af746/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f"}]},{"sha":"7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDdiNWJmNjRlMmQ2MjJiNmEwN2IyYTE3YzVmOTg4NTNhYjI5MGNiNmY","commit":{"author":{"name":"ran-taig","email":"ran@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-02T15:34:44Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-02T15:34:44Z"},"message":"feat(insights): filter input noise — opaque payloads, title-dedup, discover window (#681)\n\n## Problem\n\nFollow-up to the eToro insights quality analysis (and #679). The\ninsights engine's input is the raw memory firehose, and on a busy fleet\nthat means:\n\n- **~1/3 of all insight citations point at repetition noise** (measured\nover 4.6K citations from every post-Jun-16 insight on the eToro\ndeployment).\n- One family alone — **content-free reasoning artifacts**\n(`[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"thinkingSignature\":\"…\"}]`, an empty\nthinking block with an encrypted blob) — is 4.3% of 30-day episodes but\ndrew **23.9% of citations**: nothing to analyze, but near-identical\nembeddings form the tightest clusters discover can find. The engine has\nbeen correctly reporting this about its own input nightly for weeks.\n- The discover sample (\"newest 200\") **spans under a day** on a busy\ntenant (~700 distinct titles/day) — nightly runs cluster yesterday's\nactivity and re-discover the same shapes.\n\nNotably, **excluding episodes by type would be wrong**: measured across\n141K 30-day episodes, 63% are substantive work, and the noise is defined\nby *repetition and opacity*, not by memory type.\n\n## Changes (input-side only; nothing in storage is deleted or modified)\n\n| Change | Where | Modes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Opaque-payload guard: structural content-prefix predicate (no title\nregexes) | `_insights_scope_filters` | all 6 — an unreadable row can't\nevidence anything |\n| Title-dedup: one exemplar per exact title (newest), annotated\n`dup_count`/`first_seen`, rendered by the prompt formatter as `[repeats:\nNx, first seen: date]` | `_insights_dedup_stmt` + formatter | patterns,\nstale, failures, discover — **deliberately not**\ncontradictions/divergence, where same-titled disagreement is the\nevidence |\n| Discover window: sample spread (`ORDER BY random()` — genuinely\nrun-varying; a deterministic hash order would be a fixed permutation per\ncorpus state) over trailing `INSIGHTS_DISCOVER_WINDOW_DAYS = 30` instead\nof newest-first; patterns/failures/stale get their own scan bounds\n(`INSIGHTS_PATTERNS_WINDOW_DAYS = 30`, `INSIGHTS_FAILURES_WINDOW_DAYS =\n90`, `INSIGHTS_STALE_WINDOW_DAYS = 90` — stale's deliberately wider than\nits 30-day age threshold, so it reports the 30-90d \"recently became\nstale\" band instead of perpetually re-reporting the ancient tail) — the\ndedup subquery has no inner LIMIT, so unbounded patterns would scan the\ntenant's whole active history for 50 deduped rows (prod: 21K rows/167ms\nwindowed vs 44K/243ms and growing) | discover only | window start\ncomputed on the core-api clock (same pattern as the stale thresholds) |\n\nCorrectness details: NULL/empty titles fall back to row-id as dedup key\n(no collapse); `dup_count` is computed over the *filtered* set so it\nmeans \"N matching rows in this window\"; each mode keeps its own result\nordering; `window_start` omitted → the ORIGINAL pre-dedup query shape\n(identical results and cost — no dedup, no annotations, no full-corpus\nwindow scan), so an older core-api works against a newer storage and\nvice versa; failures sorts with `dup_count` as secondary key so frequent\npatterns aren't cut by LIMIT below one-off rows.\n\n## Validation\n\n- **Wet tests** (6 new in `test_ph5b_insights_storage.py`, against real\nPostgres): opaque exclusion across all reads incl. contradictions; dedup\nexemplar choice + annotations; NULL/empty-title survival; same-title\nsupersede pair still visible to contradictions; window bounding +\nnaive-datetime 422 guard (created_at is timestamptz) + legacy fallback;\nmode ordering preserved under dedup. 30/30 pass.\n- **Formatter unit tests** (3 new): annotation rendering,\nsingleton/legacy rows unchanged, cluster representatives annotated.\n38/38 pass in the two core-api insights files.\n- **Against production data (read-only)**: predicate catches 97.6% of\nthe opaque family; the new discover shape spans **31 distinct days\ninstead of 1**, residual noise titles drop to ~10% of the sample; ~130ms\non the busiest tenant. `ruff` clean.\n\n## Explicitly out of scope\n\n- Upstream write suppression (why agents persist empty encrypted\nthinking blocks at all) — separate track.\n- The pending-status leak, novelty guard, and fallback observability\nfrom the same analysis — separate PRs.\n- eToro deploy: the box needs a release cutover (which would also pick\nup #679's numpy fix).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"50d9a17d304911382ff393e2e6196a9d36931521","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/50d9a17d304911382ff393e2e6196a9d36931521"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqb2OUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAuaAQAEqGpeQWPJGub7RCFAii8P2v\nGQQ1txP1Gy+peb4NSOKIZWkqncVjXd/cw3EaNdHslGb/vgQug7eQB8Sz/7Y/cAs6\nGtLmiF1lIR+gbxe5NrFXpPh3upITqYCkwIccMocK0m4ZvLPy+Ewd8mEuLBQkb6Vs\nBENGVLbbINeypcZWeB6SzogLBE+leXFlDRkpE0U4HptZxUZPxci36cnzFjieeWzr\nrb9bg3bF5Q9K8qVwPXRTj6yxH+9OrWrxibqKwHrkmbGvhW4k8CI4Qu8V/L28svrc\njdRUilliXYpwWLllYMFBajh/s3Rrsxw2F37fHBy9jB/xnU+/Om0k+M2y+v+thQWg\ndv3amkQ08yBg/HM3X3OHu1iljYMuj9MF1xZqg6PW520dnnaQ2b2StoLzkWq3xPan\n9lOoGfJk6yjPnoq7KDt9ffu4AUIDOMATRtoqR/cbknRtBD6187fLISvuD5wnkA+W\nTyjpOAIT/5TOqc9j8ValurvQ/2OmIC9nHnBSLa5KeHjbcy6ttivaaLVHnPVNm7IF\nxWkwRxb9KLVb4U2uMn4QBa5GR9JaDqNViqMjuTVjOTIQ+cwtVfjnstJJ9mCMqYkZ\nmTY4CCqo/Khrg+9YIw4Jf8aa6F/NxjbEYy9SiItmKctiIIgg9WmzIvHu9GCADASC\nHDmFc4KdH2rGWMhffTKz\n=3XC4\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 50d9a17d304911382ff393e2e6196a9d36931521\nparent 8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751\nauthor ran-taig <ran@caura.ai> 1785684884 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785684884 +0300\n\nfeat(insights): filter input noise — opaque payloads, title-dedup, discover window (#681)\n\n## Problem\n\nFollow-up to the eToro insights quality analysis (and #679). The\ninsights engine's input is the raw memory firehose, and on a busy fleet\nthat means:\n\n- **~1/3 of all insight citations point at repetition noise** (measured\nover 4.6K citations from every post-Jun-16 insight on the eToro\ndeployment).\n- One family alone — **content-free reasoning artifacts**\n(`[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"thinkingSignature\":\"…\"}]`, an empty\nthinking block with an encrypted blob) — is 4.3% of 30-day episodes but\ndrew **23.9% of citations**: nothing to analyze, but near-identical\nembeddings form the tightest clusters discover can find. The engine has\nbeen correctly reporting this about its own input nightly for weeks.\n- The discover sample (\"newest 200\") **spans under a day** on a busy\ntenant (~700 distinct titles/day) — nightly runs cluster yesterday's\nactivity and re-discover the same shapes.\n\nNotably, **excluding episodes by type would be wrong**: measured across\n141K 30-day episodes, 63% are substantive work, and the noise is defined\nby *repetition and opacity*, not by memory type.\n\n## Changes (input-side only; nothing in storage is deleted or modified)\n\n| Change | Where | Modes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Opaque-payload guard: structural content-prefix predicate (no title\nregexes) | `_insights_scope_filters` | all 6 — an unreadable row can't\nevidence anything |\n| Title-dedup: one exemplar per exact title (newest), annotated\n`dup_count`/`first_seen`, rendered by the prompt formatter as `[repeats:\nNx, first seen: date]` | `_insights_dedup_stmt` + formatter | patterns,\nstale, failures, discover — **deliberately not**\ncontradictions/divergence, where same-titled disagreement is the\nevidence |\n| Discover window: sample spread (`ORDER BY random()` — genuinely\nrun-varying; a deterministic hash order would be a fixed permutation per\ncorpus state) over trailing `INSIGHTS_DISCOVER_WINDOW_DAYS = 30` instead\nof newest-first; patterns/failures/stale get their own scan bounds\n(`INSIGHTS_PATTERNS_WINDOW_DAYS = 30`, `INSIGHTS_FAILURES_WINDOW_DAYS =\n90`, `INSIGHTS_STALE_WINDOW_DAYS = 90` — stale's deliberately wider than\nits 30-day age threshold, so it reports the 30-90d \"recently became\nstale\" band instead of perpetually re-reporting the ancient tail) — the\ndedup subquery has no inner LIMIT, so unbounded patterns would scan the\ntenant's whole active history for 50 deduped rows (prod: 21K rows/167ms\nwindowed vs 44K/243ms and growing) | discover only | window start\ncomputed on the core-api clock (same pattern as the stale thresholds) |\n\nCorrectness details: NULL/empty titles fall back to row-id as dedup key\n(no collapse); `dup_count` is computed over the *filtered* set so it\nmeans \"N matching rows in this window\"; each mode keeps its own result\nordering; `window_start` omitted → the ORIGINAL pre-dedup query shape\n(identical results and cost — no dedup, no annotations, no full-corpus\nwindow scan), so an older core-api works against a newer storage and\nvice versa; failures sorts with `dup_count` as secondary key so frequent\npatterns aren't cut by LIMIT below one-off rows.\n\n## Validation\n\n- **Wet tests** (6 new in `test_ph5b_insights_storage.py`, against real\nPostgres): opaque exclusion across all reads incl. contradictions; dedup\nexemplar choice + annotations; NULL/empty-title survival; same-title\nsupersede pair still visible to contradictions; window bounding +\nnaive-datetime 422 guard (created_at is timestamptz) + legacy fallback;\nmode ordering preserved under dedup. 30/30 pass.\n- **Formatter unit tests** (3 new): annotation rendering,\nsingleton/legacy rows unchanged, cluster representatives annotated.\n38/38 pass in the two core-api insights files.\n- **Against production data (read-only)**: predicate catches 97.6% of\nthe opaque family; the new discover shape spans **31 distinct days\ninstead of 1**, residual noise titles drop to ~10% of the sample; ~130ms\non the busiest tenant. `ruff` clean.\n\n## Explicitly out of scope\n\n- Upstream write suppression (why agents persist empty encrypted\nthinking blocks at all) — separate track.\n- The pending-status leak, novelty guard, and fallback observability\nfrom the same analysis — separate PRs.\n- eToro deploy: the box needs a release cutover (which would also pick\nup #679's numpy fix).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-02T15:34:45Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/7b5bf64e2d622b6a07b2a17c5f98853ab290cb6f/comments","author":{"login":"ran-taig","id":254242316,"node_id":"U_kgDODyduDA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/254242316?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig","html_url":"https://github.com/ran-taig","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751"}]},{"sha":"8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDhkNzdiNDk1NTFlMTI3NjRjZDcyMTBhNzQ1YTRlMWZhZWI0MzI3NTE","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-02T14:08:07Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-02T14:08:07Z"},"message":"docs(ci): document that the two claude-review entry points don't share gates (#686)\n\nFixes #683. **Comment-only change — no behaviour change.** The diff\ntouches nothing but comment lines in `claude_code_review.yml`.\n\n## The gap\n\n`pre-checks` is gated on `if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'`, and\n`claude-retrigger` has no `needs: pre-checks`. So on an `issue_comment`\nevent `pre-checks` never runs, and **every condition it owns is absent\nfrom that path** — all six of them:\n\n| # | condition in `pre-checks` | on `@claude` comment path |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | PR-author org membership | not applied (commenter is checked\ninstead) |\n| 2 | synchronize-event skip | not applied |\n| 3 | **draft skip** | **not applied** |\n| 4 | bot skip | not applied |\n| 5 | **`MAX_FILES: 30` cap** | **not applied** |\n| 6 | tests/docs/config-only skip | not applied |\n\nThe user-visible consequence: a draft PR gets **no review on open**, yet\ncommenting `@claude` on that same draft produces a full review. The\neffective policy is \"drafts get opt-in review, not automatic review\" —\nsensible, but discoverable only by reading the job conditions. The skip\nmessage says `reason=draft PR` and stops there.\n\n## Why document rather than unify\n\nDeliberate, and it was the explicit decision:\n\n- Making the paths agree would remove the **only** way to review a\ndraft.\n- The `MAX_FILES` bypass is a useful escape hatch for a large but\ngenuinely reviewable PR.\n\nA member may be relying on either, so both are now recorded as\nintentional rather than quietly changed. Added a maintainer note that a\ncondition added to `pre-checks` covers one path only — if a future gate\nmust hold universally it has to be duplicated into `claude-retrigger` or\nlifted somewhere both jobs consult.\n\n## This is the second instance of one root cause\n\nThe header already documents, at length, that `author_association`\ndiffers between the two event types — *\"which is why an `@claude`\nretrigger worked for private members while the on-open review did not.\"*\n\nSame shape both times: **the entry points do not share gates, so they\ndrift.** That instance was found and written up; the draft and\n`MAX_FILES` ones were not. The new block names the pattern so the next\none is easier to spot.\n\n## Also\n\nClarified the opening paragraph, which framed `@claude` purely as the\nway to re-review after a push — so it read as though that were its only\nuse, when it is also the only route to a draft review.\n\nOne nuance worth flagging for the reviewer: membership *is* still\nenforced on the comment path, but it validates the **commenter**, not\nthe PR author. A member vouching for someone else's PR is a supported\nflow — consistent with the fork-retrigger checkout fix in `d5f60ca`.\nDocumented as such rather than treated as a hole.\n\n## How this surfaced\n\nPR #682 is a deliberately-draft migration branch that must not merge yet\nbut did need reviewing. The review was obtained by commenting `@claude`,\nafter reading the workflow to confirm the retrigger path had no draft\ngate. Without reading the source there was no way to know that was\npossible.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Diff contains no non-comment lines (verified mechanically).\n- `actionlint` reports the same single pre-existing finding as\n`origin/main`; YAML parses.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"fbbaf9df82a7f4c3f1e16dba1c7f5ae750cc0cac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/fbbaf9df82a7f4c3f1e16dba1c7f5ae750cc0cac"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqb09HCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAyOgQAH1kr+y2apADP/x/gCwqL7aQ\nbII/H4cSBbkqgYv3J25RLVlLgzQnQ5g+tmaThr9Lr31HQf4R+FpPVG2U4yfuxgqm\nc9dwhYIsYeQvYhapqTUJFWGmOaQmUAdLwiZzZfypD1gwHINwWit03e3U5mcT3PmJ\npCvMWDXi4SKK5E0JpWphpRkRSDG6OFonXHR37cYJN+tkZguUbEJ3486d/OrR+Gxy\nBdrlT4xGaK0ydPF2voiSPHr5EzkaVl42qP1vUzCV15ykrWG7V57vGq+tI5nJ/Ep3\n/rkKV5yJe4qAtAbL8jYYE+LFcYecjvtxuOKOMVsJ06Rg8AZRKO9uYrKsKCIrqq8u\nlrppDP58JG9/EkUMtDaIPw/gzgcU/4UHRZbd0m1MbvqBv9rtehLEFQ6WU0NBPUOm\ngL+OKJ2O2epMSP98D6cfkJOR/bDobSeUwQGgMPgGh56Wu98RObnssFgTB4805Pg0\n+PAWbhYGUqGEkNc+OrAxcx/xOZYhBZJ4qFtryv1ymJFDVzUUeotdeLCnlal8OZlF\nniK35fhIFuuv5PGuAdZlARtCY+9eGMWOxO3066AgoAUYn+3Wzj7HjvnofJMhBlfC\ndRQLtbmk8mzXYowu1AzSnqV/jMyWXl40/I7LycWXwwlNEmDnHG9qykpK4EMYS3mQ\nc7jo4TLJvvSmnxFZ+9uF\n=dMvH\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree fbbaf9df82a7f4c3f1e16dba1c7f5ae750cc0cac\nparent abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785679687 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785679687 +0300\n\ndocs(ci): document that the two claude-review entry points don't share gates (#686)\n\nFixes #683. **Comment-only change — no behaviour change.** The diff\ntouches nothing but comment lines in `claude_code_review.yml`.\n\n## The gap\n\n`pre-checks` is gated on `if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'`, and\n`claude-retrigger` has no `needs: pre-checks`. So on an `issue_comment`\nevent `pre-checks` never runs, and **every condition it owns is absent\nfrom that path** — all six of them:\n\n| # | condition in `pre-checks` | on `@claude` comment path |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | PR-author org membership | not applied (commenter is checked\ninstead) |\n| 2 | synchronize-event skip | not applied |\n| 3 | **draft skip** | **not applied** |\n| 4 | bot skip | not applied |\n| 5 | **`MAX_FILES: 30` cap** | **not applied** |\n| 6 | tests/docs/config-only skip | not applied |\n\nThe user-visible consequence: a draft PR gets **no review on open**, yet\ncommenting `@claude` on that same draft produces a full review. The\neffective policy is \"drafts get opt-in review, not automatic review\" —\nsensible, but discoverable only by reading the job conditions. The skip\nmessage says `reason=draft PR` and stops there.\n\n## Why document rather than unify\n\nDeliberate, and it was the explicit decision:\n\n- Making the paths agree would remove the **only** way to review a\ndraft.\n- The `MAX_FILES` bypass is a useful escape hatch for a large but\ngenuinely reviewable PR.\n\nA member may be relying on either, so both are now recorded as\nintentional rather than quietly changed. Added a maintainer note that a\ncondition added to `pre-checks` covers one path only — if a future gate\nmust hold universally it has to be duplicated into `claude-retrigger` or\nlifted somewhere both jobs consult.\n\n## This is the second instance of one root cause\n\nThe header already documents, at length, that `author_association`\ndiffers between the two event types — *\"which is why an `@claude`\nretrigger worked for private members while the on-open review did not.\"*\n\nSame shape both times: **the entry points do not share gates, so they\ndrift.** That instance was found and written up; the draft and\n`MAX_FILES` ones were not. The new block names the pattern so the next\none is easier to spot.\n\n## Also\n\nClarified the opening paragraph, which framed `@claude` purely as the\nway to re-review after a push — so it read as though that were its only\nuse, when it is also the only route to a draft review.\n\nOne nuance worth flagging for the reviewer: membership *is* still\nenforced on the comment path, but it validates the **commenter**, not\nthe PR author. A member vouching for someone else's PR is a supported\nflow — consistent with the fork-retrigger checkout fix in `d5f60ca`.\nDocumented as such rather than treated as a hole.\n\n## How this surfaced\n\nPR #682 is a deliberately-draft migration branch that must not merge yet\nbut did need reviewing. The review was obtained by commenting `@claude`,\nafter reading the workflow to confirm the retrigger path had no draft\ngate. Without reading the source there was no way to know that was\npossible.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Diff contains no non-comment lines (verified mechanically).\n- `actionlint` reports the same single pre-existing finding as\n`origin/main`; YAML parses.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-02T14:08:07Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/8d77b49551e12764cd7210a745a4e1faeb432751/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955"}]},{"sha":"abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGFiZmFiZGIzNzNiM2JlNWEwMWVjNDkxNmJjYjA5NjBiOTY2ZGI5NTU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-02T14:04:01Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-02T14:04:01Z"},"message":"fix(ci): pin common/'s ruff contract and lint-gate it (#685)\n\nFixes #684.\n\n## The problem\n\n`common/` had **no ruff config above it** — no `ruff.toml`, no root\n`pyproject.toml`, nothing in `common/`. So ruff walked up from each\nfile, found nothing, and fell back to its **built-in defaults**. The\nrule set governing shared code that every service imports was therefore\n\"whatever the installed ruff version ships as default,\" and it changes\non upgrade.\n\nConfirmed with `--show-settings`: it reported `linter.line_length = 88`\n(ruff's default) for `common/structlog_config.py` rather than the 110\nconfigured for core-api, and a bare `ruff check common/` was\nbyte-identical to `--isolated`.\n\nRuff 0.16.0's default set (YTT, ASYNC, BLE, TRY, S, DTZ, G, …) reported\n21 findings here that nobody had enabled — including two dead `noqa:\nE402` directives, meaningful under an older default set and silently\ndead under the new one. **That drift is the defect.** The specific\nfindings were only its current symptom, and would have been a different\nset after the next bump.\n\n## The fix\n\n`common/ruff.toml` mirrors core-api'\"'\"'s lint rules, so shared code is\nheld to the same standard as its consumers — but sets `line-length = 88`\nrather than 110.\n\nThat difference is deliberate and load-bearing. Three files here are\nvendored into `caura-memclaw-enterprise` under `policy=identical` and\nare format-checked at ruff defaults via `--isolated` for byte-parity.\nThis tree has been maintained at 88 throughout: **at 88 only 7 of 88\nfiles would reflow; at 110 it would be 49.** Raising it would churn most\nof the directory and break that parity for no benefit — `E501` is\nignored in both configs, so `line-length` affects only `ruff format` and\nthe 88/110 split costs nothing on the lint side.\n\nCI gains one step, `ruff check common/ --config common/ruff.toml`. The\nconfig is named explicitly rather than relying on discovery, for the\nsame reason the neighbouring vendored step uses `--isolated`: the gate\nshould not change meaning if config resolution ever does.\n\n## Clearing the 16 findings the pinned rules report\n\n- **6 auto-fixed** — 4 `I001` import order, 2 `RUF100` dead `noqa`.\n- **8 `RUF002`/`RUF003` ambiguous unicode** in comments and docstrings.\nI replaced **only** U+00D7 (`×`→`x`) and U+2013 (en dash→hyphen).\n**U+2014 (em dash) is not flagged by ruff and is this codebase'\"'\"'s\nprose style, so it is untouched** — a blanket dash replacement would\nhave mangled it. Worth noting `core-api/src` has zero `RUF002`/`RUF003`\nhits, so `common/` was the outlier here, not the rule being\nunreasonable.\n- **2 `B027`** on `EventBus.start`/`stop` — marked `noqa` rather than\ndecorated. Their docstrings define them as optional hooks (\"In-process\nbuses treat this as a no-op\"), so adding `@abstractmethod` would force\nevery subclass to implement them. That is a contract change, not a lint\nfix.\n\n## Reviewer notes\n\n- `common/events/{base,pubsub}.py` are `policy=manual` in the enterprise\nvendoring manifest, so those two edits create reconciliation work there\n— not a CI break. **None of the three `policy=identical` files are\ntouched.**\n- I also updated the neighbouring vendored-format step'\"'\"'s comment,\nwhich said `--isolated` pins defaults \"even if a root ruff config is\never added.\" One now exists nearby, so the comment would have read as\nstale.\n- The import reordering touches four `common/models` files, which is why\nthe full suite matters here rather than just the lint gate.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `ruff check common/ --config common/ruff.toml` — clean.\n- All pre-existing ruff scopes still clean; the vendored `--isolated`\ngate still passes (3 files already formatted).\n- **4051 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 3 xpassed, 0 failed.**\n- `actionlint` reports the same single pre-existing finding as\n`origin/main`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9d504f78c6ba291b19838deffae050030df283c7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/9d504f78c6ba291b19838deffae050030df283c7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqb05RCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAp5gQAH3caL+4BqN6HiwCc277v9bO\n7P4jkAic8AiJ3Vd7r2FgwIvKZVf9z4+XdzQ/X6zBYR+oVJ6h+AXwAHnZt+BtTZlY\niolJ7Acetz2Gemg2pg2AD0hIK55YdYTJXefz41I0hGztEhAZgovW7gaWpDQKVXlN\np6Lz31gqUVMiV/Mx/MmtMyx/wXRX9lXafjgpK5r3k0P19+ogzP5vx3P1AZeuNlnq\neJdzgYHCcZDpqTbTJ9pVfytMACN3UNHO68IvFMJaA3ZPTmFVNrhZPwu/D0f9+pGr\ntu2Op6jQoufOenDozryLBZYAp+fIQ7zsnh0NbXTbA6d1X6ciQwDQg+QTST2RnePC\nBFEgBWmw7Axc2vSZFL+jmXzMTjx3EVjIbckXPZ34K3ao9TeXJ1QXAKWTAkm1WRhF\n3xgvhtVYvS/00eaTXN0IW+ZNau6Epz9Laj4dTr5aRo2nZCEWnPdPlLVw+XuwreQI\nZoHSEpgEG+OfawbWxmHbNwWFxyFs7EGfUwgZaw+H2egUB9sKheyP2dbzMNPaHqtn\nhCKVpd7AinyRuqzWlVe6qDJ8JfUcyD58PfK3NuM54N9UHByh7pjPNx1dkGBrALrX\na0/7wAiS2h0uXUR/AEDPxavVtFoLcUpO+eD4GNDMDsjSO2vzsxw5UKNNv8J/Pgtf\neongQ7hS28QwiUctq98P\n=okrE\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 9d504f78c6ba291b19838deffae050030df283c7\nparent 72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785679441 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785679441 +0300\n\nfix(ci): pin common/'s ruff contract and lint-gate it (#685)\n\nFixes #684.\n\n## The problem\n\n`common/` had **no ruff config above it** — no `ruff.toml`, no root\n`pyproject.toml`, nothing in `common/`. So ruff walked up from each\nfile, found nothing, and fell back to its **built-in defaults**. The\nrule set governing shared code that every service imports was therefore\n\"whatever the installed ruff version ships as default,\" and it changes\non upgrade.\n\nConfirmed with `--show-settings`: it reported `linter.line_length = 88`\n(ruff's default) for `common/structlog_config.py` rather than the 110\nconfigured for core-api, and a bare `ruff check common/` was\nbyte-identical to `--isolated`.\n\nRuff 0.16.0's default set (YTT, ASYNC, BLE, TRY, S, DTZ, G, …) reported\n21 findings here that nobody had enabled — including two dead `noqa:\nE402` directives, meaningful under an older default set and silently\ndead under the new one. **That drift is the defect.** The specific\nfindings were only its current symptom, and would have been a different\nset after the next bump.\n\n## The fix\n\n`common/ruff.toml` mirrors core-api'\"'\"'s lint rules, so shared code is\nheld to the same standard as its consumers — but sets `line-length = 88`\nrather than 110.\n\nThat difference is deliberate and load-bearing. Three files here are\nvendored into `caura-memclaw-enterprise` under `policy=identical` and\nare format-checked at ruff defaults via `--isolated` for byte-parity.\nThis tree has been maintained at 88 throughout: **at 88 only 7 of 88\nfiles would reflow; at 110 it would be 49.** Raising it would churn most\nof the directory and break that parity for no benefit — `E501` is\nignored in both configs, so `line-length` affects only `ruff format` and\nthe 88/110 split costs nothing on the lint side.\n\nCI gains one step, `ruff check common/ --config common/ruff.toml`. The\nconfig is named explicitly rather than relying on discovery, for the\nsame reason the neighbouring vendored step uses `--isolated`: the gate\nshould not change meaning if config resolution ever does.\n\n## Clearing the 16 findings the pinned rules report\n\n- **6 auto-fixed** — 4 `I001` import order, 2 `RUF100` dead `noqa`.\n- **8 `RUF002`/`RUF003` ambiguous unicode** in comments and docstrings.\nI replaced **only** U+00D7 (`×`→`x`) and U+2013 (en dash→hyphen).\n**U+2014 (em dash) is not flagged by ruff and is this codebase'\"'\"'s\nprose style, so it is untouched** — a blanket dash replacement would\nhave mangled it. Worth noting `core-api/src` has zero `RUF002`/`RUF003`\nhits, so `common/` was the outlier here, not the rule being\nunreasonable.\n- **2 `B027`** on `EventBus.start`/`stop` — marked `noqa` rather than\ndecorated. Their docstrings define them as optional hooks (\"In-process\nbuses treat this as a no-op\"), so adding `@abstractmethod` would force\nevery subclass to implement them. That is a contract change, not a lint\nfix.\n\n## Reviewer notes\n\n- `common/events/{base,pubsub}.py` are `policy=manual` in the enterprise\nvendoring manifest, so those two edits create reconciliation work there\n— not a CI break. **None of the three `policy=identical` files are\ntouched.**\n- I also updated the neighbouring vendored-format step'\"'\"'s comment,\nwhich said `--isolated` pins defaults \"even if a root ruff config is\never added.\" One now exists nearby, so the comment would have read as\nstale.\n- The import reordering touches four `common/models` files, which is why\nthe full suite matters here rather than just the lint gate.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `ruff check common/ --config common/ruff.toml` — clean.\n- All pre-existing ruff scopes still clean; the vendored `--isolated`\ngate still passes (3 files already formatted).\n- **4051 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed, 3 xpassed, 0 failed.**\n- `actionlint` reports the same single pre-existing finding as\n`origin/main`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-02T14:04:02Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/abfabdb373b3be5a01ec4916bcb0960b966db955/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd"}]},{"sha":"72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDcyYjQ1M2VjOTQxYTMyNmQyNzk5M2RiNzI3NzM4ODU1NmJlMTg5YmQ","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-01T08:04:39Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-01T08:04:39Z"},"message":"feat(search): remote rerank provider (sidecar) + opt-in local build (#680)\n\nCompletes the reranking component (#669) so it fits **both** deployment\nshapes behind the same `RANK_PROVIDER` flag — the right tool per\nenvironment.\n\n## `remote` provider (staging/prod) — recommended\n`common/ranking/providers/remote.py`: HTTP `/rerank` to a **separate\nsidecar** (self-hosted TEI reranker / GPU bge, or a Cohere-style\nendpoint). Keeps **torch out of core-api**, mirroring how you already\nserve the TEI *embedder*.\n- TEI-native contract `{query, texts} → [{index, score}]`; also parses\nthe Cohere `{results:[{index, relevance_score}]}` wrapper. Scores\nre-projected to **input order** (caller sorts).\n- Registry LRU-caches the httpx pool per `(base_url, api_key, model)`\nwith background `aclose()` on eviction (mirrors the OpenAI embedding\ncache). `remote` with no base URL → `ValueError` → service **degrades to\nfirst-stage order**.\n\n## `local` build opt-in (local dev)\n- `core-api/Dockerfile` gains `WITH_RERANK_LOCAL` (default `0`) that\ninstalls `torch + sentence-transformers` **outside the frozen lock** —\nthe **default image stays lean and prod-unaffected**.\n- `docker-compose.rerank-local.yml` overlay builds core-api with it +\nflips `RANK_ENABLED`/`RANK_PROVIDER=local`. The reranker analogue of\n`--profile embed-local`, needed because **the TEI sidecar has no arm64\nimage** — so in-process is the only reranker path on Apple Silicon.\n  ```\ndocker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.rerank-local.yml\nup --build\n  ```\n\n## Net\n| Environment | `RANK_PROVIDER` | Model runs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Local dev (arm64 Mac) | `local` (via overlay) | in-process in core-api\n|\n| Staging / prod | `remote` | `/rerank` sidecar, core-api lean |\n\nSame pipeline step, same `RANK_ENABLED` kill-switch — just the right\nbackend per env. All still **dark by default**.\n\n## Tests\n`tests/test_ranking_remote.py` (httpx `MockTransport`): request shape,\nTEI + Cohere response parsing, input-order re-projection, empty\nshort-circuit, HTTP-error degrade, registry no-URL guard. **Additionally\nwet-tested against a real stdlib `/rerank` server** (real httpx\nround-trip → correct reorder). `.env.example` documents both providers +\nthe overlay.\n\n## Follow-ups (not in this PR)\n- Enterprise: stand up a TEI **rerank sidecar** on staging (mirror\n`staging-memclaw-tei`), set\n`RANK_BASE_URL`+`RANK_ENABLED`+`RANK_PROVIDER=remote` in core-api Helm —\nthen the **real-TEI wet test** (the one thing MockTransport/stdlib can't\ncover) runs against live infra.\n- A/B `remote`/`local` vs `noop` on the benchmark.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d40b136222c01526628b8769491e59c2c91acd29","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/d40b136222c01526628b8769491e59c2c91acd29"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqbaiXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAVeUQAEChQNxxdJ0oDRxd7pBolp3M\nrTNHX4J+a0IbkrfGfjuftLgO6XxXU9dMSBvWrz1/6zDA8Joa0YK6UljA32yzhdQs\nQF/lH7z7SuFstE3JdYkHZG1/yVycREpeX/r5T1PXWgw0nsZMOfO2wHq+0U50OyCF\nf7kdFTy/bMk42mVEaSYydWQM1N/XukYyiJ18CJlySVg+S+dMxURgRp+dlURN4Kts\n5DMHZBnvD5pqgFPbgbRfy7ducf0uZnGM1NrVxPEyxJGhB9Cf5Zv/ppfyNIulhmJ5\nfqaS4D0aoryTlrdQBZSI3I+EhenodC2fq7DFkyNK91gPS7Duw7kP+R8CrH2vNTYo\npli24BwLIReJJY5qCaDBssJKBi+eC6y1UuJv94mpfIyvQjdgI1ukMR3YDIe/Tu3o\na3NtFuFvyUFevh764HygOS4J4RZ3KWOdnvRKOwy7ZbEORNLcN3XVsC8mz/gUe57o\n/QesqGqca3jiNrO6Oqov2fy+xY3uP68Zhvt2ZMVyQ7Nn8bj/nnRJGZuOBxRmS3fu\n8XMqTUAnr6iEp0HLwiFr++QLiGf4LS1G8hWyYd0JIOwJA7Lbkf9EoIAUIPoE510D\nzpduShCxSYFW63rj20C6GWdwkM/W3x7xM5BevEpMI8Lzik3JtvIJWqW0NidhU72H\n128wuxyBAfQ9LN0821DI\n=zaVL\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree d40b136222c01526628b8769491e59c2c91acd29\nparent 443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1785571479 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785571479 +0300\n\nfeat(search): remote rerank provider (sidecar) + opt-in local build (#680)\n\nCompletes the reranking component (#669) so it fits **both** deployment\nshapes behind the same `RANK_PROVIDER` flag — the right tool per\nenvironment.\n\n## `remote` provider (staging/prod) — recommended\n`common/ranking/providers/remote.py`: HTTP `/rerank` to a **separate\nsidecar** (self-hosted TEI reranker / GPU bge, or a Cohere-style\nendpoint). Keeps **torch out of core-api**, mirroring how you already\nserve the TEI *embedder*.\n- TEI-native contract `{query, texts} → [{index, score}]`; also parses\nthe Cohere `{results:[{index, relevance_score}]}` wrapper. Scores\nre-projected to **input order** (caller sorts).\n- Registry LRU-caches the httpx pool per `(base_url, api_key, model)`\nwith background `aclose()` on eviction (mirrors the OpenAI embedding\ncache). `remote` with no base URL → `ValueError` → service **degrades to\nfirst-stage order**.\n\n## `local` build opt-in (local dev)\n- `core-api/Dockerfile` gains `WITH_RERANK_LOCAL` (default `0`) that\ninstalls `torch + sentence-transformers` **outside the frozen lock** —\nthe **default image stays lean and prod-unaffected**.\n- `docker-compose.rerank-local.yml` overlay builds core-api with it +\nflips `RANK_ENABLED`/`RANK_PROVIDER=local`. The reranker analogue of\n`--profile embed-local`, needed because **the TEI sidecar has no arm64\nimage** — so in-process is the only reranker path on Apple Silicon.\n  ```\ndocker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.rerank-local.yml\nup --build\n  ```\n\n## Net\n| Environment | `RANK_PROVIDER` | Model runs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Local dev (arm64 Mac) | `local` (via overlay) | in-process in core-api\n|\n| Staging / prod | `remote` | `/rerank` sidecar, core-api lean |\n\nSame pipeline step, same `RANK_ENABLED` kill-switch — just the right\nbackend per env. All still **dark by default**.\n\n## Tests\n`tests/test_ranking_remote.py` (httpx `MockTransport`): request shape,\nTEI + Cohere response parsing, input-order re-projection, empty\nshort-circuit, HTTP-error degrade, registry no-URL guard. **Additionally\nwet-tested against a real stdlib `/rerank` server** (real httpx\nround-trip → correct reorder). `.env.example` documents both providers +\nthe overlay.\n\n## Follow-ups (not in this PR)\n- Enterprise: stand up a TEI **rerank sidecar** on staging (mirror\n`staging-memclaw-tei`), set\n`RANK_BASE_URL`+`RANK_ENABLED`+`RANK_PROVIDER=remote` in core-api Helm —\nthen the **real-TEI wet test** (the one thing MockTransport/stdlib can't\ncover) runs against live infra.\n- A/B `remote`/`local` vs `noop` on the benchmark.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-01T08:04:40Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/72b453ec941a326d27993db7277388556be189bd/comments","author":{"login":"arkash20","id":55495209,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1NDk1MjA5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55495209?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20","html_url":"https://github.com/arkash20","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arkash20/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a"}]},{"sha":"443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDQ0M2ZkMGNjZTJlZTVlYmEzYzk5ZWMzNDc2ZGE2ODJiZjI4NDc4NGE","commit":{"author":{"name":"ran-taig","email":"ran@caura.ai","date":"2026-07-31T09:43:51Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-07-31T09:43:51Z"},"message":"fix(insights): declare numpy so discover keeps clustering (#679)\n\n## What broke\n\n`insights_service._query_discover` imports numpy directly for its\nk-means step, but **numpy was never a declared core-api dependency** —\nit arrived transitively via `pgvector`. #605 bumped pgvector 0.4.2 →\n0.5.0, which made numpy optional. numpy dropped out of\n`core-api/uv.lock`, then out of the built image, and discover silently\nstarted taking its `except ImportError` branch: flat pattern analysis\ninstead of vector clustering.\n\nThis was found while analyzing insight quality on the eToro on-prem\ndeployment. Every nightly insights run on all three tenants was degraded\nfrom **Jul 28** onward, and nothing surfaced it — the fallback logs a\nwarning, but the run still reports success and writes a full set of\nfindings.\n\n## Why CI didn't catch it\n\nTwo independent blind spots, one guard added for each:\n\n1. **The test skipped itself.** `TestNumpyKmeans::test_basic_clustering`\nopened with `try: import numpy / except ImportError: pytest.skip(...)`.\nWhen numpy vanished, the test skipped and CI stayed green. It now\nimports numpy unconditionally, plus a new `test_numpy_is_installed` that\n**fails** rather than skips.\n2. **pytest structurally cannot catch this.** CI installs core-api\n*editable* (`uv pip install -e core-api/[dev]`), while the image\ninstalls from the *frozen lock export* — so a module present only\ntransitively imports fine in the test job while being absent from the\nimage. The `image-version` job already exists for exactly this class of\nbug (the version stamp \"silently shipped for weeks\" for the same\nreason), so it now also asserts `import numpy` inside the built image.\nNo extra build cost — the image is already built in that job.\n\n## Changes\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `core-api/pyproject.toml` | Declare `numpy>=2.0,<3` — the code imports\nit directly, so it owns the declaration. Comment records the pgvector\nhistory. |\n| `core-api/uv.lock` | Re-locked: `numpy 2.5.1` added (plus a stale\nproject version-string catch-up, 2.24.1 → 2.26.0). |\n| `tests/test_insights_service.py` | Fail-not-skip numpy guard; removed\n`try/except ImportError` from the clustering test. |\n| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Assert directly-imported runtime deps are\npresent in the built image. |\n\n## Verification\n\n- `uv export --frozen --no-dev` now emits `numpy==2.5.1` into the image\nrequirements.\n- Built the amd64 runtime image from this branch and ran inside it:\n  - `import numpy` → `numpy 2.5.1`\n- the real `_numpy_kmeans` correctly separates two synthetic clusters\n(labels `0`/`1`, centroids `(2,2)`)\n- `pytest tests/test_insights_service.py -k NumpyKmeans` passes; `uv\nlock --check` clean; `ruff` clean.\n\n## Notes / follow-ups (not in this PR)\n\n- **eToro prod is still degraded** — the box runs on-prem `v2.11.13`;\nrestoring clustering there needs a release + cutover, tracked\nseparately.\n- **The fallback still reports success.** A degraded discover run is\nindistinguishable from a healthy one in the audit row. Making that\nvisible (audit-row stat or alert) is a deliberate follow-up,\nintentionally kept out of this fix.\n- Worth a look at whether other services rely on transitively-present\nmodules they import directly; `core-storage-api` also lost numpy in the\nsame bump, though nothing there imports it.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"94b4ccb0371ad3d15f69b1a0f5169d67cbabe6b7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/94b4ccb0371ad3d15f69b1a0f5169d67cbabe6b7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqbG5XCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAdcwQAJfug8D16qBR2OEIKyr+asll\n9B5HDyJqOHwvsxqcTofuGdVyhdQNrOdpNr1Y8qiwCMcXY/1pD1GuY5m0K6B2uVXk\ne1UMTW6BBYBGXeKwBHAy6J2kBAPY78vXEsplStKQHPmMe7JUbn7up1En9EZp7hpB\nVw1Ewbnv1WYpl1bBhMjd9zs3ACsWs42Q/lVPnKRCd9ESuxsa6ueR45EFljzoHIV0\nx60cIrVSaYbpluntObuVAcwsHA/LH6UYRnESt2fkDdeQO6cph0cKYtTubcjmxxXF\nXAqJ/y7+c0aDpq6IpVRauJu6nzxN+AF+mlZIB7BuGVgp/UCZ+SGZIQd9QPZa1FsR\nryZoBxLI/x4nkgzwhmL3v7bL2gm7hEwoxM80JVvih/d2vvKW1AYZ5nwLit0b8k+D\nCNT5vClP1zr1ZjDh0G7N9e/ATFAlCBhwqv6kHd2aY9+eSbyaB/403rFiJJBj2d7B\nS99IlOtn8O84Y+c62oUfcs0cbjscxSzMuAWi4EbhJg4MwTW6oPE/BrmkcVQZrqIC\ni8xG/64UIGz/mVv6qRN617X/3/xSyvL3RI8Um92h2thou/U2Ppbvkb3+6bGMmFTv\nrVcdkxbvuf9g41ecZDPxHY9l2CleCJ/1+1bEBZilzg3x6qTCqK9lBKKqupKZVEFq\n5ATylxO+qFzXAOiXRrEc\n=SlpX\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 94b4ccb0371ad3d15f69b1a0f5169d67cbabe6b7\nparent 2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5\nauthor ran-taig <ran@caura.ai> 1785491031 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785491031 +0300\n\nfix(insights): declare numpy so discover keeps clustering (#679)\n\n## What broke\n\n`insights_service._query_discover` imports numpy directly for its\nk-means step, but **numpy was never a declared core-api dependency** —\nit arrived transitively via `pgvector`. #605 bumped pgvector 0.4.2 →\n0.5.0, which made numpy optional. numpy dropped out of\n`core-api/uv.lock`, then out of the built image, and discover silently\nstarted taking its `except ImportError` branch: flat pattern analysis\ninstead of vector clustering.\n\nThis was found while analyzing insight quality on the eToro on-prem\ndeployment. Every nightly insights run on all three tenants was degraded\nfrom **Jul 28** onward, and nothing surfaced it — the fallback logs a\nwarning, but the run still reports success and writes a full set of\nfindings.\n\n## Why CI didn't catch it\n\nTwo independent blind spots, one guard added for each:\n\n1. **The test skipped itself.** `TestNumpyKmeans::test_basic_clustering`\nopened with `try: import numpy / except ImportError: pytest.skip(...)`.\nWhen numpy vanished, the test skipped and CI stayed green. It now\nimports numpy unconditionally, plus a new `test_numpy_is_installed` that\n**fails** rather than skips.\n2. **pytest structurally cannot catch this.** CI installs core-api\n*editable* (`uv pip install -e core-api/[dev]`), while the image\ninstalls from the *frozen lock export* — so a module present only\ntransitively imports fine in the test job while being absent from the\nimage. The `image-version` job already exists for exactly this class of\nbug (the version stamp \"silently shipped for weeks\" for the same\nreason), so it now also asserts `import numpy` inside the built image.\nNo extra build cost — the image is already built in that job.\n\n## Changes\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `core-api/pyproject.toml` | Declare `numpy>=2.0,<3` — the code imports\nit directly, so it owns the declaration. Comment records the pgvector\nhistory. |\n| `core-api/uv.lock` | Re-locked: `numpy 2.5.1` added (plus a stale\nproject version-string catch-up, 2.24.1 → 2.26.0). |\n| `tests/test_insights_service.py` | Fail-not-skip numpy guard; removed\n`try/except ImportError` from the clustering test. |\n| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Assert directly-imported runtime deps are\npresent in the built image. |\n\n## Verification\n\n- `uv export --frozen --no-dev` now emits `numpy==2.5.1` into the image\nrequirements.\n- Built the amd64 runtime image from this branch and ran inside it:\n  - `import numpy` → `numpy 2.5.1`\n- the real `_numpy_kmeans` correctly separates two synthetic clusters\n(labels `0`/`1`, centroids `(2,2)`)\n- `pytest tests/test_insights_service.py -k NumpyKmeans` passes; `uv\nlock --check` clean; `ruff` clean.\n\n## Notes / follow-ups (not in this PR)\n\n- **eToro prod is still degraded** — the box runs on-prem `v2.11.13`;\nrestoring clustering there needs a release + cutover, tracked\nseparately.\n- **The fallback still reports success.** A degraded discover run is\nindistinguishable from a healthy one in the audit row. Making that\nvisible (audit-row stat or alert) is a deliberate follow-up,\nintentionally kept out of this fix.\n- Worth a look at whether other services rely on transitively-present\nmodules they import directly; `core-storage-api` also lost numpy in the\nsame bump, though nothing there imports it.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-07-31T09:43:51Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/443fd0cce2ee5eba3c99ec3476da682bf284784a/comments","author":{"login":"ran-taig","id":254242316,"node_id":"U_kgDODyduDA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/254242316?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig","html_url":"https://github.com/ran-taig","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5"}]},{"sha":"2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDJiNTI1NDYzOGM3YWVlZmNkMGQxMDY0ZDBjOThhNWUxYTY1MTM4YzU","commit":{"author":{"name":"ran-taig","email":"ran@caura.ai","date":"2026-07-30T19:14:31Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-07-30T19:14:31Z"},"message":"feat(search): org flag to disable query-time entity/graph retrieval (#677)\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds **`search.entity_retrieval`** (bool, default `true`) — one org\nsetting that blocks entity lookup and graph search on the read path, so\nevery read resolves through keyword/semantic search alone.\n\nThere was no way to turn entity retrieval off. `search.graph_retrieval`\nonly gates graph *expansion* inside `ParallelEmbedAndEntityBoost`, and\n`ClassifyQuery` never consults it — so the `ENTITY_LOOKUP` short-circuit\n(which sets `skip_embedding` + `skip_scored_search` and bypasses vector\nscoring entirely) fired regardless. `graph_max_hops: 0` doesn't help\neither: the classifier still runs entity FTS, takes hop-0 seeds and\nshort-circuits.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X PUT \"$API/api/v1/settings?tenant_id=$TENANT\" -H \"X-API-Key: $KEY\" \\\n  -d '{\"search\": {\"entity_retrieval\": false}}'\n```\n\n## Related Issue\n\nN/A\n\n## Type of Change\n\n- [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change that fixes an issue)\n- [x] New feature (non-breaking change that adds functionality)\n- [ ] Breaking change (fix or feature that changes existing behavior)\n- [ ] Documentation update\n- [ ] Refactor / internal cleanup\n- [ ] Other:\n\n## What changed\n\nEntity retrieval enters the read path at **two independent points**, so\na single gate isn't enough. Both are now gated on the same flag:\n\n| File | Gate |\n|---|---|\n| `pipeline/steps/search/classify_query.py` | **1** — entity block\nskipped: no entity FTS, no graph expansion, no `ENTITY_LOOKUP`\nshort-circuit. Query falls through to temporal → recent-context →\nkeyword/semantic |\n| `pipeline/steps/search/parallel_embed_entity_boost.py` | **2** — no\n`GRAPH_HOP_BOOST`, with a log reason distinct from the CAURA-698\nover-broad decline so ops can tell a deliberate disable from the\nprecision heuristic |\n| `services/memory_service.py` | **3** — the deprecated\n`_search_memories_legacy` path honours it too, so the switch can't be\nsilently a no-op if `_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH` is ever flipped |\n| `services/organization_settings.py` | `DEFAULT_SETTINGS` +\n`_LEAF_TYPES` + `ResolvedConfig.entity_retrieval` |\n| `config.py` | `ENTITY_RETRIEVAL_ENABLED` env default — fleet-wide\nswitch for on-prem without editing every tenant |\n| `mcp_server.py`, `routes/memories.py` (×2),\n`services/recall_service.py` |\n`entity_retrieval=config.entity_retrieval`, alongside the existing\n`graph_expand` |\n\n**Defaults are unblocked at every layer** — tenant unset → env default\n(`true`) → an absent `ctx.data` key means enabled, so internal callers\nand existing tests are unaffected.\n\n**Read-side only.** Entity extraction, linking\n(`entity_linking.auto_entity_linking_enabled` is the write-side switch)\nand relation inference keep building the graph, so re-enabling needs no\nbackfill. Precedence: `entity_retrieval=false` supersedes\n`graph_retrieval` and `graph_max_hops`, which only bound expansion depth\n*after* a match.\n\nGate 3 uses a `_no_entity_boost()` coroutine rather than branching\naround the `gather`, keeping the legacy path's cancel-on-error structure\nbyte-identical.\n\n## How Has This Been Tested?\n\n```bash\npython -m pytest tests -m unit -q          # 2181 passed\npython -m pytest tests -m \"not integration and not benchmark\" -q\nruff check core-api/src/ && ruff format --check core-api/src/\ncd core-api && uv run mypy src/            # 189 files, clean\n```\n\n- **2181 unit tests pass**; 3665 pass across all non-DB tests. The 5\nremaining failures (`tests/test_events/test_factory.py` ×4,\n`tests/test_no_direct_db_outside_storage.py`) **pre-exist on `main`** —\nverified by stashing this change and re-running.\n- 15 new tests in `tests/test_entity_retrieval_flag.py`: both gates, the\nlegacy path, `TEMPORAL` / `RECENT_CONTEXT` / `KEYWORD` / `SEMANTIC`\nrouting with the flag off, flag-on regression, absent-key default, and\nsettings resolution + env fallback + type validation.\n- Six MCP config stubs gained the new attribute (they construct\n`_FakeConfig` / `SimpleNamespace` configs by hand).\n- Smoke: new `test_entity_retrieval_flag` in `scripts/smoke_test.py` — a\nmemory reachable *only* through the graph (content shares no wording\nwith the query; linked to a *related* entity) must be found with the\nflag on and absent with it off. The setting is restored in a `finally`.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] I have read CONTRIBUTING.md\n- [x] I have added tests that cover my changes\n- [x] `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` pass\n- [x] `mypy` passes\n- [x] `pytest` passes locally (pre-existing failures noted above)\n- [x] No docs to update — org settings aren't enumerated in `docs/` or\n`README.md`; the flag is documented inline at its definition\n- [x] `CHANGELOG.md` is generated by release-please from the\nConventional Commit (`feat(search):`), so no manual entry\n\n## Additional Notes\n\n**Result sets legitimately change for entity queries when the flag is\noff.** `ENTITY_LOOKUP` bypasses the `min_similarity` floor (`vec_sim` is\n`None`, so `PostFilterResults` skips). With the flag off those rows pass\nthrough the cosine gate like everything else — intended semantics, and\nwhat makes the smoke assertion sound.\n\n**Explicit entity APIs are deliberately untouched** — `GET /entities`,\n`GET /graph`, `memclaw_entity_get`. Those are direct entity fetches, not\nquery-time retrieval. Happy to add a strict mode that closes them too if\nreviewers want it.\n\n**Unrelated pre-existing bug spotted nearby:** `scripts/smoke_test.py`\n`test_settings` PUTs `{\"graph_expand\": False}`, which isn't a valid\nsettings key — `_check_keys` 422s it, so that \"Settings PUT: 200\" check\nis failing on every smoke run. Left alone to keep this PR scoped; the\nfix is the nested `{\"search\": {\"graph_retrieval\": false}}` shape.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"952cb5465a187e9d7b6af9e62614fbbf2622ba88","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/952cb5465a187e9d7b6af9e62614fbbf2622ba88"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqa6KXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAoZkQAEyv+BObFRd5+295Q9okQJ6y\nNMayasiEKJfslVjDyjmZ0Kzqubn66vMY+ReQC8VTYSUBqMFYVDVz4asx+yBwfZ0o\n8YZJlMkmBHvG1XhcHvUWGckf3BxIObF08YoXL9PmxkC+hkqoBr0pQk9DLmZ//dOV\nWOJHLO7tG0F87Cvem0nhiTO0AimY8MkFALfLWVxwcARGeiAL0ls0RZ5nhfeivhqj\nH/NCDsK8U687L0AJhua+6fiQcr5WT8VkcfDMyQFrg/injUB5V0fGbUvj+VMEgliX\n2+HWff5DvOOvojOz1+4V7NeGIUf9E5IRcfKA6LbeJyB51puH3YLjNhSYRO6C+axo\nBeLh5fsbVhJ48LYxWg1hydvR/je803aH2ixqxOzJwF7MDvMqpkSd8hFNcEczqbyC\neP92qAQ/bBno/4bCK/9zA+9V6Y5NHRc6VTh2TK/G9kTvapUxwRRNYBd9YjMW1rTA\nw9KntKyb+RWEEiEVn+bn8K9tscKO9dohgQzSFDfaCbY3N3AnpAmBP3aLev7G8TSI\nyVL/KiV1o9TM7QAjDNI6xk3CCLV8MbElyB8lQE1CBDV7FCiSnXr6Ad3UU5bTCoBx\nrdSabEZcWlYEC2ZShCKzy0TtXqnPnWUO18BXijNZf7K1BVWNWQJmeDUorkvY44/F\n9+aEU9qK4c9QAMpNkDjQ\n=OSNK\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 952cb5465a187e9d7b6af9e62614fbbf2622ba88\nparent 9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86\nauthor ran-taig <ran@caura.ai> 1785438871 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785438871 +0300\n\nfeat(search): org flag to disable query-time entity/graph retrieval (#677)\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds **`search.entity_retrieval`** (bool, default `true`) — one org\nsetting that blocks entity lookup and graph search on the read path, so\nevery read resolves through keyword/semantic search alone.\n\nThere was no way to turn entity retrieval off. `search.graph_retrieval`\nonly gates graph *expansion* inside `ParallelEmbedAndEntityBoost`, and\n`ClassifyQuery` never consults it — so the `ENTITY_LOOKUP` short-circuit\n(which sets `skip_embedding` + `skip_scored_search` and bypasses vector\nscoring entirely) fired regardless. `graph_max_hops: 0` doesn't help\neither: the classifier still runs entity FTS, takes hop-0 seeds and\nshort-circuits.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X PUT \"$API/api/v1/settings?tenant_id=$TENANT\" -H \"X-API-Key: $KEY\" \\\n  -d '{\"search\": {\"entity_retrieval\": false}}'\n```\n\n## Related Issue\n\nN/A\n\n## Type of Change\n\n- [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change that fixes an issue)\n- [x] New feature (non-breaking change that adds functionality)\n- [ ] Breaking change (fix or feature that changes existing behavior)\n- [ ] Documentation update\n- [ ] Refactor / internal cleanup\n- [ ] Other:\n\n## What changed\n\nEntity retrieval enters the read path at **two independent points**, so\na single gate isn't enough. Both are now gated on the same flag:\n\n| File | Gate |\n|---|---|\n| `pipeline/steps/search/classify_query.py` | **1** — entity block\nskipped: no entity FTS, no graph expansion, no `ENTITY_LOOKUP`\nshort-circuit. Query falls through to temporal → recent-context →\nkeyword/semantic |\n| `pipeline/steps/search/parallel_embed_entity_boost.py` | **2** — no\n`GRAPH_HOP_BOOST`, with a log reason distinct from the CAURA-698\nover-broad decline so ops can tell a deliberate disable from the\nprecision heuristic |\n| `services/memory_service.py` | **3** — the deprecated\n`_search_memories_legacy` path honours it too, so the switch can't be\nsilently a no-op if `_USE_PIPELINE_SEARCH` is ever flipped |\n| `services/organization_settings.py` | `DEFAULT_SETTINGS` +\n`_LEAF_TYPES` + `ResolvedConfig.entity_retrieval` |\n| `config.py` | `ENTITY_RETRIEVAL_ENABLED` env default — fleet-wide\nswitch for on-prem without editing every tenant |\n| `mcp_server.py`, `routes/memories.py` (×2),\n`services/recall_service.py` |\n`entity_retrieval=config.entity_retrieval`, alongside the existing\n`graph_expand` |\n\n**Defaults are unblocked at every layer** — tenant unset → env default\n(`true`) → an absent `ctx.data` key means enabled, so internal callers\nand existing tests are unaffected.\n\n**Read-side only.** Entity extraction, linking\n(`entity_linking.auto_entity_linking_enabled` is the write-side switch)\nand relation inference keep building the graph, so re-enabling needs no\nbackfill. Precedence: `entity_retrieval=false` supersedes\n`graph_retrieval` and `graph_max_hops`, which only bound expansion depth\n*after* a match.\n\nGate 3 uses a `_no_entity_boost()` coroutine rather than branching\naround the `gather`, keeping the legacy path's cancel-on-error structure\nbyte-identical.\n\n## How Has This Been Tested?\n\n```bash\npython -m pytest tests -m unit -q          # 2181 passed\npython -m pytest tests -m \"not integration and not benchmark\" -q\nruff check core-api/src/ && ruff format --check core-api/src/\ncd core-api && uv run mypy src/            # 189 files, clean\n```\n\n- **2181 unit tests pass**; 3665 pass across all non-DB tests. The 5\nremaining failures (`tests/test_events/test_factory.py` ×4,\n`tests/test_no_direct_db_outside_storage.py`) **pre-exist on `main`** —\nverified by stashing this change and re-running.\n- 15 new tests in `tests/test_entity_retrieval_flag.py`: both gates, the\nlegacy path, `TEMPORAL` / `RECENT_CONTEXT` / `KEYWORD` / `SEMANTIC`\nrouting with the flag off, flag-on regression, absent-key default, and\nsettings resolution + env fallback + type validation.\n- Six MCP config stubs gained the new attribute (they construct\n`_FakeConfig` / `SimpleNamespace` configs by hand).\n- Smoke: new `test_entity_retrieval_flag` in `scripts/smoke_test.py` — a\nmemory reachable *only* through the graph (content shares no wording\nwith the query; linked to a *related* entity) must be found with the\nflag on and absent with it off. The setting is restored in a `finally`.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] I have read CONTRIBUTING.md\n- [x] I have added tests that cover my changes\n- [x] `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` pass\n- [x] `mypy` passes\n- [x] `pytest` passes locally (pre-existing failures noted above)\n- [x] No docs to update — org settings aren't enumerated in `docs/` or\n`README.md`; the flag is documented inline at its definition\n- [x] `CHANGELOG.md` is generated by release-please from the\nConventional Commit (`feat(search):`), so no manual entry\n\n## Additional Notes\n\n**Result sets legitimately change for entity queries when the flag is\noff.** `ENTITY_LOOKUP` bypasses the `min_similarity` floor (`vec_sim` is\n`None`, so `PostFilterResults` skips). With the flag off those rows pass\nthrough the cosine gate like everything else — intended semantics, and\nwhat makes the smoke assertion sound.\n\n**Explicit entity APIs are deliberately untouched** — `GET /entities`,\n`GET /graph`, `memclaw_entity_get`. Those are direct entity fetches, not\nquery-time retrieval. Happy to add a strict mode that closes them too if\nreviewers want it.\n\n**Unrelated pre-existing bug spotted nearby:** `scripts/smoke_test.py`\n`test_settings` PUTs `{\"graph_expand\": False}`, which isn't a valid\nsettings key — `_check_keys` 422s it, so that \"Settings PUT: 200\" check\nis failing on every smoke run. Left alone to keep this PR scoped; the\nfix is the nested `{\"search\": {\"graph_retrieval\": false}}` shape.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-07-30T19:14:31Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/2b5254638c7aeefcd0d1064d0c98a5e1a65138c5/comments","author":{"login":"ran-taig","id":254242316,"node_id":"U_kgDODyduDA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/254242316?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig","html_url":"https://github.com/ran-taig","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86"}]},{"sha":"9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDkxMjJmMDgxODMwMDE4NTRmNDI1NDQ3NzU2NWM5NzVkZTdkNzFhODY","commit":{"author":{"name":"ran-taig","email":"ran@caura.ai","date":"2026-07-30T17:18:06Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-07-30T17:18:06Z"},"message":"feat(interviewer-cli): install/uninstall to schedule the cron in one command (#674)\n\nAdds `memclaw-interviewer install` / `uninstall` so a client schedules\nthe disk-parser cron with one command instead of hand-editing crontab.\nSelf-contained — no change to capture/protocol code. `memclaw-client`\n0.3.0 → 0.4.0.\n\n## Why not auto-on-install\n`pip install` runs no install-time code (wheels), so a package genuinely\n*cannot* register a cron — and silently scheduling a job that reads\ntranscripts and ships data on a bare install would be the wrong consent\nposture (and contradicts the feature's default-deny philosophy). So it's\none explicit command.\n\n## What `install` does\n```bash\nmemclaw-interviewer install --interval 30m      # + --harness cursor for Cursor\nmemclaw-interviewer uninstall                    # removes entry + env file\n```\n- **Idempotent crontab entry**, keyed by a marker comment — re-install\nreplaces (never duplicates) exactly our line; uninstall removes only\nours, leaving the user's other cron lines untouched.\n- **0600 env file it sources** — cron does *not* inherit the shell\nenvironment, so the connection identity\n(base-url/key/tenant/agent/fleet/projects) would otherwise be missing\nwhen the job fires. The API key lives only in this `0600` file, never in\ncrontab; non-secret behavior flags (`--harness`, `--all-projects`) ride\non the cron command.\n- **Refuses to schedule a no-op** — errors (exit 2, nothing written) if\ncredentials or a project allowlist are missing (default-deny).\n- **Degrades cleanly** where `crontab` is absent (Windows) — prints Task\nScheduler guidance, exits 2.\n- macOS note printed about Full Disk Access for `cron`.\n\n## Design\nNew `installer.py` holds the **pure, unit-tested** logic — interval→cron\nparsing, cron-line build, idempotent crontab merge, env rendering with\nquote-escaping + 0600 write; the `crontab`/filesystem calls are thin\nseams monkeypatched in the e2e tests.\n\n## Tests\n25 new (94 total, ruff clean): interval parse (incl. rejects),\nidempotent merge + uninstall-strips-only-ours, env\nrendering/quoting/0600, and end-to-end install→re-install→uninstall with\nIO seams faked (incl. refusal paths).\n\nBranch off `main`. Docs: `clients/python/README.md` updated; enterprise\nSetup.mdx note to follow.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"bac824438a861b88d7650ff6741b1b782b77c9bf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/bac824438a861b88d7650ff6741b1b782b77c9bf"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqa4dOCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmf4QABli5l9HflTDJTg268mwhwXX\n/PH5DE786Hiaa5sAyX0hC0mKcltw7yQvga2Kek1MA9HpkFvGfZwnGQS2BGQAVzUi\naFzHvx/TYZVFtXa3gpt3ogn8+ZZa1T33zOXmW4Cw06HYlkIi9WGqbitgujY+A4oA\n4dibTZEXPrvPnucgOCfNQEYI9NXtp7NgHtEKgIqLZk6sOFxynALCeFt2bvaUt5zy\ngZSTVseWgjZRIP9HgfB12+N3oon9X2NjpVlJ2tbKW7Jr2hTsJZ4Fs6hCj/Fznxt9\n/z8cX/drGH7eEjJl9kjAXAmliyry6A915m0pPVWzv3lvjftvAtJHI6fgttjC/vVV\nwDq1ahbcEaM6UdAsiJ4v1zKVEZz17cMOngRX6bMvM5AaBRxgCdMlojAx3LhjFl6r\nlBo1UrsNEPlz7s5x5nVGzGE5rOrvFEfBSbKN3nZIfpzl9YbI+sAUftbjRhWdQZ3r\nODgcCpV5Kj1dDQvpmnBad156cSqqbDK/B3kStuzkLC5ISysJVl3gz0Tjxgh5ICDB\n+9A0xHduScd7ejoJkJLcY9Ice7esF2zw1I8y0Mlc8F0Djj1PqAAf3IBb7JNsa+5Q\niMZJskG7KxEzO3QkWo8B5n6AdC2mjLGG5TetD0I6Lgrq723H/ytPji+TiGDpjhAv\nmIQkCzw/6zR+mwrM5JVT\n=pM72\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree bac824438a861b88d7650ff6741b1b782b77c9bf\nparent 81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7\nauthor ran-taig <ran@caura.ai> 1785431886 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785431886 +0300\n\nfeat(interviewer-cli): install/uninstall to schedule the cron in one command (#674)\n\nAdds `memclaw-interviewer install` / `uninstall` so a client schedules\nthe disk-parser cron with one command instead of hand-editing crontab.\nSelf-contained — no change to capture/protocol code. `memclaw-client`\n0.3.0 → 0.4.0.\n\n## Why not auto-on-install\n`pip install` runs no install-time code (wheels), so a package genuinely\n*cannot* register a cron — and silently scheduling a job that reads\ntranscripts and ships data on a bare install would be the wrong consent\nposture (and contradicts the feature's default-deny philosophy). So it's\none explicit command.\n\n## What `install` does\n```bash\nmemclaw-interviewer install --interval 30m      # + --harness cursor for Cursor\nmemclaw-interviewer uninstall                    # removes entry + env file\n```\n- **Idempotent crontab entry**, keyed by a marker comment — re-install\nreplaces (never duplicates) exactly our line; uninstall removes only\nours, leaving the user's other cron lines untouched.\n- **0600 env file it sources** — cron does *not* inherit the shell\nenvironment, so the connection identity\n(base-url/key/tenant/agent/fleet/projects) would otherwise be missing\nwhen the job fires. The API key lives only in this `0600` file, never in\ncrontab; non-secret behavior flags (`--harness`, `--all-projects`) ride\non the cron command.\n- **Refuses to schedule a no-op** — errors (exit 2, nothing written) if\ncredentials or a project allowlist are missing (default-deny).\n- **Degrades cleanly** where `crontab` is absent (Windows) — prints Task\nScheduler guidance, exits 2.\n- macOS note printed about Full Disk Access for `cron`.\n\n## Design\nNew `installer.py` holds the **pure, unit-tested** logic — interval→cron\nparsing, cron-line build, idempotent crontab merge, env rendering with\nquote-escaping + 0600 write; the `crontab`/filesystem calls are thin\nseams monkeypatched in the e2e tests.\n\n## Tests\n25 new (94 total, ruff clean): interval parse (incl. rejects),\nidempotent merge + uninstall-strips-only-ours, env\nrendering/quoting/0600, and end-to-end install→re-install→uninstall with\nIO seams faked (incl. refusal paths).\n\nBranch off `main`. Docs: `clients/python/README.md` updated; enterprise\nSetup.mdx note to follow.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nSigned-off-by: Ran Taig <ran@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-07-30T17:18:07Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/9122f08183001854f4254477565c975de7d71a86/comments","author":{"login":"ran-taig","id":254242316,"node_id":"U_kgDODyduDA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/254242316?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig","html_url":"https://github.com/ran-taig","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ran-taig/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7"}]},{"sha":"81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDgxZmJiYzZmYjNkOTY1NDZmYmQzZjNkNzEwNjY1ZmY4YWJkOWVhZTc","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-07-30T10:52:30Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-07-30T10:52:30Z"},"message":"docs(review): record the measured membership values on the review gate (#676)\n\nComments only — but the wrong value in them cost a full round-trip, so\nit is worth writing down what\nwas actually measured.\n\nThe gate's comments claimed a private org member's `author_association`\nsurfaces as `NONE`. It does\nnot. On the strength of \"disproving\" that, I proposed swapping the\nruntime membership API for the\npayload field; CI then skipped that change's own pull request and\nreported the real value:\n**`CONTRIBUTOR`** — unusable as a gate on a public repo, since anyone\nwith a merged commit earns it.\n\n### What was observed, same private member, same repo\n\n```\ngithub.event.pull_request.author_association    ->  CONTRIBUTOR\ngithub.event.comment.author_association         ->  MEMBER\nGET /repos/{repo}/pulls/{n} .author_association ->  MEMBER\n```\n\nThree things follow, and all three are now in the file:\n\n- **The two payloads disagree.** That is why `@claude` retriggers kept\nworking for private members\nwhile the on-open review skipped them — the retrigger path reads the\ncomment payload.\n- **The REST value is computed for whoever asks.** A personal token\nreports `MEMBER` where CI sees\n`CONTRIBUTOR`, so validating this gate with a local `gh` call proves\nnothing. That is precisely the\n  mistake behind the bad proposal, and the comment now says so.\n- **The operational requirement is stated at the top**, not implied in\npassing: a member whose org\nmembership is private is skipped exactly like a stranger — silently,\nbehind a green check — so each\n  member has to set their own membership to *Public*.\n\nThe retrigger comment carried the same claim in the *opposite*\ndirection: it warned that private\nmembers appear as `NONE` on the very field that path depends on, when\nthat is the one place the field\ndoes resolve. Corrected, with a note not to \"align\" the two gates onto\nit.\n\n### Verification\n\nZero non-comment lines changed. `actionlint` output identical to `main`\n(five pre-existing findings),\nYAML parses.\n\nThis pull request is also the end-to-end check that the on-open review\nworks now that membership is\npublic — if a verdict appears below from `claude-review` rather than a\nskip notice, the gate is fixed.\n\n### Still outstanding\n\nThe gate requires *public* membership from **every** author, and not\neveryone on the team has flipped\nit yet — those authors' pull requests will keep being skipped silently.\nWorth a nudge; nobody can\nchange it on someone else's behalf.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"b2b1b66d93c337835cc40a7191954eded636cdcf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/b2b1b66d93c337835cc40a7191954eded636cdcf"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqayzuCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAYFcQAClR8kOdQmYsOC0Xenf0H5E+\nOWgVrtCOSLmCPrSAvXsDx1qajhDD0l0prVkB4517NPJK5lFEg0JP9zSQSrpZPxMS\n93kpmPACdlbtXEjgCoO0nd0on6bN/J7aCiOypjKVpig2dqP0D6PAnb6NSgBQevmq\n2PykQso4EPqwv2CfeE59L3ncKXgs8+O64YZ2jp0Ne1UdBW9rezhlFQFCToVGW5l2\nMWaptJjQM0kYKRXZ33qWOFhYa3m2jlXgKS0QOHDLJOy8+QyYCLwL8vbZHOgQhD47\n7uMLOz03Z994ATsOIxqwufz/0tURYtyMOiHQ2E2nZW5x+PmAgmOLNBmedQdPnVQu\nR1yK86bTMTAlye3yBM8sNN1jsYiHgm2YkrFUZBp2OhmoTyG9lUmQPdsXGbZm7yu7\nxUKFjS3GIv558TZnMmubt468LGYUCyyNCUgxhe1b235Eon702SNQtYP9FhovDqZB\n09K5PjyL4oO0c2uoT0mG2uDSzk8MvjDzcIHrW+Iia1UUeUi+72gwCaantOYtCfY8\nDruUQVKADzv5LHOuiwdqF//Wcgd3b2+JYm7k29Ir+oYsFC4gUOlBzwMFepQihK99\nMwc9z1a9PDxUU5RzK6rlhL+OKiUCD1X5S9lJaCN/Ssi0L8Tr+GckLZ0cmm6iliUH\nZ3FU+HZdQ2Q5cVTwD0uV\n=hD+n\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree b2b1b66d93c337835cc40a7191954eded636cdcf\nparent bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785408750 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785408750 +0300\n\ndocs(review): record the measured membership values on the review gate (#676)\n\nComments only — but the wrong value in them cost a full round-trip, so\nit is worth writing down what\nwas actually measured.\n\nThe gate's comments claimed a private org member's `author_association`\nsurfaces as `NONE`. It does\nnot. On the strength of \"disproving\" that, I proposed swapping the\nruntime membership API for the\npayload field; CI then skipped that change's own pull request and\nreported the real value:\n**`CONTRIBUTOR`** — unusable as a gate on a public repo, since anyone\nwith a merged commit earns it.\n\n### What was observed, same private member, same repo\n\n```\ngithub.event.pull_request.author_association    ->  CONTRIBUTOR\ngithub.event.comment.author_association         ->  MEMBER\nGET /repos/{repo}/pulls/{n} .author_association ->  MEMBER\n```\n\nThree things follow, and all three are now in the file:\n\n- **The two payloads disagree.** That is why `@claude` retriggers kept\nworking for private members\nwhile the on-open review skipped them — the retrigger path reads the\ncomment payload.\n- **The REST value is computed for whoever asks.** A personal token\nreports `MEMBER` where CI sees\n`CONTRIBUTOR`, so validating this gate with a local `gh` call proves\nnothing. That is precisely the\n  mistake behind the bad proposal, and the comment now says so.\n- **The operational requirement is stated at the top**, not implied in\npassing: a member whose org\nmembership is private is skipped exactly like a stranger — silently,\nbehind a green check — so each\n  member has to set their own membership to *Public*.\n\nThe retrigger comment carried the same claim in the *opposite*\ndirection: it warned that private\nmembers appear as `NONE` on the very field that path depends on, when\nthat is the one place the field\ndoes resolve. Corrected, with a note not to \"align\" the two gates onto\nit.\n\n### Verification\n\nZero non-comment lines changed. `actionlint` output identical to `main`\n(five pre-existing findings),\nYAML parses.\n\nThis pull request is also the end-to-end check that the on-open review\nworks now that membership is\npublic — if a verdict appears below from `claude-review` rather than a\nskip notice, the gate is fixed.\n\n### Still outstanding\n\nThe gate requires *public* membership from **every** author, and not\neveryone on the team has flipped\nit yet — those authors' pull requests will keep being skipped silently.\nWorth a nudge; nobody can\nchange it on someone else's behalf.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-07-30T10:52:30Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/81fbbc6fb3d96546fbd3f3d710665ff8abd9eae7/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e"}]},{"sha":"bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGJjZDZhZDQ3NWQxNTYzODRiYjVlNTdiMzZjMDEzZjc1ZmJhOTc3OGU","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-07-30T10:26:49Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-07-30T10:26:49Z"},"message":"ci(review): cap review spend with --max-budget-usd (#673)\n\nThis repo's review pipeline ran the CLI with **no spend ceiling**, so\none runaway exploration could\nbill without bound — and this is the org's one **public** repo, which is\nwhere that matters most.\nDefault is `$10.00` per review.\n\n### Why the membership gate wasn't already enough\n\nIt stops a fork pull request from triggering a review at all, which is\nthe right control for *who*\ncan spend. It does nothing about *how much* a legitimate member's large\npull request costs. Sibling\nrepos on the org's shared pipeline have billed **$6.11 on a two-file\ndiff**. The ceiling is the\nmissing half of that control, not a replacement for it.\n\nIt's a **runaway guard, not a budget target**: the CLI checks it between\nturns, so a run can overshoot\nby about one turn, and on a single hung turn it never fires at all — the\njob timeout is the only bound\nthere. Sized above observed spend on purpose, because the expensive\nreviews are the ones that find\nreal defects; capping near the average would truncate exactly the runs\nworth paying for.\n\n### The failure branch had to change with it\n\nOtherwise the ceiling would have been the *least* diagnosable outcome in\nthe script. It discarded the\nCLI's stdout and posted \"check workflow logs\" above a log that held\nnothing — and budget exhaustion\nexits `1` exactly like a crash, so hitting the ceiling would have looked\nlike a broken pipeline.\n\nIt now logs the first 2000 chars of stdout (`VAR=$(cmd)` keeps it even\nwhen cmd fails, and claude\nreports auth, quota and budget failures there rather than on stderr) and\nbranches on the\nmachine-readable marker to name the ceiling and the spend.\n\n### Verified, not assumed\n\n**Flag support** — `--max-budget-usd` is in `claude --help` on\n`2.1.159`, the version this workflow\npins in both install steps.\n\n**Validator**, driven through the real script under bash 5.2.21 (what\n`ubuntu-latest` runs):\n\n```\naccept:  10.00  10  .5  2.50\nreject:  0  00  0.0  .0  0.00  .00  .  abc  10,00  $10  1.  -1  1e3\n```\n\nAn empty value takes the `10.00` default — bash `:-` treats unset and\nempty alike, so that's intended,\nnot a hole.\n\n**All three runtime paths**, with a stubbed CLI and `gh`:\n\n| scenario | result |\n|---|---|\n| budget exhausted | posts *\"reached the $7.50 spend ceiling after $7.61\nwithout finishing\"*, exit 1 |\n| ordinary crash | posts the exit code, logs the payload to the job log\n|\n| success | posts the review unchanged — happy path untouched |\n\n(The $7.61-against-$7.50 in that first row is the documented\nbetween-turns overshoot, not a bug.)\n\n### Two deliberate non-changes\n\n`jq -e` rather than `grep -q` for the marker: grep stops reading on a\nmatch, the upstream `printf`\ntakes SIGPIPE, and `pipefail` then turns a **match** into a non-zero\npipeline.\n\n**No `RESULT=\"\"` declaration**, unlike `caura-ai/.github`. That guard\nexists there because a CLI smoke\ntest calls the failure helper before `RESULT` is assigned, and under\nbash 5.2 `${RESULT:0:2000}` on an\nunset variable aborts. Every `$RESULT` reference in this script follows\nthe assignment, so there is\nnothing for `nounset` to trip on — I checked rather than copying the fix\nacross.\n\nNo workflow change: both invocation sites pass env explicitly, so the\nscript-side default applies.\n\n### What this does not do\n\nIt doesn't bring over the rest of the shared pipeline's hardening —\n`--bare`, the `--tools`\nallow-list, the zero-exit `is_error` guard, or the docs-only skip. Those\nare a separate change, and\nthis repo can't consume the shared pipeline at all while it is public\nand `caura-ai/.github` is\nprivate (see #668). This is the one gap worth closing independently of\nthat.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"618080b17579a7c1c0b1a60ef67f766edfb20620","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/618080b17579a7c1c0b1a60ef67f766edfb20620"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaybpCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsRIQAF2piNP7FPGB2GH5ft5mRwv4\ncAY5ci2bzFJrb5sds0dtChf5R6vBLgH02JLkn6quW8lEzq7GlYzkPuLI9C87/foY\nGKL1sp0VT+ZwNA5nxFwn7EJV/KhVK8YVuBqD5GW5dfWCB1amrO1rHE3fSa1CdjwM\nNTCwVO19slOlD7zQqIhx9hu5Z9lGAXKyEMCm27tTBJTTb47RRnE52lexc3UjZqZ7\nDYz2a1qEUMLotAvY44vErdujmBKQG+93np3w5AP8bKs2L6B3aYDBck/6G3z56pam\ncZCYhAQOFyBZo66luUDicaDynHJezJ1c2fqVW6XQPY4cF21pqS7UUKf7Bosz3C05\npK2eF9p6/v/3dkw7eMwNvKyv7NZQKC+oiGwBhgG9mjbBEhorN5ftnnMKhrRuuez4\nGwUMLUIXjtsagZBr4ECNrfiNSjOhgnJZZ6Q03I0O9wlbw5cupqL78vjz4gWnNBtt\nX1bi7PV8Z481ig6UAwotdpGHzkcfbYNN9l1ljCf6uci6/1wYAlBfbY3ciPz/Be0z\nVPwzMJ1s0CYe2z+bbGKcHwhGR/7qZD5IGDTpuoxA3dDzHUZ5tAoLWLh6hZr+PfHn\neKJUFk3GJIveK47zPfM8Sxw3sAzQmqVy2cq1WLHjZ92E+d7I8DamHTn91ugusEh2\nBVnlJgoNApqw/PNWrWG5\n=Awn4\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 618080b17579a7c1c0b1a60ef67f766edfb20620\nparent e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1785407209 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785407209 +0300\n\nci(review): cap review spend with --max-budget-usd (#673)\n\nThis repo's review pipeline ran the CLI with **no spend ceiling**, so\none runaway exploration could\nbill without bound — and this is the org's one **public** repo, which is\nwhere that matters most.\nDefault is `$10.00` per review.\n\n### Why the membership gate wasn't already enough\n\nIt stops a fork pull request from triggering a review at all, which is\nthe right control for *who*\ncan spend. It does nothing about *how much* a legitimate member's large\npull request costs. Sibling\nrepos on the org's shared pipeline have billed **$6.11 on a two-file\ndiff**. The ceiling is the\nmissing half of that control, not a replacement for it.\n\nIt's a **runaway guard, not a budget target**: the CLI checks it between\nturns, so a run can overshoot\nby about one turn, and on a single hung turn it never fires at all — the\njob timeout is the only bound\nthere. Sized above observed spend on purpose, because the expensive\nreviews are the ones that find\nreal defects; capping near the average would truncate exactly the runs\nworth paying for.\n\n### The failure branch had to change with it\n\nOtherwise the ceiling would have been the *least* diagnosable outcome in\nthe script. It discarded the\nCLI's stdout and posted \"check workflow logs\" above a log that held\nnothing — and budget exhaustion\nexits `1` exactly like a crash, so hitting the ceiling would have looked\nlike a broken pipeline.\n\nIt now logs the first 2000 chars of stdout (`VAR=$(cmd)` keeps it even\nwhen cmd fails, and claude\nreports auth, quota and budget failures there rather than on stderr) and\nbranches on the\nmachine-readable marker to name the ceiling and the spend.\n\n### Verified, not assumed\n\n**Flag support** — `--max-budget-usd` is in `claude --help` on\n`2.1.159`, the version this workflow\npins in both install steps.\n\n**Validator**, driven through the real script under bash 5.2.21 (what\n`ubuntu-latest` runs):\n\n```\naccept:  10.00  10  .5  2.50\nreject:  0  00  0.0  .0  0.00  .00  .  abc  10,00  $10  1.  -1  1e3\n```\n\nAn empty value takes the `10.00` default — bash `:-` treats unset and\nempty alike, so that's intended,\nnot a hole.\n\n**All three runtime paths**, with a stubbed CLI and `gh`:\n\n| scenario | result |\n|---|---|\n| budget exhausted | posts *\"reached the $7.50 spend ceiling after $7.61\nwithout finishing\"*, exit 1 |\n| ordinary crash | posts the exit code, logs the payload to the job log\n|\n| success | posts the review unchanged — happy path untouched |\n\n(The $7.61-against-$7.50 in that first row is the documented\nbetween-turns overshoot, not a bug.)\n\n### Two deliberate non-changes\n\n`jq -e` rather than `grep -q` for the marker: grep stops reading on a\nmatch, the upstream `printf`\ntakes SIGPIPE, and `pipefail` then turns a **match** into a non-zero\npipeline.\n\n**No `RESULT=\"\"` declaration**, unlike `caura-ai/.github`. That guard\nexists there because a CLI smoke\ntest calls the failure helper before `RESULT` is assigned, and under\nbash 5.2 `${RESULT:0:2000}` on an\nunset variable aborts. Every `$RESULT` reference in this script follows\nthe assignment, so there is\nnothing for `nounset` to trip on — I checked rather than copying the fix\nacross.\n\nNo workflow change: both invocation sites pass env explicitly, so the\nscript-side default applies.\n\n### What this does not do\n\nIt doesn't bring over the rest of the shared pipeline's hardening —\n`--bare`, the `--tools`\nallow-list, the zero-exit `is_error` guard, or the docs-only skip. Those\nare a separate change, and\nthis repo can't consume the shared pipeline at all while it is public\nand `caura-ai/.github` is\nprivate (see #668). This is the one gap worth closing independently of\nthat.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-07-30T10:26:50Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/bcd6ad475d156384bb5e57b36c013f75fba9778e/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71"}]},{"sha":"e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGUxOGRkOTVjYTE2MzQxMTliZDc2NzkxODIwYTc3NzAzMTE1YmViNzE","commit":{"author":{"name":"arkash20","email":"55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-07-30T08:22:40Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-07-30T08:22:40Z"},"message":"feat(search): pluggable reranking component (MiniLM cross-encoder), ships dark (#669)\n\n## What\nAdds a **second-stage reranking component** — `common/ranking/` —\nmirroring the embedding component one-for-one (Protocol / registry /\nservice / providers), selected by `RANK_PROVIDER` exactly like\n`EMBEDDING_PROVIDER`. A new `RerankResults` pipeline step sits at the\n**rerank seam** (between `ExecuteScoredSearch` and `PostFilterResults`)\nand reorders the candidate pool by the ranker's scores.\n\n## Providers\n| `RANK_PROVIDER` | backend | notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `noop` (default) | identity | true no-op on first-stage order →\n**ships dark** |\n| `local` | in-process `sentence-transformers` CrossEncoder |\n**MiniLM-L6 (22M)** |\n| `fake` | deterministic overlap | tests |\n| `remote` | *(reserved)* | future TEI/Cohere-shaped GPU sidecar via\n`RANK_BASE_URL` |\n\n## Why MiniLM-L6\nGrounded in the A50 quality/latency spike\n(`benchmark/a50_rerank_latency/RESULTS.md`):\n- **Quality:** MiniLM-L6 (22M) reranked **≈ bge-reranker-base (278M)**\non our data — within 1–4pp hit@k, even beating bge @1.\n- **Latency:** MiniLM at small pools ≈ **50–150ms CPU**; bge in-process\nis **1–5s** (not viable) → the big model belongs behind a future\n`remote` GPU provider.\n\n## Ships dark\n`RANK_PROVIDER=noop` is the default and a **true identity** on\nfirst-stage order, so behaviour is unchanged until a deployment/tenant\nopts in (same guarantee as A49/`score_formula`). Every failure path —\nmisconfig, timeout past `RANK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, provider down,\nlength-contract violation — **degrades to first-stage order**. Recall\nnever fails because rerank did. `sentence-transformers` stays\nlazy-imported (already an optional dep of the local embedder) → **no new\nhard dependency**.\n\n## Note on `noop`\nUnlike the design sketch, `noop` returns position-descending scores (a\ntrue identity) rather than `similarity`: first-stage rows are ordered by\nthe composite `score`, not `similarity`, so returning similarity would\nsilently reorder the pool when boosts diverge — not ship-dark.\n\n## Tests\n- `tests/test_ranking_component.py` — protocol conformance, noop\ntrue-identity, fake overlap, service degrade (empty / misconfig /\nlength-contract violation).\n- `tests/pipeline/test_rerank_results.py` — step reorders by ranker\nscore, noop keeps first-stage order, skips on entity-lookup / empty\npool.\n\nAll logic verified locally (pytest suite's DB fixture runs in CI).\n\n## Follow-ups (not in this PR)\n- `remote` provider (GPU bge sidecar).\n- A/B `local` MiniLM vs `noop` on the benchmark; validate quality on\neToro's corpus/embedder (LongMemEval numbers may not transfer).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7c0178076d57ad51d27e68b47df90782d7b5377a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/7c0178076d57ad51d27e68b47df90782d7b5377a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqawnQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAB6YQAHzF4C6dqmBvybtIG7yroGIj\nZkKgso4W/Zr72B8oOy5KFzDlk7EFzzE3m+KTq3E479Z0XBoMsDUuqUIJxOcmrFOR\nnPrjTOCDgkYNQyP0n5c6E5WBNXYkY9Fu/dOfHwwsmCJbJNOJW4wRl2h+BCsfrT8C\npCaSkzunTd26XaRf+DBnDrtrZKQyV0DInEV0s4Au0xIE0WFwlN/pCTjgVmYvN5Xw\nKkDwoxKOya6lZQSCjR0Z+KCk9ObeBzUnHYob9T5r4+0jF99h1coXfDcQUkGpxTv6\n5L/2C1wVsYlEwZ2scDKZpF+Pw6xGUtgmE2mNOqtS+PHXrOSCLw8Q5WxS2V3P0Jl1\nxrvkbFTIV+6jVrdZtPLzh8soBxMIk+S/UZZBlHCMDAVzRsNpcfv97oD+rh3iJmn0\nREz9cfMGSBXPUCOUIXolqrreann/aLRAig04xQ4te9N4ftIaG2c2LfV4Jaw2hJY6\nyTAVm3aV5K/+CPjrHbomFAr4G7ByFhBt0COaZjP+9Y0WiHC1OSZRCguz4W7BuvSo\n9U/nuObbhfYTRoJnannHD2xrXZDMtLHTK45Bl1r6uxxuXUBTc837OeRH/LvTIaev\nPJzvjyxf6zz+uc+S8xJYd1F5r9soJ2VzFYtG63y5rre3WLM4SldDvo3Q+NbcROIW\nxN1+SMQfiNfdn8pWYOmB\n=2giM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 7c0178076d57ad51d27e68b47df90782d7b5377a\nparent 2ab70bf05085f9891fa0512d2754b6e7a2fe3a75\nauthor arkash20 <55495209+arkash20@users.noreply.github.com> 1785399760 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785399760 +0300\n\nfeat(search): pluggable reranking component (MiniLM cross-encoder), ships dark (#669)\n\n## What\nAdds a **second-stage reranking component** — `common/ranking/` —\nmirroring the embedding component one-for-one (Protocol / registry /\nservice / providers), selected by `RANK_PROVIDER` exactly like\n`EMBEDDING_PROVIDER`. A new `RerankResults` pipeline step sits at the\n**rerank seam** (between `ExecuteScoredSearch` and `PostFilterResults`)\nand reorders the candidate pool by the ranker's scores.\n\n## Providers\n| `RANK_PROVIDER` | backend | notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `noop` (default) | identity | true no-op on first-stage order →\n**ships dark** |\n| `local` | in-process `sentence-transformers` CrossEncoder |\n**MiniLM-L6 (22M)** |\n| `fake` | deterministic overlap | tests |\n| `remote` | *(reserved)* | future TEI/Cohere-shaped GPU sidecar via\n`RANK_BASE_URL` |\n\n## Why MiniLM-L6\nGrounded in the A50 quality/latency spike\n(`benchmark/a50_rerank_latency/RESULTS.md`):\n- **Quality:** MiniLM-L6 (22M) reranked **≈ bge-reranker-base (278M)**\non our data — within 1–4pp hit@k, even beating bge @1.\n- **Latency:** MiniLM at small pools ≈ **50–150ms CPU**; bge in-process\nis **1–5s** (not viable) → the big model belongs behind a future\n`remote` GPU provider.\n\n## Ships dark\n`RANK_PROVIDER=noop` is the default and a **true identity** on\nfirst-stage order, so behaviour is unchanged until a deployment/tenant\nopts in (same guarantee as A49/`score_formula`). Every failure path —\nmisconfig, timeout past `RANK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, provider down,\nlength-contract violation — **degrades to first-stage order**. Recall\nnever fails because rerank did. `sentence-transformers` stays\nlazy-imported (already an optional dep of the local embedder) → **no new\nhard dependency**.\n\n## Note on `noop`\nUnlike the design sketch, `noop` returns position-descending scores (a\ntrue identity) rather than `similarity`: first-stage rows are ordered by\nthe composite `score`, not `similarity`, so returning similarity would\nsilently reorder the pool when boosts diverge — not ship-dark.\n\n## Tests\n- `tests/test_ranking_component.py` — protocol conformance, noop\ntrue-identity, fake overlap, service degrade (empty / misconfig /\nlength-contract violation).\n- `tests/pipeline/test_rerank_results.py` — step reorders by ranker\nscore, noop keeps first-stage order, skips on entity-lookup / empty\npool.\n\nAll logic verified locally (pytest suite's DB fixture runs in CI).\n\n## Follow-ups (not in this PR)\n- `remote` provider (GPU bge sidecar).\n- A/B `local` MiniLM vs `noop` on the benchmark; validate quality on\neToro's corpus/embedder (LongMemEval numbers may not transfer).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Arkady Mankovsky <arkash20@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-07-30T08:22:41Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e18dd95ca1634119bd76791820a77703115beb71","comments_